            <Title>The Blank</Title>
            <Synopsis>Just a bit of narrative about the background of the blank and where he ended up.  Explains a few things.  Can be incorporated in the book elsewhere or in conversations between characters.  The information is not vitally important, but it does explain a couple of things.  It is only 2 pages long.
</Synopsis>
            <Text>The Blank
(14 hours before bombing)
Need more information in the introduction into this section.
The blank was loaded into the coronial meat wagon and the two company operatives set about finding the best and most profitable place to deliver the dead body. There was not much money in processing the dead and consequently most of the mortuaries were publicly owned and operated. The few private institutions were attached to teaching hospitals that conducted scandalous experiments under the guise of research. No one had yet created a Frankensteinian abomination, but it did not stop them trying. They paid good money for a fresh body and the blank was a fresh as they came, but they had been re-directed to a public facility in another security zone and had spent most of the day arguing with customs officials.
The blank was Emil Kalheri, a hard-working and decent man from a large family of builders and labourers. He was not wealthy, a level 1 could not be, but his family did not go without. He was just the kind of soft-hearted fuck-wit company agents could easily manipulate. When he was diagnosed with terminal testicular cancer, it had already metastasised, leaving three months of precious life. He knew he could not possibly gather together enough money to provide for his family, but he had a stroke of luck when he was contacted by Agents from Pre-emptive Reciprocity and Pro-active Euthanasia. His initial impulse to being recruited as an assassin was to recoil in revulsion and run in the opposite direction, but his family needed money and they were offering plenty. He only had to shoot a scum-bag manager (something most workers had fantasized about at one time or another) who, it was explained to him, was a prolific paedophile.
It was early evening when Agent Jones arrived at the morgue, but Emil was waiting for him, laid out in the main necropsy room on one of the scores of examination tables used for autopsies, many already underway. Rows and rows of pathologists spoke quietly and precisely into microphones hanging from above while they hacked, sawed and sliced through bone, meat and sinew. They slopped whole organs and tissue samples into jars, under the gaze of trainee physicians who watched in both fascination and disgust, some barely keeping down their lunch, while autopsy technicians and assorted ghouls giggled in macabre amusement. The head Pathologist was laughing hysterically as she scuttled out of the necropsy room, pointing at the puking kid-doctors. 
Apart from his clothing, Emil had not a scintilla of personal property and it was now reasonably certain that the cleaner had possession of the disc. Agent Jones rightly figured that, in the ordinary course of events, this cleaner would not be too difficult to track down, but no matter how he interrogated the link, no matter what angle he took, nothing could be found. Nothing! He knew this to be impossible, the Intel-network had access to every data-base in the Dominion. Everyone left a footprint somewhere and most leave craters and divots everywhere, but he could make no headway with this fellow. There were very few people in the entire Dominion who were afforded this level of anonymity and it was an absolute certainty that none of them were low-life cleaning contractors. Agent Jones and his ilk, by necessity, skulked around in the shadows to accomplish their dastardly deeds and were non-existent in any public record. Was the cleaner a member of another clandestine organisation? If so, he was unaware of them, but that did not mean they did not exist.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="5CE48815-129F-4643-A11D-0461DA098AFE">
            <Title>Hierarchy of ideas</Title>
            <Text>1. Most important is the virus. 

I wanted to look at what would happen if someone created a really good/helpful virus.  How would the structure of power etc react.  I decided that the ultra wealthy would not want the virus to get out as a number of industries would be destroyed. 

Do any characters express the POV of these industries?

Basically fuck the poor they can all die horribly, just as long as the money keeps flowing.

Only a really shitty society would completely reject a virus of that nature.  So it is set in a dystopia where the poor are crushed under foot. (As George Orwell put it - "a boot stamping on a human face forever." ) I have written that the Primary Trade Dominion rejects religion, but carries on with Business/Economic Theory as if it is religion - initially stealing Icons from religion as a recruitment tool (just like actual religions).  So they carry on like business is a religion and doctrinal divergence is not tolerated.  Death to anyone, airborne or cloud born, who challenges the structure of the Business Theology. 

I have called upon "Science and Reason" to undermine the structures of the PTD.

3. Cracker (references in 47 files) and Sarah (43)

Refer to character summaries.

It is only the intervention and interaction with The Three Sages (or other literary device) and the Elvi that either are able to make a difference.

2. Evolution and The Dawkins (27)

The particular area of science and reason I have focussed upon is, of course, evolution and its clash with religion.  Evolution and religion are fundamentally opposed and cannot co-exist.  Whether Dawkins is the great prophet of the book or one of a number of prophets (Carl Sagan, Stephen Hawking, Christopher Hitchens.....the list could be very long.) is probably not vital, but I am still keen on someone becoming God.  (To become god all you have to do is save the universe or alter one of the universal constants or reverse engineer failed universes).

3. Elvi’s (30)

Refer to character summaries.

4. The Disc (~20)

This "McGoober" (McGuffin) is important, but it does not necessarily have to be a disc.  It just has to have the capacity (or alleged capacity) of great destruction by messing with gravity and space-time.  As discussed it can have some feature that is most sought after - invisibility,  can slow down time or alter gravity on a localised level or make your dick big.  You know, something that everyone will think is interesting and of some considerable value. 

The "McGoober" has to be partially biological as its nature is altered when it is infected by the virus.  The alterations in the dics later allows Richard Dawkins to stop the cycle of failed Universes (boom and bust universes).

5. Sages (7)

Not that important. 

Refer to character summaries.

6. Alexander Shakti (~30)

I think quite important. 

Refer to character summaries.

7. Eloise Shakti (13)

Refer to character summaries.

8. Dr Kathy Logan (17)

Refer to character summaries.

9. Haley Lanning (~25)

Refer to character summaries.

10. Agent Jones (~14)

Refer to character summaries.

11. The Johnny (15)

Refer to character summaries.

</Text>
        </Document>
