        <Document ID="1F327B57-ACD4-44C9-A31E-A0A6A4D894A9">
            <Title>Was music the winner?</Title>
            <Text>Was music the winner? 
At the end of the day, after the gospel hymns have been sung, and the bacon fat returned to the larder, that’s the question historians would argue over for centuries. While they dressed their arguments with scholarly words, and laced them with logical phalluses and professional enmities, the debate boiled down to a simple, consumer-need question:
If one Elvis was great, how good would it be if every home could own their very own Elvis?
Or, put more simply:
How good was Elvis?
The answer is, of course, he was the greatest. Thank you very much.
A longer answer requires we go back, perhaps surprisingly, to nine months before The Dawkins, to a time of explosions and viral inseminations.
It was also a time of such religious fervour, the like of which this universe never saw before or since.
Well, we warned you about the gospel music, didn’t we?</Text>
            <Notes>What is the perspective of the narrators? Are they in the future timeline after the Elvi become the dominant version of humanity? Are they products of the Elvi Enlightendment?</Notes>
