        <Document ID="C7073512-07FA-4DF4-8D5F-ABDDAB2AEA1B">
            <Title>Panacea</Title>
            <Synopsis>Back story of the development of the virus.  
Jamison Hardy requests to get innoculated.  Professor Herrman (Hate the name) injects him, but it is nothing but saline. 
Another employee of Hardy requests an innoculation - He calls himself Kleo.  (Kleo gets the virus but nothing is mentioned here or anywhere else for that matter.</Synopsis>
            <Text>Panacea
20 years Before the Dawkins
Ulyissia.
 
Professor Hamish Hermann was a hyperactive basket case when Jameson and his entourage arrived, but he looked great. His skin was glowing, rosy red cheeks, with the age lines, bumps and weird lumps that had appeared over the years, all gone. It was not only his skin, he stood tall, bolt-upright and muscular, like a marine in boot camp. This was not the same frail dweeb Jamison had lunch with a few weeks prior. He was wholly riddled with good health. 
“It is good to see you sir, so good to see you.” Said the professor, genuinely. Then, looking at the horde of sycophants gathered in a tight bundle behind the boss, “We will have to speak alone. This is very sensitive and very exciting.” 
Jameson’s entourage stood where they were, looking at the ceiling or the floor, anywhere but in the eyes of their boss. “Brown-nosers be gone.” He cheerfully ordered and the gormless gang reluctantly scuttled out the door. “You too, Kleo.”
“You are going to cure the world Chairman Hardy. The whole world.” He could not suppress his inner Cheshire cat.
“Yeah….I gotta say I am very concerned. You or, rather we, will be destroying entire industries. Not to mention over population. Where are we going to put all these healthy fuckers?” Jameson said appearing to be joking but was not. 
“Well, we won’t have to put them in hospital, ever.” He wasn’t going to let his boss bum him out. He had achieved what no one else in human history had achieved. How many people could say that?
“Does everyone here know what’s going on?” Jameson said, hoping that the Professor had implemented some security measures.
“My team and I have told no-one else of our conclusions, however, others must suspect something. Everyone must have noticed I am looking younger by the minute.”
“How does it work?” 
“We don’t know exactly. It seems to optimize every biological process within every cell of the body. Thinking is clearer, memory perfect, breathing easier and taste – in fact all senses have been fine tuned. The immune system is on hyper-drive, it destroys invaders efficiently, mercilessly, but causes no anaphylaxis. I don’t need glasses anymore. Both short and long sight are far superior. I see, hear and remember everything.”
“How did you stumble across it? I can’t imagine you did it on purpose.” Surely no one would be stupid enough to do this on purpose.
“It is a by-product of our bio-weapons research. As you are no doubt aware we succeeded in creating biological devices that target specific sections of the human genome and we were working on phase two, devices targeting specific individuals. We were trying to make the virus hardy enough to survive without water or air so we used etremomoprh DNA to horizontally transfer genetic traits, but instead of making the virus impervious to attack, it causes the host to become impervious.” The professor was the happiest camper Jamison had ever seen, but he clearly had no idea what he had done. 
Jameson was perfectly aware of the target-specific virus research. He ordered the research himself, but the concept had not been his idea. For a couple of centuries it had been the dream of depots of all kinds to secure control of a virus that would target his enemy. The old nation of China had various and numerous failures in trying to target the Caucasian race. One city in central China (find the location Wuhan) China was the hub of all bio-weapons research, but had the cover of being the largest manufacturer of medical masks, supplying 80% of the the entire world’s need. The city itself consumed 10% of all the world’s masks, a most peculiar statistic (what were those fuckers up to?) that only became relevant after numerous containment failures of their viral catastrophes. Although the release of the bird-flu and swine flu had been deliberate, just to test the waters, SARS and MERS somehow escaped containment and set about indiscriminately killing their countrymen. The final straw was the premature release of COVID-19, a virus that had promised to target Caucasians, wiped out half of the city and then on a rampage world wide. The death rate was nothing like they were aiming for, but it well truly fucked the economies of most countries, including their own. The epi-centre of the outbreak made it clear where the manufactured virus had originated and the standard denial were greeted as lies. A whistle-blower clinician had tried to get the word out, but even though he was a health man in his prime, he somehow succumbed to the disease, quickly and silently. The research was shut down and all involved were re-educated. 
Jameson had no problem picking up the research. The pay-off for success was just too big to ignore.
“Can you die?” Jameson hoped so.
“Not from disease or even catastrophic injuries although, no doubt, I will die of old age eventually. Wounds and broken bones repair themselves at 10 times, no 20 times the normal rate. I can hold my breath for an inordinate period of time. Hours and it doesn’t seem to affect me. I suspect the body simply draws oxygen out of the air directly through the skin – the adaptive abilities of this organism are amazing, although, if I was blown up or torn into small pieces, I think my life would be irretrievable.” 
“How many are infected?”
“Just the research team, four all here.” He fibbed. “It can only be transferred through the exchange of bodily fluids, blood and semen.” 
“It’s an STD?”
“Yep. The clap that will save the world.” Professor Herrman was a now laughing loudly.
“I see the virus has had no effect on your sense of humour.” Jameson kindly pointed out.
“What’s next, sir?”
“We will have to keep this under wraps. This facility goes into total lock-down. You and your team cannot leave, under any circumstances. We do not want this virus getting out.”
“I understand. What will I tell them?” His team would not be very happy. They had discussed fleeing the jurisdiction, but President Kruger’s war on everything had recently shut down all commercial air traffic, worldwide, until he could work out what was going on. Professor Hermann accurately predicted that Kruger would never work out what was going on and international flights would not be re-scheduled for quite some time. They would be trapped in Ulyssia and hunted down. No, their best chance was to stay put and for the time being, play ball.
“Tell them not to worry, that everything is under control. Tell them we have received credible intelligence about a terrorist plot to destroy this facility and it has been locked down for their safety. Do not tell anyone else about the virus.”
“Of course, sir.”
“One more thing. I would like to be infected, Professor.” As soon as he got back to his building he planned to jump back into his Full Body Scanner and test the Professor’s claim. His results were pretty much the same from one day to the next and if the Professor was right, the results would be wildly different to this morning’s readout.
“I’m not that sort of man, sir. I don’t think I could manage, you know?” The prof was red faced and genuinely embarrassed.
“Professor, I was thinking more along the lines of an injection.” The look on the professor’s face was priceless and Jameson could not help but be amused. 
“The easiest way for me to cut my finger and jab you in your eye. Are you up for that?” 
“No. An injection will be fine.” He had already rolled up his sleeve and was waiting. “Hurry up, I haven’t got all day. It’s nearly 6.00am already.”
The professor moved quickly, smoothly, with purpose and although he did not have a vial of virus just lying around, in a bewildering blur of activity Jameson was inoculated and then out the door. He waited a short time, before one of Jamison’s staff made and appearance. “Who are you?”
“Call me Kleo,” he said.
</Text>
        </Document>
