            <Title>Exorcism</Title>
            <Synopsis>Kleo conducts an exorcism.  The dialogue is based upon the dialogue in "The Exorcist" (movie)  Supposed to be a parody.
There is a good debate between one of the possessed Elvi and Kleo - many of Dawkins's arguments are covered.
</Synopsis>
            <Text>Exorcism
2 months after bombing
The High Elvis had forbid direct contact with the infected, lest the contagion escape. To stop Christianity creeping into their brains, all but a few select Elvi were ordered to wear ear-plugs when within ear-shot and even then the exposure was kept to a minimum. Notwithstanding the danger these Christians represented, the High Elvis was most reluctant to beat or torture one of their own but, as it turned out, the Epileptic Elvi were proud of what they had done, freely admitting to seditious acts against science and reason. The Dawkins wept.
* * *
“They present very calm, great Sages, but when met with rational argument, they become wild eyed and agitated. Much to our shame, we had to resort to chaining them to their beds. They are through here, the four rooms immediately to the right. Beware, there is something godly happening here.” Security Supervisor Elvis was genuinely frightened of the process of non-thinking called religion. His genome was susceptible to this meme and he did not fancy the prospects of losing his curiosity or his individualism. 
“It does not matter what you do to our flesh.” Christian Elvis No 1 was adamant no one could change his mind about the Jesus. “We will be born again in the house of the lord. Jesus is the way the truth and the light. Praise him!” 
“You have not been born of water.” Kleo was well versed in Christian fiction.
“I have been baptised.” Christian Elvis 1 quickly countered.
“Being born of woman is being born of water. You are a clone. You are born of syringe and petri-dish.” Kleo heaped scorn upon ridicule. “You have no soul.”
“You lie. I have a soul.” He hissed as his eyes popped and arteries in his forehead bulged and throbbed. “I have faith in the ancient scriptures.”
“Your scripture has too many lies and inconsistencies to be regarded as anything but fantasy.” 
“The scriptures are the word of god and therefore the truth. The life of Jesus of is no fantasy.”
“It is an historical fact that the Gospels were scribed by those who almost certainly never met Jesus personally and were all devoutly convinced that his life must fulfill Old Testament prophecies. So sayeth the Dawkins.”
“They are the stories of witnesses to my Lord Jesus and his miracles.” 
“They were written decades after his death, are glaringly contradictory and simply implausible.”
“Jesus gave his life for all our sins.”
“Jesus knew dad was going to revive him. In those circumstances, dying is no sacrifice at all. He was sent on a suicide mission for no good reason.”
“You insult the Lord with your blasphemy.”
“I cannot insult something that does not exist.”
“Your Evolution is nothing but a theory and your Dawkins was not an intellectual, but a failed celebrity.”
“The elegance of Darwinian Theory, has withstood punctilious examination for nearly two centuries, with no credible threat emerging. As science has advanced, Darwin has been proven correct, over and over again. The existence of God has been considered by the brightest minds for millennia, with no reliable evidence surfacing. Other than blind faith, there is no reason to believe in God.” 
 
“What about Thomas Aquinas?” Christian Elvis 1 was impressed with the logical progression of Aquinas and was unable to fault him.
 
“The Chronicles of the Dawkins tested Aquinas reductio ad absurdium and made him look a fool. Praise be to his wisdom.” Kleo immediately countered. “In the absence of any evidence and in the same manner we reject Santa Claus, we must adopt the default position that God does not exist. We are then left with the explanations offered up by science, that time and space started with a single event and the expansion of the universe has unfolded under the influence of natural forces, one being evolution. If evidence of God’s existence does come to light, then I would reassess my position on the matter. This is the way of science, this is the way of the Dawkins” 
“Hartle-Hawking requires no single event.” He was struggling now. He had read something about it recently, but had not really taken it all in.
“Whether the Universe started with a single event or simply exists is another issue. Neither theory lend credence to god. In fact, Hartle-Hawking leaves no room for the existence of god. If you want to keep up with me you had better do some more homework, young Elvis.” 
“I just need my faith. Praise be to Jesus. Pledge Now.”
“Clinging to faith is a rebuke of our natural curiosity, a rejection of reasoned thinking. Why would a God give us such a large brain and then ask us not to use it?”
“God gave us such a brain so we could worship him.”
“He created the universe and then waited 14 billion years to be worshipped? The god described in your Bible is, in the words of the great one, ‘the most unpleasant character in all of fiction – jealous and proud of it, vindictive, unjust, unforgiving, racist, an ethnic cleanser urging his people to acts of genocide.’ He asked Abraham to serve up his son to him as a burnt offering. In the name of the Dawkins, does that sound like the request of a moral god?”
“He is the truth from which all morality flows. I am in no position to judge the lord and neither are you.”
“Then I shall speak of the characters mentioned in your scriptures. Moses brought the Ten Commandments down from the mountain and if my memory serves me correctly, one of those commandments was ‘Thou shall not kill’. The book of Numbers sees Moses displaying wanton disregard for the inscriptions carved upon those tablets. He became very angry at the merciful restraint of the Israelites towards the Midianite people and ordered all the men and the older women be put to death. He said ‘but all the women children that have not known a man by laying with him, keep for yourselves.’ Genocide and paedophilia does not a moral tale make.”
“One example.”
“What of the man who was confronted, at his doorstep, by a mob demanding he give up his male guest for rape? He said, ‘Behold here is my daughter, a maiden, and his concubine. Them I will bring out now and humble ye them and do with them what seemeth good unto you, but unto him do not so vile a thing.’ It’s okay to rape a woman, but not a man? How can giving up your own child for rape be morally justifiable on any grounds? There are many stories your bible that could never be mistaken for moral tales.”
“I love Jesus of the New Testament.”
- - - 
Kleo, Silo and Chilo sat before the High Elvis and his nervous cabinet in the committee room. They were hopeful of good news from the enlightened ones, but they looked somber and caught no-one in the eye.
“They are all devout, have no sense of humour and follow neither logic nor reason. This infection has blocked their capacity to comprehend simple scientific facts. They are, for want of a better term, possessed by the Jesus and we have no alternative but to excise this meme and purge them of faith.” Kleo had not seen faith manifest itself with such fervor and doubted anything, at least in the short term, was going to work. 
“How is that achieved? I do not want them to come to harm.” The High Elvis had led a long cabinet session on Elvi detention policy and had concluded, under no circumstances, could the ‘Fucked-up-five’ be deliberately harmed, beaten or tortured. That sort of thing was contrary to any civilised moral code and completely beneath their dignity.
“Of course, High Elvis. I think we should start off by playing rock and roll music, twenty four hours a day. Then science documentaries, interspersed with de-programming sessions where my apprentices and I will challenge their beliefs with rational argument and scientific truth. If this fails we may have to call on ancient methods to rid them of evil.”
“I authorise you and your apprentices to commence the rock n’ roll therapy and the follow up documentary and counselling sessions, wise one.” The High Elvis felt like he was getting used to this leadership thing. “I want regular reports. We will reassess as each day goes by.”
“Thank you, High Elvis. What if the therapy fails?”
“We will continue to try. We have no legal authority to hold them but they represent an unacceptable threat to Elvi culture and we cannot afford to let them go. If we exile them, into the general PTD population, the Directory of Moral Observance will have them killed the moment they out themselves as Christian. I cannot allow that. They must be kept away from the rest of us until they are rendered safe.”
“That could be many years, High Elvis.”
“I fear that to be true, wise one, but I cannot allow my Elvi to be harmed, let alone killed. They are not being held to punish, but to protect.” High Elvis did not think that sitting in the big chair would be so fraught with moral predicaments. When he entered the race for the top job, he reasoned it would be a largely ceremonial position where he would make a few speeches, maybe kiss a few babies and shake some hands. The position itself had no authority within the Primary Trade Dominion, making the detention of five Elvis clones highly illegal. If discovered, he and his leadership team would most certainly be detained by the PTD. “I am not satisfied it is morally unjustifiable to kill an Elvis or by omission allow an Elvis to die.” 
“You will be a great leader, High Elvis.” Kleo and his crew were impressed. This Elvis had done some serious thinking and seemed to have a finely tuned moral compass which he confidently applied to the ever changing circumstance of politics. A skill mostly unknown in the Primary Trade Dominion, where moral compasses were very rare indeed.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="1AA05EBB-27A6-4AF0-8835-34DFF9118653">
            <Title>Random Camera</Title>
            <Text>Shakti became a household name very quickly. His arrival at Public Affair Bureau coincided with the launch of Police Video Streaming. His face was on every Vid-Screen; Vid-board; poster; milk carton, leaflet and some smart-arse even put his face on toilet paper. Although paper products were prohibitively expensive, they sold like hot-cakes, mostly to police officers as it provided an opportunity to do something they had long wanted to do. There were two channels transmitted to levels 1 to 3 and one other channel for the consumption of Level 4 and above. The programming was almost entirely real life docu-dramas and with his flare for publicity and milking it was worth, his show took off. It was the first time in his life that he had useful and, in some cases, interesting ideas that took reality vid-streams to new lows - exactly what was required. Reality TV shows had long since proven that any idiot can be a star and Shakti, even with his advanced level idiocy, gained a level of acclaim.
One program, Random Camera, revolved around surveillance and covert police operations, but not wrong the Public Affairs simpletons stomping around in their domain, Operational Police refused to assist with criminal intelligence. Starved of timely data, Shakti made a decision that only the truly unenlightened could make. He targeted houses at random. With this single decision, Shakti rejected all investigative theories and presumptions – that potent crime suppression can only be effected by basing operations on timely and accurate intelligence. All senior police knew the show would be a failure as their collective experience brought them to a collective conclusion – random policing yields random results. However, with a zero tolerance policy in effect, every show resulted in the unfortunate targets being detected committing some sort of criminal offence. 
Although the judiciary complained bitterly about the creation of an amateur parallel justice system, the amount of revenue raised ensured the program would survive. Clandestine video devices were installed into level 1 and 2 homes, at random, and the action was broadcast live into level 4-plus homes. The upper levels of society lapped it up. Most of them had never set foot on the ground. Living their lives above cloud height they had no idea how the common lived. 
Corporate Infidelity was, by and large, the major crime detected. This was not surprising, considering that to constitute the offence one merely had to breach employer confidentiality or defame a manager. A crime of absolute liability, the simple act of complaining to your spouse about work practices would establish this very serious offence. It struck at the very heart of the corporate structure and disrespect for managerial classes was inexcusable. It was the modern equivalent of heresy and doctrinal divergence of this magnitude was punishable by death and forfeiture all goods and chattels. Guilt and sentencing was decided by the viewers through on-line voting and carried out immediately. Very few in the audience were weak enough to feel compassion or deliver mercy to the accused lows. Most viewers correctly postulated that their voting habits were monitored and analysed for moral weakness –in this case compassion for one’s inferiors. Indulging one’s empathy was a waste of effort and interfered with the ability to make hard business decisions. Being outed for moral weakness had a monumentally negative effect on career paths and, consequently, there was hardly a show without at least one execution. Shows that did not feature the removal of someone’s breathing privileges rated rather poorly and advertising revenue for subsequent shows would plummet. When profits were threatened the corporate machine immediately rolled into action and issued a decree from on high – One show, one body, no excuses. 
To upper society, this was the best show on the link. At 8.00pm every Tuesday and Thursday most, if not all, available level 5-pluses, were plonked in front of their Vid-screens or at level 5 cocktail bars, mesmerized by the outrageous and often bizarre behaviour of the lows. The subtitles at the bottom of the screen assisted them in understanding the gutter talk and expletives used incessantly by the common man. Many obtained no end of amusement in the lows use of syntax and the grotesque linguistic mutations that had developed below the layer. They especially loved it when they lows beat the shit out of each other – domestic violence was hilarious, even to the female viewers. The level of violence was so extreme that it seemed the only thing commoners hated more than management, was each other.
Shakti also had the balls, or the naïve stupidity, to suggest that a similar show be conceived for the consumption of the lows. He envisioned corporate fraudsters and rogue traders being brought to book live on his program. He would be an icon to the unwashed masses. The show went ahead, but Shakti was not made aware of some of the minor changes. All of the people featured on show were actors or impostors and no level 5’s were harmed during the production of any episode. Fueled by total ignorance, Shakti took to his new second show with a fervent enthusiasm. His genuine belief in the integrity of the program gave the façade a credibility that struck a chord with the discontented lows. At last someone was doing something about the morally bankrupt managers and the corporations they serve. 
In reality, anyone caught on the show was a convicted low already on a death sentence. They knew that the lows had an extraordinary and inexplicable attachment to family members and, like vultures, they pecked and tore at this devotion until leverage was found. To ensure they played along the production company promised social promotion and employment for their families. They were going to die anyway and figured that they may as well do some good for the kids before they got zapped. The final carrot dangled in front of the impostor was the possibility, as slim as it might be, that the audience of the lows might vote to spare him or her. Fat chance, they loved their payback. </Text>
            <Notes>Competitive Show: Curb your Individualism … a public safety show designed to scare people into corformity “Safety is Conformity”


First appears with: the Commissioner (Which one?)

alternative names:

	•	Alexi Smearoff
</Notes>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="D1B2106C-ADA9-47D1-8AC4-AB1FF83C1549">
            <Title>The Big Sleep</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="E2B2295C-D33C-490D-BA60-89F6550D654D">
            <Title>20 years before The Dawkins</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="DA81AECA-FBE8-4099-9A2E-ECE50154185B">
            <Title>Fin</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="33FFF69F-D222-4CA3-A8AD-93CE7A649809">
            <Title>Dr Logan</Title>
            <Synopsis>Hayley Lanning visits the morgue to locate Emil Kalheri, the blank.  This is important, but obviously it can be edited and superfluous information can be removed.
Intro to Dr Logan
Find out intelligence agents have been also asking about the blank.
There is a minor circular burn on the blank's leg - later find out it is a radiation burn.</Synopsis>
            <Text>Dr Logan
Day after bombing
Finding the body of Emil took Detective Supt Lanning next to no effort at all. She figured he might be dead and issued all hospitals, morgues and funeral homes with a “Search and Comply Order”, immediately getting a response from an enthusiastic and giggly pathologist. By the end of the day she had a copy of the preliminary report, along with the all gruesome holographs and vids, which unequivocally found the cause of the death to be testicular cancer and related metastases. The body was yet to be transferred and she took the opportunity to make a visit to the morgue presently holding him on ice. 
Pungent fumes of hospital strength disinfectant promptly mugged her senses and triggered a stream of salt water from the corner of each eye. She did not know why, but she expected all morgue attendants and pathologists to be pale skinned ghouls with black lipstick and an even darker sense of humour, so it was with a little surprise when she was met by a pretty young blonde with pig-tails, a deeply tanned complexion and a wide bright smile. 
“Dr Katherine Logan MBBS PhD L.5, but please call me Kathy. I am so happy to meet you Superintendent!” Kathy gushed through the ever broadening grin.
“Hayley Lanning, PhD, L.5. I want to see all records relating to Emil Kalheri. I was notified by this office that he is here at the moment.” Announced Detective Supt Lanning in her most official voice, hoping the tone might make the happy face disappear. 
“Emil Kalheri? Yes, he’s a guest at the moment. DNA identification, definitely him. Checked in…..yep, yesterday.” The bubbly smile said as she checked the data files. “He’s not due for checkout until….Ooh that’s interesting.”
“What’s that?”
“The body can be released immediately. The fee is already paid. Wow!” She was wide-eyed and seemed gob-smacked by this little revelation, but still very cheerful. “Very unusual.”
 
“Who paid?” Detective Lanning was interested. Whoever holds the purse strings, runs the show.
“That is the interesting bit, I can’t get that information up.” Dr Logan laughed. “What’s all the curiosity for this man anyway?” She probed, “The spooks were here yesterday asking all sorts of questions about him.”
“Spooks?” Supt Lanning’s attention level was immediately raised. “Do you mean Covert Company Contractors?”
“I don’t know what they are called. He was creepy, slimy and scary as all hell.” She recalled with a small shudder. “He was not fooling around, I’m telling you. He made sure I saw his gun, too. He asked loads of questions, searched the body and his property and then left.”
“What did he want?” Hayley softly pressed the pretty ghoul.
“He asked a lot of questions, but he was mainly interested in the property. Was there any other property? Where is the rest of his stuff? We told him he had access to all the artifacts the body arrived with, but he was not happy with that.” 
“Do you think you would recognise him?”
“Yes, I think so, but I am better with dead bodies.” She laughed out loud. Hayley figured that was what passed for humour in pathology circles.
“Can I examine the body?” She never looked forward to checking out the deceased, but it was necessary and quite often productive.
“Of course. He’s in drawer 213, this way.” Dr. Cheerful skipped down a long corridor, past the main necropsy room to central storage where hundreds of the recently unlucky lay, mutely awaiting their final ride home. “Here he is, the man of the hour, Emil Kalheri. Ta Da.” Kathy clapped and laughed as she stepped back, allowing the now very annoyed Police Detective access.
She took her time and examined the body carefully, methodically, forensically. His hard worked hands were huge and layered in a miscellany of callouses, cuts and grazes and adorned with disfigured bent digits, many times broken and re-broken. His body swore silent testament to a life of burdensome physical labour and inaccessible health care, with lumpy poorly knitted fractures and jagged scars punctuating a ruddy malnourished frame. She identified only one minor blemish that appeared to be recent, a small circular red mark, no larger than a thumb nail on the inside of the right thigh.
“What do you think this is?” Hayley asked, pointing to the small perfect circle.
“My best guess would be a mild burn of some sort. It looks pretty recent, but it’s a bit hard to tell. I’ll get a sample.” Kathy burst into action and within a minute she had sliced a decent chunk out of the Emil’s dead leg and sent it for testing. “We should get that back tomorrow, unless it is urgent, but you know how much we charge for rush jobs, don’t you?”
“It’s a rush job.” Lanning
“What about the cost?” Kathy did not want to get stuck with an unpaid invoice.
“Bill the anonymous benefactors. If you get a complaint, we will know where the money is coming from.” Lanning. “Now, the report says the cause of death was testicular cancer. Is that correct?” 
“Yes, it had spread to his bones and he was terminal, but he also had a massive dose of AMT in his blood, enough to keep an elephant awake for a month” Kathy explained.
“AMT? Where would a labourer get hold of AMT?”
“It’s not available at all and as far as I know has not been manufactured in years. I could not even hazard a guess as to where he got it.” Kathy was most sincere, but still cheery and permanently exposing her teeth.
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="AFF26F64-A6EB-4E86-8837-B17296338C32">
            <Title>A Big Bang for the Big Bang</Title>
