        <Document ID="CE8D918E-3991-435E-98EE-C7C8C3F3A4A7">
            <Title>Hangover</Title>
            <Synopsis>Standard hangover scenario.  Some of it may be worthwhile, but not necessary to the story. 
A small description of Cracker's premises.  
Good description of Crackers face after the bombing - must stay in.  "His blackened left eye was firmly closed shut and continually seeped a dilute blood-ooze that trickled over the collection of lumps, cuts, scratches and bruises that together contrived to construct something not too dissimilar to a face."
Cracker and Sarah discover that Cracker must have picked up the gyges disc from inside the office because he is seen by surveillance video to enter the office, but not to exit.
They rifle through Crackers "spoils: of the day and find ID for Emil Kalheri and a general access chip - access to all electronic doors in the PTD.  The general access chip becomes important at a later stage.

They find the disc and decide that they should speak to someone with computer engineering/hardware expert.  Luckily Sarah knows just the guy.  However, Cracker cannot go with her as her friend is airborne and he cannot know she has been hanging out with the cloudborne.  </Synopsis>
            <Text>Hangover
Morning after Bombing -Year Zero
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="639B1C34-7A88-43CD-8647-C3EFA33FD134">
            <Title>Fight and the end of the tunnel.</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="76342923-EA25-41ED-BD9C-74111954DD2F">
            <Title>Unexpected Expecting</Title>
            <Synopsis>More information about Dr Kathy Logan and her love of "patching".


Cracker and Sarah find out Emil Kalheri had been there.  (Dr Logan bills the anonymous benefactor who paid for the processing of Emil Kalheri's body.)</Synopsis>
            <Text>Unexpected Expecting
8 to 10 days after the bombing
It had only taken him a few days to organise the patches, all his old contacts still in business, were obliging and well stocked. The biggest issue arose when Cracker tried to arrange a work visa for the Health, Respite and Retreat Zone (HRRZ), which took another 7 days of submitting forms, arguing, negotiating and bullshitting Quarantine and Immigration Officials. 
The HRRZ, a Level 10 Security area was highly restricted, existing solely for the purpose of pampering the imagined ailments of the ultra-rich. Only those workers absolutely necessary to meet the needs of the well-heeled clients were allowed the honour of breathing the same air as the chosen few. That meant a bloated workforce replete with over-paid sycophants and underpaid slaves. There were so many dirty working class drones, in fact, that they required their own medical centre, the very facility Cracker and Sarah were to meet.
Foremost on Cracker’s mind was, against all odds, impressing Sarah when he met up with her again. Acting cool was not something he had ever been able to pull off, falling painfully short of the mark each occasion he had made the attempt. Pretending to be someone else did not come easily to him and the façade could only be sustained for a short time before eventually trapping himself in a nonsensical network of deception. In all but exceptional cases, he cared not what others thought of him, but Sarah was most exceptional and he did not want to look the fool. It would be the worst of all fates to be dismissed by her as a joke and although he would not openly admit it, his most dreadful fear was to be laughed at by the woman he loved. The shock of being cut down so easily by a mere giggle and being left so deflated, triggered within him an unconscious primeval explosion of rage, erupting from somewhere deep in his caveman brain. His validity as a man rejected in its entirety, or so it felt. He had always managed to swallowed it down long enough for more coherent thought to return, but was ashamed something so innocuous could inspire such primordial anger. 
 
Cracker would be nothing but polite and obliging as possible, holding out no hope she would ever even like him. He would not tell jokes and he would keep sexual innuendo to a bare minimum, exposing Sarah to the full brunt of his banality. With no expectations he would have no disappointments and when he finally got her, he thought, it would be even sweeter. 
Sarah did not know why they had to meet at the seedy rear of the neglected medical centre where, she feared, they threw out bio-hazard waste and hacked off limbs. “What are we doing here? I put the security cameras are on a loop, there is no chance of being recorded.”
“This is where we are going.” Cracker pointed to a faded sign, hanging 30 degrees or more out of level, bearing one word – Morgue. 
“The morgue? This is where we get our tests?” This made Sarah nauseous, even more so than she had been over the past couple of weeks.
“They’ve got the best resources in the entire public hospital system. C’mon, I’ll introduce you to Dr Logan. You are in for a treat. She is the second smartest person I have ever met.”
“Second, huh? Sounds impressive.” Sarah replied, assuming she was the smartest person he had ever met.
They entered through unlocked security doors and were immediately hit with the overpowering stench of industrial grade disinfectant that and scorched their nostrils, causing Sarah’s stomach to spasm as she tried to hold down her dinner. 
“Cracker!” Yelled the good Dr Logan from behind a dirty, cluttered reception desk. She hurdled the desk and hugged Cracker like a much loved brother.
“Kathy, it’s so good to see you. How long has it been?” Cracker grinned, only now recalling the delight of her company.
“About 4 years I think. I’m not working the wards now. They decided working on the dead was the best option for me. Just as long as everything gets done, they let me do what I like.” 
“And I know what you like!” Cracker broke out a box full of patches, some old favourites and some very new experiments. 
“Wow, Surprise Party. I love these, they are so unpredictable. Thank you.” It was well after hours and well past the time to patch-up again. 
Cracker first bumped into his contact in the world of pathology when he was contracted for regular maintenance in the public medical centres of The Pale. The first year intern had knocked off for the day, stumbled out of the staff exit and staggered off into the night. Hours later they crossed paths again in the quarter when he spied her, arms littered with sticky and variously coloured patches, dancing solo in the street. She had been hyper-patched by a crew of scoundrels and by that time Cracker found her, she was completely incoherent and unable, even, to remove the patches. Her pre-rape stagger saw Kathy a prime candidate for abuse and although his natural reaction should have been leave her to her fate, Cracker adopted the role of protector and managed to fight off a few would-be assailants to get her back to the hospital dorm intact. For the next couple of years, he would fetch patches for her, limiting the good doctor’s exposure to the vultures on the street, but had lost contact when she was transferred to the Health, Respite and Retreat Zone.
Patches were perfect recreation for the self-medicating urban professional. Hallucinogens, stimulants, depressants, performance enhancers and anything else medical miscreants can cook up were infused into patches, adhered to the upper arm and a party ensues. Some patches were medication masala, a combination of many flavours, but Kathy’s favourite was “Organised Party”. You could plan the perfect night out with the release of different drugs at specific times, seamlessly slipping from one buzz to the other or into periods of absolute clarity then plunging into psychedelic hilarity. Unless you had been the victim of hyper-patching, the effects begin to wear off directly upon removal, but Kathy patched all day, everyday, her work schedule neatly wedged in between periods of incoherence of one type or another. She rolled up her sleeve, closed her eyes and fished her hand around in the box, picking a patch at random.
“Wait a minute. Can we get to why we are here?” Sarah wanted to leave the morgue as soon as possible. “Sarah Jacobson, PhD, L.5.” She offered her hand and they shook in business-like fashion.
“Kathy Logan, MBBS, L.5. Is this your girlfriend, Cracker?” She asked with a tone of suspicion. This L.5 is stunning and Cracker is, average at best, but I must admit he is looking healthier than ever. How on earth did he make friends with her?
“No.” Sarah pursed her lips into a cats-bum configuration. 
“Sounds unequivocal. I was going to say she is a bit out of your league, young man. How did you two meet?”
“We met at work.” Cracker said truthfully.
“Cleaning out her office I bet. Ha, Ha.” Even though she was not patching at that point in time, she still giggled after everything said.
“Yep.” Cracker admitted as he shuffled his feet.
“So from what Cracker tells me you want routine bloods and scans? What am I looking for?
“We were exposed to a bio-agent. We don’t know what it is.” Sarah decided to be short and to the point. Brevity would speed up the process.
“Well, that was a bit silly, wasn’t it? Do you have a sample?” Kathy was hopeful. It makes it a hell of a lot easier when you know what to look for.
“Sorry.” Cracker silently scolded himself. He should have thought of getting a sample.
“Pathology blindfolded, my favourite.” Kathy laughed. “I am not a microbiologist and these machines only test for known human pathogens. Foreign bio-matter is sometimes easy to chase down, but other times it takes quite a while to ID.”
Cracker had been correct about the facility, the building was be falling down around them, but the testing and scanning equipment were state of the art and all in house. Dr Logan took samples of tissue and blood from all parts of their bodies. They were x-rayed, ultra-sounded, full body scanned and poked at a bit.
“The results should not take too long. Do you want a coffee or something, I am going to hit a patch for about half an hour.” A patch slapped into her upper arm.
“What about billing?” Sarah did not want her name appearing anywhere in the records.
“Not a problem at the moment. I will use false idents and bill to an old case number. Good old Emil Kalheri.” 
“Emil Kalheri?” Cracker asked and stared in open-mouthed amazement at Sarah. 
“How do you know of Emil Kalheri?” Sarah asked, hoping Kathy was still able to reply. 
“He had died of cancer and was brought in by a coronial meat-wagon, dropped in the street according to the report. There was a lot of undue interest in such a mundane case. Company agents and police asking questions, demanding to look at the body and the next day a woman posing as his wife signed him out. The police ordered me to keep his account open. So I can bill what I like to it.” She slipped into incoherence and curled up on the floor. 
Precisely half an hour had passed and Dr Logan burst back in to life, her mind perfectly clear. “Well, I’ve got 40 minutes of lucidity so let’s look at the results.”
“Apart from slightly raised body temperatures, both of you are exactly 0.15 degrees above normal, you are in remarkable health. (Rattle off usual results – liver function, etc) DNA sequences clean, no sign of any foreign matter in your blood, no known viruses or bacteria and toxicology clear. Cracker, I am impressed. When did you give up the gear?”
“I didn’t as far as I know.” Cracker was as surprised as the good doctor.
“Sarah, there was a result with the scans that I wanted to confirm with the blood tests. Oh yes! It is odd because my examination indicated you are virgo intacto, but congratulations you guys! You are pregnant!” She went to hug Sarah but was pushed aside.
* * * 
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="829E11E7-4688-4429-BDC4-145AA650E317">
            <Title>The Blank</Title>
            <Text>The Blank
(14 hours before bombing)
Need more information in the introduction into this section.
The blank was loaded into the coronial meat wagon and the two company operatives set about finding the best and most profitable place to deliver the dead body. There was not much money in processing the dead and consequently most of the mortuaries were publicly owned and operated. The few private institutions were attached to teaching hospitals that conducted scandalous experiments under the guise of research. No one had yet created a Frankensteinian abomination, but it did not stop them trying. They paid good money for a fresh body and the blank was a fresh as they came, but they had been re-directed to a public facility in another security zone and had spent most of the day arguing with customs officials.
The blank was Emil Kalheri, a hard-working and decent man from a large family of builders and labourers. He was not wealthy, a level 1 could not be, but his family did not go without. He was just the kind of soft-hearted fuck-wit company agents could easily manipulate. When he was diagnosed with terminal testicular cancer, it had already metastasised, leaving three months of precious life. He knew he could not possibly gather together enough money to provide for his family, but he had a stroke of luck when he was contacted by Agents from Pre-emptive Reciprocity and Pro-active Euthanasia. His initial impulse to being recruited as an assassin was to recoil in revulsion and run in the opposite direction, but his family needed money and they were offering plenty. He only had to shoot a scum-bag manager (something most workers had fantasized about at one time or another) who, it was explained to him, was a prolific paedophile.
It was early evening when Agent Jones arrived at the morgue, but Emil was waiting for him, laid out in the main necropsy room on one of the scores of examination tables used for autopsies, many already underway. Rows and rows of pathologists spoke quietly and precisely into microphones hanging from above while they hacked, sawed and sliced through bone, meat and sinew. They slopped whole organs and tissue samples into jars, under the gaze of trainee physicians who watched in both fascination and disgust, some barely keeping down their lunch, while autopsy technicians and assorted ghouls giggled in macabre amusement. The head Pathologist was laughing hysterically as she scuttled out of the necropsy room, pointing at the puking kid-doctors. 
Apart from his clothing, Emil had not a scintilla of personal property and it was now reasonably certain that the cleaner had possession of the disc. Agent Jones rightly figured that, in the ordinary course of events, this cleaner would not be too difficult to track down, but no matter how he interrogated the link, no matter what angle he took, nothing could be found. Nothing! He knew this to be impossible, the Intel-network had access to every data-base in the Dominion. Everyone left a footprint somewhere and most leave craters and divots everywhere, but he could make no headway with this fellow. There were very few people in the entire Dominion who were afforded this level of anonymity and it was an absolute certainty that none of them were low-life cleaning contractors. Agent Jones and his ilk, by necessity, skulked around in the shadows to accomplish their dastardly deeds and were non-existent in any public record. Was the cleaner a member of another clandestine organisation? If so, he was unaware of them, but that did not mean they did not exist.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="3B24E9A3-4D43-475D-A643-0108DF3BDD6E">
            <Title>Global Environmental Catastrophe</Title>
            <Synopsis>Global Environmental Catastrophe</Synopsis>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="4ACD2737-AF3D-4613-AADD-C308CAEB1C2B">
            <Title>Leiutenant Shakti</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="2E6DFE58-6BAA-40C2-9C1E-241CF67295F9">
            <Title>Total Global Clarification</Title>
            <Text>Total Global Clarification
55 years Before The Dawkinis
Pathetic rays of sunlight barely penetrated the permanent layer of condensation, emissions and miscellaneous airborne particulate matter known as “the layer.” Decade upon decade of fossil fuel emissions, chemical misuse, pesticides and cow farts had pushed the atmosphere well beyond the point of no return. No meaningful attempt was made to reverse the degradation of the environment. There was no profit in that. The natural and according to the company, rational course of action was to spew yet more noxious chemicals into the atmosphere. Unprecedented amounts of “air-cleaner” over extended periods. The sales pitch to the world was “total atmospheric clarification”. Clean air, clean living and, with “Marlborough Country” ads once again possible, a surge in cigarette sales.
Governments everywhere swallowed the hype without question. Why would they questions it? Some of the greatest scientific minds on the planet (that are employed by the company) concluded that this was the only solution to global air pollution. Sales went beyond anyone’s wildest expectations. Financially, the company and the Primary Trade Dominion really cleaned up, unfortunately however, the atmosphere did not. Rather than clarifying” the air, all pollutants condensed together at about 300 metres above sea level. A floating pea-soup acid-mist representing the sum of all the filth and foul muck ever spewed out of a smoke stack, exhaust pipe, chimney or cow’s bum. Pure concentrated evil. 
Governments from all over the world protested, sent envoys and some even threatened military action. The company “CEO” laughed at that prospect, Western Ulyssia was a nuclear wasteland and PTD now manufactured most of the world’s armaments and all of the high-tech bio-weapons. Very few had the balls to attack and even fewer had sufficient armaments to sustain such unwise undertaking. The company explained to their disgruntled customers that the process had not failed, it had merely worked in an unexpected way. The final desired result would merely take a little longer and require the purchase of more product. With the addition of a little dye in the mixture, “the layer” was changed from spew green to a pleasant silvery white hue. Apart from the aesthetic improvement, the extra purchases did nothing to alleviate the problem. 
The layer blocked half of the sunlight and, as such, was insufficient to allow photosynthesis to take place. Poor and low-lying countries were first to die out. No sun, no crops and no life. Unless they possessed enclosed habitats and radiation shielding, communities at higher altitudes fared little better. In the space of 20 years the world’s population shrank by 62%. Places fit for human habitation were reduced by 80%. The sins of old Ulyssia were visited upon the entire world.


</Text>
        </Document>
