        <Document ID="C11F2CD5-5B9A-44E4-AA79-6FCC306E6E26">
            <Title>I am not human</Title>
            <Text>For a moment, Special Agent Ajent Jones believed Sarah must me too good-looking to hit. For a time after, his blood-hot fury remained unquenchable and eroded any rational thought, even the question of the blank, Emil Kalheri.
He should have hit her. He rather enjoyed messing up a woman’s face, the beautiful ones all the more. He savoured their shock and surprise, the shattering of their misplaced confidence that any fuck-cunt mother-goddess could subdue a man-boy in any circumstance. We’re all man-boys to them. It was precious, watching their world-view crumble, with the fear of realising the world is a grittier, more brutal, more truthful place. Until that realisation, all women believed that, with their clever words and their psychological manipulations, they could subdue the harshness of reality. Sooner or later, they all learned how wrong they were. He enjoyed being the bearer of the good news. By exposing their delusions, he was providing a community service.
The truth was, since childhood, Ajent Jones ached to please women. That’s why he hated them.
So, what stayed his hand with the computer jockey in Travis Teh’s office? When he advanced on her, she stared him down, unflinching, a condescending smile painted across her face. I’m  invincible, the smile said, which enraged him all the more.  He hated condescending smiles. They begged to be broken. So, why hadn’t he broken hers?
He punched the steering wheel of his cruiser. He punched the driver-side window. He punched the steering wheel again, to calm the thumping in his head.
The still-beautiful computer jockey could have the documents—the disc, the Gyges Disk—that Emil Kalheri should have retrieved from Travis Teh. She was obviously in Cyber Security (her tits were too small to be in Finance), so she might have some professional interest in the disc. Perhaps she was an operative, perhaps a PAPER agent. It would not be the first time the Company had tasked two competing operations with the same objective. I’ll give you both Preemptive Reciprocity and Proactive Euthanasia, he swore.
Once he calmed down, the importance of Emil’s disappearance was all the clearer. He could have scarpered because he failed to retrieve the disc. Or Miss Cyber-Security-Invincible could be responsible for both his failure and his disappearance. Either way, he must find Emil.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="288E5387-D63E-40CF-87A5-83BF0D751953">
            <Title>A Big Bang for the Big Bang</Title>
            <Synopsis>Halo had seen the woman on the other side of the bar, just minutes before the explosion. He picks her up in double quick time and they are out the door. They kiss and are knocked over by the explosion. The go back to her house.
The woman, AVI, is an agent from the Department of Pre-emtive reciprocity and Proactive Euthenasia, who has been sent to place a bomb in the Stick it Inn (it is uncertain, but Cracker may have been the target).
They go back to her place - she plans to kill him, he plans to kill her. She is an assassin and he is a serial killer. (He is later identified as the “Nightclub Stalker/killer”)
The have sex and he kills her with a special knife/blade he had made. It is hollow in the middle so the blood spurts really high. However, because he stabbed her through the heart, the blood stopped pumping after a short time. He was not satiated and he goes out to find another victim.
He finds himself in the same crush as Cracker and Sarah. He stabs a few people and melts back into the crowd, but them he sights Cracker ex girlfriend, Angela. She is crying because her boyfriend suddenly disappeared and she thought he may have been trampled. Halo knows he must have stabbed him and he is greatly amused. He tells Angela they are is real trouble, but he might be able to get them off the street. He takes her back to Avi’s apartment. ‘He thought himself a simple man with simple needs – if he had a girl to fuck and stab, he was sweet.’ </Synopsis>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="24B96513-EBB7-4253-9683-EC19A60EF9F3">
            <Title>Effects of Pollution</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="EABAE2AD-E90C-4D40-8F68-50F83EFF3D75">
            <Title>Leiutenant Shakti</Title>
            <Synopsis>Quick introduction to Eloise Shakti - one fierce operator.
Introduction to the incompetent Lieutenant Shakti - I like the idea of this character bumbling his way through life, always trying but never quite getting there.  Another example of a privileged person being granted the keys to the kingdom.  His brain does not function correctly it seems, as his brain would not accept brain-dumps - "Monkeys retain information better".  He eventually joins the police - a complete humiliation for his mother and father.  Later you find out that the rank of "Lieutenant" does not even exist and it was created just for him so he would not be taking orders from the 'cloudborn'.

Shakti is only good at reading off a cue card and is placed in the public affairs division - and then has his own TV show - Random Camera.  All this is a bit of a wank and didn't really go anywhere.  So it can be re-cycled or dumped.  There are a couple of further mentions of Random Camera later in the book.  Random Camera is a trope-fest.</Synopsis>
            <Text>
LIEUTENANT SHAKTI – PEOPLE’S HERO
9 months BTD
Alexander Shakti was not an unintelligent man, some said quite the opposite was true, although these people were few and far between. His capacity to engage in abstract thought was minimal. He was socially retarded, romantically inept and had no ability or, perhaps, desire to embrace any meaningful discourse. A conversation with Alex, as he liked to be called, was an awkward blend of pregnant pauses and obscure humour, usually ending after a few excruciating minutes. Invariably there was some ruse employed by the victim in a desperate attempt at extricating themselves. “Is that the time?” or “I’ve got a meeting” or, simply, “Please don’t have children.” Alex had heard them all before, but oddly enough, he did not realise that he was a social pariah and had no idea he was a target of derision and ridicule. Such was his ego and unwavering confidence in his abilities, he held the strong belief that he was cool and well liked.
Born into a comfortable level 5 home he became a disappointment and later, a total embarrassment, to his hardworking professional parents. Although his mother was born level 2, she had owned three houses and two apartments by the time she was 20 years old. By age twenty-two, she was running her own Accommodation Directorate and the fact that she managed to land a level 5 husband, whilst extremely rare, was no accident. With pure force of will she took on larger and by reputation, more ferocious competitors and crushed them beneath her stylish heels. When her business was publicly listed, there were protestations from the Stock Exchange directly to the Ministry of Moral Compliance. Although there was no legal impediment, it seemed that a Level 2 CEO could not list a company on the Stock Exchange, they simply would not have it. Before any public trading on her stock commenced, however, she was made an offer she could not refuse, the sale of the company at an inflated price and allocation with an upwardly mobile level 4. She knew that with her talent she and her new husband would be at level 5 in double quick time and their children would want for nothing, breaking into the senior executive ranks – level 7 and above.
	
Alexander’s father was an astute executive, but lacked the flare and drive of his wife. After the initial adjustment period (allocations never run smoothly at first), they actually formed a close bond, a partnership of trust and mutual respect that, for him, even extended to the metaphysical. Like everyone else he had had been taught at school that the economy was of paramount importance. The family unit was an historical oddity that had endured to provide a steady supply of labour for industry and the professions. The means of production had a veracious appetite and demanded fresh bodies, caring not who it consumed. 
Following the allocation, Shakti Senior had been very surprised by his genuine warm feelings for his wife. The birth of his only child Alexander had been a major source of happiness as a young married man. He knew he had genuinely loved his wife, but he could not tell her. What would she think of him? Love undermines rational thought. Profit demands rational thought. Love, therefore does not make economic sense and his wife needed a home based in economic rationality. Like a good husband, he never told his wife that he loved her, even when the words were bursting from his very core. As for his wife, she never gave love a single thought. She was very happy with her social position and would have reacted in a typically aggressive manner if anything, even her husband, threatened that position. He would not have survived. 
The home they created for Alex was the picture perfect environment for the budding young corporate raider. Every activity engaged in; every toy played with; every vid-stick watched and playgroup attended was training for the corporate sector. None of it sunk in. Well, that was not entirely true. He could recount the information that he had been taught but, seemingly, could not apply the theory to practical situations. To his parents he was a dullard and would only progress with a great deal of luck. 
When adolescents reach the age of 16 they are introduced to mind-dump technology as a part of the school curriculum. By wearing what appeared to be an ordinary pair of glasses, but connected to the school data-base, a student could instantly transfer vast amounts of information directly into longterm memory. Student progress accelerated exponentially, but it did not make a student any smarter, it just gave them more information for there existing intellect to draw upon and process as best as their minds could. The degree to which the information was retained varied from student to student, but Shakti’s brain refused to retain any data dumped into his uniquely tiny mind. He was the only student in who had ever completely rejected mind-dumps. He was promptly shipped off for medical examinations, such was the shock and embarrassment the news caused his mother. 
Testing of his brain function showed the information was dumped his brain in the normal and expected fashion, but immediately began to fade and was gone within minutes. Eloise Shakti purchased the best scientific minds in an effort to cure Shakti of his evolutionary curse, but the consensus unanimously agreed upon was devastating. Shakti was an ordinary boy and his brain physiology was incompatible with digital knowledge acceleration. “Monkeys retain information better” was said on more than one occasion.
Returning to school he soon fell behind while his compatriots progressed in leaps and bounds. He was required by the curriculum to undertake all brain-dump sessions which were as painful as they were embarrassing. He was greeted at every session by the sight of a stuffed monkey strapped into his chair and hooked up to the data base. “Monkey Boy” would be whispered and the class would roar with laughter, reaching its peak when he gingerly unhooked the monkey and pointlessly took its place. 
Both parents earnestly apprehended the fear that he would end up in the public service and when he applied for law enforcement training it was embarrassment and shame heaped upon humiliation. When they shared their concern with their Level 5 peers, it was met with incredulous facial expression and cries of disbelief. Short term contracting to the public sector was acceptable, but no one at level 5 or above had actually joined the public service. One could only expect, at best, to gain level 5 status after a lengthy and extremely successful career in public office. Without immersing oneself in corruption, there was no way of accumulating personal wealth in the public sector. Not that anyone was all that concerned by a bit of graft, but corrupt practices were expected to remain where they always have flourished – in the private sector. To the management elite, the petty corruptions of public officials were paltry and laughably amateur compared to their own acts of moral turpitude.
To the police Shakti was a perfect candidate for employment. Most of the police recruits were level 1 or 2 and occasionally a loser from level 3 might apply, but they had never had an application from a level 5 candidate. The recruiting officers were completely dumbfounded when he transmitted his application. In social terms, he outranked all senior executive and was level pegging with the Commissioner. This pleased them greatly and it was with immense satisfaction and diligent zeal that they fast-tracked the application. 
 
Most of the recruiting processes were aimed at weeding out scoundrels and rapscallions from level 1 and 2. Expecting a level 5 candidate to suffer the ignominy of the entire recruitment nightmare would be frowned upon and be seen by upper society as an attack on them. Fancy forcing one of our own to undertake those silly tests. Besides, what if he failed? As a moral certainty, the entire recruiting section would be canned and the Police Department would move one step closer to total privatisation. The only ordeal he had to undertake was the medical examination. The last thing that the cops wanted was to employ some diseased weakling, level 5 or not. The Senior Recruiting Officer had made one mistake in his career, allowing a recruit with cancer to commence training and he knew, because of this blunder, he would never, ever, make it to level 4. He was not going to make that mistake again. Shakti’s health screen revealed no congenital conditions or acquired diseases and he commenced training within days.
Progression of this candidate was monitored with interest by the Commissioner’s Office and very early in his training it was realised that he did not possess any of the qualities generally attributed to the law enforcement officer. This caused much consternation in the staff rooms as they knew that they could not fail this person, but he was simply the worst candidate they had ever seen. Like a pain-in-the-neck mature age student, his only contribution to theory classes was incessantly asking idiotic questions or stating the painfully obvious. Each and every time he opened his stupid gob, his comments were promptly followed by pained groans from his classmates. There was always one in the class, but no one had expected it to be Shakti. What did they teach in those expensive private schools? They would all ask themselves. Whatever it was, he must have been sick that day.
He did not meet the physical demands of the program either. He could not shoot – which was a major achievement in itself. The Particle Pulse Pistols (PPP), now on standard issue, had the new “tag and bag” firing system. Not unlike the old laser sights on the obsolete gas compression models, the PPP had a unique targeting system. The sights left a unique electro-magnetic signature on the target which was identical to the signature of the next burst from the barrel. After tagging, the trigger is depressed and the projectile (really a short particle beam burst) always hit the tagged target. You just had to point the pistol in the general direction of the target and squeeze the trigger. It was foolproof, but not Shakti-proof. He was unable to tag his target and, consequently, he was also unable to bag anything either. Weapons instructors spent many, many hours on the firing range with Shakti and following failure upon failure, he was passed. He was passed not only because of political and internal pressures, but also for the sake of their own mental health. Getting him the hell out of the training college and, hopefully, the hell out of their lives, became the only means of preserving their collective sanity.
The entire class of ’52 hated him without exception. Initially some latched onto him and made aborted attempts at friendship. They figured that a Level 5 was, most probably, going to be fast tracked to promotion and if they got in on the ground floor, they would be dragged up with him. However, even the slimiest sycophants in the class had their limits. They would not place their future in this imbecile’s hands. It seemed he had absolutely no laudable qualities whatsoever. 
It took no time at all for Shakti to acquire many unflattering nicknames. Firstly his class mates dubbed him Scott – ‘Scott-no-brains’ or ‘Scott-no-hope’. Then they moved to ‘Fuck Wit’ and finally just ‘F.W.’ Even with this derision directed straight at his face he was under the impression that this was how all level one’s and two’s interacted. He found it a compliment that the common man would treat him as an equal, one of their own. He was happy to have been able to breach the gulf between the higher community and the common as some sort of social pioneer.
It was not until the final weeks of training that he finally showed aptitude to any policing duties. Not unlike academy dweebs, members of the Public Affairs Bureau were usually fuck-sticks who could not hold their own on the street or who were too scared to try. They kidded themselves into thinking that they were actually real police officers. To the rest of the rank and file, these people were nothing more than pretenders who liked to tell bullshit war stories at parties in order to jag a fuck. The training to deal with the Public Information Outlets involved many mock media interviews, press releases and “talking to camera.” Although he could not think on his feet, if the copy was written for him Shakti excelled in this environment. The news resonated throughout the academy. To the Chancellor, this represented the removal of a great weight from his shoulders. He knew how Damocles had felt. The pressure from above had been almost unbearable. Now the dangling sword was gone and he could send Shakti to the Public Affairs Bureau with the honest recommendation that he was suited to this area. 
Shakti became a household name very quickly. His arrival at Public Affair Bureau coincided with the launch of Police Video Streaming. His face was on every Vid-Screen; Vid-board; poster; milk carton, leaflet and some smart-arse even put his face on toilet paper. Although paper products were prohibitively expensive, they sold like hot-cakes, mostly to police officers as it provided an opportunity to do something they had long wanted to do. There were two channels transmitted to levels 1 to 3 and one other channel for the consumption of Level 4 and above. The programming was almost entirely real life docu-dramas and with his flare for publicity and milking it was worth, his show took off. It was the first time in his life that he had useful and, in some cases, interesting ideas that took reality vid-streams to new lows - exactly what was required. Reality TV shows had long since proven that any idiot can be a star and Shakti, even with his advanced level idiocy, gained a level of acclaim.
One program, Random Camera, revolved around surveillance and covert police operations, but not wrong the Public Affairs simpletons stomping around in their domain, Operational Police refused to assist with criminal intelligence. Starved of timely data, Shakti made a decision that only the truly unenlightened could make. He targeted houses at random. With this single decision, Shakti rejected all investigative theories and presumptions – that potent crime suppression can only be effected by basing operations on timely and accurate intelligence. All senior police knew the show would be a failure as their collective experience brought them to a collective conclusion – random policing yields random results. However, with a zero tolerance policy in effect, every show resulted in the unfortunate targets being detected committing some sort of criminal offence. 
Although the judiciary complained bitterly about the creation of an amateur parallel justice system, the amount of revenue raised ensured the program would survive. Clandestine video devices were installed into level 1 and 2 homes, at random, and the action was broadcast live into level 4-plus homes. The upper levels of society lapped it up. Most of them had never set foot on the ground. Living their lives above cloud height they had no idea how the common lived. 
Corporate Infidelity was, by and large, the major crime detected. This was not surprising, considering that to constitute the offence one merely had to breach employer confidentiality or defame a manager. A crime of absolute liability, the simple act of complaining to your spouse about work practices would establish this very serious offence. It struck at the very heart of the corporate structure and disrespect for managerial classes was inexcusable. It was the modern equivalent of heresy and doctrinal divergence of this magnitude was punishable by death and forfeiture all goods and chattels. Guilt and sentencing was decided by the viewers through on-line voting and carried out immediately. Very few in the audience were weak enough to feel compassion or deliver mercy to the accused lows. Most viewers correctly postulated that their voting habits were monitored and analysed for moral weakness –in this case compassion for one’s inferiors. Indulging one’s empathy was a waste of effort and interfered with the ability to make hard business decisions. Being outed for moral weakness had a monumentally negative effect on career paths and, consequently, there was hardly a show without at least one execution. Shows that did not feature the removal of someone’s breathing privileges rated rather poorly and advertising revenue for subsequent shows would plummet. When profits were threatened the corporate machine immediately rolled into action and issued a decree from on high – One show, one body, no excuses. 
To upper society, this was the best show on the link. At 8.00pm every Tuesday and Thursday most, if not all, available level 5-pluses, were plonked in front of their Vid-screens or at level 5 cocktail bars, mesmerized by the outrageous and often bizarre behaviour of the lows. The subtitles at the bottom of the screen assisted them in understanding the gutter talk and expletives used incessantly by the common man. Many obtained no end of amusement in the lows use of syntax and the grotesque linguistic mutations that had developed below the layer. They especially loved it when they lows beat the shit out of each other – domestic violence was hilarious, even to the female viewers. The level of violence was so extreme that it seemed the only thing commoners hated more than management, was each other.
Shakti also had the balls, or the naïve stupidity, to suggest that a similar show be conceived for the consumption of the lows. He envisioned corporate fraudsters and rogue traders being brought to book live on his program. He would be an icon to the unwashed masses. The show went ahead, but Shakti was not made aware of some of the minor changes. All of the people featured on show were actors or impostors and no level 5’s were harmed during the production of any episode. Fueled by total ignorance, Shakti took to his new second show with a fervent enthusiasm. His genuine belief in the integrity of the program gave the façade a credibility that struck a chord with the discontented lows. At last someone was doing something about the morally bankrupt managers and the corporations they serve. 
In reality, anyone caught on the show was a convicted low already on a death sentence. They knew that the lows had an extraordinary and inexplicable attachment to family members and, like vultures, they pecked and tore at this devotion until leverage was found. To ensure they played along the production company promised social promotion and employment for their families. They were going to die anyway and figured that they may as well do some good for the kids before they got zapped. The final carrot dangled in front of the impostor was the possibility, as slim as it might be, that the audience of the lows might vote to spare him or her. Fat chance, they loved their payback. 
Within a few short years Shakti, in his own right, was extremely wealthy and known throughout the PTD as a fighter for justice - a level 5 with the heart of a level 1. Due to the massive amounts of revenue raised by “Random Camera” and associated programs, Public Safety Television thrived and became the largest operational command, with no less than 3000 workers – techs, locksmiths, arrest teams and, of course, executioners. Promotion to Lieutenant, with the help of his mother’s clout and negotiating skills, was a mere formality for Shakti. Anyone who brought in that much revenue was rewarded, notwithstanding the apparently lifeless organ inside his skull. It was never brought to his attention, nor did Shakti ever notice, that the rank of Lieutenant did not previously exist and stood outside the normal rank structure, somewhere to the side, where no-one could work out where he fit in. His mother thought it to be a suitable metaphor for her idiot son’s life.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="254E63C3-2DDD-408E-978A-2DB04790CAEE">
            <Title>Vershlingbessern</Title>
            <Synopsis>“Bad to Worse”

Pre-history of the Primary Trade Dominion.  Not that important, but I do like the bit about President Dunning Kruger.  
The nuking of Western Ulyssia (later to become the a sovereign state) is kind of important as it shows why the PTD is so shitty.  This could be skipped or the information used in another way.


</Synopsis>
            <Text>When Donald Dunning-Kruger was president of the United Free States of Atlantia, and Vietnesian President Sucarn served words of peace and goodwill, with a side-salad of excuses and caveats:
“As part of our peace-keeping initiatives in our southern territorial waters, a small flotilla of our craft regrettably ran aground on the shores of Ulyssia. I assure the good people of our dearest neighbour, our intentions are peaceful. In the interest of bilateral relations, especially the safety of both peoples, we accept our unfortunate mistake, and will rescue our service folk on conclusion of exercises.”
The small flotilla, it transpired, represented a significant, specialised chunk of the Vietnesian Navy, all trained in the art of lifting gold, uranium and iron ore from whichever sovereign soil they happened upon running aground.
After receiving intelligence, such as it was, of the Vietnesian boosting Ulyssian natural resources, the Prime Minister of Ulyssia, the Right Honorable Spot Muddleham, realised he must invoke treaty, and contact, “that moron Dunning-Kruger.”
Muddleham knew he was inviting the devil to dinner, but he had no option. And so he crafted words of diplomacy for the Atlantian ear: “Mr President, We are in deep shit here. Can you bail us out?” 
Dunning-Kruger exploited many moments of tension before he spoke, or rather, it took a fucking eon for him to reply with, “We of the UFSA are mindful of our friends in the south.” He spoke slowly, reinforcing the rumour he recited everything from a tele-prompter. “I am also mindful of my responsibilities to my fellow Atlantians.”
Muddleham scratched his balding melon. “You’re saying you can’t afford it.”
A flash of animal anger ignited the wispy rendering of Dunning-Kruger’s soft, entitled face as it hovered on the other side of Muddleham desk. Then, after another interminable wait, in which rumours flourished that Dunning-Kruger’s SecDef vets all his utterances, he said, “We are currently fighting infractions in ten theatres.”
You always bankroll the losers, Muddleham thought, but held his tongue.
“Resources have their limits, Mr Prime Minister.” 
“Mr President, two million foreign troops have foothold on our soil, occupying our gold fields, uranium mines and iron ore deposits, plundering our resources.”
Dunning-Kruger pursed his lips in an excessively sensual pout and shrugged.
“Our economy will be ruined,” Muddleham said, as if explaining the alphabet to a child. We will not be able to pay off our loans, Mr President.” He knew this would get his attention. Although they habitually neglected to pay their own bills, holding fast to belief in their exceptionalism, Atlantians despised states that did not pay their bills. 
“You guys owe us 3 trillion as it is.” Kruger, soft as his head was, did not like to be stiffed.
“This is an intolerable situation and we do not have the resources to repel them. Our great peoples have fought and died alongside each other for close to two centuries. Where is your sense of history? Where is your sense of honour?”
Raising the concept of “honour” in diplomacy struck Kruger as immensely amusing and he could barely choke down the urge to laugh hysterically. SecDef and Sec State were not able to display the same degree of self-discipline and spent the next several minutes cackling on the floor. 
When he was finally able, the President choked out, “Honour costs money. I can’t afford honour. Honour is the rallying cry of losers. As for history, we make history. We are not slaves to it. Any history we do not like, we re-write. In any case, where are your defence forces, Mr Prime Minister?” 
“Well, Mr President, most of them are fighting anti-Semites, in all ten theaters ” Don’t you remember? You fucking idiot!
“You’ve only given us one million of your troops – where are the rest?” Inquired the ignoramus.
“That’s pretty much it. We’ve got reservists and retirees, but no front line troops. Relocating troops currently deployed will take time. Time is something we do not have.”
“You sent all of your troops offshore? Are you insane?” Questioned the inane President.
“It was in response to your ‘Plea to the Free World’ speech – you know, ‘If you’re not with us you’re against us’. It scared the shit out of us. We asked how many and you said one million, no less.”
President Kruger was pleased. Fear is power! He loved the idea of scaring the entire planet with a one-liner. He’d have to promote his speech writer - If you’re not with us you’re against us. He giggled again. “It was supposed to scare you into action. We seem to be the only defender of freedom and democracy on this planet.”
“We have been alongside you all of the way.”
“You’re contribution is token at best, Prime Minister. We pay the bills.”
“Yeah and you take all the loot.”
 “Yes, we do. What do these people want anyway? Free Trade? A seat on the League of Nations Security Council? Cheaper loans? What?” Asked the President, directing the conversation away from his country’s well-documented history of pillaging and plundering.
 
“They say they are running war-games, but they mostly want to punish us for intellectual property infringements. They say they want us to change the way we do business. They want free trade agreements to be adhered to.”
“Agreements adhered to? Don’t you guys know how to write an exit clause? If we can’t renege on a deal, we don’t sign.” These Ulyssians are like children. No wonder their economy sucks.
“It’s also a good excuse to steal our gold, uranium and iron ore. While we sit on our backsides, they are stealing my country. They’re just thieves like the rest of us and we cannot ignore them or their demands. Basically they want, you know, fair and equitable treatment in the future.” 
Fair and equitable treatment between trading partners was an alien concept to those involved in commerce. It was inconceivable to build an economic powerhouse on evenhanded treatment of others. Taking advantage of weaker nations was (and is) the foundation of international business. A high living standard for a few in one location, by definition, insists that there are low standards for many somewhere else. Those suckers dumb enough to be born in poor nations deserve exploitation, and by remaining vulnerable, invite yet more exploitation. It was simply a fact of life.
Dunning Kruger found himself entirely bemused at the nativity of the Vietnesians. Are they that unsophisticated? “Mr Prime Minister, I am told by my Defence Secretary that there may be something we can do for you. I don’t have all the details yet, I’ll get back to you as soon as I have them. It will comply with our obligations under the Nuclear Disarmament Treaty, but, whatever happens, however, there will be no troops sent from here” 
“I don’t care about details. If you can help, just do it.” He hung up the phone and breathed a sigh of relief. Scratched his head and then stopped dead in his tracks. Huh? Nuclear Disarmament Treaty?
Twenty-seven minutes later, in excess of 100 nuclear missiles air-burst over the Vietnesian positions and in the following ten microseconds two million Vietnesians were vaporized into plasma smoke and radioactive gas. In the process, 80% of arable land in Western Ulyssia was annihilated. After irradiating the entire mineral reserves of a small continent, President Kruger called it a good day, lit a cigar and poured himself a scotch. Nothing was left of the invasion and nothing much was left of the countryside. A glowing moonscape was left to fester and expand. There was nothing worth invading now.
- - -
“Jesus Christ, Dunning, what the hell did you think you were doing? Nuking my country!” A somewhat irate Prime Minister screamed down the hot line. 
“You said and I quote – ‘I don’t care about details. If you can help, just do it.’ I have it recorded. Turn on your vid-screen, it’s on WWNN right now,” he replied, barely able to stop himself from laughing. The Defence Secretary and the National Security adviser were again on the floor, wetting themselves. No end of amusement at that end of the phone.
“Did you seriously think that this would help?” queried the PM.
“We had to do something about complying with our Nuclear Disarmament Treaty. We had to dismantle them, so we figured we might as well deploy them.”
“How does that comply with the fucking treaty? You are supposed to dismantle them!”
“I dismantled them very quickly and into many, many small pieces. What are you complaining about anyway? Do you currently have an invading army on your doorstep?”
“No, but half of my country glows in the dark.”
“Be that as it may, those nukes come at a price. Your little shit-hole country owes the Union of Capitalist Atlantic States 6 trillion. In Atlantian dollars, not your southern fucking pesos! There is no need to thank us, just pay up. While you are at it you can pay the 3 trillion you already owe.” 
“What will you do if we don’t pay? Nuke us?”
“Our response will be proportionate to your debt.” He knew this would frighten him. He had wiped out entire countries for less.
“You’ll get your money. You’ll get your money.” Even as he terminated the call, the PM knew his career was over. Nothing could save him now. He’d be lucky to live until the next election and he knew was not that lucky.
Dunning and the crew were now backslapping and congratulating each other. Another customer satisfied and another diplomatic crisis solved. “Man, one day we are going to have to take this shit seriously!” Teased the president. 
“Nine trillion in one day. The treasury is going to love you, Mr. President. Yours will be remembered as the most fiscally responsible administration in history. Your policies are epoch making,” said SecDef, sucking up to the chief.
“Do you ever stop brown-nosing? For the last time, you will not get the nomination next term.”
“Oh, but..”
“No buts. There is no way this country is ever going to vote for President Mohamed Goldberg. Go plan your next war.” He pointed to the door and both of the cabinet members left. “Everyone wants my job. Now, I wonder if they will really pay up.” Epoch making? What the hell does that mean?
Pay up they did. It amused the Prime Minister to deliver the payment in gold bullion, all from the glowing west, radioactive and useless for several hundred thousand years. 
Following the nuclear strike on their lands, the Provincial Government of Western Ulyssia withdrew from the Federation and called the new land the Western Liberated Dominion. It sounded pretty cool, but the Western Liberated Dominion was nothing more than a nuclear landfill with a couple of unaffected cities. They would never be an economic force again. They could have their pathetic little Nation-State. No one in the east cared.
The political ramifications for the PM were ugly and immediate. Outraged citizens and their pitiful street demonstrations were simply ignored, but the corporate sector could not be so readily disregarded. Any company that owned businesses in the Western Liberated Dominion, were screaming for blood. The Prime Minister’s political stocks plummeted and the party invoked a time honoured tradition of assassination – stabbed to death in the chamber of the lower house. As he lay dying he could not help but think of Caesar. Parallel lives, he ridiculously thought. With his last breath he wheezed, “Et tu Brute?” 
The new Prime Minister stood over the body and mocked, “Et tu, Brute? Et tu, Fucking Brute! Who the fuck did he think he was?” Slamming his boot into the emotionless death mask he said, “Oh well, at least he didn’t say ‘Rosebud’.” Some of the older party members were mildly amused, but others could not place the reference, being more concerned with how they were going to get the blood off their shoes before the press conference.
Riding on a wave of anti-Atlantian sentiment, the new regime became tough, adopting an isolationist policy, combining the geniality of communist regimes with the human compassion of a military junta. Ulyssia, a country now split in two, was renamed the Primary Trade Dominion and the economy, previously based upon consumer goods and service industries, would now focus on the manufacture of arms. How hard could it be to sell weapons? Any idiot could do it. The trick was staying under the radar so you could sell to all sides in a conflict. Better still, create the conflict, having first arranged the contracts. Everyone else sold weapons that way and everyone knew it, but it was bad form to be caught unloading them, particularly when the recipient is an enemy of an ally. It was the biggest market and needing money desperately, the PTD became the most prosperous war-monger in history. They sold to anyone and everyone and siphoned up cash from all comers.
It took only a few short years for the radiation zone in the Western Liberated Dominion to expand and engulf urban centres. Terrified citizens fled to the east, to where it was said to be safe from the rads. There were millions of them, mostly from the Western Liberated Dominion, but a few malformed Vietnesians made the trek as well. They had escaped the radiation storms with nothing but their lives and a few meagre possessions. Any prior land ownership was meaningless. The wealth they had created and the trinkets they had owned were now throbbing with radioactive isotopes of iodine, caesium, strontium, thorium, xenon and barium.
As the trickle of radioactive refugees became a flood, PTD cities began to bulge and then burst from the pressure. The wretched throngs kept coming in massive human waves, ever-increasing, laying wast to vast areas of urban sprawl. The only saleable resource they possessed was their labour, gleefully exploited by local business, happy to see the return of slavery. The workers were used, abused and discarded. In traditional blue-collar work there were no protective labour laws and no benefits. Thanks for coming, champ. Sorry about the severed limb, but we do have a two-arm policy here. I think you will find the exit is behind you.
There were a few benevolent souls who, against all good sense, actually assisted the refugees. One stood out head and shoulders above the rest. Jameson Hardy was the wealthiest man in all of Old Ulyssia (some said the world), but to his peers, he was the most eccentric of men. Had he been poor, he would have been merely regarded as insane, but he controlled the finance and resources sector, manufacturing, media, ship yards, universities, hospitals, pharmaceuticals, schools, utilities, multi-national conglomerates and, much to the amusement of the boys at "The Club", he also delved in charitable foundations, training schemes and general tree-hugging, hippy non-sense. Of course, there were hardly any trees left to hug, but the term was now synonymous with any unworthy left wing activity. 
He poured obscene amounts of money into apparently useless research. His interest in things medical was peculiar in that it was focused on epidemiology and genetic diversity amongst “the common”, level 1 and level 2 citizens. Very strange indeed - Who cares if “the common” get Ebola? Who cares if they are all inbred? They need to be culled back a bit anyway. 
All in all, Jamison Hardy was an oddity, some sort of throwback, who somehow came to the conclusion that helping others enriched your life. What was he playing at? Where was the profit? Can you still claim that sort of thing on tax? In fact there was no profit in helping innocent victims of war. Jameson Hardy knew he would never get a return on this investment. Apparently, shocking as it seemed, he was on a humanitarian mission. He was a Dutch boy inserting his digit into a dyke holding back a deluge of human misery. He knew that eventually the walls would fail and a flood of pain would follow. Fatalistic or not, he was going to relieve the suffering of as many as he could before cataclysm engulfed them. He purchased, from the filthy throngs, every parcel of land he could. He purchased millions of hectares - all radioactive and uninhabitable. While the vendors were eternally grateful for the money, it was insufficient to purchase comparable lodgings in the swelling metropolis. Demand for housing far-outstretched supply, causing a price surge. Anyone with a proprietary interest in land rubbed their hands together with delight.
Jameson embarked upon unprecedented building programs employing hundreds of thousands of workers, refugees all. Toiling against time, they constructed housing estates, hundreds of stories tall, with Spartan, but clean facilities. Each gargantuan structure was designed to house 30,000, but eventually squeezed in 100,000 with the introduction of time share and sleeping drawers. 
The popularity of time-share, the equivalent of camping in the city, was astonishing. Some chose to time-share an entire house or flat for a fixed period (up to 6 months), preferring to spend an equivalent time on the streets rather than permanently living in a drawer. Although they were not officially called drawers, "Discreet Living Capsules" were nothing more than suffocating coffins. They were relatively cheap and provided security when in repose, but sharing bathroom facilities necessarily involved running the gauntlet through a permanent gaggle of rapists and paedophiles. They were harmless compared to ‘Gazumpers’ who would perpetually circulate dorms looking for unlocked drawers, hoping to spend a few unconscious hours in comfort and at someone else's expense. These people rarely had the opportunity to bath and being gazumped meant disinfecting the whole drawer, including the bedding and any personal other items that were, for some reason, not stolen.
Forlorn home-owners riddled with debt, sold off or leased 8 to 12 hours access a day to their humble abodes. Usually shift workers would take the opportunity to get a few hours’ sleep, a meal and a shower before going back to the grind. These marriages of convenience were the source of a great deal of unrest, but in stark contrast to life on the streets, disputes rarely ended in death or loss of limb. In the ultimate housing boom, housing sales management became a most lucrative vocation and owning an Accommodation Directorate franchise was comparable to printing money. Realtors joined the new elite and property ownership became the symbol of social status and power. 
When the plague came Jameson Hardy disappeared, presumed dead. The refugees from the west mourned his death loud and long. Many had been given the chance to grow into adults, some of which did not live entirely miserable lives. The evil epidemic, which was said to have come from Asia (most suspected Vietnesia) was swift, smiting 40% of the population before the disease and the rioting dissipated. Even though it could be proven the bug was a genetically modified virus, the plague was blamed on the immigrants in Jameson Hardy's towering cesspools. 
Although a huge swath of the population had been cut down, there were still plenty of street-sleepers and gazumpers to go around. In fact, a disproportionate amount of “the common” had survived the plague. Was it luck or natural immunity? Very odd, indeed, but no-one bothered to find out why this was so. Fuck the poor.
The property owners and accommodation directorates got down to the business of perpetuating a housing crisis. Real Estate moguls properly thanked a non-existent god that there was still enough demand to apply upward pressure on prices. Many apartment blocks were destroyed or condemned in order to maintain a shortage. People could go ahead and die en-masse, but property values could not fall.
</Text>
            <Notes>He scratched his balding melon and simply said

Each response compiled as the conversation took place by the SecDef. The Secretary of State hovered over the Sec Def, nodding in agreement at every key stroke. “Fucken brown nose” the Sec Def thought as he typed. 

He squinted and smiled arrogantly</Notes>
        </Document>
