        <Document ID="3B02430F-0837-4746-99A2-B807D3669A95">
            <Title>Cracker meets his drinking buddies</Title>
            <Text>Cracker’s afternoon jobs were drudgery and gave him no distraction from Sarah or the embarrassment of running over a body in the business district, where dozens of cameras watched every warm body. He was sure there’d be hundreds of memes of him by now on Cloq Toq, deriding his buffoonery. 
At home, Cracker waved his armpit at the front lock and pushed the door open. As soon as he was closeted in the tiny hall, he kicked his shoes off. The still air told him he was alone. He sighed with relief.
He and Andrea had been fighting for weeks. Not just raised voices, not only verbal abuse, but physical assaults, such as knees to the groin (her knee to his groin) and hair pulling (his hair and her pulling). Cracker had no appetite for violence. It left him feeling sick, and Andrea rampant. Andrea wanted commitment; in Cracker’s mind that meant a commitment to taking beatings.
With beer in mind, Cracker went to the fridge and triggered a proximity event which played a video on the fridge’s door. It was Andrea.
“I put myself up for random allocation,” the video said, with Andrea’s image planting her fists on her hips and jutting her jaw out in defiance.
“So what?” Cracker said to the rotting bananas in a bowl by the jar of whey protein. Both were on top of the cooking hubs, which neither he nor Andrea ever used. “That’ll take forever, if it happens at all.”
Andrea beamed out of the fridge and Cracker’s heart chilled.
A table of data appeared, covering Andrea’s torso. Cracker was annoyed he could no longer see her breasts. The table scrolled and highlighted a row. There was her name, Andrea Novetine, allocated to James Marrin.
“He’s an Information Integrity Manager,” she boasted from the fridge’s surface, and Cracker felt flatter than her cold image. “I’m moving up, boy. A Cleaner? What was I thinking?” She laughed.
Cracker decided on affirmative proactiveness, then wondered if proactiveness was a real word.
Tax it! He was going to the club.

The Stick It Inn was an open club, allowing entry to all and sundry, if Miss All and Master Sundry met the dress regulations. Cracker had no illusions about his dress sense; he was irretrievably in the deep end of the sundry pool, and he never wore the correct bathing suit. But Cracker knew the bouncers at the Stick It (all corrupt ex-cops) and could slip in with a wink and a nod.
His mates were already there, not at their regular table, but a table on the other side of the club, still near enough the dance floor for gawking, and far enough for conversation. Halo was already scanning the dancers like a sniper. 
“Found her yet?” Cracker asked.
Halo did not acknowledge his presence. 
“He’s already in search-and-destroy mode,” Major said. Cracker rolled his eyes and nodded. 
“C’mon Halo,” Abe said. “Cracker’s here.”
“Can’t talk. Hunting,” Halo said in a psycho-killer monotone. The others laughed. Halo was a riot.
“Say hello, Halo,” pleaded Abe. “Think of Cracker. He’s single again. He needs cheering up.”
“Andrea is a fake Stacy bitch,” Halo said. “She deserves to die.”
In spite of the crowd, the music, and the gaming machines, silence descended around their table.
Halo broke his trance and lightened up. “Too much?” He grinned and shone his lighthouse eyes upon them. With relief, the others joined him in his sudden humour. “So, I hope you know,” he said to Cracker, “your friends are looking after you. Never be sad.”
“Andrea doesn’t matter,” Cracker said. “I met the perfect woman today. You’d crawl across 3 k’s of broken—”
“—glass and suck a dick to make her happy,” everyone but Halo chorused in.
“Two o’clock!” Halo said, and scrambled to the dance floor.
“There he goes,” Major said with dismay. “He’ll score again for sure.”
“What’s he got that I haven’t?” Abe challenged.
Cracker and Major allowed the embarrassed silence to build. They didn’t even sip their beers, so it was clear Abe should never ask such a question again.
Halo had a technique, but he’d never tell. “I know the shortcut to a woman’s heart,” was his complete and repetitious explanation. Whatever it was, Halo and his cible d'amour would usually be out of the club so fast, she would leave coat and handbag. However, this time, Halo’s technique failed him, and he returned to the table, sullen and doubly determined. The others knew to leave him to his ruminations and move the conversation elsewhere.
As the boys were discussing the form of last year’s premiership winners, Halo offered, “Love is the stuff that ejaculates from your cock.” Then he brightened, and—mouth agape with wonder, and pupils dilated—his engorgement discovered its current lodgings in his underwear needed French windows. “Oh, tax!” he cursed. “It’s her. The one.”</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="B5C5195F-1460-4E91-8D5F-F8244154C03F">
            <Title>The Big Sleep</Title>
            <Synopsis>The story here stretches credulity a bit.  At the end I want the virus locked down in the vault, but Sarah (who is only young girl at the time) also has to get the virus.  The lab gets locked down - so how does she get the virus?
I chose a pretty banal storyline whereby the Professor is the partner of Sarah's mother.  Knowing that they will be locked down, he sends a sample to Sarah's mother so she could inoculate herself and Sarah (and I think I might have a sister somewhere too, but I may have taken her out of the book)  I think this bit needs a re-think/edit.
Further to this, (in another chapter) the lab complex is dug up 20 years later and I have the occupants inside the lab alive because they have the virus and they had planned for such an eventuality. They weren't even affected by mental illness because of the virus.</Synopsis>
            <Text>
			The Big Sleep

The unmistakable sound of the outer security doors of the complex closing and sound of the interlock rang out as the small team of micro-biologists and genetic engineers gathered in the meeting room.
“What did he say?” Dr. Janine Anstey asked. She was the real genius in this gathering of the scientific elite. A virologist in her mid-fifties, but she looked thirty, tops. Not one silver hair atop her shapely skull and her eyes sparkled brightly a youthfully iridescent pale blue. She had little need of make-up, the faint acne scarring of her youth completely faded, giving way to silk smooth skin.
“We are to remain in lock-down until further notice. No messages in or out. Chairman Hardy says there is a credible terrorist threat and he doesn’t want the virus getting out.” Professor Herrman tried out the official lie. “We would be a high value target.” He added to see if he could whip up a little anti-terrorist hysteria.
“He doesn’t want us getting out.” Interjected Howard Millicent, the youthful post doctoral research assistant. “Look at me! I am 29 and I look like I just finished high school! Someone was bound to notice and Mr Hardy does not want this secret to get out. We are now a problem, a problem to be solved. I shudder at what Mr. Hardy has in store for us.”
“I concur. They don’t want to lock terrorists out, they want to lock us in. What do you have to say Professor Herrman?” Dr. Anstey politely inquired.
“We all agreed that this was a possibility. I must admit I had hoped he had embraced it for the earth shattering discovery that it is, but that appears to have been wishful thinking. What of the packages, Sun Ye? Were they delivered?”
“I only got yours out, professor. The rest of us thought we would have more time.” She took a short breath. “I may never see my family again.” She too was blooming from the effects of the virus. Her fine Asian features had always been pleasing to the eye, but her two front teeth, possessed of a bitter hatred, had grown far apart, each twisting to face away in disgust. The other teeth, cramped for room, had poked out at peculiar angles making her most able to eat an apple through a tennis-racquet. Of late however, her teeth had reconciled and become good friends, cuddling up in neat rows of gleaming white. “You are not married, are you professor? Who did you send it to?”
“Someone very reliable.” He hoped that was true. He had only known her for a short time, but was certain she was the one for him. Her delightful daughters were as intelligent and stunning as her mother and brought much longed for joy to his soulless life of science.
“Did you tell Mr Hardy how it effects evolution?” Dr Anstey was stern, but just bursting out of that blouse.
“I didn’t have time.” Professor Herman was unconvincing. He thought to tell his boss but then got caught up in preparing the innoculant and then the leading man of Ulyssia was gone. “Where was I supposed to begin? Mr Hardy, sir, in the name of pure research……”
“Pure research? In the name of your vainglorious curiosity more like it!” Anstey was having none of it. “What on earth made you splice tardigrade DNA into the virus? Such a cliche.”
“Because they are nearly impossible to kill. They survived 5 extinction events over millions of years, almost unchanged. It seems to me that they are either a perfect organisms or so immediately adaptable to vastly different conditions, evolution can do no more. They are so tough that they can live in boiling water, in ice, on the highest mountain and depths of the sea. They can survive with no water or food, they can survive in open space. Why wouldn’t I want to make my designs just as durable?” It seemed to be a no-brainer to the Professor. 
“Don’t try to teach me how to suck eggs. I know how tough a tardigrade is.” Dr Anstey did not like to be lectured on the incredibly obvious. Everyone knew about tardigrades, didn’t they? 
 “I didn’t just use the tardegrade, I spliced in a number of other gene-profiles including three types of extremo-phile organisms from deep sea volcanic vents and from anaerobic lakes filled with sulpher and cyanide. I have spliced together the toughest hybrid DNA known. This is tardegrade DNA on steroids.”
She was, however, most interested to know how the gene transfer was achieved without contamination. “What was your methodology? How did you keep the sample clear of impurities?”
“I did not want to transfer any foreign genes from unwanted bacteria so the selected individual was placed in a sterile agar dish, prepared with with 1% penicillin streptomycin. I left it there for 48 hours without food to clear the gut content and remove surface microorganisms. After 48 hours, it was repeatedly washed on a sterile nylon mesh with 30 μm pores and observed under the microscope at 1000 times magnification to ensure there were no remaining surface bacteria. Then I just followed normal protocols. You know the drill.”
“How many freeze-thaw cycles?”
“Two. The cleaned animal was placed in a low-binding PCR tube and a genomic lysis buffer with 0.5% beta-mercaptoenthanol. It was then subjected to the two freeze-thaw cycles and the genomic DNA was extracted and sheared to target fragments. It was a mere matter of collecting, purifying and quantifying for sequencing. I was prepared for a long trial and error period of splicing and re-splicing, but the very first attempt produced our Tardigrade-Hybrid Virus. It is every bit as tough as the Tardigrade itself.” He could not believe his luck.
“Your ego has condemned us to a slow death, no doubt even slower with the un-killable virus prolonging our agony.” Dr Anstey walked towards the door, as if to make a grand exit, but stopped and turned to looked at her colleagues. “They are going to lock us in here forever, you know that don’t you?”
“We have made certain plans.” Professor Hermann sounded confident, but his mind was filled with doubt. “I think we can hold out for as long as it takes.”
“I hope those plans involve strippers and hookers. I am presently suffering with the hormone profile on a 17 year old boy and I don’t think I can hold out for even 10 more minutes, let alone 10 years. My balls are about to explode.” Howard cupped his crotch gently. His post graduate work could wait, there was other business, most urgent.
“Sorry, no hookers.”
“Man, this is going to a be a drag.”
“I wouldn’t be too certain.” Dr Anstey eyes were lit up with mischief. “I presently have the hormone profile of a 30 year old woman and we are going to have a bit of time on our hands. Besides, you look so cute as a 17 year old.”
“Thank you, thank you. If you don’t mind Dr Anstey. My need appears to be a little urgent.” He said.
“Of course. My office?” She suggested, but he was already there. “This conversation is not over Dr Hermman, just postponed” She said as she scurried out. 

</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="DF34DB09-7588-4575-84C0-7839390B055C">
            <Title>Meet the Boss</Title>
            <Synopsis>I have no idea what was going on there.  I think I was going to introduce the CEO -  he was going to give a soliloquy on the PTD social structures and hierarchy, but it looks like I did not get around to it.  A good spot to put in what you think is appropriate.
</Synopsis>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="CAB83626-B4AB-45FB-8156-D6AAACF1B2D5">
            <Title>Gravity Well</Title>
            <Synopsis>There is a bunch of little snippets from different arcs of the story.  (All, generally, setting up the attempt by the Elvi to obtain/destroy the virus samples in the old lab.  The method of "assault" up the lab is to set up a hopefully amusing scene where the main characters are shot out of a tunnel along with about 10.000litres of liquid shit.  The tunnel  is supposed to be a giant arse from which they are explode out las a massive attack of the squirts.)

This is the start of the "official investigation" into the nature of the discs.  Hayley sets it up on her own authority.  Johnny has to be co-opted to assist - he doesn't like the idea of working for the public sector.  This is to set up the bit towards the end where Richard obtains all the knowledge of mutliple universes.
The meeting chaired by Damien Hardman is to give the reader information about the Black operation to obtain samples o virus from the old research labs.
The meeting between Sarah, Hayley and Agent Jones is to let the Elvi know that they are under an existential threat.  (Hayley (a cop) does not like intelligence agents and has there is no big conflict in choosing to assist the Elvi)
Meeting between Hayley and the High Elvis - to clue the Elvi up about the threat of genocide and to get to the point where they have to dig up the lab themselves and try to beat the agents of the PTD to the virus samples.
Planning to dig up the lab - Cracker thinks up a plan.  He has to speak to the owner of the nightclub that was bombed.
Short snippet of The Johnny standing in a singe lab coat in a smoking laboratory - he had spun the discs.
Scene with Damien Hardman and some research scientists - They accidently made a virus that only kills red heads - Damien wants to keep the virus because he is not too keen on red heads.  ("The Director imagined a world without redheads and concluded they wouldn’t be missed.  Freckles and orange pubes?  Alabaster skin that turns to cancer under the glare of a 50 watt bulb?")
There is an Elvis benefit concert - to hide the tunnelling. 
</Synopsis>
            <Text>Gravity Well
3 to 4 weeks after bombing
The Johnny was none too happy when he was dragged into Police H.Q. by a couple of uniformed rock-apes. He had never had occasion to speak to a low life-walloper for any reason, nor had he harboured any no desire to do so. He had made the mistake of refusing their kind invitation outright and slammed his door in their unsmiling faces. A mistake that was something, shortly thereafter, as they dragged him into the elevator and down to street level, he came to regret. To his credit he concluded antagonising the cops any further would be counter-productive to future breathing and metabolic functions and he offered up no further resistance. The silent gorillas physically carried him to a tiny office where Sarah was smugly seated behind an embarrassingly small desk.
“Sarah? This is your new contract? Public Service? I can’t believe you were behind this. They assaulted me! On your instructions, no less.” Johnny was not happy, but he was never happy about anything, ever. “Whatever you want, I am not interested.”
“Thank you for taking up my invitation, Johnny. I really need your help again.” Sarah placed an evidence bag on the desk.
“Invitation? They dragged me here.” He picked up the plastic bag and eyes widened with fright when he saw the Gyges disc inside. “No, No. I am not going down that road again. Not for you, not for anyone.”
“C’mon Johnny. You can examine the disc with the blessing of the PTD police department. It is still top secret, but there will be no legal ramifications and no damage to your reputation.”
“I am worried about damage to my body,” He gingerly ran his fingers along the length of the red-raw scar atop his crown. “Not my reputation.”
“It gives you character, Johnny. Besides, I told you to behave yourself and you dismissed my advice outright.” Sarah counselled. “Perhaps you might want to listen in future.”
“I see they have rewarded you for shooting that low-life cleaner. What a lovely office.” His sarcasm was exaggerated and unmistakable. 
“No, this is your new office. For a couple of days, anyway.” Sarah tried desperately not to laugh, but the look of horror on Johnny’s face was just priceless and she had to let go with a giggle.
“Not on your life, Sarah. I am not staying here. Goodbye.” He turned and walked out the door and was immediately escorted back by the two large uniforms.
“Johnny, you will be expected to examine this disc and compare it with the first one we located. Okay? We want to know what it is, what it does and where it was manufactured. This part is important, so listen carefully. The information we have is that the discs are, indeed, powered by gravity waves and they must never come into close contact with one another or spun in the centre axis or you run the risk of creating a gravity well. They is the only limitations on your examinations. Apart from that you have carte-blanche to do whatever you deem fit.”
“In that case, let me at it.” Johnny was still in sarcasm mode. “A gravity well, what could possibly go wrong?”
“I want you because you have a some experience with these discs. If you don’t want to help I will get someone else. Do you know anyone else you would trust to do the work?” Sarah knew appealing to his ego would bring him around. “There has to be someone else. Someone smarter than you, perhaps?”
“Very funny. As far as I can tell, there isn’t anyone smarter that me.” Johnny’s pride took hold. “Nobody I know would have the talent.”
“There must be someone else, but...” Sarah knew she had him. “You are the only one I can trust. Anyone else might destroy us.”
“I will need a fully fitted out lab and at least 3 assistants.”
“There’s two labs in the forensic facility, one for each disc, with six assistants awaiting your instructions. You will collect the other disc and bring it in.” 
“I will only do it if I get paid more than you.” He didn’t care how much, just as long as it was more than Sarah.
“It’s a deal.” Sarah did not really mind who was paid more, she was really cleaning up and had no problem with Johnny getting amongst the lucre. “Go fetch the disc now. There is a possibility the company agents may try to steal it back, so be careful. The two constables outside will escort you there and back. They have been instructed to protect you with their lives. You are now officially on contract to this department, and until all your tests are complete you are a contemptible public servant, just like me.” She again allowed herself to giggle at Johnny as embarrassment and anguish comically distorted his chubby pale face.
“This will not look good on a resume.” He knew that wasn’t true. If it was okay for corporations to squeeze the state dry, it was certainly okay for him. The private sector had a long and questionable history of sucking at the public teat and who was he to criticise?
* * *
Director Hardman sat at the head of a long conference table, chairing the first meeting of his highly secretive team tasked with retrieving viral agents from the Hardy Bio-Weapons facility. Damien had given the briefing a couple of days before and let his minions digest the enormity of the assignment. He now expected some brilliant ideas as to how they could dig through 40 metres of rock and 10 metres of concrete without raising any suspicion and recovering the bio-agents without starting a second viral cleansing. 
Agent Jones sat quietly, trying not to look guilty while, at the same time, not too relaxed either, but with four shattered ribs he was in no danger of looking relaxed. The feeling of being compromised by an external agency sickened him. He had always been a company man and spying for the police was not a comfortable fit. He wanted desperately to cleanse his soul and tell the Director about his encounter with the PTD Police, but that would be suicide. Not only would he have to confess to losing another disc, he would have to admit to being beaten up by a slender beauty queen. His ego would not let him look there. 
“Director, are you able to tell us why we are digging up something so dangerous?” Agent Kimberley asked innocently.
“I have not been able to tell you until now, until all your security checks and transfer orders had been completed. Now I can unequivocally say, ladies and gentlemen, you are the elite of the agency and have been selected for this task due to your commitment to freedom and democracy.” Also because you are all so eminently disposable, he thought. “The Primary Trade Dominion faces the greatest peril since its formation 50 years ago. There is a cancer, creeping silently through our souls, eating away at our very way of life. This cancer is the Elvi.” 
“Surely not, Director. They are just entertainers, aren’t they?” Agent Kimberly again showed his naivety. Director Hardman decided this guy would be the first to go.
“That is precisely what they want us all to think. That they are merely a lovable bunch of singers, dancers and bartenders, whose sole purpose for being is to entertain us, but they are nothing of the sort. They organise and conspire against the PTD. Their concerts are filled with subversive political commentary aimed at undermining the natural order and to ultimately change the structure of our society, placing themselves at the top. The lyrics to many songs are direct threats to vital industries that thrive under our deregulated system. Coal producers are livid and they are threatening mass sackings if something is not done. It’s the same with the power companies.”
“Can’t we just kill them? It sounds like doctrinal divergence to me.” Agent Jones wanted to be the first to offer up that option. He knew it was a ridiculous suggestion, but he wanted to sound hard core in front of his juniors.
“No, we can’t kill them. Not directly. There are too many and it would be too costly. Not only that, for every Elvis there are five Dawks who would no doubt riot.” 
“What is the plan, boss?” Agent Jones knew when to suck up.
“The Bio-weapons labs that we are breaking into were working on viral agents targeting one specific genome, one specific target. We are going to recover that research and if we are lucky, safely recover some samples. It is hoped that our scientists will be able to complete the research. A virus that targets one Elvis, targets all the Elvi.”
“We are going to kill them after all.” Agent Jones was all smiles. He would be involved in an actual genocide! Wow! That will look good on a resume, he automatically thought, before being brought back to earth by the reality he was now a Barbie doll’s bitch and promotion prospects were grim. He had seen her shoot the cleaner on Random Camera, but he could have sworn, two hours before, she would have given her life to protect him. There was no way he was crossing a crazy, fickle bitch like her.
“I will now introduce you to Professor James Ararie, PhD, Fellow of the International College of Bio-engineers, L.5.” The Director sat down and left the pointy head to illuminate his collection of dullards on the finer points of micro-biology. The only information they really needed to know was how to identify the specimens and transport them safely. Unfortunately they were all about to sit through a four hour lecture comprised of scientific gobbledygook and intellectual self indulgence.
* * *
With trust in their police colleagues completely evaporated, Hayley and Sarah, decided to rendezvous with Agent Jones in the quarter, using Elvi as surveillance and security. The Minister for Internal Security had given them a team of 50 Elvis and Elvine operatives to secure the area, expelling any non-Elvis in a 200 metre radius. Neither company agent nor cop could get close, even if Agent Jones was tailed. Upon crossing the security perimeter he was blindfolded and taken before the Blessed Mother and Holy Aunt Hayley.
His vision returned with a violent jerk of the blindfold. Agent Jones was seated on single chair in the centre of an abandoned auditorium with at least 10 Elvi standing along each wall, vacantly staring ahead. Hayley threw the piece of dark cloth on the floor and stood silent behind him while Sarah stood directly in front, 2 metres distant.
“You said you had something for us?” Sarah stepped forward.
“Yes, but I don’t think it should be said with your entourage within earshot.” He leaned back in his chair and away from the advancing Blessed Mother.
“Could all Elvi please wait outside?” Sarah asked in her best Blessed Mother voice. She had not quite perfected it yet, but she was going for a soft, serene and confident monotone. Without a word the room cleared in a choreographed military dance.
“I have found out why they are digging up the bio-weapons labs. They are planning to release a virus and kill off the Elvi.” He delivered with a smirk and a sickening tone of approval.
“Won’t they kill everyone else?” Sarah did not allow her alarm to ripple the calm surface. 
“They plan to target the Elvi genome specifically. Our scientists are in their labs just waiting for the samples and the research data, practicing their gene transfer procedures. They have already cooked up some new and deadly pathogens and plan to eventually kidnap a group of Elvi, to conduct tests, but at the moment the Bio-Engineers are confident they have more than enough data to make significant headway.”
“How long will it take to drill down?” Hayley asked.
“We haven’t even started yet. The security issues alone will take a month. Transferring that shit to another facility will be a nightmare. They haven’t even manufactured the canisters yet. I’ve got the plans for them in my implant if you want to download them.”
“I have been trying to get maps and blueprints for all the buildings in that area and can’t get hold of anything. I keep getting told the buildings are so old the plans have not been digitised, but that’s bureaucrat-speak for classified.” Hayley did not like being thwarted by her own employer.
“I have those plans too.”
“Good work, Agent Jones.” Sarah was genuine, but Hayley remained silent. “We will see you next week. Make sure you contact us if they start drilling.”
“Yes, Mrs Shakti.” Agent Jones smiled and looked directly at her, unblinking.
“Yes, I am to be allocated to the son of Eloise Shakti.” Sarah fired back, pleased his sudden discomfort showed her salvo had hit her target. No-one wanted to cross Eloise Shakti. “Yes, you have yet another woman to fear.” 
“I will report anything of interest and download all the documents I can.” His courage melted away as his spine returned to its natural gelatinous state.
“They are on a deadline and will be moving fast. We will have to react just as quickly and somehow get ahead of them.” Sarah stood perfectly still, staring into Agent Jones’ eyes, hoping he comprehended the urgency of this unfolding situation.
“I understand.” Agent Jones stood up and walked to the door where he was met with his Elvi escort party.
They waited until they were certain their voices could not be heard. The enormity of what they had been told was hitting hard. Genocide.

* * *
“What is your proposal Holy Aunt Hayley?” He had been hoping for a mundane day of kicking back in his office and writing a song or two, but the High Elvis once again found himself mid-crisis. This one was a real beaut, but he was growing as a leader and took it in his stride.
“We have a man on the inside who will slow their progress as best he can and if necessary we can opt for sabotage, but the laboratories and their contents will still be there. Someone else is bound to try. We must be the first into that research complex and we must destroy the samples where they are stored, nothing can be permitted to get out.” Holy Aunt Hayley had no idea how to accomplish this and hoped someone else might have some ideas. Her discussions with Blessed Mother Sarah had not been particularly fruitful. “That is my proposal High Elvis. We have to stop this or die trying.”
“You will have at your disposal all the resources of the entire Elvi nation. What we lack in technology, we make up for in man power. You must succeed or the Elvi will perish, Holy Aunt.”
“Once we have examined the plans for pipes, conduits and tunnels we might be able to work out a way of getting there first. This should be on a need to know basis only. Our success depends entirely on strict security. I hold no fears the Elvi will betray us, but we need them to carry on as if nothing is happening.”
“The Elvi will never betray your trust, but I agree, knowledge of the conspiracy would place them in a difficult position. Only those who need to know.” Paternalism now. Is there no line I will not cross? He was fast learning that leadership meant compromising everything, fondly held ideals included. “I insist you enlist The Great Teacher to assist in your quest. I fear you think him a buffoon, but he observes from a most obscure angle and he is schooled in the way of the street. He will see what you are unable to see and he can obtain what is out of your reach. I do not think the Blessed Mother will be able to give you all of her attention in the coming months as the arrival of the one-after-the-one approaches. Praise be to the Dawkins.”
“He is hardly a replacement for Sarah, High Elvis, but he could be of some assistance.” She didn’t think so, but like everyone, else she had been told never to insult an Elvis, particularly the High Elvis. She thought Cracker was a smart-Alec unable to take any situation seriously and apart from his recent resurrection, he had not done anything to impress her. Although she she did quietly admit, resurrection was a pretty good trick.
* * *
“We have to get into this bunker, through 10 metres of concrete. We need covert access to the site. Any ideas?” Hayley was confident Cracker would be of as much assistance as a gorilla, but for the sake of keeping the High Elvis happy she decided to go through the motions. Cracker reached his right hand over the top of his head and scratched the left ear and hunch over the emitter and then dragged his knuckles across the floor to the far side of the holographic presentation. His little primate brain ticked over as his scratched at his chest and adjusted his groin.
“Yeah, I see your problem, but I think I might have a solution. Do you recognise that building?” Cracker pointed to an old multi storey stone structure. 
“No, what of it?” Hayley had no idea where he was going with this. The building was at least 100 metres from the bio-weapons bunker.
“You should recognise it, Detective Lanning. The ground floor and basement was the site for the Stick It Inn nightclub.”
“So?”
“Don’t you see? That is the site of the terrorist bombing.”
“I know that.”
“They are finishing up the demolition of the building right now and have cleared right down to 3rd sub basement level.” He then waved his hand over the hologram emitter and raised a 3-D map of the city’s underground. “Look, the sub-basement levels are about the same depth as the bunker. There are a few pipes and conduits in the way, but we could dig across, on top of the sandstone layer, through clay rather than concrete.”
As a matter of policy she wanted to reject the idea of her inferior, but could not immediately poke a hole in the proposal. She and Sarah had been staring at the maps and scratching her their heads for days trying to come up with a plan. Perhaps it was a manifestation of a devious, level 2 type of intelligence, she thought, nothing for her to be concerned about. “It’s a long way. Do you think we can dig through 100 metres of clay quicker than they can drill through rocks and concrete?” 
“I don’t know, but I can find out easily. I know a few guys in demolition. You know, the types of people you would never to talk to.” Cracker had been getting a superior vibe from Hayley and wanted her to know it had not gone unnoticed.
“Can you get us on site?” She let his jibe slip through to the wicket-keeper.
“I think I can, but we are going to be there for quite a few days, so we are going to need a pretty good cover story. I mean we can bribe the contractors quite easily, but digging a tunnel of that nature will raise some suspicion. I will speak with the High Elvis.”

* * * 
Cracker double checked the address he had been given. This place seem for too plain for the residence of a wealthy night club owner, but the addresses matched. It was a average building in a district that housed mostly lower-level management on their way up the ladder or executives on their plummet down. The proprietor of the Stick it Inn was a businessman on a downward spiral with no hope of recovery and no parachute. 
“Mr Siddig?” Cracker smiled and thrust out his hand. “Tony Hardy, but call me Cracker. Thank you for agreeing to see me. I understand you must be very busy at the moment.”
“What do you want, Mr Hardy?” Ahmed Siddig, the former owner of the “Stick it Inn” had hit on hard times since the bombing. “If you are after money, you have come to the wrong place.”
“I understand you have no plans to re-open your nightclub?”
“That’s right, the fucken insurance company is refusing to pay. I funded the demolition myself, figured the insurance would come through eventually, but they are still wriggling and squirming. I now own the most expensive hole in the ground in the Primary Trade Dominion.” Siddig would complain about the insurance industry to anyone within ear shot, whether they wanted to hear it or not. Being rendered destitute was very unnerving to the successful businessman and his misery perpetually sought release.
“I may be of some assistance to you Mr Siddig.” 

* * *
The fire had been put out and the singed lab-coats were rushing about assessing the damage to the smoking laboratory. Spent fire extinguishers and tangled fire hoses were strewn about the floor as the extraction fans worked overtime trying to clear the room of smoke, allowing scorched lungs some relief.
“Well, we have learned something today. Never spin the disc!” The Johnny coughed out, trying to smile. Blue electric bursts had shot out of the disc like tiny lightening bolts, penetrating concrete, ballistic glass and metal lab equipment like they were tissue paper. Looking in awe at the scorched pattern on the wall, he could feel the disbelieving stares of his underlings, each wondering what sort of a fool had been placed in charge of this contract. “Okay, clean up. We will transfer everything to the other lab and make sure you keep those discs apart.”
* * *
“What happened?” Director Hardman yelled at the hologram.
“We have been conducting some preliminary experiments in viral gene transfer. It is very interesting, Director. We know our predecessors used tardigrade DNA to horizontally transfer plant and algae genes and we have had remarkable success in replicating their processes. The genes, they, they transfer so easily, the possibilities are limitless. It is an astonishing field of research. Paths previously blocked are now open.” Dr Kitsen was speaking way too fast for Damien to keep up. 
“You are babbling doctor, what happened?”
“We tested new prototype pathogen. Our calculations assured us that no-one was in danger.” The nervous junior geneticist stuttered. “It killed every red-head in the room.”
“Anyone I know?” 
“I would not think so, sir. They were just random lows.”
“Document the experiment and safely store the pathogen.” 
“Don’t you want us to destroy it?” Dr Kitsen hoped so. His flame haired wife would not be happy to know he was behind a Ranga Holocaust.
“No, it might have some commercial value in the future.” Director Hardman did not want to lose an opportunity to create a valuable weapon. 
“But it only targets redheads.” 
“Yes.” The Director imagined a world without redheads and concluded they wouldn’t be missed. Freckles and orange pubes? Alabaster skin that turns to cancer under the glare of a 50 watt bulb? He visibly shuddered. “Continue with your experiments, I want you well practiced when the samples arrive. We will be on a tight schedule when the time comes, but in the meantime please conduct no further tests on live subjects.”
“Yes, Director.”
Director Hardman then contacted the drill team by audio link. “Agent Jones, I need an update.” It had taken weeks just to build the giant airtight structure, sitting over the drill site. The outside of which was plastered with posters depicting a statue, some 5 metres tall, of a mother holding her dying child and a sign reading ‘The future site of the Great Viral Cleansing Memorial and Museum.’ The sign was covered with ‘The one-after-the-one is coming’ posters within hours.
“We have plasma drilled as far as we dare, about 5 metres into the concrete. Lasers were not doing the job so we have resorted to concussion drills, but the concrete is very hard and we keep snapping drill bits. Our progress is slower now, but we have to be careful where we penetrate the labs. We must hasten slowly, if we poke a hole in the wrong spot there might be viral Armageddon.” Agent Jones was under strict instructions from his handlers to go as slow as he dared without alerting the boss. He had been horrified to see how easily the plasma cutter and lasers sliced into the massive chunk a bio-crete and had acted quickly to stymie progress.
“Hasten, Agent Jones.” The clock was ticking.
 * * *
The massive stage spanned clear across the enormous square hole that once was the Ulyssian Trade Tower, a historic and ugly stone relic, neglected for decades before its final demise. Elderly lime cement, exhausted by a century long vigil, had turned to grit and poured out of joints like hourglass sand, gradually bleeding away structural integrity. The small, but powerful terrorist bomb had found a perfect mark, cracking the structure to its foundations and rendering it nothing but smoldering rubble. 
Above the stage a huge banner read “Elvis Arts and Entertainment Benefit concert - Bring back the Stick it Inn” (Description of stage equipment) A benefit concert was an event that no-one in the PTD had ever attended. The concept of earning money and then giving it away was so novel, that a massive crowd of Dawks and bemused citizens arrived on the street many hours before the music began. They began chanting, “Dawks, Dawks, Dawks!” and singing their favourite Elvis tunes loudly and horribly. A few roadie-Elvises quickly tired of their songs being destroyed in such a manner, broke out their guitars and waded into the crowd. The crowd cheered loudly and parted to allow entry to the minstrels as they serenaded the throng, killing off the wretched singing that had offended their ears so. 
Underneath the frantic activity on stage, some 20 metres down on the floor of the vast excavation, was a menagerie earth moving of machines, digging tools and augers that a group of black clad Elvi were climbing all over, trying to work out the confusing bevy of levers and buttons that controlled the metal monsters. Mr Siddig had left his plant equipment in the hole, awaiting float to the next job. In a effort to distance himself, he had gone on a business trip but had kindly let it slip that the machines would not be moved for 4 weeks and no-one would be on site that entire time. He hoped his trip might provide some some distance and plausible deniability, but figured it would be a stretch, even if he gave them no further assistance. 
“Safety is our paramount consideration, you mad Elvises. Let’s get some practice before we use these machines in earnest.” Cracker wanted no injuries on his watch. Digging a tunnel under roads and multi-storey buildings was a specialist activity for engineers, but they could not allow a security breach and decided to roll the dice. Each Elvis had brain-dumped every byte of data they could find on the operation excavators and the intricacies of tunnel engineering, but even the most optimistic of them recognised the gulf between theory and practice. They faced death, but were happy to sacrifice themselves for their brothers and sisters. They had a legion of Elvi to use up if their were any injuries or fatalities, each of whom would gladly place themselves in harms way.
Like a bunch of toddlers in a giant sand pit, they took to the machines and began an infantile and highly dangerous game of dodgem-cars. It took many crashes and some cracked heads for each Elvis to become familiar enough to commence some preliminary excavation. They commenced by using a giant augur, ordinarily used to drill vertical shafts, strapped negligently between two excavators horizontally and they hoed in - balls out. In only a few hours they had dug out a twenty metre shaft, two metres high, before the auger thing collapsed into a spinning mess of metal and dirt.
“The Dawkins save us.” The Great Teacher crossed his fingers and they got to work. To mitigate flooding it was decided to dig the tunnel on a gentle grade upwards for 20 metres before diving under the massive tangle of sewer pipes and sundry conduits blocking a direct path. Despite the keystone-cop start, they made admirable progress until the threat of cave-in became grave and exponentially more likely for each metre of digging. Being unable to secure re-enforcing steel, spray on foam-crete was the best, but most expensive option to sure up the tunnel. The Minister for Finance was furious at the giant chunk of revenue it removed from the coffers, but she was trumped by the Minsters for Defence and Graceland Security. For safety reasons, it would have been preferable to remove the digging equipment before spraying each five metre section, but it took too long and they could not stop to wait for the foam to set. 
The concert above had gotten into full swing, the amplified sound and the approving crowd drowned out any noises that might waft to street level. Most of the digging was now muffled by the length of the tunnel but the concert continued and from all accounts they were raising a fortune. After three days the benefit was wound up, raising more than enough for Mr Siddig to commence reconstruction and with the promised investment from the Elvi, he could secure a loan and the Ulyssian Trade Tower would rise out of the ashes. 
- - -</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="9F791755-23A0-4F59-A82C-5079079935F7">
            <Title>I think you got me</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="A8E3A979-3C27-4B83-8883-86BAF1DF7933">
            <Title>Sacrificial Elvis</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="33883715-077F-46E8-A0FF-95C81100424A">
            <Title>The Commissioner</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="4148BDBC-8238-459F-A9DE-BCF32E6A7866">
            <Title>Fireworm</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="C51658B6-8A41-45A0-823E-C2B55D2B7B5F">
            <Title>Hayley</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="FF1A3CE6-5DC3-4A84-AEB1-CDE65F52C521">
            <Title>A Big Crush-1</Title>
            <Text>A Big Crush
90 minutes after bombing - year zero.
By the time Cracker and Sarah made it back to street level a mob had gathered and swollen to a dense unending thicket of humanity, sucking the very life out of the air. Gasping for breath Cracker and Sarah waded into the vast seething mass and, although their progress frustratingly slow, they incrementally plotted a course through many thousands of street people, vendors, whores and shit-bags towards the throbbing lights of the entertainment district. Rank fumes assaulted Sarah’s nose and eyes, making both run uncontrollably, obliging her to tear a square of fabric from her skirt for a makeshift handkerchief. Putrid airborne particles violated her pristine, unblemished skin, producing a maddening desire to scratch, which she did not do, not once. Analyzing the ebb and flow of the crowd, as a distraction from her screaming skin, she was soon able to predict the path of least resistance having realised, in this environment, the quickest way between two points was not a straight line. Shuffling sideways or even backwards, at the right moment, when the cadence of the crowd changed, provided an opportunity to take advantage of a surge or avoid a crush. As advancement accelerated, they became increasingly aware that it was becoming rather damp underfoot and the brown water seemed to be rising at an alarming rate. 
“Is it usually like this?” Sarah asked, genuinely surprised by the enormity of the crowd.
“No. The security zone around the explosion must have been expanded and they are all being funneled this way. We have got to get out of here; it’s going to get dangerous.” Cracker was on clean up at “The Big Crush” six years previous. The crowd had been so vast that thousands were crushed or suffocated where they stood and he could never forget the macabre sight of the dead, all standing, melded tightly together by rigor mortis in huge human sheaves. Wedging them apart took so much time that they were ordered to simply scoop them up with a front-end loader and dump them in a truck. A very gruesome duty, considering the odd few were still alive. He had met Shakti after ducking off to throw up only to find the new police recruit already occupying the best spot, upchucking what appeared to be an exceedingly un-chewed steak. 
“Is that real beef?” He had said, suddenly hungry.
“You can have it. I don’t think I’ll need it.” Shakti said gagging, his abdomen jerking with uncontrollable spasms. 
“Na. I’ll only spew it up as soon as I get back to work. I don’t think people are meant to see things like that.”
“Things like that should not happen.”
They hadn’t actually become friends that night, Shakti lacked the ability to connect, but they had become allies. The shared distress over the fatalities of “The Big Crush” was the foundation of their alliance, also the starting point of their respective journeys to claustrophobia. 
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="C8DABA65-1B54-480D-B963-925B4A828A3F">
            <Title>The Elvi</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="C777090C-D33F-4672-98D9-781BF6A100B9">
            <Title>Cancelled out discs</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="A783AD88-3F87-467E-B5D4-F3B1F52CD678">
            <Title>Farwell Kleo</Title>
            <Synopsis>This is supposed to be a mega-event.  100,000 Elvises and 100,000 guitars to mourn Kleo at the funeral. I need assistance to make this bit MEGA. The funeral has 10's of thousands of fire torches .....    Cracker gets permission to invite Hayley and Kathy to the funeral - He tells the High Elvis they are the Holy Aunts (just as a joke, but it sticks)
They sing a song - "The eighth concentric circle of Dante's Hell"
</Synopsis>
            <Text>Farewell Kleo
2 to 3 Weeks after bombing

The mammoth size of the proposed funeral ceremony caused the cabinet a great deal of concern. Every available Elvi would be present, forcing them to commandeer the decrepit old sports stadium in the quarter, long abandoned and overgrown. According to legend, long ago this tired structure had hosted a big international sports carnival involving, some said, Greeks running around nude, but it had been rarely used since. The exterior of the concrete and steel monolith, sprawling across several hectares of mud and slop, was completely covered in slippery moss, fungus and lichen with sickly weeds poking out of every crack and join.
Since the moment of Kleo’s passing, one in ten Elvi had spent every waking moment clearing, cleaning and buffing the entire stadium. Some of the grandstands were well past their use-by date and had the be condemned, but that did not mean they could not be filled to the brim with flashing lights and speakers. Due to lack of grass growing anywhere in the PTD, a lawn mower had not been manufactured for about 50 years, but would have come in handy. The field was mowed perfectly flat to what resembled a lawn, but was really a collection of weeds and undergrowth that had been hacked down to the same length by 1,000 Elvis wielding 1,000 pair of clippers. At full capacity the ground held 80,000 or so in the stadium, but with the a few of the stands closed, the Elvi were planning to squeeze in 65,000, with the rest of their number forming the funeral parade.

Hayley arrived in the stadium just in time for the official ceremony to begin. Seated in the private box at the southern end of the ground was the official entourage of the High Elvis, the Holy Mother and the Great Teacher. In the centre of the box was Sarah who, it appeared to Hayley, was the subject of reverence and love by every Elvis and Elvine. They adored her and also, much to her amazement, they seemed to have high regard for Cracker. A level 2 nobody is The Great Teacher? How ludicrous. It seemed, for whatever reason, they occupied an influential position in the quarter. As Hayley sat down she was pleased to see Dr Logan, seated in between Cracker and the High Elvis, was on her best behaviour, not giggling or fidgeting - not one bit. Her amazement morphed to amusement when she gleaned, from the conversations in the private box, that the these black haired entertainment clones had formed themselves a little government and were passing laws. Although the ideas of an ethnic minority creating their own parliament tickled her funny bone, but she knew that the Moral Compliance Directorate would take a very dim view. This was a challenge to the legal authority of the Primary Trade Dominion and the novice government will be soon crushed under foot.

The lights dimmed throughout the stadium and kettle drums beat out a tribal rhythm as the first of approximately 25,000 fire-torch wielding Elvi entered the stadium at the northern end. In the darkness the torches were thrown high into the air and between the participants in a choreographed, well practiced but dangerous dance of flames. The kettle drums became louder and more insistent as the flames continued to dance about in a manically, before the Elvi commenced stomping, clapping and singing a foot-tapping funeral song. The drums continued but faded into the background of resurgent stomping and clapping in syncopation. 
The Eighth Concentric Circle of Dante’s Hell
Since my Kleo left me,
I’ve been on a carousel,
In the eighth concentric circle,
Of Dante’s Hell.
I’ve been so lonely,
I’ve been so lonely, 
Since you died.
I might meet up with Virgil,
Ride Geryon for a spell,
In the eighth concentric circle ,
Of Dante’s Hell
I’ve been so lonely
I’ve been so lonely
Since you died.
You gave us all a purpose
The Dawkins did foretell
Without him we’ll be living 
 In Dante’s Hell.
We are so lonely 
We are so lonely
‘Cause you died.
 
The song continued to be belted out when the funeral procession parted, from the northern to the southern end of the stadium and a small group of 6 Elvi, carrying a coffin, entered the stadium. Spotlights burst into life and stung the coffin detail with beams of pure white as they made their way slowly and rhythmically to the centre of the ground where the coffin was placed high atop a massive pile of combustible material comprising mostly of rubbish collected during the stadium clean-up, but also some serious lumps of wood they had stumbled across. The funeral parade became silent and then, in a much practiced spiral pattern, the entire funeral parade began passing by the pyre and throwing their torches on top and then fading back into the crowd. 
The spotlights faded as the flames reached higher, lighting up the entire stadium with orange sobbing faces and as it died down one acoustic guitar, at the far end of the stadium, was heard strumming lightly. Then another, just near to the private box, replied to the lonely instrument, which return serve as another guitar was strummed, then another and another until 65,000 acoustic guitars were playing in unison. Voices from the field below chimed in and then every light in the stadium flashed brightly before fading to nothing. Neon lights, lasers and strobes all joined into the deafening cacophony of 65,000 guitars and 100,000 voices all slipping seamlessly from song to song. The black clad Elvi in the parade, in time to the music and in unison with each other, removed their outer garments to reveal highly colourful and glittering outfits that reflected both light and joy. The continuum song ended abruptly in perfect synchronicity and the entire crowd jumped to their feet. It was was over all too quickly, only 90 minutes, but the wake was planned to run over several days. The entire stadium then broke out into wild applause and cheering. The partying and dancing began.
“That was truly amazing! Wow, I am glad I was patching for that!” Dr Logan looked like a smile on legs. “That was an unprecedented cultural event. Thank you for inviting me, Cracker, thank you.”
“In the quarter I am the Great Teacher, Holy Aunt Kathy.” He joked. “These guys take it all seriously, even if I can’t.”
“Where are Hayley and Sarah?” Kathy asked, shooting glances in all directions. “Okay, I see them. It looks like they are having a deep and meaningful.”
 
“Lets get down to the field and join in. Got patches?” Cracker was ready to let his hair down, again. 
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="BDEBCDF0-F46B-4DBB-B7A5-5B8DBFF7564E">
            <Title>Sarah meets Agent Jones</Title>
            <Synopsis>14 hours before the bombing
9 months BTD

Why is Ajent Jones even looking here? He’d be expecting Emil to deliver the Disc.

He must know (somehow) that something has gone wrong with the plan and Emil either didn’t collect the Disc or he subsequently lost it</Synopsis>
            <Text>It was not now or never for Cracker, it was simply never. Soon, the elevator would bing, open, and swallow him whole, together with his steam cleaner and his cart with the medical-waste bag of suicide goo discretely closeted inside.
Cracker felt like suicide goo. He and the hapless Travis Teh were equals. As the elevator dropped him the 216 storeys down to the Cloudborne street, away from the office of angels, the dominion of Sarah, he grew lonelier. As fast as the elevator went, Cracker’s heart plummeted faster.
Out on the street, under the wheeze of the Layer, Cracker’s shoulders slumped and he crumpled. After Sarah, he doubted he’d ever be erect again.

A receptionist’s head bubbled out of Sarah’s spreadsheets.
“What?” Sarah seethed as she poked her holoscreen for the option to block unwanted office calls.
“There’s a man here …” The way the receptionist said it left no doubt he meant, distasteful man.
“And?” Sarah prompted impatiently.
“He’s asking after …”
“Who? Jesus? Santa? Jesus in a Santa suit?” Sarah resolved she’d get the name of the receptionist later and fire him.
“Mr Teh …”
Well, shit. The distasteful man could be important, and Sarah, on the morning of her first day, felt her insides chill. “Could you ask him to make an appointment?”
“Will do.” The receptionist’s holo-head spangled to pixellated stars and receded into the spreadsheets.
Sarah ground her teeth as she gestured through config menus, looking for ways to prevent the office productivity software from interrupting her, when the receptionist’s head bubbled up again.
“What, dammit? Can’t you see I’m …” still without a clue here?
“The man. He’s gone to Mr Teh’s office.”
“Call security,” Sarah said.
“Aren’t you in security?”
“Cyber security!” Sarah suppressed her rage … kinda. “Taxing gonads!” she cursed at no one in particular. The fading expression of the receptionist wondered if Sarah was calling him that. Sarah slipped on her immaculate shoes with ignoble haste, and set off, lady-sprinting down the hall.

“Can I help you?” she challenged the man in Shotgun Dude’s office.
The man looked at Sarah briefly, then resumed his sleazing around the room. Sarah decided it was sleazing, because he was a suspicious-looking, heavy-breathing, square-headed character. He had eyebrows that reminded her of caterpillars, and teeth that spoke of drug addiction. Questionably tall and suspiciously fidgety, he went about the office touchy-feeling shelves, opening cupboards, sliding out drawers and examining the undersides of coffee table figurines.
Sarah held her breath while convincing herself it was not from fear. “Well?”
“I am to sign some documents,” he said, and scratched into his black hair.
“Are you expecting to find them under the office art? How about the picture on the wall?”
The man smiled like a snake. “Ah, yes. Very good,” he said, wagging a crooked finger. He sleazed the painting from the wall, looked at its back, and ran his fingers around the frame. Disappointed, he set the artwork down. “I will see the files now.”
If Sarah’s blood was chill before, it became frozen margarita slashing through her veins. She looked about the wider office, searching for security or a brave man (Ha! A brave man!) but saw only colleagues gawking over partitions with startled eyes and mouths that formed vowels of horror.
“What kind of documents?” she asked.
“The kind you have, and I want,” he said, and Sarah knew she was being threatened.
“Mr. Teh has retired.”
“Oh?” This information alarmed him, to the extent he stopped prowling the office and bore his full attention on Sarah.
“It will take some time to put his outstanding matters in order,” she answered. “Perhaps, if you make an appointment—”
“I will see the files now. Please.”
Sarah winced as he advanced on her, and pulled a gun from his jacket. She screamed, which stopped him cold. She blinked; he furrowed his brow and cocked his head, as if that would loosen the reason he hadn’t killed her yet. As he became more confused, Sarah became more lucid.
“It’s clear you’re not looking for documents,” she said to the suddenly placid giant. “Who are you?”
The question reacquainted him with his train of thought, but it seemed to Sarah, by the hardened look in his eyes, his train was on a track of an entirely different gauge. He scowled and blinked, looked to the floor, lifted his foot and saw his dark shoe-print on the still-moist, freshly steamed carpet. He sniffed the air, and smelled death and cleaning fluids.
“Mr Teh retired here, in this office,” he said.
Sensing she had the measure of him now, Sarah said, “It’s not policy to divulge—”
“There was a cleaner.”
“Yes,” Sarah conceded, before she’d thought much of it.
“He left recently.”
“Yes. You didn’t see him on his way out?”
The man’s face hardened and his jaw chewed, as if that was the way he processed information. “The cleaner has the documents,” he concluded.
“No, I doubt—“
“The cleaner has the documents,” he repeated, and pushed Sarah aside easier than a cardboard cut-out. He marched off for reception, and took a lift for the building’s secure hover-pad floor, where his stealth copt was waiting. As soon as he was safe in the starboard couch, it’s military-grade over-engineered fans revved up and carried the dark man away.
</Text>
        </Document>
