        <Document ID="7CFD2E02-CC2A-48CB-B0CA-7C2296E67567">
            <Title>Attending the morgue</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="F85C9711-6097-45D7-833D-2DFA59414B3C">
            <Title>Elvi Rising</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="AC9C8D40-BF11-4DC4-B1C4-B3CA5BB50E89">
            <Title>Blessed Mother meets Parliament</Title>
            <Synopsis>This bit is to introduce Elvi Parliament, the Blessed Mother, The Great Teacher and for Sarah to confront Silo and Chilo.  (Ethical dilemmas left and right)
Sarah is introduced to the Elvi and finds that she is worshipped by all the Elvi and to her surprise Cracker is also revered as "The Great Teacher."
The parliament is in a former Elvi brothel formerly named "Vulva Las Vegas" (How's that for an Elvis reference?)
The confrontation of Silo and Chilo is another opportunity to discuss the Ethics of the Elvi - they will not execute torture or incarcerate them - Silo and Chilo are banished to the Travelator.</Synopsis>
            <Text>Blessed Mother meets Parliament
2 to 3 weeks after bombing
The rail car rolled slowly towards the nominated arrival/departure point where a crowd of Elvi, all clad in black, had gathered and were crying loudly and smacking themselves repeatedly in the forehead. Karate jumped down and grabbed the nearest black-clad Elvis, “What has happened, my sister?”
“Oh, the Dawkins save us! One of the great Sages, Kleoboulos, is dead! Killed by the Jesus.” She wept and recommenced smacking her forehead and pulling at her hair. “He gave his life to save our brothers.” 
“How could this be? He was healthy just a few days ago.” Karate descended into denial, but upon seeing his martial arts brothers crying and receiving comfort from the mourning throngs, he too succumbed to weeping. “The Dawkins save us.”
Sarah grabbed Karate and looked him in the eye. “Elvis, your duty is to protect the keep of the great Sages.” He did not respond. “Has anyone relieved you? No? Then get back to your duty, Elvis. Kleo would not want his home to be lost, um, to non-believers.” She resisted the urge to roll her eyes.
“Of course, you are correct, Blessed Mother. I will return to my duty immediately.” Karate Elvis gathered together his security team and returned to the Travelator and stood to attention at the open door as the carriage faded into the distance. 
“That was impressive, Sarah. He was about to completely lose it.” Cracker looked across the gathering of mourning Elvi and was amazed by the outpouring of grief for someone none of them knew.
“I wanted to separate them from the rest of this mob. Can you imagine what would happen if he had told them what I was considering?” She thought throwing octane onto this fire would lead to an unfortunate outcome.
“This crowd would turned ugly, very quickly. Let’s find the other Sages.” He grabbed her by the hand and waded into the crowd. “Much better than the last crush we were in, eh?”
“Its just as claustrophobic.” She was not keen on crowds anymore.
“Oh, yeah?” Cracker smirked. “Make way for the Blessed Mother. Blessed Mother coming through.” The entire crowd stopped sobbing, split in half and dropped to their knees, leaving a wide pathway for the pair to freely stride their way through. “Not bad, huh?”
 
“Nicely done, but where do we go now?” 
“The Blessed Mother wishes to see the great sages. Where are they, my Elvi?” All right index fingers pointed towards an imposing freestanding building. Formerly an Elvine brothel, the Vulva Las Vegas was now the seat of power in the Elvi Nation and recently renamed “Parliament House.”
“The old brothel is parliament house?” Cracker said, breaking into a smile. “The makes sense, there’ll still be a pack of cunts working there.” He almost let out an uproarious laugh, but caught a glimpse of the mourning Elvis a decency managed to take hold. ‘Thanks guys. You can get back to your mourning now, but put your backs into it! I want to see some real emotion, c’mon.” He turned to Sarah and shrugged. “It’s what the Great Teacher wants.”
“Cracker, you might try a little restraint in the exercise of power.”
“Call me the Great Teacher, Blessed Mother.” Cracker liked the idea of being the subject of a little hero worship, even if his fans were completely delusional. 
“You are kidding, of course.” The Blessed Mother was not amused.
“Of course. Do you think I can take any of this seriously? I mean look at them.” Cracker pointed his finger around, randomly pointing at individual Elvi, all balling their eyes out. “How dumb are these pricks? They didn’t even know Kleo.”
“Yes, but you know what he and his colleagues did to me, they do not. I cannot mourn his death, but I understand their grief. They are children who have just lost a father and they are hurt. We must tread carefully here or see their grief flash over into anger without warning. I would strongly suggest you show a little respect even if you don’t have any for them.”
“Of course you are right, but I think it is a little unfair to say I have no respect for the Elvi.” Cracker always had difficultly taking anything seriously, in fact, the more serious a situation, the more jokes and ridiculous comments popped into his mind. He was unable to behave himself at a funeral or a wedding, blurting out anything that manifested in his head. “I love the Elvi. They are the only thing that makes this shit-filled city tolerable. I cannot imagine a world without Elvis.” Imagine that, not one Elvis in the entire world!
As they approached two Roman Elvis guards saluted and opened the massive wooden double doors of the main entrance of Parliament, revealing a cavernous atrium that echoed their footsteps and gasps of amazement. The formidable stone structure had housed the largest brothel and burlesque house in the PTD, sporting 50 rooms and 50 bathrooms, augmented with saunas, steam rooms, bars and restaurants, plus a 300 seat theatre. The advent of Elvi governance, saw all Elvine prostitutes freed from their servitude. Although some of the girls did not like sudden unemployment, the Elvi nation would not tolerate any of their number, male or female, being sexually exploited by the sick fucks the Primary Trade Dominion seemed to produce. The freehold title of the site was purchased by the Elvi taking a huge chunk from the treasury, but they were determined to end the exploitation no matter what the cost. 
The Parliamentary Sergeant-at-arms stood sentry upon the freshly polished marble floor outside the doors to the Chamber, an ornate silver coated gold mace held tightly in white gloves, across his chest. The bulbous bejewelled head of the metre long ceremonial weapon rested gently against the elaborate gold braided lapels of his gaudy red jacket, etchings of the Dawkins Holy Cypher and emblem of the Elvi dutifully pointed outward. As the VIP’s approached, he silently bowed low and ushered them into an ante-room to the side of the parliamentary chamber. 
“The High Elvis will be informed of your visit, Blessed Mother and Great Teacher, immediately. Please make yourselves comfortable. I apologise for the wait, great ones.” He bowed and walked backwards three steps before he turned, striding confidently out the door. The High Elvis was in parliament with his cabinet and the two remaining sages, debating revenue policy. The Sergeant-at-arms entered the chamber and stood respectfully silent as the Minister for Revenue completed his maiden speech.
“…..and to fully fund the public works I propose to levy a modest tax on each Elvis. We are one hundred thousand strong and a 10% levy on income will collect 10 million per month. We will be financially self sufficient.”
“What about taxation from the Primary Trade Dominion? They will want a piece of the action.” The Finance Minister was not wrong.
“We will invest the revenue collected along with our royalties on music sales in the Primary Trade Dominion, as a single player with some considerable clout. We will be able to take advantage of the tax concessions offered only to corporate players. The Elvi nation will make a profit on the investments and pay little or no tax to the PTD. If we invest wisely we can fund all our projects with the dividends alone.”
“Make sure we spread our exposure.” The Treasurer wanted the High Elvis to know he was keeping up.
“Yes, I have already engaged the geniuses at ‘Spread Doctor’. They have been around since before the formation of the Dominion. Not many companies can say that.”
“Is it compulsory?” The Minister for the Arts inquired. The arts portfolio insisted that he stick his mitt our for money as often as possible without ever making a contribution to the economy. “The Tax?”
“None of our motions have the force of law. It is purely voluntary, but I am confident my Elvi brothers and sisters will see the sense in raising revenue for public works that will make a difference to the amenity of our little enclave.”
“Thank you, Minister.” The Speaker pointed to the doorway. “The Chair recognises the Sgt at Arms.”
“Mr Speaker, High Elvis. Please forgive my intrusion, but the Blessed Mother and the Great Teacher are in the parliamentary ante-room.” The sergeant-at-arms was beside himself with excitement. I met the Blessed Mother and the Great Teacher! 
“The Dawkins be praised! This is truly a blessing on us all. The Blessed Mother, no less.” The High Elvis was just as excited as the rest. All except for Silo and Chilo who sat quietly and exchanged looks of alarm. “Perhaps they will be kind enough to address parliament. Can you bring them to us?” The Sergeant-at-arms disappeared. “Quick, Quick! We have to set out the chairs so as to reflect their holy status. Who outranks who?”
“Well, they both outrank you!” Homeland Security Elvis blurted out. The others looked around at each other, wondering who would break first, but it was the High Elvis that let go of a belly laugh first and they all joined in. Being an Elvis was heaps of fun. 
“Obviously, but between them?” The High Elvis finally responded.
“She’s carrying the one-after-the-one, but he’s the prophet of the one. I really don’t know.” The Attorney General injected. Lawyers only ever muddy the waters, so it was not expected that the leading law officer of the Elvi would make a decision or, in fact, be of any assistance. 
“She’s pregnant, we have to give her the best chair.” The High Elvis said, pointing to the only chair with a cushion, the Speaker’s chair. “Decision made. Now let’s get this room organised. She will sit in the good chair opposite you guys and I will sit to her left and slightly behind and the Great Teacher will be seated on her right.” After a great deal of scraping and scratching of the floor, the chamber was set in a configuration that satisfied the structure and order demanded by the High Elvis.
            
The Sergeant-at-arms had fetched the honoured guests and found himself standing in the presence of greatness, legendary figures on either flank and he was about to introduce them to parliament. His rise to such stratospheric heights was dizzying. He lifted his official hand that held the official club and officially bashed on the doors with the official three bangs, before entering and proudly announcing in a loud, clear Elvi voice, “Ladies and gentlemen, the Blessed Mother and the Great Teacher. Thank you, very much.”
The cabinet, the High Elvis and the entirety of parliament in the fledgling Elvi Nation, all stood and clapped loudly, maintaining the ovation until both had been guided to their designated chairs and were comfortably seated. The smiles of the beaming faces and red raw hands testament to the reverence with which both visitors were held.
“The last time I was in here it was a strip club.” The Great Teacher whispered into Sarah’s ear. “Ah yes, Elvine the flexible.”
“We thank you, High Elvis and your cabinet for your warm welcome.” The Blessed Mother looked about the chamber, every face serenely smiling upon her. ”The Great Teacher and I are honoured and humbled by the warm ovation from the chamber.” 
“Thank you, thank you both for taking the time to visit us. Blessed Mother you carry the future of our civilisation, not just the Elvi Nation, but for the entire Primary Trade Dominion. You are most revered and most loved, by every Elvis.” Sarah nodded in approval and the cabinet again clapped loudly. “Great Teacher, without your wise counsel we would never have coalesced into a nation. If it were not for your wise teachings, we would still be mere buskers, cabaret acts and bar tenders.”
“We still are!” Said a jovial Minister for External Affairs. All the cabinet laughed. “No, don’t laugh. I’ve got a gig tomorrow.”
“We will never be able to repay the debt to you.” The High Elvis managed not to chuckle, a feat of great discipline.
“I don’t accept cheques, High Elvis, cash will be fine.” The room burst into laughter and Cracker was pleased. He could do with a bit of cash, though.
“Your sense of humour matches your greatness.” The High Elvis laughed.
“What about my good looks?” This time no-one laughed, not even a titter. It seemed if you were not an Elvis, you were just plain ugly.
“Thank you, Great Teacher.” Sarah cut him off, his banter serving no purpose. “We have made the pilgrimage to the Quarter to discuss matters grave and we wish a private audience with The High Elvis and the two remaining sages, Silo and Chilo. For the rest of parliament, please do not think we are ignoring you, I wish to meet with each of you in due course.”
“You heard the Blessed Mother, please leave.” The High Elvi ordered. They left in an orderly fashion and without question or complaint. Sarah was amazed. If she had tried to clear a boardroom in such a manner, she would have been thrown out the window. These Elvi are obedient and polite. 
“High Elvis we have a major issue that must be discussed. You have been told I am carrying the re-incarnation of the great Richard Dawkins”
“Praise be to him.” The High Elvis and the Sages automatically responded.
“For the purposes of this conversation could you please try to keep the praising to a minimum? It is going to take forever otherwise.” 
“Of course, Blessed Mother.”
“Please call me Sarah.”
“I cannot do that, Blessed Mother. You and the one-after-the-one are most important and most revered.”
“So I keep hearing. Getting back to what I was saying. You have been told I am carrying the re-incarnation of The Dawkins. Don’t say it.” She stared daggers around the chamber and no one dared to praise the Dawkins on this occasion. “I have confirmed that the DNA is largely that of Richard Dawkins, but do you know how he came to be gestating within me?”
“We all assumed immaculate conception, Blessed Mother.” The High Elvis quickly replied. “But it is certainly gratifying to hear that we can scientifically prove he is The Dawkins. The Elvi will rejoice.”
“There may be no reason to rejoice. Silo? Chilo? Can you shed any light on this so-called immaculate conception?” Sarah’s sarcasm dripped on the floor. 

“Our leader, the most revered Kleoboulos, was profoundly disturbed with the manner the Primary Trade Dominion had evolved.” Silo began his confession. “He had a dream of a real democracy and an egalitarian society. He was once in the employ of a icon of our recent history, Jamison Hardy and they worked hard to improve the standard of living for all of us. After Jamison Hardy died, the Primary Trade Dominion, with no great leaders or visionaries, fell into disrepair. Greed became god and the gap between the wealthy and everyone else broadened to the point that we now have millions of working poor. The management class punishes anyone for lack of wealth. A boot stomping on a human face forever? That is nothing compared the punishment meted out to our poor, to us. Kleo decided we needed a new great leader and placed his faith in evolution, but evolution is slow, glacially slow and it was necessary to intervene. After many years he managed to procure an archived tissue sample of The Dawkins, praise be to him, and it was placed in a cloning unit for processing.” 
“Where on earth would he get a tissue sample of a long dead Richard Dawkins?” She wondered who would keep such an inventory?
“Linus Grawling.”
“Okay, yep. That makes sense.” The Holy Mother saw everyone was nodding in agreement. Linus Grawling really got up to some sneaky shit and it was not too hard to believe he had been the author of this little disaster.
Silo spoke very slowing, carefully and deliberately. He did not want to say anything that would upset Sarah any further, but he knew this was practically unavoidable. “When the Holy Mother and the Great Teacher presented themselves before us, some time ago, Kleo saw it as a sign from the universe. I could try to blame it all on Kleo, but we were all in agreement, the timing was perfect and we could not ignore what the universe had planted in front of us. I was the one who used the device upon you and I should be the one to suffer any punishment, even if that means death.” Silo was sincere and Chilo placed his arm around his fellow apprentice in solidarity.
“Thank you for your candor, I expected you to twist into a pretzel trying to justify yourself. Apart from the abject criminality and shocking immorality of your actions, we have another problem.” Sarah stood up an began to walk around the room and behind Silo and Chilo. “On the same night you pair assaulted me, I was exposed to a biological agent, artificial DNA, it did not harm me, but it has attached itself to the foetal Dawkins on a genetic level. I have had the genome of this child mapped and there is clearly extra pieces of genetic information added to the helix. In short, no-one can say, with any degree of accuracy, how this child will develop. Technically, it isn’t even human and may not resemble anything like a human being. I have been made very aware the importance this child has for the Elvi nation, but what do I do? Produce for them a mutant? Do I terminate the pregnancy and walk away? Do I roll the dice and have the child? Do I even want a child? I wasn’t asked. I had a child literally forced into me. Do I want to be a single mother? What are my career prospects as a single mother? What happens if it is a monster? The questions are unending.”
 
“I cannot tell you what to do. Kleo would have, no doubt, been most insistent on the child being brought to full term and given the opportunity of life.” Silo did not want to be the cause of any further damage.
“That is just avoidance, Silo. I have been mulling this over and I am torn in every direction at once. I want to hear what you have to say before I decide what to do with the child and what to do with you.” 
“The possibility that the child will be born deformed is concerning, very concerning. I think this all is also an opportunity, presented by pure luck, of course, but an opportunity nevertheless. On that night three fateful and unusual events occurred. You stumbled into our car on the travelator. The odds are 7200 to 1 that you would find us. You could have just as easily have been killed in another car. Secondly you arrived on a very auspicious night for us and you fitted the profile perfectly. We had been circling the city, searching for someone like you for years. Thirdly the bio-agent seems to be interested in the Dawkins clone, but not you. This seems very peculiar. All that in one night? The odds must be astronomical! Perhaps it was meant to happen. I don’t believe in signs, but I think the universe has served up a particularly unusual set of circumstances and a difficult choice.” Silo sighed lightly and ran his fingers slowly through his unwashed hair, damp with sweat. “The child is a potent symbol that has brought the Elvi together as one - and he is yet to be born. Can you imagine what he will achieve after he is born? I fear depriving the Elvi of their leader will cause great upheaval across the entire Elvi Nation. The High Elvis can tell us how the Elvi might respond.”
“Blessed Mother, every clone, every Elvis is sterile and can never know the delight of having children of their own. The thought of aborting any child requires at least some moral justification, but the thought of The Dawkins being ripped from us is intolerable. The reaction would be as violent as it would be indiscriminate. I am not so ignorant as to be unaware of the pro-choice stance of the rest of the PTD, but I am not among that number and neither are my Elvi brothers or sisters. I think I can say, on behalf of every Elvis, that we look forward to the arrival of The Dawkins and we don’t care if he has three heads and tentacles.” The High Elvis was again amazed at the conundrums that presented themselves to him. Did these issues exist before we formed a parliament? 
“I will not be told what to do with my body by a group of men.” Sarah said, raising her voice in pitch and volume. Although I did ask.
“With the greatest respect Blessed Mother, this is why I was reluctant to to offer any advice. We all may have differing views on abortion, generally, but I would suggest, again most reluctantly, that issues relating to terminations should be determined on a case by case basis. I have no doubt a termination has a profound effect on all concerned, but this would deeply scar in excess of 100,000 people, not to mention the blood that will be shed. But no matter what we or anyone else says, you have to make the final determination.” Silo was trying walk tightrope. He knew he should have kept his mouth shut.
“Oh, you acknowledge my dominion over my own body now, but where was that when we met? I would not be in this position if it was not for you and your silly ideology. Yes, I said silly! You must think you possess a special brand of righteous need to justify a forced pregnancy. It was no better that raping a girl in her sleep.” Sarah was now livid and ready to let rip. No matter what angle she looked at it, she was the victim of rape and that was justification enough to terminate. However, the reaction from the Elvi could not be ignored. 100,000 insane musicians on a rampage did not make for pleasant dreams, then again neither did giving birth to The Fly. “Precisely what were you trying to achieve endlessly circling the city? What you failed to do over a period of years, Cracker here managed in a few hours….while drunk!”
“I do not offer up a defence for my actions that night. Kleo was a most powerful personality, but I will not evade culpability. The events since have unfolded the way Kleo predicted, with the exception, of course, of the bio-agent. We could not have predicted that intervening event.” Silo remained calm. He had resigned himself to a terrible fate.
“I do not have to make my decision right at this moment, but whatever decision I make, you are all forbidden to speak of this ever again. As for Silo and Chilo, what should be done? I could go just complain to the PTD police. In fact, I have been contracting for them and could arrange a very unpleasant and lengthy incarceration, but being a rape victim I would be exposed to ridicule and my career prospects would be ruined. I would be expected to terminate the pregnancy in any case. No level 5 could bring a child of rape into the world - he would be treated as a leper. So, I ask, what do we do with you two?” Sarah had nothing and hoped someone in the room could offer some assistance.
“You have got to at least shoot them. I did nothing and you shot me! I got hit three times, check it out.” Cracker lifted his shirt to show off his war-wounds, but the skin had almost entirely repaired with only the slightest sign of 3 pale pink circles where the blasts had entered. 
“What do you think High Elvis?” Punishing the Sages was not something Sarah was prepared to do unilaterally. 
“Thank you, Blessed Mother. Crime and punishment is an issue that the cabinet and I have found quite vexing. Five of our number were infected by the Jesus recently and how to deal with them was much discussed. We did not form any solid crime policy, but the cabinet was of the unanimous opinion that we do not possess the legal authority or the special morality required to punish anyone.” The High Elvis recalled with noticeable anguish. “We find capital punishment and torture completely abhorrent. It was decided that we could not incarcerate our brothers for the purposes of punishment, however, we found it acceptable to offer them treatment and detain them for their own safety. We sent for the great sages and they cured all five, but Kleoboulos died during the final stages of the treatment. He gave his life for the Elvi and the sages will always be honoured among our number. I cannot bring myself to incarcerate Kleoboulos’ apprentices, they could just as easily been killed along with him. They were prepared to give their lives as well.”
“We can’t execute them, torture them or incarcerate them?” Sarah sighed with exasperation.
“Are we allowed to tickle them?” Cracker laughed alone. I started well, but since then, every joke a swing and a miss.
“If I may, Blessed Mother.” The High Elvis was having a light bulb moment. “I have commenced a regime of public works in the Quarter, its mostly busy work, but important to improve the amenity and aroma of our home. The travelator is a resource that could be useful if it managed properly. With your permission I would like to set Silo and Chilo a task difficult and, I think, important.” 
“I am listening High Elvis.” Sarah was glad to hear any suggestion.
“The travelator is overrun with criminals of all kinds and I proposed that Silo and Chilo lead my Elvi in taking it back. They will no doubt encounter violent resistance and I expect some of my Elvi brothers will die. I want the Elvi to take control of the Travelator, I want at least one Elvi in each car and I want to make public transport safe. Blessed Mother, what say you?” High Elvis hope the Blessed Mother would be in agreement.
“I cannot think of anything better at this point.” Sarah appeared relieved. “Silo, Chilo was do you think?”
“The Dawkins be praised! We would be honoured to take on such an important task.” Silo said. “Safe transport within the Primary Trade Dominion? That is something to strive for and something to be proud of.”
“I will put all my efforts into this assignment, High Elvis. There are about 7200 cars and we will need considerable resources, but I am happy to assist.” Chilo chimed in. “The truth be the Dawkins and the Dawkins be the truth.”
“I know that this cannot excuse us for what we have done to you Blessed Mother, but I hope this may, in some small way, place us on the road to atonement.” Silo was again sincere, wearing a long, pale mask of regret.
“It will be a long time, Silo.” Sarah was not yet anywhere near forgiving them and thought she never would. “Well Great Teacher, I think we have achieved all we are going to achieve here today. Shall we take out leave?” 
“Yes, it has been another very long day.” Cracker wanted to get back to Sarah’s new apartment. He had only ever contracted for corporate gigs and had never seen the inside of a manager’s private residence before, much less live in it.
“Blessed Mother, the funeral for Kleoboulos will be held today. I would like you to participate in the ceremony and afterward we will be having the biggest wake that has ever been held in the quarter.” The High Elvis was very keen to be seen in public rubbing shoulders with the Blessed Mother, The Great Teacher and the Sages. “It would be my greatest honour to have you all as my guests at my table for the wake.”
“Thank you for your kind invitation, but…..” Sarah said before being suddenly cut off.
“Yes, thank you. We would be delighted.” The lure of free piss was too much for Cracker to ignore and he did not want to be a part of insulting the High Elvis. Sarah’s apartment could wait.
“We are supposed to meet up with Hayley later this evening.” Sarah whispered.
“High Elvis, may we invite the Holy Aunts to the wake?” Cracker smirked. “They are both sworn protectors of the one-after-the-one and rest high in our esteem.” He desperately wanted to rolls his eyes.
“The Holy Aunties? Of Course, I would be honoured to meet them both.” The High Elvis replied. More V.I.P’s meant greater exposure for him and he was very pleased.
“I have said it before and I’ll say it again, Sarah.” Cracker whispered into her ear. “How dumb are these pricks?”
“Keep it down, Cracker. I will contact Hayley and I guess the other sworn protector is our self-medicating pathologist? You can try to get hold of her, if she is in any condition to answer a call. Holy Aunties, really?”
“It’s what came to mind.”</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="79D697C7-41CC-4D44-8925-5EB602CF899D">
            <Title>Pre-Emptive Reciprocity</Title>
            <Synopsis>Introducing the incompetence of Agent Jones and intro to the workings of the "Directorate of Pro-active euthenasia and Pre-emptive Reciprocity".  I like the Directorate name.
Also gives an idea of how important the discs are.  He is told to find the missing blank and the missing disc.  This could easily be incorporated elsewhere or perhaps dumped.  
</Synopsis>
            <Text>Pre-emptive Reciprocity
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="F91F9BE8-DFA8-466D-AD46-BC8A16A2B448">
            <Title>Sages of the Travelator-duplicate?</Title>
            <Synopsis>Looks like I have copied the chapter twice.</Synopsis>
            <Text> 
 
Sages of the Travelator
Day After Boming - Afternoon to evening - Year Zero
A lone Sarah arrived a few minutes early at the Memphis Mafia Bar and was met at the reception desk by an Elvi-girl named Elvine. As one might expect, she was the spitting image of the waitress from the night before, but knowing she was one of many, many clones in the quarter did not diminish the wonder of precise human replicas at large. Their faculties were exactly the same, displaying the self-same mannerisms, affectations and gait.
“Good afternoon. My name is Elvine and I will be your hostess here at the Memphis Mafia Bar and Grill today. Will you be dining or would you like to take a place at the bar?”
“Thank you, Elvine. Do you think I would be able to get a private room?” She asked, most politely.
“Of course, but it will cost you.” Elvine replied, smiling widely. “Girls will cost you more again, but you know that don’t you honey?”
“Just the room will be fine, thank you Elvine.” Sarah was very glad she decided to leave Cracker at home. Apart from his lumpy, scorched head drawing attention, she just avoided the hassle of shutting him down when he took up the offer of prostitutes. “I am expecting a friend, shortly.”
“Just follow me.” Even in the early afternoon, the Memphis Mafia bar was full of drunks and the drug-fucked scum from the quarter and as they snaked their way to the rear, it was clear that most patrons were far more interested in drinking than eating. Not one table had any evidence of a meal having been consumed, not a packet of crisps nor a stray salt crystal. The “private room” was nothing more than a grandiose booth, separated from the rest of the bar with sleazy burgundy velvet drapes. “Here you are, the finest in the house.” Elvine proudly announced as she drew back the ugly curtains with gaudy faux-gold ropes to reveal large U-shaped booth with red leather upholstery and an ornately carved wooden table that managed to gleam, notwithstanding the dim light. 
 
“Wow is that leather? I mean authentic leather?” Sarah faux-gushed, pretending to be genuinely impressed.
“Sure is sweetie. It will even squeak when you sit.” Replied a smiling Elvine.
She wasted no time in being seated, gleefully fidgeting to maximise the squeaks while Elvine rolled her eyes.
“Can I help with any drinks?”
“Thank you Elvine, but no. Not at this stage. I think I will wait for my friend.” Sarah said, politely as possible.
Elvine headed for the bar and Sarah immediately pulled out her palm device, made a quick entry and then began to point it randomly around the room. She then made a beeline to the far corner, patting down the drapes and locating an ancient microphone and transmitter. She then spun and headed to the opposite corner where she was able to locate a small, but equally elderly camera. When she was satisfied the room was clean she squeaked back into the booth. 
Sarah fidgeted, trying to garner some comfort from the cheap, red sinewy cardboard assailing her backside. John Finagle, known to Sarah as Johnny, entered the booth with a look of horror and disgust. Never having been on street level he could not get over the stench or the countenances of the butt-ugly inhabitants of this alternate universe. He was, naturally, totally over-dressed for such a sordid venue – the black leather shoes (quality leather) gleamed; artfully understated dark blue trousers sported knife edge creases and his choice of shirt, tie and jacket complimented and contrasted. 
“Did you get a look at some of the heads out there? It looks like a leper’s grog den. Haven’t they heard of cosmetic surgery? And what is that smell? My God, I am going to have to burn these clothes when I get home.” Johnny was not happy unless he had something to complain about and he was in full whinge-mode. “What is this stuff stuck to my shoes?”
“That would be mud.”
“This is some crappy dive, Sarah. What is so important that you have to drag me here?” Johnny inquired as he took up a seat in the booth, deliberately sitting close, too close to Sarah. She calmly slid away. 
“Have a look at this.” Sarah slid a small copper mesh container to Johnny under a cheap napkin.
 
“A Faraday-mini! What’s in here then?” Johnny opened the small cage and his eyes immediately widened and as he examined it, a look of pure amazement took over his face. “Holy tax audit! Where did you get this?” 
“I found it.”
“That does not sound likely, my friend. You don’t just find one of these.” Johnny said, punctuating each word with incredulity. 
“It was left in my office after the death of my predecessor. What do you think it is, Johnny?” asked Sarah, transparently deflecting his question.
“I have never seen one, only heard of them. It’s a Gyges Disc. They provide a cloak of invisibility to all electronic surveillance. Cameras, microphones don’t see or hear you. It’s a hall-pass for every building in the Primary Trade Dominion – you could really get yourself in trouble with one of these, Sarah.”
“Yes, discretion is everything.” She threatened. 
“If that thing is working, there was no need to drag me to this disgusting excuse for a bar, all surveillance in a fifty metre radius will be out of action.” 
“What about the cage?”
 
“A Faraday cage will do no good, I’ve heard they pick up on gravity waves. The only way I can think to render it inert is to spin it, very fast. The acceleration at the out edge should overcome the influence of gravity waves.” He projected confidently.
“Do they cancel each other out?” Sarah inquired. 
“Something like that.” He really had no idea how the disc operated, but hubris insisted he puff out his chest and display colourful plumage. “My pension fund runneth over! Look, I think it has bio-components and if I am not mistaken this is a bio-quantum processor – I have to get this back to my lab.” 
 
Sarah did not fancy the idea of either Johnny or Cracker with the keys to the kingdom in hand. No decent girl would be safe in her bed! As an adolescent she had been schooled in the world of men, exposed to the awful truth when successfully deflecting her very first date-rape. Since that sobering revelation, she had witnessed precious little to overturn the view that “men are just selfish oafs groping, slobbering, farting and ejaculating at will” leaving a swathe of destruction in their paths. She and the rest of female kind were eternally dumbfounded that males are ignorant of their own malevolence or, worse still, men are aware of it and it simply does not bother them. Johnny, she had to admit, had never given her reason to distrust, but she figured, it was just a matter of time before his true colours emerged.
“No. I don’t think this should be in the hands of a man, any man.” She said with a hint of sarcasm. 
“Overt sexism from you? I never would have thought, Sarah. Have I ever behaved in any manner other than a gentleman?” Johnny waited for Sarah to answer, but found himself answering for her, “No, No I haven’t.”
“It’s not sexism if a woman says it.” She quoted the widely acknowledged truth. Doesn’t he know that? “What are you going to do with it? What tests?” 
“I don’t know exactly, but the first thing is to get this device spinning. I don’t suppose you have a centrifuge on you?” Johnny let out a small, self-conscious laugh, hoping Sarah might too. 
“No, no centrifuge.” She forced a vague smile, but there was no laugh. Cracker is funnier.
“No problems.” He poked his head out of the kitsch curtain configuration and managed to get the attention of Elvine. 
“Be polite.” Sarah warned.
“To an L.1 waitress? You are kidding of course.” He said with the confidence of the uninitiated. 
“Yes, darlin’ how can I help you?” Elvine asked courteously.
“Give me a small cordless stick blender or food processor. Now.” Johnny demanded.
“This is a bar and restaurant, sir, not an appliance store.” Elvine retorted.
“There will be one behind the bar. Go get it.” He ordered, not even making eye-contact.
“Yes, sir.” She spun around and headed in the direction of the bar.
“She took that pretty well. I was told they are not fond of the impolite.” Sarah was most surprised. The way Cracker had explained it to her, you would find yourself in a very dangerous place if you are discourteous to the Elvi.
“She knows how to take orders from her superiors.” He smiled back. There was no way he would suck-up to a fucking beverage delivery system, his dignitas demanded otherwise.
Elvine returned in double quick time, carrying a old glass blender. The glass ‘jug’ was in her right hand and the small base in her left. 
“I will just need the base. You know, the motor.” Johnny said, sticking his hand out, again no eye contact.
“You will get both.” Elvine brought down the jug onto Johnny’s head, the glass shattering and slicing into his arrogant baldness. Blood raced out across his skull, then in-between a mash of interwoven fingers that failed to stem the flow. He collapsed and without a sound, he curled up in a ball on the sticky red carpet.
“You will not leave until you pay for the damage and the cleaning bill.” She placed a small slip of poly-paper on the table, whirled around and paced angrily to the bar, this time chatting sternly to a security guard who immediately headed in their direction. Sarah snatched the disc, depositing it inside her brassiere, the metal warming her supple skin. She then considered Johnny who was now motionless, barely conscious and haemorrhaging badly. She would defend him from further damage, his poor behaviour notwithstanding. 
She would be ready when the bouncer arrived and he would be in for the shock of his life, however, he seemed to take an age to arrive. She poked her head out again and saw that a tall, broad shouldered young man, face concealed by a black hooded jacket, was in an animated discussion with the absentee bouncer. She was unable to glean any of the conversation but arms flapped about, fingers poked and wagged, middle digits were unleashed, but shortly they appeared to arrive at some accommodation. The two parted ways, the black hood headed towards the private booths and presented himself before Sarah, revealing a burnt and swollen face.
“Cracker!” She laughed, noting that, in identifying himself, he suddenly seemed shorter and generally less impressive. “What are you doing here?”
“Saving your behind, again.” That’s twice. She’s gotta at least give me a hand-crank now.
“Thank the laws of economics you are here. What did you tell him?”
“That you would pay double the bill.” He low-balled the figure to see how she reacted.
“Double? That seems excessive.” She didn’t really care, just as long as they got out of there without any further trouble. She crouched next to Johnny and firmly placed a cushion over the gash in his crown and lashed it down using his stylish tie as a chin strap. Double the bill?
“Well they did not see it that way at all. So we ended up settling on three times the bill.” He decided to come clean.
“Remind me never to let you negotiate for me again.” She was almost smiling, but lugging Johnny-boy out was proving to be difficult, but Cracker pitched in and they slowly made the journey to the exit, settling the bill on the way. “Could we not have just paid 100%?” Sarah seemed a little puzzled.
“Yes, but that comes with a complimentary beating.” Cracker informed her. “They near kill you.”
“Not me” Sarah said with some degree of confidence. “I might have been able to give them a surprise.”
“Yes, but that would have drawn some unwanted attention as well.” 
“Maybe a little.” She giggled with pride. She could not imagine living in fear, enduring a pathetic, cowering, shadow of an existence, like the many wasted lives feminine kind had suffered through the ages. She had many times pondered what the crushing oppression would have done to her. What sort of a person would she have become? She figured women of her ilk were especially hated and hunted down as witches. Yes, I would have been burned alive.
“I think you would have attracted way too much attention if you cleaned up that bouncer. He was fucking huge.” He was not kidding.
 
“You are right of course, but at least we have the disc. We should not appear on any security logs anywhere along the way.”
“Where to now?” Cracker asked.
“It’ll have to be your apartment. We can’t take him to a Medical Centre around here and we won’t be able to get anywhere near the Management District without questions being asked. No-one else can be present when he wakes, there’s no telling what he will say. Chances are he won’t be too discreet, especially when he’s in pain.” Johnny was not one to bare discomfort in silence. He could scarcely handle a pin prick without moaning about it for weeks. His current soul destroying malady came in the form of a mild case of contact dermatitis, but you could be forgiven for thinking he was suffering from necrotic chancres on his ball bag, such were the unending complaints. 
Dragging the semi-responsive, bleeding carcass of Johnny was hard work and with no taxis willing to take a half-dead customer, they had no choice but to chance the travelator across town and through The Pale. The implementation of the travelator, a closed circle of small box cars connected front to tail on a prehistoric tram track, was greeted by many as a great step in servicing the transport needs of the lows. The 37 kilometre ring of train carriages perpetually circumnavigated the city at brisk walking pace and for a small fee anyone could jump on and off at any time as it meandered through some of the shittiest sites in the all the dominion. 
The Travelator, one of the last investments in public transport infrastructure, however, proved to be an archetypal demonstration of chaos theory in action - the best laid plans of men immediately turning to shit. Directly upon the first wheel hitting the track, homeless families, criminal gangs and opportunists of every variety moved in, taking over many of the cars and fiercely defending their territories. Carriages became wandering brothels, drug dens and mobile homes for the lucky homeless who managed to stake a claim. Subsequently, for those idiotic enough to make the attempt, the cost of a ride was dependent entirely on which car you jumped up upon. Cracker had never taken a ride but, the word among his contemporaries was to address the Travelatorisi as you would a king and they may only seek to fleece you. He knew with certainty, however, that some would pretty much kill you no matter what. At a glance there was no way of telling which car was the right one for you, but as each slowly clicked by, an obvious scent of rotting flesh and a swarm of blow-flies was a rank clue to give that one a miss. Travelatorisi stood at entrance ramps touting for business with promises of a cheap ride, your safety their primary concern, while others attracted fresh prey with gaudy colours, flashing lights and loud music. Sometimes they snatched-up hapless pedestrians that had strayed too close to the tracks, demand payment for the ride before push them off, occasionally still alive.
“We have to just pick one and hope for the best. Only the desperate and the stupid take this ride and at this point I think we qualify as both.” Cracker told Sarah as they approached the track. “You get on first and then pull him up, as I push.”
Sarah seamlessly sashayed from the grimy cement onto the entrance ramp and began dragging Johnny up by his coat, Cracker pushing at his flabby backside with sufficient force to knock Sarah on her back and throw Johnny on top. 
“Geez, smooth operator, isn’t he?” Cracker laughed as Sarah pushed and squirmed to extricate herself from under the dead weight. Rather than being met by raised voices and gun muzzles, they were relieved the only other occupants, three men wearing hooded cloaks drawn in at the waist with cord, were seated quietly in the corner and barely seemed to notice their arrival. Cracker rightly assumed they were the resident travelatorisi, but most atypical. Silent still, the elder of the three slowly raised his eyes and momentarily reflected upon the trio before returning his gaze to his feet, hood concealing his face.
“Travelatorisi, we seek safe passage through the Pale and wish to pay tribute.” Cracker gingerly commenced. 
“In the name of science and reason, who are you to receive such munificence?” Inquired the elder calmly, serenely. 
“I am Cracker of the Hardy clan, wise one. I am escorting my friends to safety, but as you see, one is wounded, I think badly, great Travelatorisi.” He thought he might be laying it on a little thick, but it seemed to be working.
“You are most humble come forward and let me have a look at you. What has entropy brought to me? A pretty girl and two crash victims?” said the elder. “Tell me traveller, do you flee justice or do you seek it?”
“We seek justice.” Cracker replied, hoping it was the right answer.
“Then you shall have safe passage. The protection of The Dawkins shall be yours” He stood up and hugged Cracker lightly and then Sarah, tightly, kissing her on both cheeks. “I am Kleobou and these are my apprentices, Silo and Chilo.” They both nodded, but remained silent. “It seems this one is in need of assistance.” He glanced at his two apprentices and immediately they were kneeling either side of Johnny, gently removing the cushion from his head. 
“Silo and Chilo will clean his wounds and we shall talk.” Kleobou smiled widely, exposing a set of stained teeth and motioned them to a cluster of wooden chairs and a small table in the corner, one small bulb swinging above. They sat and Kleobou offered wine which they both accepted, against their better judgment. Cracker and Sarah were offered small wooden cups, but Kleo, his preferred name he explained, gulped the red liquid from a golden bowl. Cracker followed suit and when Sarah was convinced the offering was neither drugged nor poisoned, she sipped lightly and finding herself pleasantly surprised by the taste, quaffed half the cup down. 
“This is a really quite an acceptable vintage. Thank you.” Sarah smiled brightly, face glowing. “Shiraz?”
“It seems you know grapes.” Her response to the wine had confirmed what he already knew. “You are not of the third estate. How do you find yourself here, young miss?” He gently gazed into her eyes, penetrating deeply but causing her no alarm or unease. To the contrary she felt awash with a warm tranquility. 
“I could claim a set of unforeseen and unfortunate circumstances, but I have only myself to blame.” She confessed. She did not know why she implicitly trusted this fellow, but she felt a compulsion to unburden her soul and tell him whatever he wanted to know. Alarm bells in her head rang silent.
“We are here because fuck-knuckle over there can’t play nice with the plebeians.” Cracker insisted on pointing out. 
“I put Johnny in an alien situation and I should have known better. It was his first time at street level.” She insisted. “It was never going to end well.”
“Do not desire the impossible, Sarah. Your friend is a product of his environment, he could not have acted any differently.” Kleo counseled.
“But what of my upbringing? I have the same pedigree.” She knew she was divulging far too much information, but it felt good. 
“You are different, but you already know that, don’t you?” He spoke with such a degree of certainty that Sarah found herself compelled to listen. “You are unique, you will do great things if you use your intelligence, but follow your heart. Don’t be duped by your conditioning, your world is not as it seems, the structures holding it fast are made of chalk, not steel.”
“Aren’t you supposed to be staring into a crystal ball or magic fire or something?” Cracker was in no doubt that this was a load of crap. What would some idiot hiding out in a train carriage know about life? This is not a Himalayan cave.
“I see you are a skeptic, young man. Let me look at you. You claim membership of the great Hardy clan?” Kleo said, now staring into Cracker’s pupils, but young Hardy did not feel warm and fuzzy, but violated. It felt like Kleo was somewhere deep in his brain, poking about and periodically interrogating neurons. “You are of the Hardy clan! Perhaps even a descendant of Jamison Hardy himself, if I am not mistaken”
“Who?” Cracker did not know.
“The last man to try.” Kleo said cryptically, a hint of sadness seeping out. “He rejected violence and walked the path of peace and love.”
“What a pussy.” Cracker blurted out without thinking. 
“He once stood as the only man that could stop that.” Kleo pointed out the entrance of the carriage and into the dim daylight. “The Primary Trade Dominion. Jamison Hardy sought to understand his fellow man, to pick him up not stomp him into the mud. He was no pussy, young Hardy. I thought he was the one-after-the-one.”
“The one-after-the-one.” Silo and Chilo chimed in unison.
“My apologies, Kleo.” Cracker’s apology was genuine, but he still did not believe the Jamison Hardy rubbish. “I find it hards to accept you can tell anything, just by looking in my eyes.”
“Your eyes are virtually identical. Colour, pattern, right down to the red fleck below the left pupil.” Kleo quietly insisted.
“Did you know him?” Sarah was now quite intrigued.
“I had the honour of being in his employ for many years. How is the patient, Silo?”
“We have bound the wound on his head, but he is concussed. I don’t think there is any bleeding or swelling to the brain. His pupils are the same size and reactive to light. That is all we can do, Master. I expect he will recover in a few days.” Solo replied, reverently bowing his head.
“I have heard the legend of Jamison Hardy, but I thought he was an urban myth.” Sarah said, drawing from distant memory. “If I recall correctly it was a cautionary tale. Jamison Hardy, traitor to capitalism.” 
 
“His memory is much maligned. He was a true man of the people, so much so he, too, was caught up in the Great Viral Cleansing and died with the masses.” Kleo recalled with some obvious distress. “We are approaching The Pale.” Silo and Chilo set to work locking doors and shutters, double checking each other’s work. “We take on no passengers in The Pale it is far too dangerous, but we will be safe enough in here. Well my new friends we should be through the worst of it in about an hour.” 
* * *

</Text>
        </Document>
