        <Document ID="07EEFE45-A62E-4124-B5C5-7CB02EA188F5">
            <Title>Spare first chapter</Title>
            <Text>
Many argued that the mass migration of millions of refugees to the east was the genesis of the new class system, but it was not. It was merely the catalyst, heating up the social chemistry and speeding the rate of collapse of the old order. The Primary Trade Dominion, previously known as Ulyssia , had considered itself somehow removed from the concerns of the rest of the world. Geographically isolated and culturally barren, the massive land mass, although coveted by lesser powers, held no interest to the hulking economies of the north. 
The spread of uninspiring architecture was truly nauseating as urban redbrick shantytowns sprawled endlessly outwards, punctuated at regular interval with the enormous Hardy Towers. The shear volume of people inevitably overburdened the infrastructure. Entire suburbs were inundated with sewage rising, over time, to several metres deep. Mortality rates in these areas were inexcusable. Although some robust souls etched out a living wading and sifting through the shit for trinkets to sell, most died from dysentery or fled for their lives leaving a yellow, stinking trail in their collective wakes. 
With little or no investment, manufacturing industries had been withering away for decades before the realisation hit - they had created an economy that made nothing. Sure, there were some industries, but they were on such a scale as to be insignificant on a world stage. The elites of the day, to create the obscene wealth required to maintain lifestyle, made speculative investments on foreign stock exchanges. (****More on Market forces here*****) More often than not, they failed and the wise investor stayed with the traditional land ownership and building developments, getting out before the bubble they created bursts. The lack of appropriate acreage caused prices to soar. Feudalism was no longer a distant memory, but a distinct reality. The wealthy began to think of themselves as modern day Lords, owning land and anyone on it and without the burden of having to raise a peasant army to fight a monarch's war.
As the ethos of society increasingly viewed the world in terms of profits and production, employer influence increased exponentially. The enactment of the "Workplace Negotiations Flexibility Act, No 20/2025" was accompanied by all the fanfare of a tax cut. The Minister for Moral Compliance claimed the Act was a "Fair go for all" in that it mandated wage negotiations between the employer and the individual worker. Collective bargaining and union involvement was not only forbidden, it was criminalized to the point that most leftist scum and unionists found themselves standing blindfolded in front of a pocked marked wall.
Employers occupied a powerful position at the negotiating table and few individual employees were adept at bargaining. As government revealed a clear class preference for employers, employee rights declined. Instead of Industrial Law determining conditions in the workplace, employers sought relief under Commercial Law, treating workers as commodities with no inherent rights or privileges. There were millions of "lows", Westerners and Vietnesians, who would work for half the money of a local. Employers ran the show, set the rules and changed them to suit the occasion. They had the ascendancy and were not going to let go of their strangle hold on the industrial landscape. Why would they? They had been butt-fucked by unions for decades. It did not go unnoticed that, as workers rights eroded, productivity and profits soared. A happy worker might be an efficient worker, but a frightened worker is a fast worker. 
In the new Primary Trade Dominion, the overwhelming and inescapable characteristic of the so-called "new" corporate ethos was the inability for any individual, group or even government to engage the management class in any meaningful discourse. To engage the managers, one was obliged to use their language and play by the rules set by management theologians. Bizlan, the language used by the management class, the bureaucrats and technocrats, was impossible to avoid and more difficult to penetrate. By using their language, management-speak, you were playing their game and you could not win. There were degrees of losing, with some outcomes more desirable, but there could be no victory. Unionism was dead and no one spoke for the worker.
To management it made perfect sense. Managers, bureaucrats, technocrats and power brokers were all educated at one or another of the Management-Theology Colleges (MTC). All educated in the same theories with the same limited world-view. They were not trained to untangle intricacies and nuances of business; rather they were stripped away of all independent thought and were tutored only to produce answers that satisfy management-theology. There was nothing pragmatic about the methodology - if it worked but did not conform to current management theory, it had to be changed. Uneducated managers had no business in business. Executives without and MBA were an abomination - a blasphemous reminder of a system that was crying-out to be managed. All MTC students knew, as a matter of moral certainty, that the planet was replete with organisations that needed managing, whether these organisations realised it or not. To them, boardrooms were stuffed with confused, feeble-minded drop kicks that had spent their working lives impatiently anticipating the arrival of the new MTC graduate. With problems solved and profits increased, power would be theirs to wield. Everyone would live happily ever after - especially the management team. Of course, concern for the employee was never a consideration. Workers were merchandise, abused until broken and then replaced with a newer model.
The game they played was private capital, land ownership and profiteering in an allegedly free market. These things ensured they had power to exert over others - the other classes. The lows were denied all paths to personal power - they were denied the ability to own land, to own capital and, therefore, to make profit. After all, they were really foreigners anyway. Who cared about them? If you did not create wealth, you were a citizen of the lowest order, if you were a citizen at all.
Although the government catered almost exclusively to the requirements of the market, Corporate culture never returned the favour. Corporations gathered power by ignoring local governance and relying on economic pressure from head office - usually in the U.A.C.S. When the locals, in any particular country, passed bills that were opposed to company policy, vast economic pressure was brought to bear. "If you don't change your laws, you don't do business with us."  Countries that didn't play ball couldn't buy or sell anything, anywhere and quickly gained membership as a third world country. The Primary Trade Dominion played ball.
Unfettered profiteering had ensured that environmental affairs were permanently on the fringe of political thought, assuming, of course, that concerns for the environment ever entered their heads. A calamity of biblical proportions was inevitable and the planet would die, it was just a matter of when. Two centuries of multi-national excess had created a bulging, bilious world order gorging on profit and power. They were perfectly aware that nature would take revenge and every single life, human or otherwise, would suffer as global emphysema strangled the planet. The oceans were little more than giant septic tanks oozing filth and belching evil gases from within. Inland waterways were grandiose sewers and marine life only existed as commodities in aqua culture or as single celled animals that somehow managed to survive in the slime.
When they had realised it was too late, or rather, finally admitted it was unlikely the environment was going to self-correct, they announced a grand plan. They were not going to stop greenhouse gases. Don’t be stupid. They were going to increase them. They were not going to clean up the seas and waterways, they were going to spew more carcinogens, poisons and yet more shit into the water. Why? The logic was astonishingly idiotic and disturbingly simplistic - to raise the capital required for a meaningful study, they needed to make more profit. They further determined that the hotter and more productive the economy became, the faster they would find the solution and the more money they would have to implement it. The private sector firmly set their sights on making as much profit as possible before Armageddon. Who ever had the most toys would win. 
Investing heavily in research and development, weapons production expanded and diversified exponentially, creating a sales boom. An industrialist’s utopia sprang up in the Primary Trade Dominion where business ventures met with no regulation, no morality and, therefore, no impediments. Copyright laws were virtually non-existent and left a gaping void that was exploited for all it was worth. Duplication of foreign business strategies naturally extended to outright thievery of intellectual property. Outraged foreign businesses and governments petitioned through diplomatic channels and the courts, but to no avail. No one guessed just how severely this affected the adjacent economies – no one predicted their response.

* * *

Sir Issac Newton has been described as the greatest intellect that ever lived, but when he was stumped to mathematically explain all of the interactions of the planets in the solar system, he turned to god as the answer and he stopped his inquiries. It took another 100 years for Laplace to solve the riddle with perturbation theory mathematics. When god is invoked, all progress is halted until someone else comes along and picks up where the others left off. 

</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="EFDB5924-9C16-4E1D-A34A-F369CA8B3DE5">
            <Title>A hot time in the ol</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="3DF20B09-6D21-4EA1-96B0-DECCCF161D79">
            <Title>Tit Virus</Title>
            <Synopsis>Cracker wakes up healthy and his face is almost healed.  He has been infected by the disc.
Sarah is very worried about the consequences. 
She tells him the discs must have done something to him, deliberately omitting to tell him about the virus.
She works out the disc is infected.
</Synopsis>
            <Text>TIT VIRUS
2 Days after Bombing
When Cracker woke he felt good. Fantastic in fact. He didn’t cough up anything black or brown just a healthy yellowy-green. He had not hacked up anything green since he was a kid. His back was straight and erect, no pain whatsoever and neck movement was unhindered and fluid. Carved from ivory, his morning erection was busting against the inside of his shorts, desperately waiting to announce itself to the world. Ordinarily, he spent the first 20 minutes of the day coughing, gagging and dry retching, but today he felt hungry and actually looked forward to breaking his fast. Then he caught a glimpse of himself in the mirror and saw his face was, for want of a better term, normal. In truth better than normal, he had actual colour in his cheeks, his eyes were sparkling and teeth gleaming. All of the injuries from the explosion were, inexplicably, faded and almost gone. 
“What the fuck, Sarah?” Cracker smiled. His hair was still a bit patchy, but in staring he could swear it was growing before his very eyes. “Sarah!” He called out, unable to drag himself away from the mirror. It had been many years since he looked this good. She appeared almost immediately at the yellow stained bathroom door and upon seeing Cracker screamed loud and long.
“Oh, no. This is not good. Not good at all.” She, too, was looking great and seemed to have shaken whatever had ailed her the night previous. The nausea and stomach cramps all a vague memory.
“What’s the problem? I feel fantastic.” Cracker flexed his biceps. “Check out them cannons.”
 “The disc has done something to you.” Trying to impress a sense of urgency, but prudently neglecting to tell Cracker the contagion she carried had, no doubt, invaded his system and caused the overnight makeover. The infection was progressing rapidly, just as she would have expected. Inside her body the virus operated in an entirely positive way, but the long term effects on Cracker’s constitution was anyone’s guess. She had feared for decades, when she finally erred and unleashed danger upon others, the moment when she had to either come clean or manufacture some credible fiction. Shrewdly, she had ignored instinct and decided to fictionalise, blaming the disc. The truth can wait for another day. Not too soon I hope.
“If this is sick, then bring it on.” Cracker did not see a problem.
“We cannot assume that your current symptoms will last. We should assume that you might pass it on to others. No exchanging body fluids with anyone.” She did not like misleading him, or anyone else for that matter, but she had to ensure Cracker would not infect others. 
“No sex then?”
“No sex or anything else.” She rubbed her right breast, the pain was gone, but the disgust remained. Cracker’s hand automatically raised for a high-five but he was, yet again, denied.
“No sex? That should not be too much of a problem.” Cracker wasn’t exactly a lady’s man and had come to terms with his lack of appeal. “I don’t suppose there is any reason to think my system is free of the goop?”
“The drastic changes in your appearance cannot be ignored.” She left it at that. 
* * *</Text>
        </Document>
