        <Document ID="07576709-109D-4FD4-BF07-7B3A20441457">
            <Title>Confession</Title>
            <Synopsis>"All that happened in a couple of days".  This part is for Hayley to find out 
They are not the bombers, but they did re-direct the water supply
Cracker is responsible for the sudden change in the entire Elvi nation
Cracker had been helping Shakti run crime scenes
Hayley works out that the Commissioner sent Shakti in to balls up three investigations
Hayley works out that Cracker is in danger - the case against Cracker would never reach conviction.  She realised Cracker is in police custody so he can be neatly disposed of in his cell.
Hayley announces that Cracker must be in mortal danger. (whoever kills him gets a promotion)</Synopsis>
            <Text>Confession
2 to 3 weeks after bombing
“All that happened in a couple of days?” Hayley had heard some stories in her time and had stumbled across some pretty bizarre circumstances, but she did not know what to think. “So you are not the terrorists that placed the bomb, but you are the idiots that redirected the water.”
“Yes, but we fixed it and handed Shakti a suspect.” Cracker decided to do all the confessing.
“And you, Mr Hardy, are single-handedly responsible for the sudden change in the ethos of the entire Elvi nation? You turned them all from bunch of harmless musicians into rabid science freaks.” The senior detective sank deeper into disbelief.
“I guess. I was drunk and I told them a few things I had heard and they ran with it. Man, did they run with it.” Cracker didn’t know what the big deal was. The songs they had written since he last spoke to them really kicked ass. Who doesn’t like songs about science and reason? Far better subject matter than cabbages and kings. 
“You have been helping Shakti run crime scenes, from a remote location. Anything else?”
“Well, there’s the disks and the death of Mr Lee, the disappearance of Emil Kalheri. The murder of the Director of Proactive Euthanasia seems awfully suspect, at least that’s what it seemed like from my vantage point.”
“All of those matters will be re-investigated if I have any say. It seems obvious that Shakti was sent in by the Commissioner to balls up all three investigations, but to what end?” Asked in the rhetoric, Hayley did not expect an answer and would ponder over the matter for the next couple of days.
“What are you going to do with us?” Sarah had heard confession was good for the soul, but had remained completely silent to this point, desperate to avoid disclosing the complete and awful truth. Unless it was completely unavoidable, she would not allow the truth of her viral infection to get out. The anguish and guilt she felt for exposing Cracker to the virus would, no doubt, increase exponentially should anyone else be infected. She was deeply moved Cracker was taking the heat from Hayley for her. He had clearly been interrogated a number of times before and seemed to know what areas to avoid, what matters to confess and when to make up some plausible alternate truth. 
“I should just drop you both in the shit and walk away, but I think that is exactly what the Commissioner wants. He sent Shakti after Mr Hardy clutching a brief with so many holes in it, he knew it could never proceed to conviction.”
“That’s good isn’t it? I would have been acquitted anyway.”
“I don’t think you get it. You were never going to be brought to trial. The Commissioner had you placed in custody in order to have you eliminated.” Hayley announced as fact. “Once you are placed into processing, you will be completely out of my control. There is no way to protect you.”
“If I am in danger, I suggest you get me the hell out of here.” Cracker was frightened. He knew what cops were capable of and how they always weaseled their way out of trouble. They could kill him with virtually no questions asked.
“I am trying to think of a safe-house that is actually safe, but nothing is coming to mind. We could put you in custody under an assumed name, perhaps.”
“No thanks.”
“I purchased an apartment in the management district a couple of months ago, the old apartment of Mr Lee actually, and I have not had the time to even inspect it. You can stay there, but you will have to be discrete, the neighbours will spot you as L.2 and file a report if you are not careful.” Sarah had been waiting for an occupancy approval, but more recently she had found herself too preoccupied to follow it up. 
“Yes, I like it. The last place anyone will be looking for an L.2 is in the management district. We should leave as soon as possible.” Hayley was now in close protection mode, a prisoner in her charge was in danger and it fell to her and Sarah to ensure his safety. No doubt the Commissioner has promised promotions for quietly hanging the Nightclub Vampire in his cell, knowing no-one would bother mourning the suicide of a killer obviously overwhelmed by guilt. “Getting out of the building may prove to be difficult. We should consider everyone we see as a threat. If we take him in the direction of the cell complex, we should not arouse suspicion until we break for the exit. You will have to wear cuffs, Cracker.”
“Okay, but don’t lock them. I want to be able to defend myself.” Cracker hated being handcuffed, even that time when Andrea was going through her kinky phase.
As they stepped out of the interview suite, the chaos and noise of the main office instantaneously ceased. Every cop froze in place, hands resting on the gun handles, eyes slowly following them across the room and down towards the processing facility. A few followed, rubbing their chins, trying to work out how to shoot the three of them in the back and make it look like self-defence. Hayley, Sarah and Cracker upped the pace, took a left turn where they should have turned right and broke into a sprint. 
 “Looks like an open contract, Cracker.” Hayley thought only a discrete few would have been in on the kill, but from the interest they had already received, the promotion opportunity was open to all and sundry. “We had better think of another plan, quickly.”
The passageway was blocked by a giant metal security door, but it may as well have been made of paper, such was the speed at which Sarah defeated the electronics. The exit door lay ahead, but Hayley’s heart sank when she saw it required a access chip to go further.
“We are trapped.” Hayley could hear their pursuers, now a small mob, trundling down the corridor towards them. “They are getting close, sorry Cracker.”
“Not so.” Sarah put her hand in her jacket pocket and pulled out the company ‘all access chip’ she had been carrying since she first met Cracker and opened the door without fuss. “I knew this would come in handy eventually.”
“Alright, something is finally going right for us.” Cracker said with relief. The doors slid open, very wide and they found their escape was blocked by a line of 10 or so uniforms, pistols drawn, in firing squad configuration. A senior sergeant walked forward from the line. “This man is trying to escape, Superintendent, I suggest you and your girlfriend step back.”
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="B6C11008-2B3D-4C48-A32C-5A4F0415EC97">
            <Title>Jesus takes an Elvis</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="30D0BD87-C149-431A-BE2A-E5CEEA69579D">
            <Title>Prologue - ELVIS Warranty</Title>
            <Text> 
Congratulations on purchasing your new Elvis Entertainment and Hospitality Unit.

No matter whether this is your first Elvis, or you have previously leased an Elvis, you’re sure to appreciate the safety, economy and superb engineering of your new Grayling Corporation product. 
In this Warranty and Service Book you’ll find all the information you need to ensure your Elvis is maintained in the best possible condition. You will also find an explanation of everything covered by your 5 year/10,000 hour comprehensive Elvis Entertainment and Hospitality unit warranty.
Nothing helps the resale value of your Elvis like a complete service history from your authorised Elvis Service Centre. It demonstrates to any potential buyer that you’ve taken care of your Elvis, making it an invaluable asset for negotiating the best possible resale price. We suggest you ensure each Elvis keeps this Warranty and Service Booklet on his or her person so your Elvis Service Centre can easily stamp it at every service. The book must be stamped, as electronic records or service invoices are not accepted as evidence of a service.
Your responsibilities
You must operate, maintain and care for your Elvis properly, in accordance with the instructions in this book and your Elvis owner’s manual. The best way to maintain your Elvis through an authorised Elvis Service Centre. 
What is not covered by your Elvis Entertainment and hospitality unit
		Clothes, shoes and musical instruments. It is the responsibility of the owner to clothe your Elvis and provide him or her with suitable equipment to carry out their duties.
		Misuse and neglect as defined in the owners-manual.
		Providing insufficient or poor quality housing and food. 
		Feeding after midnight
		Feeding bacon fat and coffee grounds
		Working excessive or lengthy hours without sufficient rest and repose.
		Exposing your Elvis to amphetamine, crystal methamphetamine, cocaine, ‘crack’ cocaine or any other stimulant drug or drug patch.
		Exposing the Elvis to religion or religious memes/icons including religious texts, psalms, hymns, gospel music or revelation of any kind. 
		Accident damage.
		Choking on or inhaling rhinestones.
		With the exception of the free 100hr service – medical and dental costs.
  </Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="33823C7C-AD95-4E46-B9B9-15272DBAD69F">
            <Title>The Gyges Disc</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="4B471055-E778-4775-B1BA-D52C86422D19">
            <Title>A Big Crush</Title>
            <Synopsis>Due to the police operation at the bombing site, streets are closed and crowd have been re-directed, creating a "Crush".  This is not unusual in the PTD.  There is a bit of background about when Cracker and Shakti first met at another crush incident.  I don't mind this bit.  Seems okay.
</Synopsis>
            <Text>A Big Crush
90 minutes after bombing - year zero.
By the time Cracker and Sarah made it back to street level a mob had gathered and swollen to a dense unending thicket of humanity, sucking the very life out of the air. Gasping for breath Cracker and Sarah waded into the vast seething mass and, although their progress frustratingly slow, they incrementally plotted a course through many thousands of street people, vendors, whores and shit-bags towards the throbbing lights of the entertainment district. Rank fumes assaulted Sarah’s nose and eyes, making both run uncontrollably, obliging her to tear a square of fabric from her skirt for a makeshift handkerchief. Putrid airborne particles violated her pristine, unblemished skin, producing a maddening desire to scratch, which she did not do, not once. Analyzing the ebb and flow of the crowd, as a distraction from her screaming skin, she was soon able to predict the path of least resistance having realised, in this environment, the quickest way between two points was not a straight line. Shuffling sideways or even backwards, at the right moment, when the cadence of the crowd changed, provided an opportunity to take advantage of a surge or avoid a crush. As advancement accelerated, they became increasingly aware that it was becoming rather damp underfoot and the brown water seemed to be rising at an alarming rate. 
“Is it usually like this?” Sarah asked, genuinely surprised by the enormity of the crowd.
“No. The security zone around the explosion must have been expanded and they are all being funneled this way. We have got to get out of here; it’s going to get dangerous.” Cracker was on clean up at “The Big Crush” six years previous. The crowd had been so vast that thousands were crushed or suffocated where they stood and he could never forget the macabre sight of the dead, all standing, melded tightly together by rigor mortis in huge human sheaves. Wedging them apart took so much time that they were ordered to simply scoop them up with a front-end loader and dump them in a truck. A very gruesome duty, considering the odd few were still alive. He had met Shakti after ducking off to throw up only to find the new police recruit already occupying the best spot, upchucking what appeared to be an exceedingly un-chewed steak. 
“Is that real beef?” He had said, suddenly hungry.
“You can have it. I don’t think I’ll need it.” Shakti said gagging, his abdomen jerking with uncontrollable spasms. 
“Na. I’ll only spew it up as soon as I get back to work. I don’t think people are meant to see things like that.”
“Things like that should not happen.”
They hadn’t actually become friends that night, Shakti lacked the ability to connect, but they had become allies. The shared distress over the fatalities of “The Big Crush” was the foundation of their alliance, also the starting point of their respective journeys to claustrophobia. 
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="925C0C15-5790-4370-95A2-19C2B0584C0D">
            <Title>Haloran Smythe</Title>
            <Text>Haloren Smythe
		Role in Story:		Central Male Character
		Occupation:		Contract Cleaner Halo was not the sort of bloke to be bothered with small talk or much talk at all for that matter. He said as little as possible, but he was clearly a formidable intellect and what he said was generally worth hearing. His pale blue eyes sported pinpricks for pupils and the constant back and forth of his head left him resembling a psychotic radar array. His gaze cut through the crowd until he located tonight’s object of his affections.</Text>
            <Notes>9 references

First appears with: Cracker</Notes>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="B53AD136-5E5A-4BA8-B3C2-508A5ECB380D">
            <Title>Emil</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="7A6CD119-B905-43E1-A4DE-B53C740CE362">
            <Title>Dark Forces</Title>
            <Synopsis>Introduction to …

Det Supt Hayley Lanning - she is one of the few honest cops on the force.
She is not happy about being put on a missing persons gig. She is in charge of the “Nightclub Killer” investigation and did not want to drop it to find a L1 labourer.


The Commissioner of Police hangs up the phone on Shakti and makes a call to the CEO telling him Shakti fucked it up more than they had hoped. He called it suicide and went back to bed. No evidence collected.
The CEO is told the blank completed the job, made it look like suicide, but lost the gyges disc in the process. The blank had gone missing. The CEO wants the blank (Emil Kalheri) located as a priority.
The Commissioner was amoral and completely untrustworthy - making him a model public servant. He was a genius with malleable ethics. He contacts his most competent investigator,, Det Supt Hayley Lanning - she is one of the few honest cops on the force.
</Synopsis>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="D968F67E-5367-446C-8F8B-E1AC7D806AC2">
            <Title>Fire-worm</Title>
            <Synopsis>I had to get Sarah to work with Hayley so that they can later corroborate on more important matters.  nI this case the search for Emil Kalheri's body.  Sarah is supposed to be doing some tricky computer-shit to interrogate systems etc to gather info.  They discover the Commissioner is using a "Fireworm" (A computer virus/program placed in the system at the time it was built) to delete records and (probably) help disappear people.  

The important bit here is they decide to visit Cracker when Sarah finds out all of his records have been wiped.  The Commissioner is listening in and decides to sent Shakti to arrest Cracker.  He tells Shakti that Cracker is the nightclub vampire (all bullshit).  This is so the Commissioner can get Cracker no custody and have him quietly killed and claim suicide.  
</Synopsis>
            <Text>Fire Worm
14 days after bombing
Sarah arrived at work as usual about 30 minutes early to find her little signals-directorate already hard at work. They had been working on cracking scrambled computer records, communications transmissions and had made some significant progress. They had traced a million dead-ends, but had tracked down the source of the covert information adjustment, but …………
The algorithms of the surveillance programs had identified a company agent, at an unknown location, who was interrogating the link, trying every possible angle to gather information on Cracker Jack Cleaning services. As the focus of attention from company agent, Cracker had a target on his back and they were closing in fast. She tried desperately to locate where the inquiries had originated, but hadn’t had any luck before Supt Lanning interrupted, calling her in for a meeting to discuss progress in the Emil Kalheri conundrum.
“The records were deleted by a “Sleeping Fire-worm”. It’s primitive, but effective enough to fool most security systems. I’m in the process of clearing the system, but it moves about and hides very well. I don’t think it breached the system, I think it was put there as a part of the system. It has always been there.”
“What about Mr Kalheri?”
“All information about him is lost. He could be anywhere.”
“Any idea who is using the fire-worm?”
“Yes, as a matter of fact, but you may not like it.”
“Who?”
“It is used almost exclusively by the Office of the CEO, The Director of Pre-emptive Reciprocity and Proactive Euthanasia and the Office of the Commissioner of Police. The most recent user was our boss. It took 300 solid hours of processing to track down the location to the office next door.”
 “That’s is a surprise.” She was, in fact, not surprised at all. Every senior official in the Primary Trade Dominion was corrupt and she had no reason to suspect her boss was different. “Anything else?”
 
“The Fire-worm was has been used infrequently over the past several months. Personal information is deleted and then the subject disappears with no trace. It was used recently on a no-name cleaner from Sector 3. All records regarding an Anthony Hardy L.2.were deleted from the Commissioner’s Desk.” 
“Do you think he is connected to our inquiry?”
“Perhaps. If he disappears or is killed we will know for sure.” Sarah did not want to give the game away and hoped Hayley would make the suggestion.
“Well, we can’t let that happen. I think we should bring him into protective custody and see why the Commissioner finds him so interesting.”
“We?”
“Yes. You are hereby deputised.” Hayley raised a holo-document from the emitter.
“I don’t want to be a cop, I’m a privateer. I am happy to lend you my expertise in the office, but I am not an operative.” It would be difficult for Sarah to wash away the stigma of public service.
“Relax, you will still be a private contractor. Shit, you get paid about twice what I am getting and in any case it’s compulsory for long term contractors. You can’t leave the office otherwise and sometimes your duties will demand go out on operations. Like now for example. Your clearance came through today and you are, from today, a fully operational contractor. Do you know how to shoot?” 
“This is the PTD. The only thing we make is weapons. Everyone knows how to shoot.”
“Alexander Shakti can’t.”
“I have heard that.” They both giggled.
The Commissioner, listening in from the comfort of his office chair, called his secretary. “Get me Shakti.”
* * *
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="66F1C2CC-EE8E-422C-B8AC-9ADAE3537023">
            <Title>Cloning the Arts</Title>
            <Synopsis>Background on the criminal geneticist, Linus Grawling (name should be changed) who hated modern music and went off gathering DNA samples from all of the old time singers.  He stumbled across an old time singer only named Elvis whose DNA is perfect for replicating.  You later find out he had to leave the country because of all the shocking cloning experiments he had undertaken.  </Synopsis>
            <Text>
Cloning the Arts
3 hours after Bombing &amp; Flashback to 20 or so years BTD
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="65FCBC06-DF3C-42C2-89C8-85174BA05508">
            <Title>The Dawkins</Title>
            <Synopsis>Second part of the Travelator series.
The Sages mention "The timing is perfect- 283 days exactly."  283 is the expected length of a pregnancy and they are also 283 days away from Christmas. They want their saviour born on Christmas day.  I think I have mentioned (later) that Richard is born on Christmas day.
Lots of Praising the Dawkins.  
The narrative of Kleo and the philosophy surrounding the Dawkins should remain, but it needs a little more pep.  Editing needed. 
Touches on the concept of certainty and the truth.  "When we embrace faith, the truth is locked down tight."
Touches on how truth is subverted - "Falsehood possesses an unending assortment of strategies.  The truth merely exists."
Touches upon how god may have been invented.
"The god being deliberately made us flawed and then set about punishing us for it."
Kleo makes the observation that economics is the religion of the PTD.
Explains how (actual) religion was removed from the PTD.  You may be amused or bemused by this explanation. Slightly ambiguous, too.
Bit worried about the attack on the travelator car.  It's a distraction to impregnate Sarah with the clone. I don't think it works - I am sure you can come up with a better idea.  (You later find out that Linus Grawling manufactured a device that injects the clone and implants it.  Luckily she has the virus, so any cloning errors are corrected in utero.)At this stage she still has the disc inside her bra.</Synopsis>
            <Text>
The Dawkins
Afternoon/evening after bombing
The Travelator continued slowly, relentlessly clicking, wobbling and lurching along the warped steel rails. Kleo, Silo and Chilo were whispering among themselves, debating something of importance and nodding in agreement.
“The timing is perfect.” Chilo was nodding and even cracked a bit of a smirk as he glanced at Cracker and Sarah. “Two hundred and eighty three days exactly.”
“From what I can tell she is the best candidate we have seen or are likely to see.” Kleo whispered and then turned to his guests and began speaking, exposing dark stained teeth. “The Great One was not the first to cast light upon the malady of human imperfection, but he was the first to coherently challenge the structures of ignorance. Praise be to The Dawkins and the wisdom of his word.” Kleo was quiet, serene and very serious.
“Um, yay the Dawkins.” Cracker was really hoping someone would clue him up about this Dawkins thing. He could not place the reference but, as always, was unable to admit ignorance.
“Let him be praised” Silo replied automatically.
“Wisdom be The Dawkins.” Chilo added, looking sideways at Kleo.
“And the Dawkins be wisdom.” Kleo was silent for the longest time before finally casting his gaze upon Sarah. “One of the most perplexing aspects of the human condition is our predisposition to believe in the promise of certainty, whenever it is peddled. We crave the comfort of knowing what will come to pass. Certainty, however, is illusory, it can only exist as a trick of the mind. It only draws breathe in an atmosphere of fantasy, in a place of shadows, far away from rational thought, where lies are laundered into comfortable truths and the impossible is plausibly accepted as fact.” 
Kleo took a deep draught of air and saw Sarah sat upright on the chair, listening intently. Cracker hoped no-one would notice he was scratching his balls, or be polite enough to ignore it. Johnny was still out cold, but breathing steadily, quietly moaning at irregular intervals. 
“There are those who cynically hawk certainty in a myriad of forms. They seek to profit from human frailty and take advantage of our fears and prejudices. I am here to warn you against embracing the faith of certainty. Reason is our Saviour and doubt is our guide. ‘Follow your reason as far as it will take you, without regard for any other consideration. Do not pretend that conclusions are certain when they are not demonstrated.’ So sayeth The Dawkins.” 
“Praise be to him.” Chilo was again tending to Johnny, checking his pulse and breathing.
“Why do we crave certainty?” Cracker was certain Kleo wanted him to ask that question. 
“I am glad you asked young Hardy. At the moment we were gifted sentience, we were overwhelmed by the realisation the world was a desperately dangerous place. Our reptilian brain stems caused us fright and panic at every minor peril, but gradually the mammalian and human upgrades were able to control fear by collecting and passing on knowledge. Over untold generations the collective wisdom became tradition, something to be followed without question.” He took a deep breath. He had said this a few time before. “Wisdom, collective wisdom in the form of tribal lore, is only an assemblage of guesses as to the nature of things and falls well short of granting the certainty we crave. We are unable to ignore certainty when it is on offer, especially when it is served up as faith. When we embrace the faith of certainty, the truth is locked down tight.”
“Even when smothered, the truth still exists.” Sarah was not convinced.
“Ah, the much vaunted power of the truth. It is a romantic notion to see the truth as an incontrovertible, immovable monolith that, when finally uncovered, shines magnificently, scorching ignorance away. The truth is fragile and delicate, it can never gift the certainty of a well-constructed falsehood and it wilts without support. Lawyers never enter court armed merely with the truth! They cannot possibly deflect every fib contrived by their adversaries. To obtain a favourable result, they fortify the truth with a false, but more palatable narrative and suppress valid evidence, highlighting improbable alternate scenarios to muddy the waters. Indeed, in an apparent search for the truth, many will do everything to avoid it.” Chilo chipped in.
“Who lies wins?” Cracker thought he was doing well by keeping up, thus far.
“The prevailing truth of the day is simply the opinion that has survived. Falsehood possesses an unending assortment of strategies and the real truth merely exists. When an inconvenient truth is exposed to the light of day, it is instantly subjected to attacks designed to undermine and weaken its power.” Kleo gesticulated with upturned palms. “It is not always beautiful, indeed it can be ugly and distasteful. A pleasant fiction can be infinitely more desirable and if we cannot be certain of our security, we accept the illusion of it. The unreality created is held fast by blind faith that ignores absurdity and is hostile to challenge. So sayeth The Dawkins”
“It does not seem to make sense. Why would anyone prefer to live in delusion?” Sarah could not comprehend a civilisation where ignorance was a virtue.
“Fallacious memes, religion being the most prominent example, have much positive reinforcement – warm and comforting feelings of being loved and protected from a dangerous world. So say the Chronicles of The Dawkins. Praise be to him.” Chilo cheerfully quoted. 
“There is a tendency for humans consciously to see what they wish to see. We literally have difficulty seeing negative connotations in our beliefs, while seeing all things that are positive. The diversity of irrational human beliefs, once entrenched in a culture, persist, evolve and diverge in a manner reminiscent of biological evolution. So sayeth the Dawkins.” Kleo had reached a place of bliss, uplifted by the treasured words of his prophet. “What do you know of cult of religion?”
“I studied it in Business Theology at School. I know that people used to worship gods and the people grouped themselves into different sects that had wildly differing views on how to worships these gods and were often in conflict with one another.” Sarah said recalling distant memories from her early school days. “It all seemed a bit silly to me.”
“I saw a few religious rallies broken up by the cops when I was a kid, but I can’t say that I took much notice. They were carrying around a giant lower case “t”, but I have no idea what they were squawking about.” Cracker chuckled. “Man, the cops belted the absolute shit out them. I heard they were interrogated for weeks before their bodies were released.” He continued to laugh. Religion was pretty funny.
 
“Human kind, in its infancy, could only fathom the inexplicable as being the work or the will of a divine being. It is a common phenomenon for young children to conjure up a friend unseen, a playmate and protector, someone that will always be there for them. Not unlike children, early man desperately wanted the solace of that personal guardian watching over them always, protecting them from spiders, snakes, lightening, floods, volcanos and diseases that would appear from nowhere. It is impossible to know, but it is most likely the first person to claim friendship with unseen spirits was one of those children who continued to carry undetectable guardians through to adulthood or, perhaps, the first to make contact with benevolence hidden were the insane, whose curious behaviours and incantations were mistaken for channelling the divine. However it happened, at some stage someone claimed to be in communication with God and others took him at his word.”
 
“An invisible defender-guy? Who would believe that?” Upon thinking a little further, Cracker decided an invisible body guard on duty 24/7 might not be such a bad idea. 
“Everyone as it turned out, everyone. The work of the proto-god was not limited to protecting the tribe from lice infestation and those sub-human unbelievers from over the hill. His area of responsibility rapidly expanded to the punitive, demanding far more than a mere pound of flesh from believers and infidels alike. The rock-fall that killed half the tribe was a sign your invisible friend was unhappy with you, personally. How did you make him happy again? What did the disembodied and the eternal want? As mankind grew from infancy to childhood he experimented with many different models of placating his protector, most cults settled on an intense program of ritualism and sacrifice. A ludicrous and pointless endeavour.”
“Human sacrifice?” Sarah asked. 
“Sometimes yes, my word, yes. The adolescence of man saw humanity increasingly formalise rituals into compulsory unbending dogma, with rival sects clashing violently over who was had the correct set of beliefs and rituals.”
“Men killed men because they didn’t grovel in the right way or served up the wrong dead things?” Cracker just shook his head.
“They did it to spread their particular variety of beliefs and rituals. Priests gathered money and garnered power, influencing Generals and Kings with promises of favourable consideration from above.”
“You speak of paganism and idolatry. Isn’t it all ancient history?” Sarah thought his overview of human history was more than a little sketchy.
“I speak of ignorance wielding power and influence. I speak of unyoked delusion dressed as truth!” Kleo was now animated, pacing up and down. “Living in an atmosphere of ignorance is very dangerous, particularly to those who challenge the status-quo, those who recognize the absurdity of unexamined faith. To insulate the meme from meaningful examination, citizens were controlled by filling their daily lives with the pointless busy-work of observance to sacrament. They were told what to do, how to do it and when.”
“Everything, even how to fuck?” Cracker was now not amused. That sort of intrusion of privacy was completely unacceptable, particularly if he was prevented from doing it doggy-fashioned.
“Wow, is there anything else in there? Sarah inquired genuinely wanting to know if there was anything else on the mind of a man.
“Yes. They were told who to marry and in what position to act out their predetermined love.” Kleo did not like the tangent the conversation had slipped onto. “Religion demanded your faith and your faith devoured your life.”
“Why would anyone submit to this without proof?” Sarah attempted to keep the conversation above the waistline. 
“To quote the great one, ‘many religions teach the objectively implausible but subjectively appealing doctrine that our personalities survive our bodily death.’ All men must come to terms with the knowledge of their own mortality, but god provided the ignorant masses with a promise of eternal life in paradise. Some sects even had virgins thrown into the deal and in the absence of any other alternative, the post-mortem paradise plan seemed pretty good. Ridiculous egos of men readily accepted this insurance policy as fact. Rejecting the notion would expose a throbbing raw nerve - that there is nothing after death and we are, after all, unimportant. The thought of fading into non-existence was intolerable – all knowledge and experience of a lifetime lost? Life after death was impossible to resist.”
“Virgins? A pretty rare commodity.” Cracker was looking directly at the only virgin in a 10 kilometre radius, but Sarah, who could feel his mocking gaze, did not give him the satisfaction of glancing his way. “Do you reckon they could chuck in a couple of professionals? You know, so you can get a good workout.”
“The Dawkins tells us the life after death doctrine reduces the wonder of human existence, the delight and improbability of being alive, to a test. Whether we have been good enough to get into paradise. The invisible friend as protector was gone, having morphed into an all seeing all knowing accountant, carefully scribing every transgression in a heavenly black box recorder.” Kleo seemed to be saddened a little. “The God-being deliberately made us flawed and then set about punishing us for it.”
“Piety for life? No-one could pass that test.” Cracker knew he would have no hope. Some of the shenanigans he had got up to over the years would make Satan shake his head in disbelief.
“One sect did provide sinners with a loop-hole by allowing into their heaven, anyone who accepts Jesus as lord and saviour, following his famous suicide mission in the middle-east.” 
 “How did we get rid of them?” Cracker was actually interested now, he guessed it was pretty violent.
 “Science began to make inroads here and there, but god-followers fought back at every turn, forcing greater minds to recant and abandon legitimate science and drop on bended knee to kiss a priest’s ring. Scientific advancement can only be slowed, not halted and as the centuries passed, snippets were gathered here and there and built upon until Darwin, the One before the One, realised the glimmering beauty of evolution and natural selection. Science and reason gave man the chance to release himself from a fantasy world of angels, demons, heaven and hell, but many refused to see the light, remaining devout and even militant.”
“I see a war coming.” Cracker liked war-vids and had an unfortunate romantic view of human conflict, erroneously figuring that he, just like the central protagonist in all the best movies, would also survive until the credits rolled. He would stroll into the sunset, with a huge machine gun slung across his sweaty, blood splattered back, on the arm of an impossibly attractive blonde soldier-babe who, for reasons unknown, chose to do battle in a bikini top and short-shorts.
“Killing others in the name of God became increasingly popular with the devout, in fact the more devout one became, the more reasons existed to puncture, shoot and blow up the opposition. Indifferent as it is, however, evolution had other plans and fashioned a golden strand of DNA of an infinitely superior kind, The Dawkins. The Dawkins constructed his elegant defence of evolution, what we now call Rational Evolutionism, giving rational thought room to take root and grow.”
“Can’t religion and evolution co-exist?” Sarah questioned.
“No. Science demands proof, actual evidence that can stand up to scrutiny. Religion demands belief without proof and sometimes belief notwithstanding proof to the contrary. As the Dawkins correctly asks, ‘Why should science respectfully tip-toe away?’” Kleo waited for an answer but none came. “If a religious man accepts evolutionary science, then he must accept the notion that God created the universe only to wait 14 billion years for the advent of human worship. It seems an awfully long time to wait for a bit of adoration.” 
“Seems a little self-absorbed too. I am very glad this nonsense has been eradicated.” Sarah felt glad that science had inoculated her against such backward thinking. 
“Not eradicated, still here but in a different form. In the Primary Trade Dominion we worship money and make no mistake it is a religion. We are conditioned by fear to worship at the altar of profit, with the market cast as vengeful god. We are drones, trained by rote to follow fiscal dogma without question.” Kleo fidgeted as he spoke, ill at ease with the knowledge that the market, unlike most other gods, was easily manipulated. “We televise human sacrifice to sell soap powder. We contrived a system that ensures many live in poverty and then seek to punish them for being poor. We are no different and we are no better than our ignorant forefathers. Portable communicators, home delivery pizza and interactive holo-vids do not make us anymore enlightened.”
“How did the PTD dump religion?” Cracker was intrigued.
“Decades ago, an accident of history allowed a demagogue, Dunning Kruger, to seize the Presidency of the Union of Capitalist Atlantic States, the most powerful nation on earth at the time. He was a buffoon with no shame and possessed of a cynical, chameleonic ability to shape-shift into anything the moment demanded. He had no regard for truth and little regard for his fellow man, even the rich and powerful were unceremoniously cast aside when they dared challenge him. He lied and then lied about lying and then lied about that. Unable to work from a common set of facts, observers and pundits were so distracted, so obsessed with proving the President a liar that none thought to look through the web of disinformation to devine his actual agenda. He had created his own reality and in this alternative truth environment he convinced many that he and only he had the answers.”
“Lying is the cornerstone of politics, is it not?” Sarah
“Politicians are very miserly with the truth, that is a fact, but Kruger made falsehood an art form, deliberately confusing issues to promote the notion that he was the only one who could navigate to safe waters. No matter what else, he sold certainty and being unable to resist the promise of certainty voters flocked to his cause. He promised voters the world and delivered nothing. He promised fair dealing with the international community, but delivered hostility and ultimatums. He strong armed and invaded oil rich nations to commandeer their resources and killed thousands just so Atlantians could fill up on cheaper fuel. When he was gifted an opportunity to deploy nuclear weapons, as he had threatened many times, he launched without hesitation.”
“He nuked the oil fields?” Cracker was gob-smacked.
“No, he wasn’t that stupid. He vaporised two million of their religious brothers who had invaded this very country.” Kleo was clearly distressed by the demise of the old country.
“The Dominion?” Cracker had not been a particularly attentive student at school and was missing vast swathes of commonly known facts.
“No, silly, Ulyssia.” Sarah interrupted. “This whole continent used to be one country.”
“Most criticism of his polices had come from middle-eastern countries, but were quickly silenced with threats no longer empty. He continued his policy of ultimatum diplomacy, deploying the nukes whenever he found disagreement.”
“Didn’t anyone retaliate?” Cracker figured someone would have.
“Yes, of course, but the defence net in place over the country managed to bring down all of the missiles, with the mysterious exception of missiles in-bound for cities that did not vote Kruger. These places, mostly on the east and west coast, were devastated.”
“How does this have anything to do with The Dawkins?” Sarah thought this was getting way off topic.
“The Dawkins despised ignorance in all forms and Kruger was idiocy personified, but his moronic policy of random, but regular, nuking of wrong thinking people, wiped out a couple of religions entirely and many others came to the conclusion that if Dunning Kruger can be the most powerful person on earth, there is no god. God could be forgiven for bone cancer in infants or for allowing the holocaust, but this was a bridge too far. The hitherto largely unread Chronicles of The Dawkins began a resurgence, enunciating the ultimate truth, that there is no ultimate truth. Praise be to him.” 
“In the newly formed Primary Trade Dominion, it was obligatory for any organisation, religions included, to contribute to economic activity and behave as responsible corporate citizens, complying with all tax, corporate and consumer legislation. It was the latter that brought them to their knees. Countless prayers had remained unanswered and consumers sued their local god franchises in their millions, all crying fraud as the God they had invested in so heavily was not producing dividends. Religion was required to present proof that God did live up to the hype, but no evidence could be produced. The payouts were so enormous, that the sects were bankrupted or insurance premiums became so exorbitant that they had to shut down operations. Religious gatherings became unjustifiably expensive due to public liability insurance premiums.” 
“In the absence of religion, why didn’t science and reason take root?” To Sarah it seemed natural that science and reason would have filled the vacuum.
“Do you not attend Sunday morning financial statements at your local Chamber of Commerce?”
“Yes, but it would be noticed if I didn’t”
“When religion was in it’s final death throws, the viral cleansing hit Ulyssia and wiped out people by the millions. Some jumped back on the religious bandwagon, citing the plague as a sign from an angry god, but those were quickly shouted down. Opportunists used the viral cleansing as a chance to promote their own private agendas. As the country of Ulyssia broke up into the Primary Trade Dominion and the radio-active Western Trade Dominion, rabid anti-Atlantian sentiment grew into a nationalist policy of isolationism. It was decided to base the economy on high-tech weapons development and manufacture. To ensure the success of the new order, icons, rituals and symbols of religion were recycled into the ideology under which we currently suffer. We do not yet live in the age of reason, but the age of shameless lies and unfettered profiteering. The age of reason will begin when Dawkinsian Evolutionism is fully embraced.” Kleo looked spent. 
 
“But our society is the very definition of survival of the fittest. Does that not fit snugly into Evolution?” Sarah liked stirring the pot a bit, even if she was a big fan of Darwin.
“The Dawkins was very explicit about this, his name be praised.” Silo faced glowed when he said the name of the great one. “It is written in his chronicles - survival of the fittest is a good way for nature to select species but a bad way for men to run a civilisation. His words be wise and wise be his words.” 
“Praise be to the Dawkins,” both Chilo and Kleo chimed in with perfect timing.
 “The Chronicles of The Dawkins unmistakably document his strong conviction that the individual should be given the greatest opportunity to develop, evolve if you will, without being chained down by dogma. Can you imagine all the brilliant minds that have gone to waste? Young minds should be allowed to grow without being infected with viral memes. That cannot occur in our society of yawning inequality. Evolution will not let it stand. Another will take the place of the Dawkins, the one-after-the-one.” 
“The one-after-the-one.” Silo and Chilo in perfect unison.
“The Messiah? Give me a fucking break.” Cracker was greatly amused, these guys really believed their own bullshit.
“No, not a messiah, the next great intellect. The great leader to come. The nature of genetics insists that evolution will, eventually, throw up the precise DNA required. It is an operation in probability not prophesy. The Dawkins would forgive your ignorance.” Kleo was calm and remained unflustered in the face of Cracker’s disrespect. 
	
“Cometh the hour cometh the man?” Sarah was not too sure of his understanding of probability. “This can only be conjecture, how can anyone know what will come to pass?”
“Prophecy, messianic or otherwise, is always self-fulfilling. We retrofit the personality to neatly dovetail with revelation.” 
“Is that your Plan? Find someone to retrofit?” As with his ignorance, Cracker’s cynicism was always close to the surface and exerted itself often enough to be a defining characteristic. 
“The identity of the one-after-the-one will be undeniable.” Kleo knew this, almost certainly, to be true.
“The one-after-the-one. Praise be to him.” Silo and Chilo incanted perfectly synchronised.
A loud bang from ceiling of the car startled the group, all suddenly glancing above attempting to discern the origin. Several more hits overhead saw dust and splintered wood shower down, covering Cracker and Sarah with debris and fine powder.
“Jesus Christ!” As Cracker had said thousands times before, but on this occasion he was finally aware he had, for much of his life, been invoking the name of some ancient deity. Blinded by clumps of dust and fine splinters he was in some obvious distress, raising his arms to the horizontal and swinging them wildly, back and forth, desperately trying to locate something or someone to grab. Failing miserably, he dropped to his knees and rubbed his eyes vigorously with the heel of his hands and the back of his forefingers, but only managing to scrape the fragments across his corneas, increasing discomfort. Sarah too, was struggling with smarting eyes, but had the presence of mind not to scratch or rub and was sufficiently oriented to relocate her seat, throwing the remainder of her wine into her eyes. It stung like crazy, exactly as she had expected, but most of the offending material was flushed free granting her blurred vision.
“Intruders!” Chilo yelled and brought Sarah to the ground. “Stay here young miss. We will repel these unbelievers.” That was okay with her, she needed time to regain her sight, but as the attack from above continued, the car filled with fine particulate matter, cancelling out any gains in vision. Judging by the ruckus, their three hosts were doing fine against the unknown intruders, Kleo yelling instructions triggering his apprentices into immediate and decisive action, shoring up vulnerable spots on the skin of their fortress. 
“Hole in the roof!” Chilo barked. “Smoke grenade.”
“Fight for The Dawkins my apprentices!” Kleo rallied as the combat intensified. “Be gone foul interlopers!” He wondered if he was over-doing it.
Sarah was reasonably able to see now and stood to her feet to find she was only able to discern shadows slipping through the dust and smoke suspended in the stifling air. Without warning she was knocked backwards with a sharp blow to her abdomen, nothing to make her scream, but certainly painful enough for her to roll into the foetal position rocking for a moment or two as the discomfort dissipated. She bounced to her feet to join in the fight, but the din had been replaced with silence.
“We are out of The Pale. We are safe now.” Kleo opened the main entrance door allowing the dust and smoke to escape and visibility to return to the car, revealing a jagged fissure in the roof easily broad enough for a man to drop through. “Is anyone injured?”
“I can’t see, but apart from that I’m okay.” Cracker was still rabidly rubbing his eye sockets.
“I’m fine.” Sarah picked up a chair and sat down slowly. Looking about, she saw Silo and Chilo were already busily waving drills, patching the hole, fixing down rusty misshapen bits of scavenged sheet metal with massive screws that bit deeply, penetrating the roof. The ceiling was scattered with similar patches leaving the roof outside, Sarah imagined, resembling a porcupine with a crew cut. “What just happened?”
“Gazumpers.” Kleo panted as he scratched his beard. 
“Where did they go?” Sarah felt the urge to deal out some justice, but she was left with no candidates.
“They slipped back out into The Pale.” Silo counselled calmly. “They are gone.”
“They are not to be reviled, Sarah. They have been denied the wisdom of The Dawkins and their personal evolution has been stifled by lies and greed. The word of the Dawkins will change all of this.” Kleo sat down and gently took Sarah’s hand.
“They are thieves!”
“Yes, they are, but perhaps not forever. The one after the one will come.”
“His words will be known.” Silo and Chilo were well practiced.
“How is Johnny?” Sarah suddenly remembered her companion and glanced fleetingly at the crumpled suit on the floor.
“Sleeping beauty can wait. I can’t fucking see!” Cracker was in no mood. “I need a bit of a hand over here thanks.” 
Sarah grabbed the jug of wine and stomped over to Cracker and stood before him, feet shoulder width apart and one hand on her hip. She smiled and motioned to empty the contents on his head, but baulked and looked over to Kleo, “Don’t suppose you have some water?”
“Yes. Please don’t waste anymore.” She smiled, returned to the table and poured herself a drink.
Silo was up and already handing Cracker a cup of water which was maniacally hoisted directly into his eye socket, cracking his skull. Without a word he waved the cup about which, when replenished, was unceremoniously slammed into his other eye. “Oh, thanks. That is so much better.”
“You okay now?” Sarah return to Johnny who appeared to be waking up. “Don’t worry, we will take care of you.”
“Well my friends, we will arrive at your destination shortly.” Kleo smiled. “Gather up your friend. We will meet again, I know this to be true. Praise be to The Dawkins.”
“Is there enough time to tell us of Jamison Hardy?” Cracker was interested in his new relative.
“I can only tell you of the days leading up to the Great Viral Cleansing. It is rumoured that one of his research facilities was quarantined suddenly and sealed off permanently under circumstances that were never really clear. It was an underground facility that produced some of the nastiest microbes ever cooked up. Mr Hardy had no interest in bio-weaponry and was in the process of selling off the assets when he suddenly shut it down. No explanations, not to his closest confidants, not even to me.” Being left out of the loop by the great Jamison Hardy had sliced Kleo to the core.
“The Viral Cleansing.” She figured this was where he was going. “Something got out didn’t it?”
“That is a mystery even to me, Sarah. A mystery to solve.” Kleo replied. 
“Can I ask an obvious question? If religion is the enemy, why are you and your disciples dressed like Druids and delivering sermons? Is this not a religion?” Sarah finally pointed at the elephant in the corner. 
“Ritual and ceremony are not necessarily religious in nature and people seem to derive comfort from customarily repeated practices. If you want to bring the concept of god into our belief system, we are talking of the god of the great prophet, Karl Sagan. Quoted by The Dawkins himself, If by “God” one means the set of physical laws that govern the universe, then clearly there is such a god.” Looking at Sarah, Kleo smiled brightly. “We will use the comfort of ritual to spread our word and you will be a part of it. You will bear witness to it all, Sarah.” 
Praise be to the Dawkins.
* * *
