        <Document ID="D569600A-D101-4EA9-AD41-71CAA5050989">
            <Title>Leiutenant Shakti</Title>
            <Text>
LIEUTENANT SHAKTI – PEOPLE’S HERO
9 months BTD
Alexander Shakti was not an unintelligent man, some said quite the opposite was true, although these people were few and far between. His capacity to engage in abstract thought was minimal. He was socially retarded, romantically inept and had no ability or, perhaps, desire to embrace any meaningful discourse. A conversation with Alex, as he liked to be called, was an awkward blend of pregnant pauses and obscure humour, usually ending after a few excruciating minutes. Invariably there was some ruse employed by the victim in a desperate attempt at extricating themselves. “Is that the time?” or “I’ve got a meeting” or, simply, “Please don’t have children.” Alex had heard them all before, but oddly enough, he did not realise that he was a social pariah and had no idea he was a target of derision and ridicule. Such was his ego and unwavering confidence in his abilities, he held the strong belief that he was cool and well liked.
Born into a comfortable level 5 home he became a disappointment and later, a total embarrassment, to his hardworking professional parents. Although his mother was born level 2, she had owned three houses and two apartments by the time she was 20 years old. By age twenty-two, she was running her own Accommodation Directorate and the fact that she managed to land a level 5 husband, whilst extremely rare, was no accident. With pure force of will she took on larger and by reputation, more ferocious competitors and crushed them beneath her stylish heels. When her business was publicly listed, there were protestations from the Stock Exchange directly to the Ministry of Moral Compliance. Although there was no legal impediment, it seemed that a Level 2 CEO could not list a company on the Stock Exchange, they simply would not have it. Before any public trading on her stock commenced, however, she was made an offer she could not refuse, the sale of the company at an inflated price and allocation with an upwardly mobile level 4. She knew that with her talent she and her new husband would be at level 5 in double quick time and their children would want for nothing, breaking into the senior executive ranks – level 7 and above.
	
Alexander’s father was an astute executive, but lacked the flare and drive of his wife. After the initial adjustment period (allocations never run smoothly at first), they actually formed a close bond, a partnership of trust and mutual respect that, for him, even extended to the metaphysical. Like everyone else he had had been taught at school that the economy was of paramount importance. The family unit was an historical oddity that had endured to provide a steady supply of labour for industry and the professions. The means of production had a veracious appetite and demanded fresh bodies, caring not who it consumed. 
Following the allocation, Shakti Senior had been very surprised by his genuine warm feelings for his wife. The birth of his only child Alexander had been a major source of happiness as a young married man. He knew he had genuinely loved his wife, but he could not tell her. What would she think of him? Love undermines rational thought. Profit demands rational thought. Love, therefore does not make economic sense and his wife needed a home based in economic rationality. Like a good husband, he never told his wife that he loved her, even when the words were bursting from his very core. As for his wife, she never gave love a single thought. She was very happy with her social position and would have reacted in a typically aggressive manner if anything, even her husband, threatened that position. He would not have survived. 
The home they created for Alex was the picture perfect environment for the budding young corporate raider. Every activity engaged in; every toy played with; every vid-stick watched and playgroup attended was training for the corporate sector. None of it sunk in. Well, that was not entirely true. He could recount the information that he had been taught but, seemingly, could not apply the theory to practical situations. To his parents he was a dullard and would only progress with a great deal of luck. 
When adolescents reach the age of 16 they are introduced to mind-dump technology as a part of the school curriculum. By wearing what appeared to be an ordinary pair of glasses, but connected to the school data-base, a student could instantly transfer vast amounts of information directly into longterm memory. Student progress accelerated exponentially, but it did not make a student any smarter, it just gave them more information for there existing intellect to draw upon and process as best as their minds could. The degree to which the information was retained varied from student to student, but Shakti’s brain refused to retain any data dumped into his uniquely tiny mind. He was the only student in who had ever completely rejected mind-dumps. He was promptly shipped off for medical examinations, such was the shock and embarrassment the news caused his mother. 
Testing of his brain function showed the information was dumped his brain in the normal and expected fashion, but immediately began to fade and was gone within minutes. Eloise Shakti purchased the best scientific minds in an effort to cure Shakti of his evolutionary curse, but the consensus unanimously agreed upon was devastating. Shakti was an ordinary boy and his brain physiology was incompatible with digital knowledge acceleration. “Monkeys retain information better” was said on more than one occasion.
Returning to school he soon fell behind while his compatriots progressed in leaps and bounds. He was required by the curriculum to undertake all brain-dump sessions which were as painful as they were embarrassing. He was greeted at every session by the sight of a stuffed monkey strapped into his chair and hooked up to the data base. “Monkey Boy” would be whispered and the class would roar with laughter, reaching its peak when he gingerly unhooked the monkey and pointlessly took its place. 
Both parents earnestly apprehended the fear that he would end up in the public service and when he applied for law enforcement training it was embarrassment and shame heaped upon humiliation. When they shared their concern with their Level 5 peers, it was met with incredulous facial expression and cries of disbelief. Short term contracting to the public sector was acceptable, but no one at level 5 or above had actually joined the public service. One could only expect, at best, to gain level 5 status after a lengthy and extremely successful career in public office. Without immersing oneself in corruption, there was no way of accumulating personal wealth in the public sector. Not that anyone was all that concerned by a bit of graft, but corrupt practices were expected to remain where they always have flourished – in the private sector. To the management elite, the petty corruptions of public officials were paltry and laughably amateur compared to their own acts of moral turpitude.
To the police Shakti was a perfect candidate for employment. Most of the police recruits were level 1 or 2 and occasionally a loser from level 3 might apply, but they had never had an application from a level 5 candidate. The recruiting officers were completely dumbfounded when he transmitted his application. In social terms, he outranked all senior executive and was level pegging with the Commissioner. This pleased them greatly and it was with immense satisfaction and diligent zeal that they fast-tracked the application. 
 
Most of the recruiting processes were aimed at weeding out scoundrels and rapscallions from level 1 and 2. Expecting a level 5 candidate to suffer the ignominy of the entire recruitment nightmare would be frowned upon and be seen by upper society as an attack on them. Fancy forcing one of our own to undertake those silly tests. Besides, what if he failed? As a moral certainty, the entire recruiting section would be canned and the Police Department would move one step closer to total privatisation. The only ordeal he had to undertake was the medical examination. The last thing that the cops wanted was to employ some diseased weakling, level 5 or not. The Senior Recruiting Officer had made one mistake in his career, allowing a recruit with cancer to commence training and he knew, because of this blunder, he would never, ever, make it to level 4. He was not going to make that mistake again. Shakti’s health screen revealed no congenital conditions or acquired diseases and he commenced training within days.
Progression of this candidate was monitored with interest by the Commissioner’s Office and very early in his training it was realised that he did not possess any of the qualities generally attributed to the law enforcement officer. This caused much consternation in the staff rooms as they knew that they could not fail this person, but he was simply the worst candidate they had ever seen. Like a pain-in-the-neck mature age student, his only contribution to theory classes was incessantly asking idiotic questions or stating the painfully obvious. Each and every time he opened his stupid gob, his comments were promptly followed by pained groans from his classmates. There was always one in the class, but no one had expected it to be Shakti. What did they teach in those expensive private schools? They would all ask themselves. Whatever it was, he must have been sick that day.
He did not meet the physical demands of the program either. He could not shoot – which was a major achievement in itself. The Particle Pulse Pistols (PPP), now on standard issue, had the new “tag and bag” firing system. Not unlike the old laser sights on the obsolete gas compression models, the PPP had a unique targeting system. The sights left a unique electro-magnetic signature on the target which was identical to the signature of the next burst from the barrel. After tagging, the trigger is depressed and the projectile (really a short particle beam burst) always hit the tagged target. You just had to point the pistol in the general direction of the target and squeeze the trigger. It was foolproof, but not Shakti-proof. He was unable to tag his target and, consequently, he was also unable to bag anything either. Weapons instructors spent many, many hours on the firing range with Shakti and following failure upon failure, he was passed. He was passed not only because of political and internal pressures, but also for the sake of their own mental health. Getting him the hell out of the training college and, hopefully, the hell out of their lives, became the only means of preserving their collective sanity.
The entire class of ’52 hated him without exception. Initially some latched onto him and made aborted attempts at friendship. They figured that a Level 5 was, most probably, going to be fast tracked to promotion and if they got in on the ground floor, they would be dragged up with him. However, even the slimiest sycophants in the class had their limits. They would not place their future in this imbecile’s hands. It seemed he had absolutely no laudable qualities whatsoever. 
It took no time at all for Shakti to acquire many unflattering nicknames. Firstly his class mates dubbed him Scott – ‘Scott-no-brains’ or ‘Scott-no-hope’. Then they moved to ‘Fuck Wit’ and finally just ‘F.W.’ Even with this derision directed straight at his face he was under the impression that this was how all level one’s and two’s interacted. He found it a compliment that the common man would treat him as an equal, one of their own. He was happy to have been able to breach the gulf between the higher community and the common as some sort of social pioneer.
It was not until the final weeks of training that he finally showed aptitude to any policing duties. Not unlike academy dweebs, members of the Public Affairs Bureau were usually fuck-sticks who could not hold their own on the street or who were too scared to try. They kidded themselves into thinking that they were actually real police officers. To the rest of the rank and file, these people were nothing more than pretenders who liked to tell bullshit war stories at parties in order to jag a fuck. The training to deal with the Public Information Outlets involved many mock media interviews, press releases and “talking to camera.” Although he could not think on his feet, if the copy was written for him Shakti excelled in this environment. The news resonated throughout the academy. To the Chancellor, this represented the removal of a great weight from his shoulders. He knew how Damocles had felt. The pressure from above had been almost unbearable. Now the dangling sword was gone and he could send Shakti to the Public Affairs Bureau with the honest recommendation that he was suited to this area. 
Shakti became a household name very quickly. His arrival at Public Affair Bureau coincided with the launch of Police Video Streaming. His face was on every Vid-Screen; Vid-board; poster; milk carton, leaflet and some smart-arse even put his face on toilet paper. Although paper products were prohibitively expensive, they sold like hot-cakes, mostly to police officers as it provided an opportunity to do something they had long wanted to do. There were two channels transmitted to levels 1 to 3 and one other channel for the consumption of Level 4 and above. The programming was almost entirely real life docu-dramas and with his flare for publicity and milking it was worth, his show took off. It was the first time in his life that he had useful and, in some cases, interesting ideas that took reality vid-streams to new lows - exactly what was required. Reality TV shows had long since proven that any idiot can be a star and Shakti, even with his advanced level idiocy, gained a level of acclaim.
One program, Random Camera, revolved around surveillance and covert police operations, but not wrong the Public Affairs simpletons stomping around in their domain, Operational Police refused to assist with criminal intelligence. Starved of timely data, Shakti made a decision that only the truly unenlightened could make. He targeted houses at random. With this single decision, Shakti rejected all investigative theories and presumptions – that potent crime suppression can only be effected by basing operations on timely and accurate intelligence. All senior police knew the show would be a failure as their collective experience brought them to a collective conclusion – random policing yields random results. However, with a zero tolerance policy in effect, every show resulted in the unfortunate targets being detected committing some sort of criminal offence. 
Although the judiciary complained bitterly about the creation of an amateur parallel justice system, the amount of revenue raised ensured the program would survive. Clandestine video devices were installed into level 1 and 2 homes, at random, and the action was broadcast live into level 4-plus homes. The upper levels of society lapped it up. Most of them had never set foot on the ground. Living their lives above cloud height they had no idea how the common lived. 
Corporate Infidelity was, by and large, the major crime detected. This was not surprising, considering that to constitute the offence one merely had to breach employer confidentiality or defame a manager. A crime of absolute liability, the simple act of complaining to your spouse about work practices would establish this very serious offence. It struck at the very heart of the corporate structure and disrespect for managerial classes was inexcusable. It was the modern equivalent of heresy and doctrinal divergence of this magnitude was punishable by death and forfeiture all goods and chattels. Guilt and sentencing was decided by the viewers through on-line voting and carried out immediately. Very few in the audience were weak enough to feel compassion or deliver mercy to the accused lows. Most viewers correctly postulated that their voting habits were monitored and analysed for moral weakness –in this case compassion for one’s inferiors. Indulging one’s empathy was a waste of effort and interfered with the ability to make hard business decisions. Being outed for moral weakness had a monumentally negative effect on career paths and, consequently, there was hardly a show without at least one execution. Shows that did not feature the removal of someone’s breathing privileges rated rather poorly and advertising revenue for subsequent shows would plummet. When profits were threatened the corporate machine immediately rolled into action and issued a decree from on high – One show, one body, no excuses. 
To upper society, this was the best show on the link. At 8.00pm every Tuesday and Thursday most, if not all, available level 5-pluses, were plonked in front of their Vid-screens or at level 5 cocktail bars, mesmerized by the outrageous and often bizarre behaviour of the lows. The subtitles at the bottom of the screen assisted them in understanding the gutter talk and expletives used incessantly by the common man. Many obtained no end of amusement in the lows use of syntax and the grotesque linguistic mutations that had developed below the layer. They especially loved it when they lows beat the shit out of each other – domestic violence was hilarious, even to the female viewers. The level of violence was so extreme that it seemed the only thing commoners hated more than management, was each other.
Shakti also had the balls, or the naïve stupidity, to suggest that a similar show be conceived for the consumption of the lows. He envisioned corporate fraudsters and rogue traders being brought to book live on his program. He would be an icon to the unwashed masses. The show went ahead, but Shakti was not made aware of some of the minor changes. All of the people featured on show were actors or impostors and no level 5’s were harmed during the production of any episode. Fueled by total ignorance, Shakti took to his new second show with a fervent enthusiasm. His genuine belief in the integrity of the program gave the façade a credibility that struck a chord with the discontented lows. At last someone was doing something about the morally bankrupt managers and the corporations they serve. 
In reality, anyone caught on the show was a convicted low already on a death sentence. They knew that the lows had an extraordinary and inexplicable attachment to family members and, like vultures, they pecked and tore at this devotion until leverage was found. To ensure they played along the production company promised social promotion and employment for their families. They were going to die anyway and figured that they may as well do some good for the kids before they got zapped. The final carrot dangled in front of the impostor was the possibility, as slim as it might be, that the audience of the lows might vote to spare him or her. Fat chance, they loved their payback. 
Within a few short years Shakti, in his own right, was extremely wealthy and known throughout the PTD as a fighter for justice - a level 5 with the heart of a level 1. Due to the massive amounts of revenue raised by “Random Camera” and associated programs, Public Safety Television thrived and became the largest operational command, with no less than 3000 workers – techs, locksmiths, arrest teams and, of course, executioners. Promotion to Lieutenant, with the help of his mother’s clout and negotiating skills, was a mere formality for Shakti. Anyone who brought in that much revenue was rewarded, notwithstanding the apparently lifeless organ inside his skull. It was never brought to his attention, nor did Shakti ever notice, that the rank of Lieutenant did not previously exist and stood outside the normal rank structure, somewhere to the side, where no-one could work out where he fit in. His mother thought it to be a suitable metaphor for her idiot son’s life.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="63F5A920-F43D-4BD1-B502-B3FEA80AF46F">
            <Title>Police interrupt Random Camera</Title>
            <Text>Alexander Shakti—police lieutenant and feted media celebrity—manufactured his famous smile for the enthralled audience. Despite falling ratings, disillusioned sponsors, and increasing pressure to fake evidence, the show must go on. Specifically, this the final episode recording for the day must go on. 
“Good evening my friends. Welcome to another episode of Random Camera,” he said and paused.
“If you hadn’t done it, we wouldn’t have to vote!” the studio audience chorused back, then erupted in applause and cheers.
“Yes, indeed,” Alexander said. “Let’s not keep out special guest waiting.”
“Guilty! Guilty! Guilty!” the audience chanted.
Alexander chuckled and waved down the noise. “Airborne ladies and gentleman, please join me in reviling Cloudborne Hamish Hammersmith.”
The audience erupted as two giant body-builder types—their skins glistening with oil—dragged an emaciated sod before Alexander’s raised dias, and dumped him with rehearsed ceremony on the bare studio floorboards.
“You have the wrong man,” the sod said, as instructed by his production handler. “I haven’t done anything.” The audience laughed at his pathetic petition, as Alexander hoped everyone streaming at home would laugh.
“Play the package,” Alexander said to camera, exactly as he had hundreds of times before. He hated the money production spent on the multiple holographic sequences of ‘evidence’ they played. But their design schtick was an integral part of the show: grainy, high-contrast imaging with most of the colour leached out, all calculated to cast the ‘special guest’ as loathsome and reprehensible. Each ended with a brand-conscious dissolve of a Hammersmith’s magnified, evil face.
“Guilty! Guilty! Guilty!” the audience resumed. 
In the early days of the show, Alexender begrudgingly conceded the evidence packages were a major attractant of eyeballs to the show, eclipsed only by his talent as celebrity anchor. Years on, he resented the production costs all the more.
The QUIET PLEASE sign above the studio floor illuminated. The audience settled to murmurs.
“What have you to say now, Hamish Hammersmith?” Alexander read from the autocue. The next part of the show—where he challenged the special guest, and tricked them into inconsistent testimony—passed with Alexander on autopilot. He’d given so many such performances, they were all a blur to him. While they were passing, it wasn’t uncommon for him to be reminiscing, say on a meal he’d once had, perhaps a spectacular mac and cheese.
“It’s time for the verdict!” Alexander boomed, his engagement with proceedings reignited.
“If you hadn’t done it, we wouldn’t have to vote!” The audience knew the formula.
Votes poured in. After time had expired, Alexander announced, “The votes are in, my friends.” The audience hushed. “We received 2.17 million votes. There might be some folks voting more than once, but here on Random Camera…”
“…we value the enthusiasm!” the audience answered.
“Oh, yes we do!” Alexander sparkled at the camera. No one would deny he was a handsome son of a … military conglomerate magnate mother (with all due fear and respect), but Eloise is for later in the story. Alexander gazed towards the back of the studio as if reading a faraway autocue. He beamed a smile, as if surprised by news. “The decision’s in, and it’s—”
“Unanimous!” The audience drowned Alexander out, as they always did.
“There you have it, folks! Stay tuned for the sentencing. We’ll be right back after these important messages.”
“Cut!” the director called. Alexander went straight to make-up while an expectant carnival buzz pulsed around the studio.
When recording resumed, Alexander parroted, “Thanks for those important messages from our sponsors.” Turn to Camera 3. “Remember, for all your family planning needs, Pattinson’s Mobile Abortions, come to you, and take your worries away. Just call 1800-2229-4663. That’s 1800 BABY-GONE.” 
Turn to Camera 1. Alexander whipped up the audience with, “And now it’s time for… !”
“The Wheel of Death!” they responded. “Wheel…of…Death. Wheel…of…Death.” 
A large, intentionally low-rent chocolate wheel was wheeled out by a pretty extra in an elaborate head-dress and swimsuit.
“How lucky can you get?” Alexander said. “Each episode, we put ten randomly generated sentences on the Wheel of Death. Let’s see which will be Hamish Hammersmith’s fate. Here are the options. Number 1: boiling in oil.”
The audience went, ooh. 
“Number 2: trebuchet slap.”
The audience went, ahhh. 
“Number 3 is clubbing to death. And we don’t mean drugs and late-night dancing, folks.”
The audience laughed, inexcessively less than Alexander wanted.
“Number 4: shark tank. Number 5: one thousand cuts. Number 6 is acid injection. Number 7: packet of peanuts—the get-out-of-jail-free card, folks!”
The audience booed.
“But I’m anaphylactic,” Hammersmith complained weakly.
The audience cheered.
“We know,” Alexander said behind his hand to Hammersmith, loud enough so the mics would pick it up. “Number 8: snake pit. Number 9: drowning in a cesspool of fetid milk products.”
“I’m lactose intolerant,” Hammersmith bemoaned. 
“And finally, number 10: helium chamber.”
“But I haven’t done a thing.” Hammersmith shook. His copious tears eroded the dirt from his cheeks and gave him and appearance of frothing at the mouth.
“Well, C’mon,” Alexander chided Hammersmith. “Spin the Wheel.”
“Spin the wheel! Spin the wheel! Spin the wheel!” the chorus ascended over the stomping of feet. 
Hammersmith quaking, approached the wheel. “Oh, please! Someone help me!”
By perhaps the only grit of luck the wretched Cloudborne Hamish Hammersmith ever tasted, someone did help him. A squad of riot cops infiltrated the studio and escorted Alexander away.
“Show’s over, folks,” Alexander beamed from the exit.

The Wheel of Death had been rigged to stop at TREBUCHET SLAP—Alexander’s favorite episode climax, both for its effect on ratings and for the production intricacies it required to pull off. While all execution scenes were, by necessity, one-take wonders—there could be no do-overs—the trebuchet slap, in particular, required perfect timing and execution (if you’ll pardon the pun). Random Camera’s trebuchet was the typical medieval sturdy, wooden siege engine—an A-frame set upon a wheeled platform—complete with hulking iron brackets, giant round head nails, ropes and pulleys. A thick beam about 6 metres long was axled at the frame’s apex. At one end of the beam hung a large counter weight; at the other, a lengthy sling and pouch where the convict would be strapped in. The counter-weight would be winched up and ratcheted in place. When the executioner (a swimsuit model, as mandated by every focus group ever) released the ratchet, the counter-weight would fall, and over would sling convict, cradle and all. The magic of Random Camera’s corrupted trebuchet was, it would not hurl its payload many metres in the air, such as when laying siege to a castle. No, this trebuchet, by perversion of its design, hurled its hapless passenger straight into the ground within fractured reach of its splintery wheels.
Convict pancake anyone?
Yes, the trebuchet slap was Alexander’s favorite. It was difficult to perfect. In the early days, if a convict appeared to survive the engine, they were pardoned. But freeing convicts lodged awkwardly in the audience’s craw, and eventually Marketing demanded a trebuchet slam survivor be subject to a second spin of the wheel, which was then usually rigged to CLUBBING. As much as his fans loved a double event, Alexander preferred a successful first execution. He was a professional.

Alexander complained all the way from the studio to the cruiser—a typical over-sized police design crossing a bayou fan-boat and a dragonfly with a bloated pig. “You couldn’t wait for the finish of the show?” he demanded.
“We gotta get there before the ambulances,” one of the riots cops said.
Shit, Alexander thought. Must be serious.“Get where?”
“Wheels up in ten seconds,” was all the answer he got.
In the back of the police cruiser, Alexander was incensed. This was the second time today he’d been kidnapped by his own department. In the wee hours of the morning, the order came down for him to investigate a suicide. He hated investigative work. He was indolent about it; celebrity work was more entertaining and less work-like. He knew most of his colleagues despised him, assuming his good fortune had accrued only by the curation of his mother. So unfair! They’d rather envy him than credit his talent.
He’d learned quickly he was assigned to such cases exactly because he was so lackadaisical. If word came down—possibly from as high up as the Commissioner—he was assigned to a suicide, then he knew to do the minimum to rationalise it as suicide, then go back to his studio make-up and A-list social life. As it turned out, the case was obviously suicide. Alexander found the weapon—a shotgun, would you believe?—with the deceased’s prints all over it. Now, with Alexander prints all over the case file, it would remain forever CLOSED. He was being exploited; no one would ever reopen a case of the son of Eloise Shakti. He was the perfect tool for a cover-up.

“Is it real?” one of riot cops mumbled.
Another cop gave the first a slap witha riot shield.
“What?” Alexander.
“Is it real?” a different cop asked. “Do you really execute those low-lives?”
Alexander snorted and smiled for his own satisfaction.
“Cos, if it is, how do we get in on it?”
“Yeah, is there, like, a way you can apply to work on the show?” asked another.
“The waiting list is nine years,” Alexander said sympathetically.
The cops moaned. “Just tell us then. Are the executions real?”
A cop sitting at the far end of the cabin, clutching a sniper rifle, grimaced and shook his head.
“Tell me where we’re going first,” Alexander said.
“Bomb attack. In The Pale.”
Shit. They’re assigning me the game of Pick the Terrorists. They only required his presence to ward away questioning eyes. The riot cops will be the ones applying the terrorist label to whoever they pleased. This is no more than an opportunity for socio-economic cleansing. Alexander eyed off the cop with the sniper rifle.
“So. How about it?” a cop persisted.
“How about what?” Alexander was becoming antagonistic.
“Are the executions real?”
Alexander rolled his eyes. “That’s commercial in confidence.”</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="AFEC67AD-6A56-407D-B9FD-023193BDE74C">
            <Title>Ajent Jones' thoughts about Sarah</Title>
            <Text>Was this some sudden rush of morality flooding his body, washing clean the cankered crust of his licentious, black soul? He rejected that idea outright. He thought she must be just too good looking to hit, but that seemed unlikely too. He rather enjoyed messing up a beautiful face. They were just so damned punch-able and the looks of shock and surprise were just priceless. </Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="6E8A3279-0E35-4432-9F62-3F0435D3F0E7">
            <Title>Pre-Emptive Reciprocity</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="E7C5760D-D57D-4B56-83BE-2A143A2661A3">
            <Title>2-3 Months After the Bombing</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="D523EAA5-70DF-42C6-B209-9B3EFCB71C5B">
            <Title>A Big Crush</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="0DDEC797-2EDB-468B-A637-6BBA26AAC393">
            <Title>Blessed Mother meets Parliament</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="B351A855-81FF-4E37-AF8D-9DAF108EF02B">
            <Title>CSI by remote control.</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="9A9BA178-53DD-405C-BE53-3609E3527FC9">
            <Title>The showering</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="160E632F-0FA2-4780-886F-34FE8D79B94D">
            <Title>The Allocation of Richard's mother</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="09E60FE7-4B9B-4904-9866-5A8FB71FBCE6">
            <Title>Sages of the Travelator</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="27BF3F80-54C6-4A09-8459-3C3E530998A5">
            <Title>Cracker and Sarah in the explosion</Title>
            <Text>A snap of the fingers made the silence black.
Less than a second later, when Cracker’s eyes started working again, the hallway wall cracked and toppled, like a truck hit it from the other side. Sarah pulled cracker to the floor as the blank compression of the blast receded and left his ears ringing. The ceiling buckled, ruptured, and spewed chips and splinters and chunks down. Sarah grunted out the grey dark and slumped. Grey lumpy icing sugar dusted Sarah’s hair and caked in her blood. Too late, Cracker, crouched over as the asteroid shower out of the ceiling slowed.
Cracker assessed the space; the toppling wall’s complete collapse had been arrested by the other, still resolutely bearing the load of who-knows-what above it, forming a narrow triangular crawlspace around them. Cracker doubted it would hold long.
“Sarah!” Cracker slapped both her cheeks. There was no rousing her.
The sound of falling chunks subsided, replaced by moans and wails.
On his feet, crouching under the low lean-to of the toppled wall, Cracker hooped his arms under Sarah’s, and shuffled backwards, hoping the lobby of the club provided some form of escape. There was a whomp, like a gas burner igniting. Swirling orange flared around and past him. The muted sounds of screams bloomed. Some of the screams were Cracker’s.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="C8836FBE-9522-46A3-9705-71D68FCD4A95">
            <Title>Sages of the Travelator</Title>
            <Synopsis>The first part is to set up the meeting with Sarah's friend John Stoat. Known to Sarah as Johnny.  (I decided on Stoat as a surname as it is in the weasel family).  Cracker later names him "The Johnny" .  The Johnny is deliberately rude to a female Elvis and gets a blender smashed over his head for his troubles.  They are in a bit of trouble with the management, but Cracker has followed them and saves them (by promising to pay 3 times the bill).  None of this is of much consequence, the only thing that really matters is by the end of the evening, Sarah is carrying the clone of "Richard Dawkins."

The travelator itself is not important.  I just figured it was an easy way for the three sages to hide in plain sight.  
The sucking up to the "travelatorisi" probably could be wound back a bit.
The "Praise to the Dawkins" stuff  turned out to be a wank.  I was using it as a piss-take of Islamic extremists and the way they praise Allah whilst planning murder.
Anyway "Praise to the Dawkins" is also an acknowledgement that I have used some of Richard Dawkins' wording and concepts.
A great deal of the conversation is peculiar and probably should be changed/edited quite a bit.  
The whole chapter is to give an idea of the beliefs of Richard Dawkins and how they were co-opted into a religious framework by people who dislike religion.
I do have comments on male/female relations - "She and the rest of female kind.....does not bother them" &amp; "It's not sexist if a woman says it."
If you are prepared to accept the manner in which Sarah becomes pregnant, then we probably won't have to change too much.
The term "One-after-the-one" refers to great profit to come and is later used in an Elvis song.  We probably should reduce the amount of times the term is used.
</Synopsis>
            <Text> 
 
 Sages of the Travelator
Day After Bombing - Afternoon to evening - Year Zero
A lone Sarah arrived a few minutes early at the Memphis Mafia Bar and was met at the reception desk by an Elvi-girl named Elvine. As one might expect, she was the spitting image of the waitress from the night before, but knowing she was one of many, many clones in the quarter did not diminish the wonder of precise human replicas at large. Their faculties were exactly the same, displaying the self-same mannerisms, affectations and gait.
“Good afternoon. My name is Elvine and I will be your hostess here at the Memphis Mafia Bar and Grill today. Will you be dining or would you like to take a place at the bar?”
“Thank you, Elvine. Do you think I would be able to get a private room?” She asked, most politely.
“Of course, but it will cost you.” Elvine replied, smiling widely. “Girls will cost you more again, but you know that don’t you, honey?”
“Just the room will be fine, thank you Elvine.” Sarah was very glad she decided to leave Cracker at home. Apart from his lumpy, scorched head drawing attention, she had just avoided the hassle of shutting him down when he took up the offer of prostitutes. “I am expecting a friend, shortly.”
“Just follow me.” Even in the early afternoon, the Memphis Mafia bar was full of drunks and drug-fucked scum from the quarter and as they snaked their way to the rear, it was clear that most patrons were far more interested in drinking than eating. Not one table had any evidence of a meal having been consumed, not a packet of crisps nor a stray salt crystal. The “private room” was nothing more than a grandiose booth, separated from the rest of the bar with sleazy burgundy velvet drapes. “Here you are, the finest in the house.” Elvine proudly announced as she drew back the ugly curtains with gaudy faux-gold ropes to reveal large U-shaped booth with red leather upholstery and an ornately carved wooden table that managed to gleam, notwithstanding the dim light. 
 
“Wow is that leather? I mean authentic leather?” Sarah faux-gushed, pretending to be genuinely impressed.
“Sure is sweetie. It will even squeak when you sit.” Replied a smiling Elvine.
She wasted no time in being seated, gleefully fidgeting to maximise the squeaks while Elvine rolled her eyes.
“Can I help with any drinks?”
“Thank you Elvine, but no. Not at this stage. I think I will wait for my friend.” Sarah said, politely as possible.
Elvine headed for the bar and Sarah immediately pulled out her palm device, made a quick entry and then began to point it randomly around the room. She then made a beeline to the far corner, patting down the drapes and locating an ancient microphone and transmitter. She then spun and headed to the opposite corner where she was able to locate a small, but equally elderly camera. When she was satisfied the room was clean she squeaked back into the booth. 
Sarah fidgeted, trying to garner some comfort from the cheap, red sinewy cardboard assailing her backside. John Finagle, known to Sarah as Johnny, entered the booth with a look of horror and disgust. Never having been on street level he could not get over the stench or the countenances of the butt-ugly inhabitants of this alternate universe. He was, naturally, totally over-dressed for such a sordid venue – the black leather shoes (quality leather) gleamed; artfully understated dark blue trousers sported knife edge creases and his choice of shirt, tie and jacket complimented and contrasted. 
“Did you get a look at some of the heads out there? It looks like a leper’s grog den. Haven’t they heard of cosmetic surgery? And what is that smell? My God, I am going to have to burn these clothes when I get home.” Johnny was not happy unless he had something to complain about and he was in full whinge-mode. “What is this stuff stuck to my shoes?”
“That would be mud.”
“This is some crappy dive, Sarah. What is so important that you have to drag me here?” Johnny inquired as he took up a seat in the booth, deliberately sitting close, too close to Sarah. She calmly slid away. 
“Have a look at this.” Sarah offered a copper mesh container, under a napkin, to Johnny.
 
“A Faraday-mini! What’s in here then?” Johnny opened the small cage and his eyes immediately widened and as he examined it, a look of pure amazement took over his face. “Holy tax audit! Where did you get this?” 
“I found it.”
“That does not sound likely, my friend. You don’t just find one of these.” Johnny said, punctuating each word with incredulity. 
“It was left in my office after the death of my predecessor. What do you think it is, Johnny?” asked Sarah, transparently deflecting his question.
“I have never seen one, only heard of them. It’s a Gyges Disc. They provide a cloak of invisibility to all electronic surveillance. Cameras, microphones don’t see or hear you. It’s a hall-pass for every building in the Primary Trade Dominion – you could really get yourself in trouble with one of these, Sarah.”
“Yes, discretion is everything.” She threatened. 
“If that thing is working, there was no need to drag me to this disgusting excuse for a bar, all surveillance in a fifty metre radius will be out of action.” 
“What about the cage?”
 
“A Faraday cage will do no good, I’ve heard they pick up on gravity waves. The only way I can think to render it inert is to spin it, very fast. The acceleration at the out edge should overcome the influence of gravity waves.” He projected confidently.
“Do they cancel each other out?” Sarah inquired. 
“Something like that.” He really had no idea how the disc operated, but hubris insisted he puff out his chest and display full plumage. “My pension fund runneth over! Look, I think it has bio-components and if I am not mistaken this is a bio-quantum processor – I have to get this back to my lab.” 
 
Sarah did not fancy the idea of either Johnny or Cracker with the keys to the kingdom in hand. No decent girl would be safe in her bed! As an adolescent she had been schooled in the world of men, exposed to the awful truth when successfully deflecting her very first date-rape. Since that sobering revelation, she had witnessed precious little to overturn the view that “men are just selfish oafs groping, slobbering, farting and ejaculating at will” leaving a swathe of destruction in their paths. She and the rest of female kind were eternally dumbfounded that males are ignorant of their own malevolence or, worse still, men are aware of it and it simply does not bother them. Johnny, she had to admit, had never given her reason to distrust, but she figured, it was just a matter of time before his true colours emerged.
“No. I don’t think this should be in the hands of a man, any man.” She said with a hint of sarcasm. 
“Overt sexism from you? I never would have thought, Sarah. Have I ever behaved in any manner other than a gentleman?” Johnny waited for Sarah to answer, but found himself answering for her, “No, No I haven’t.”
“It’s not sexism if a woman says it.” She quoted the widely acknowledged truth. Doesn’t he know that? “What are you going to do with it? What tests?” 
“I don’t know exactly, but the first thing is to get this device spinning. I don’t suppose you have a centrifuge on you?” Johnny let out a small, self-conscious laugh, hoping Sarah might too. 
“No, no centrifuge.” She forced a vague smile, but there was no laugh. Cracker is funnier.
“No problems.” He poked his head out of the kitsch curtain configuration and managed to get the attention of Elvine. 
“Be polite.” Sarah warned.
“To an L.1 waitress? You are kidding of course.” He said with the confidence of the uninitiated. 
“Yes, darlin’ how can I help you?” Elvine asked courteously.
“Give me a small cordless stick blender or food processor. Now.” Johnny demanded.
“This is a bar and restaurant, sir, not an appliance store.” Elvine retorted.
“There will be one behind the bar. Go get it.” He ordered, not even making eye-contact.
“Yes, sir.” She spun around and headed in the direction of the bar.
“She took that pretty well. I was told they are not fond of the impolite.” Sarah was most surprised. The way Cracker had explained it to her, you would find yourself in a very dangerous place if you are discourteous to the Elvi.
“She knows how to take orders from her superiors.” He smiled back. There was no way he would suck-up to a fucking beverage delivery system, his dignitas demanded otherwise.
Elvine returned in double quick time, carrying a old glass blender. The glass ‘jug’ was in her right hand and the small base in her left. 
“I will just need the base. You know, the motor.” Johnny said, sticking his hand out, again no eye contact.
“You will get both.” Elvine brought down the jug onto Johnny’s head, the glass shattering and slicing into his arrogant baldness. Blood raced out across his skull, then in-between a mash of interwoven fingers that failed to stem the flow. He collapsed and without a sound, he curled up in a ball on the sticky red carpet.
“You will not leave until you pay for the damage and the cleaning bill.” She placed a small slip of poly-paper on the table, whirled around and paced angrily to the bar, this time chatting sternly to a security guard who immediately headed in their direction. Sarah snatched the disc, depositing it inside her brassiere, the metal warming her supple skin. She then considered Johnny who was now motionless, barely conscious and haemorrhaging badly. She would defend him from further damage, his poor behaviour notwithstanding. 
She would be ready when the bouncer arrived and he would be in for the shock of his life, however, he seemed to take an age to arrive. She poked her head out again and saw that a tall, broad shouldered young man, face concealed by a black hooded jacket, was in an animated discussion with the absentee bouncer. She was unable to glean any of the conversation but arms flapped about, fingers poked and wagged, middle digits were unleashed, but shortly they appeared to arrive at some accommodation. The two parted ways, the black hood headed towards the private booths and presented himself before Sarah, revealing a burnt and swollen face.
“Cracker!” She laughed, noting that, in identifying himself, he suddenly seemed shorter and generally less impressive. “What are you doing here?”
“Saving your behind, again.” That’s twice. She’s gotta at least give me a hand-crank now.
“Thank the laws of economics you are here. What did you tell him?”
“That you would pay double the bill.” He low-balled the figure to see how she reacted.
“Double? That seems excessive.” She didn’t really care, just as long as they got out of there without any further trouble. She crouched next to Johnny and firmly placed a cushion over the gash in his crown and lashed it down using his stylish tie as a chin strap. Double the bill?
“Well they did not see it that way at all. So we ended up settling on three times the bill.” He decided to come clean.
“Remind me never to let you negotiate for me again.” She was almost smiling, but lugging Johnny-boy out was proving to be difficult, but Cracker pitched in and they slowly made the journey to the exit, settling the bill on the way. “Could we not have just paid 100%?” Sarah seemed a little puzzled.
“Yes, but that comes with a complimentary beating.” Cracker informed her. “They near kill you.”
“Not me” Sarah said with some degree of confidence. “I might have been able to give them a surprise.”
“Yes, but that would have drawn some unwanted attention as well.” 
“Maybe a little.” She giggled with pride. She could not imagine living in fear, enduring a pathetic, cowering, shadow of an existence, like the many wasted lives feminine kind had suffered through the ages. She had many times pondered what the crushing oppression would have done to her. What sort of a person would she have become? She figured women of her ilk were especially hated and hunted down as witches. Yes, I would have been burned alive.
“I think you would have attracted way too much attention if you cleaned up that bouncer. He was fucking huge.” He was not kidding.
 
“You are right of course, but at least we have the disc. We should not appear on any security logs anywhere along the way.”
“Where to now?” Cracker asked.
“It’ll have to be your apartment. We can’t take him to a Medical Centre around here and we won’t be able to get anywhere near the Management District without questions being asked. No-one else can be present when he wakes, there’s no telling what he will say. Chances are he won’t be too discreet, especially when he’s in pain.” Johnny was not one to bare discomfort in silence. He could scarcely handle a pin prick without moaning about it for weeks. His current soul destroying malady came in the form of a mild case of contact dermatitis, but you could be forgiven for thinking he was suffering from necrotic chancres on his ball bag, such were the unending complaints. 
Dragging the semi-responsive, bleeding carcass of Johnny was hard work and with no taxis willing to take a half-dead customer, they had no choice but to chance the travelator across town and through The Pale. The implementation of the travelator, a closed circle of small box cars connected front to tail on a prehistoric tram track, was greeted by many as a great step in servicing the transport needs of the lows. The 37 kilometre ring of train carriages perpetually circumnavigated the city at brisk walking pace and for a small fee anyone could jump on and off at any time as it meandered through some of the shittiest sites in the all the dominion. 
The Travelator, one of the last investments in public transport infrastructure, however, proved to be an archetypal demonstration of chaos theory in action - the best laid plans of men immediately turning to shit. Directly upon the first wheel hitting the track, homeless families, criminal gangs and opportunists of every variety moved in, taking over many of the cars and fiercely defending their territories. Carriages became wandering brothels, drug dens and mobile homes for the lucky homeless who managed to stake a claim. Subsequently, for those idiotic enough to make the attempt, the cost of a ride was dependent entirely on which car you jumped up upon. Cracker had never taken a ride but, the word among his contemporaries was to address the Travelatorisi as you would a king and they may only seek to fleece you. He knew with certainty, however, that some would pretty much kill you no matter what. At a glance there was no way of telling which car was the right one for you, but as each slowly clicked by, an obvious scent of rotting flesh and a swarm of blow-flies was a rank clue to give that one a miss. Travelatorisi stood at entrance ramps touting for business with promises of a cheap ride, your safety their primary concern, while others attracted fresh prey with gaudy colours, flashing lights and loud music. Sometimes they snatched-up hapless pedestrians that had strayed too close to the tracks, demand payment for the ride before push them off, occasionally still alive.
“We have to just pick one and hope for the best. Only the desperate and the stupid take this ride and at this point I think we qualify as both.” Cracker told Sarah as they approached the track. “You get on first and then pull him up, as I push.”
Sarah seamlessly sashayed from the grimy cement onto the entrance ramp and began dragging Johnny up by his coat, Cracker pushing at his flabby backside with sufficient force to knock Sarah on her back and throw Johnny on top. 
“Geez, smooth operator, isn’t he?” Cracker laughed as Sarah pushed and squirmed to extricate herself from under the dead weight. Rather than being met by raised voices and gun muzzles, they were relieved the only other occupants, three men wearing hooded cloaks drawn in at the waist with cord, were seated quietly in the corner and barely seemed to notice their arrival. Cracker rightly assumed they were the resident travelatorisi, but most atypical. Silent still, the elder of the three slowly raised his eyes and momentarily reflected upon the trio before returning his gaze to his feet, hood concealing his face.
“Travelatorisi, we seek safe passage through the Pale and wish to pay tribute.” Cracker gingerly commenced. 
“In the name of science and reason, who are you to receive such munificence?” Inquired the elder calmly, serenely. 
“I am Cracker of the Hardy clan, wise one. I am escorting my friends to safety, but as you see, one is wounded, I think badly, great Travelatorisi.” He thought he might be laying it on a little thick, but it seemed to be working.
“You are most humble come forward and let me have a look at you. What has entropy brought to me? A pretty girl and two crash victims?” said the elder. “Tell me traveller, do you flee justice or do you seek it?”
“We seek justice.” Cracker replied, hoping it was the right answer.
“Then you shall have safe passage. The protection of The Dawkins shall be yours” He stood up and hugged Cracker lightly and then Sarah, tightly, kissing her on both cheeks. “I am Kleobou and these are my apprentices, Silo and Chilo.” They both nodded, but remained silent. “It seems this one is in need of assistance.” He glanced at his two apprentices and immediately they were kneeling either side of Johnny, gently removing the cushion from his head. 
“Silo and Chilo will clean his wounds and we shall talk.” Kleobou smiled widely, exposing a set of stained teeth and motioned them to a cluster of wooden chairs and a small table in the corner, one small bulb swinging above. They sat and Kleobou offered wine which they both accepted, against their better judgment. Cracker and Sarah were offered small wooden cups, but Kleo, his preferred name he explained, gulped the red liquid from a golden bowl. Cracker followed suit and when Sarah was convinced the offering was neither drugged nor poisoned, she sipped lightly and finding herself pleasantly surprised by the taste, quaffed half the cup down. 
“This is a really quite an acceptable vintage. Thank you.” Sarah smiled brightly, face glowing. “Shiraz?”
“It seems you know grapes.” Her response to the wine had confirmed what he already knew. “You are not of the third estate. How do you find yourself here, young miss?” He gently gazed into her eyes, penetrating deeply but causing her no alarm or unease. To the contrary she felt awash with a warm tranquility. 
“I could claim a set of unforeseen and unfortunate circumstances, but I have only myself to blame.” She confessed. She did not know why she implicitly trusted this fellow, but she felt a compulsion to unburden her soul and tell him whatever he wanted to know. Alarm bells in her head rang silent.
“We are here because fuck-knuckle over there can’t play nice with the plebeians.” Cracker insisted on pointing out. 
“I put Johnny in an alien situation and I should have known better. It was his first time at street level.” She insisted. “It was never going to end well.”
“Do not desire the impossible, Sarah. Your friend is a product of his environment, he could not have acted any differently.” Kleo counseled.
“But what of my upbringing? I have the same pedigree.” She knew she was divulging far too much information, but it felt good. 
“You are different, but you already know that, don’t you?” He spoke with such a degree of certainty that Sarah found herself compelled to listen. “You are unique, you will do great things if you use your intelligence, but follow your heart. Don’t be duped by your conditioning, your world is not as it seems, the structures holding it fast are made of chalk, not steel.”
“Aren’t you supposed to be staring into a crystal ball or magic fire or something?” Cracker was in no doubt that this was a load of crap. What would some idiot hiding out in a train carriage know about life? This is not a Himalayan cave.
“I see you are a skeptic, young man. Let me look at you. You claim membership of the great Hardy clan?” Kleo said, now staring into Cracker’s pupils, but young Hardy did not feel warm and fuzzy, but violated. It felt like Kleo was somewhere deep in his brain, poking about and periodically interrogating neurons. “You are of the Hardy clan! Perhaps even a descendant of Jamison Hardy himself, if I am not mistaken”
“Who?” Cracker did not know.
“The last man to try.” Kleo said cryptically, a hint of sadness seeping out. “He rejected violence and walked the path of peace and love.”
“What a pussy.” Cracker blurted out without thinking. 
“He once stood as the only man that could stop that.” Kleo pointed out the entrance of the carriage and into the dim daylight. “The Primary Trade Dominion. Jamison Hardy sought to understand his fellow man, to pick him up not stomp him into the mud. He was no pussy, young Hardy. I thought he was the one-after-the-one.”
“The one-after-the-one.” Silo and Chilo chimed in unison.
“My apologies, Kleo.” Cracker’s apology was genuine, but he still did not believe the Jamison Hardy rubbish. “I find it hards to accept you can tell anything, just by looking in my eyes.”
“Your eyes are virtually identical. Colour, pattern, right down to the red fleck below the left pupil.” Kleo quietly insisted.
“Did you know him?” Sarah was now quite intrigued.
“I had the honour of being in his employ for many years. How is the patient, Silo?”
“We have bound the wound on his head, but he is concussed. I don’t think there is any bleeding or swelling to the brain. His pupils are the same size and reactive to light. That is all we can do, Master. I expect he will recover in a few days.” Solo replied, reverently bowing his head.
“I have heard the legend of Jamison Hardy, but I thought he was an urban myth.” Sarah said, drawing from distant memory. “If I recall correctly it was a cautionary tale. Jamison Hardy, traitor to capitalism.” 
 
“His memory is much maligned. He was a true man of the people, so much so he, too, was caught up in the Great Viral Cleansing and died with the masses.” Kleo recalled with some obvious distress. “We are approaching The Pale.” Silo and Chilo set to work locking doors and shutters, double checking each other’s work. “We take on no passengers in The Pale it is far too dangerous, but we will be safe enough in here. Well my new friends we should be through the worst of it in about an hour.” </Text>
            <Notes>The meeting with Johnny could be folded into The Vegas show

The can’t take Johhny to Cracker’s apartment

The travelator is a fun idea. Research problems with light rail and monorail. BUT, the introduction of it as a plot device is weak.

The Elvi Mafia can save Cracker and Sarah from Agent Jones</Notes>
