        <Document ID="05687B8A-C1C7-4088-B9E4-A99BCC405737">
            <Title>A Deeper Mystery</Title>
            <Synopsis>Cracker and Sarah find out Emil Kalheri had been there.  (Dr Logan bills the anonymous benefactor who paid for the processing of Emil Kalheri's body.)</Synopsis>
            <Text>A Deeper Mystery
8 to 10 days after Bombing

“What? I am not the mother and he is not the father. How can this be?” Sarah was trying to comprehend what she was hearing, but this was really testing the limits of credulity.
“You and Cracker have no DNA in common with the child. According to the data base we have records of many people, male and female, that possess parts of the DNA sequence. I can superimpose the strands over one another and you can see the points of similarity. It seems that many of them have been dead for years and those guys possess more DNA in common with the child than the ones still alive. I think that should be the other way around. At a guess I would say that they are all distantly related and have a common ancestor a few generations back. I will have to do some manual research, maybe even look in some books. This is exciting!  See, look, I can construct almost the entirety of the helix from the common genes of our list of candidates here. There is just a little bit that is different, but I think there are more than sufficient points of commonality to locate the ancestor. I can reconstruct a family tree using the genetic information.”
“Can’t you chuck the names through Ancestor-Link? Cracker joked.
“We do use those resources when absolutely necessary, but prefer to rely on the genetic information. Family trees lie, science does not. On this occasion, however, it’s a great idea my friend. An extra patch for you tonight. Okay let me get these names on the link and see what turns up.”
* * *</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="97510A05-3118-4C43-996C-2C19073BC6E0">
            <Title>The Elvi</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="52EC2DAE-1789-4392-9A3B-89903DB78230">
            <Title>Just a Few Questions</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="98766BD0-B1CB-4D80-ACCF-2D8B0893D66B">
            <Title>Vershlingbessern</Title>
            <Synopsis>Bad to Worse</Synopsis>
            <Text>When Donald Dunning-Kruger was president of the United Free States of Atlantia, and Vietnesian President Sucarn served words of peace and goodwill, with a side-salad of excuses and caveats:
“As part of our peace-keeping initiatives in our southern territorial waters, a small flotilla of our craft regrettably ran aground on the shores of Ulyssia. I assure the good people of our dearest neighbour, our intentions are peaceful. In the interest of bilateral relations, especially the safety of both peoples, we accept our unfortunate mistake, and will rescue our service folk on conclusion of exercises.”
The small flotilla, it transpired, represented a significant, specialised chunk of the Vietnesian Navy, all trained in the art of lifting gold, uranium and iron ore from whichever sovereign soil they happened upon running aground.
After receiving intelligence, such as it was, of the Vietnesian boosting Ulyssian natural resources, the Prime Minister of Ulyssia, the Right Honorable Spot Muddleham, realised he must invoke treaty, and contact, “that moron Dunning-Kruger.”
Muddleham knew he was inviting the devil to dinner, but he had no option. And so he crafted words of diplomacy for the Atlantian ear: “Mr President, We are in deep shit here. Can you bail us out?” 
Dunning-Kruger exploited many moments of tension before he spoke, or rather, it took a fucking eon for him to reply with, “We of the UFSA are mindful of our friends in the south.” He spoke slowly, reinforcing the rumour he recited everything from a tele-prompter. “I am also mindful of my responsibilities to my fellow Atlantians.”
Muddleham scratched his balding melon. “You’re saying you can’t afford it.”
A flash of animal anger ignited the wispy rendering of Dunning-Kruger’s soft, entitled face as it hovered on the other side of Muddleham desk. Then, after another interminable wait, in which rumours flourished that Dunning-Kruger’s SecDef vets all his utterances, he said, “We are currently fighting infractions in ten theatres.”
You always bankroll the losers, Muddleham thought, but held his tongue.
“Resources have their limits, Mr Prime Minister.” 
“Mr President, two million foreign troops have foothold on our soil, occupying our gold fields, uranium mines and iron ore deposits, plundering our resources.”
Dunning-Kruger pursed his lips in an excessively sensual pout and shrugged.
“Our economy will be ruined,” Muddleham said, as if explaining the alphabet to a child. We will not be able to pay off our loans, Mr President.” He knew this would get his attention. Although they habitually neglected to pay their own bills, holding fast to belief in their exceptionalism, Atlantians despised states that did not pay their bills. 
“You guys owe us 3 trillion as it is.” Kruger, soft as his head was, did not like to be stiffed.
“This is an intolerable situation and we do not have the resources to repel them. Our great peoples have fought and died alongside each other for close to two centuries. Where is your sense of history? Where is your sense of honour?”
Raising the concept of “honour” in diplomacy struck Kruger as immensely amusing and he could barely choke down the urge to laugh hysterically. SecDef and Sec State were not able to display the same degree of self-discipline and spent the next several minutes cackling on the floor. 
When he was finally able, the President choked out, “Honour costs money. I can’t afford honour. Honour is the rallying cry of losers. As for history, we make history. We are not slaves to it. Any history we do not like, we re-write. In any case, where are your defence forces, Mr Prime Minister?” 
“Well, Mr President, most of them are fighting anti-Semites, in all ten theaters ” Don’t you remember? You fucking idiot!
“You’ve only given us one million of your troops – where are the rest?” Inquired the ignoramus.
“That’s pretty much it. We’ve got reservists and retirees, but no front line troops. Relocating troops currently deployed will take time. Time is something we do not have.”
“You sent all of your troops offshore? Are you insane?” Questioned the inane President.
“It was in response to your ‘Plea to the Free World’ speech – you know, ‘If you’re not with us you’re against us’. It scared the shit out of us. We asked how many and you said one million, no less.”
President Kruger was pleased. Fear is power! He loved the idea of scaring the entire planet with a one-liner. He’d have to promote his speech writer - If you’re not with us you’re against us. He giggled again. “It was supposed to scare you into action. We seem to be the only defender of freedom and democracy on this planet.”
“We have been alongside you all of the way.”
“You’re contribution is token at best, Prime Minister. We pay the bills.”
“Yeah and you take all the loot.”
 “Yes, we do. What do these people want anyway? Free Trade? A seat on the League of Nations Security Council? Cheaper loans? What?” Asked the President, directing the conversation away from his country’s well-documented history of pillaging and plundering.
 
“They say they are running war-games, but they mostly want to punish us for intellectual property infringements. They say they want us to change the way we do business. They want free trade agreements to be adhered to.”
“Agreements adhered to? Don’t you guys know how to write an exit clause? If we can’t renege on a deal, we don’t sign.” These Ulyssians are like children. No wonder their economy sucks.
“It’s also a good excuse to steal our gold, uranium and iron ore. While we sit on our backsides, they are stealing my country. They’re just thieves like the rest of us and we cannot ignore them or their demands. Basically they want, you know, fair and equitable treatment in the future.” 
Fair and equitable treatment between trading partners was an alien concept to those involved in commerce. It was inconceivable to build an economic powerhouse on evenhanded treatment of others. Taking advantage of weaker nations was (and is) the foundation of international business. A high living standard for a few in one location, by definition, insists that there are low standards for many somewhere else. Those suckers dumb enough to be born in poor nations deserve exploitation, and by remaining vulnerable, invite yet more exploitation. It was simply a fact of life.
Dunning Kruger found himself entirely bemused at the nativity of the Vietnesians. Are they that unsophisticated? “Mr Prime Minister, I am told by my Defence Secretary that there may be something we can do for you. I don’t have all the details yet, I’ll get back to you as soon as I have them. It will comply with our obligations under the Nuclear Disarmament Treaty, but, whatever happens, however, there will be no troops sent from here” 
“I don’t care about details. If you can help, just do it.” He hung up the phone and breathed a sigh of relief. Scratched his head and then stopped dead in his tracks. Huh? Nuclear Disarmament Treaty?
Twenty-seven minutes later, in excess of 100 nuclear missiles air-burst over the Vietnesian positions and in the following ten microseconds two million Vietnesians were vaporized into plasma smoke and radioactive gas. In the process, 80% of arable land in Western Ulyssia was annihilated. After irradiating the entire mineral reserves of a small continent, President Kruger called it a good day, lit a cigar and poured himself a scotch. Nothing was left of the invasion and nothing much was left of the countryside. A glowing moonscape was left to fester and expand. There was nothing worth invading now.
- - -
“Jesus Christ, Dunning, what the hell did you think you were doing? Nuking my country!” A somewhat irate Prime Minister screamed down the hot line. 
“You said and I quote – ‘I don’t care about details. If you can help, just do it.’ I have it recorded. Turn on your vid-screen, it’s on WWNN right now,” he replied, barely able to stop himself from laughing. The Defence Secretary and the National Security adviser were again on the floor, wetting themselves. No end of amusement at that end of the phone.
“Did you seriously think that this would help?” queried the PM.
“We had to do something about complying with our Nuclear Disarmament Treaty. We had to dismantle them, so we figured we might as well deploy them.”
“How does that comply with the fucking treaty? You are supposed to dismantle them!”
“I dismantled them very quickly and into many, many small pieces. What are you complaining about anyway? Do you currently have an invading army on your doorstep?”
“No, but half of my country glows in the dark.”
“Be that as it may, those nukes come at a price. Your little shit-hole country owes the Union of Capitalist Atlantic States 6 trillion. In Atlantian dollars, not your southern fucking pesos! There is no need to thank us, just pay up. While you are at it you can pay the 3 trillion you already owe.” 
“What will you do if we don’t pay? Nuke us?”
“Our response will be proportionate to your debt.” He knew this would frighten him. He had wiped out entire countries for less.
“You’ll get your money. You’ll get your money.” Even as he terminated the call, the PM knew his career was over. Nothing could save him now. He’d be lucky to live until the next election and he knew was not that lucky.
Dunning and the crew were now backslapping and congratulating each other. Another customer satisfied and another diplomatic crisis solved. “Man, one day we are going to have to take this shit seriously!” Teased the president. 
“Nine trillion in one day. The treasury is going to love you, Mr. President. Yours will be remembered as the most fiscally responsible administration in history. Your policies are epoch making,” said SecDef, sucking up to the chief.
“Do you ever stop brown-nosing? For the last time, you will not get the nomination next term.”
“Oh, but..”
“No buts. There is no way this country is ever going to vote for President Mohamed Goldberg. Go plan your next war.” He pointed to the door and both of the cabinet members left. “Everyone wants my job. Now, I wonder if they will really pay up.” Epoch making? What the hell does that mean?
Pay up they did. It amused the Prime Minister to deliver the payment in gold bullion, all from the glowing west, radioactive and useless for several hundred thousand years. 
Following the nuclear strike on their lands, the Provincial Government of Western Ulyssia withdrew from the Federation and called the new land the Western Liberated Dominion. It sounded pretty cool, but the Western Liberated Dominion was nothing more than a nuclear landfill with a couple of unaffected cities. They would never be an economic force again. They could have their pathetic little Nation-State. No one in the east cared.
The political ramifications for the PM were ugly and immediate. Outraged citizens and their pitiful street demonstrations were simply ignored, but the corporate sector could not be so readily disregarded. Any company that owned businesses in the Western Liberated Dominion, were screaming for blood. The Prime Minister’s political stocks plummeted and the party invoked a time honoured tradition of assassination – stabbed to death in the chamber of the lower house. As he lay dying he could not help but think of Caesar. Parallel lives, he ridiculously thought. With his last breath he wheezed, “Et tu Brute?” 
The new Prime Minister stood over the body and mocked, “Et tu, Brute? Et tu, Fucking Brute! Who the fuck did he think he was?” Slamming his boot into the emotionless death mask he said, “Oh well, at least he didn’t say ‘Rosebud’.” Some of the older party members were mildly amused, but others could not place the reference, being more concerned with how they were going to get the blood off their shoes before the press conference.
Riding on a wave of anti-Atlantian sentiment, the new regime became tough, adopting an isolationist policy, combining the geniality of communist regimes with the human compassion of a military junta. Ulyssia, a country now split in two, was renamed the Primary Trade Dominion and the economy, previously based upon consumer goods and service industries, would now focus on the manufacture of arms. How hard could it be to sell weapons? Any idiot could do it. The trick was staying under the radar so you could sell to all sides in a conflict. Better still, create the conflict, having first arranged the contracts. Everyone else sold weapons that way and everyone knew it, but it was bad form to be caught unloading them, particularly when the recipient is an enemy of an ally. It was the biggest market and needing money desperately, the PTD became the most prosperous war-monger in history. They sold to anyone and everyone and siphoned up cash from all comers.
It took only a few short years for the radiation zone in the Western Liberated Dominion to expand and engulf urban centres. Terrified citizens fled to the east, to where it was said to be safe from the rads. There were millions of them, mostly from the Western Liberated Dominion, but a few malformed Vietnesians made the trek as well. They had escaped the radiation storms with nothing but their lives and a few meagre possessions. Any prior land ownership was meaningless. The wealth they had created and the trinkets they had owned were now throbbing with radioactive isotopes of iodine, caesium, strontium, thorium, xenon and barium.
As the trickle of radioactive refugees became a flood, PTD cities began to bulge and then burst from the pressure. The wretched throngs kept coming in massive human waves, ever-increasing, laying wast to vast areas of urban sprawl. The only saleable resource they possessed was their labour, gleefully exploited by local business, happy to see the return of slavery. The workers were used, abused and discarded. In traditional blue-collar work there were no protective labour laws and no benefits. Thanks for coming, champ. Sorry about the severed limb, but we do have a two-arm policy here. I think you will find the exit is behind you.
There were a few benevolent souls who, against all good sense, actually assisted the refugees. One stood out head and shoulders above the rest. Jameson Hardy was the wealthiest man in all of Old Ulyssia (some said the world), but to his peers, he was the most eccentric of men. Had he been poor, he would have been merely regarded as insane, but he controlled the finance and resources sector, manufacturing, media, ship yards, universities, hospitals, pharmaceuticals, schools, utilities, multi-national conglomerates and, much to the amusement of the boys at "The Club", he also delved in charitable foundations, training schemes and general tree-hugging, hippy non-sense. Of course, there were hardly any trees left to hug, but the term was now synonymous with any unworthy left wing activity. 
He poured obscene amounts of money into apparently useless research. His interest in things medical was peculiar in that it was focused on epidemiology and genetic diversity amongst “the common”, level 1 and level 2 citizens. Very strange indeed - Who cares if “the common” get Ebola? Who cares if they are all inbred? They need to be culled back a bit anyway. 
All in all, Jamison Hardy was an oddity, some sort of throwback, who somehow came to the conclusion that helping others enriched your life. What was he playing at? Where was the profit? Can you still claim that sort of thing on tax? In fact there was no profit in helping innocent victims of war. Jameson Hardy knew he would never get a return on this investment. Apparently, shocking as it seemed, he was on a humanitarian mission. He was a Dutch boy inserting his digit into a dyke holding back a deluge of human misery. He knew that eventually the walls would fail and a flood of pain would follow. Fatalistic or not, he was going to relieve the suffering of as many as he could before cataclysm engulfed them. He purchased, from the filthy throngs, every parcel of land he could. He purchased millions of hectares - all radioactive and uninhabitable. While the vendors were eternally grateful for the money, it was insufficient to purchase comparable lodgings in the swelling metropolis. Demand for housing far-outstretched supply, causing a price surge. Anyone with a proprietary interest in land rubbed their hands together with delight.
Jameson embarked upon unprecedented building programs employing hundreds of thousands of workers, refugees all. Toiling against time, they constructed housing estates, hundreds of stories tall, with Spartan, but clean facilities. Each gargantuan structure was designed to house 30,000, but eventually squeezed in 100,000 with the introduction of time share and sleeping drawers. 
The popularity of time-share, the equivalent of camping in the city, was astonishing. Some chose to time-share an entire house or flat for a fixed period (up to 6 months), preferring to spend an equivalent time on the streets rather than permanently living in a drawer. Although they were not officially called drawers, "Discreet Living Capsules" were nothing more than suffocating coffins. They were relatively cheap and provided security when in repose, but sharing bathroom facilities necessarily involved running the gauntlet through a permanent gaggle of rapists and paedophiles. They were harmless compared to ‘Gazumpers’ who would perpetually circulate dorms looking for unlocked drawers, hoping to spend a few unconscious hours in comfort and at someone else's expense. These people rarely had the opportunity to bath and being gazumped meant disinfecting the whole drawer, including the bedding and any personal other items that were, for some reason, not stolen.
Forlorn home-owners riddled with debt, sold off or leased 8 to 12 hours access a day to their humble abodes. Usually shift workers would take the opportunity to get a few hours’ sleep, a meal and a shower before going back to the grind. These marriages of convenience were the source of a great deal of unrest, but in stark contrast to life on the streets, disputes rarely ended in death or loss of limb. In the ultimate housing boom, housing sales management became a most lucrative vocation and owning an Accommodation Directorate franchise was comparable to printing money. Realtors joined the new elite and property ownership became the symbol of social status and power. 
When the plague came Jameson Hardy disappeared, presumed dead. The refugees from the west mourned his death loud and long. Many had been given the chance to grow into adults, some of which did not live entirely miserable lives. The evil epidemic, which was said to have come from Asia (most suspected Vietnesia) was swift, smiting 40% of the population before the disease and the rioting dissipated. Even though it could be proven the bug was a genetically modified virus, the plague was blamed on the immigrants in Jameson Hardy's towering cesspools. 
Although a huge swath of the population had been cut down, there were still plenty of street-sleepers and gazumpers to go around. In fact, a disproportionate amount of “the common” had survived the plague. Was it luck or natural immunity? Very odd, indeed, but no-one bothered to find out why this was so. Fuck the poor.
The property owners and accommodation directorates got down to the business of perpetuating a housing crisis. Real Estate moguls properly thanked a non-existent god that there was still enough demand to apply upward pressure on prices. Many apartment blocks were destroyed or condemned in order to maintain a shortage. People could go ahead and die en-masse, but property values could not fall.
</Text>
            <Notes>He scratched his balding melon and simply said

Each response compiled as the conversation took place by the SecDef. The Secretary of State hovered over the Sec Def, nodding in agreement at every key stroke. “Fucken brown nose” the Sec Def thought as he typed. 

He squinted and smiled arrogantly</Notes>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="527872CA-87FE-4278-8A63-BF2CFC03BDCC">
            <Title>Fireworm</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="D4F52ED7-7913-41CE-8897-8D9A685A14C9">
            <Title>The Gyges Disc</Title>
            <Text>THE GYGES DISC
14 hours before the bombing
9 months BTD
It took Cracker 35 minutes to get down to the ground floor, some 216 floors below Sarah’s office and 100 floors below the layer. There had been a major delay when an L.5. suit decided to slum it with the workers and got in the service elevator at level 100. Without a word he entered his code and the lift began ascending again, straight back to floor 216. Cracker could smell a mild scent of something burning, hair or skin perhaps, but it dissipated as soon as the L.5 scurried off the lift. Cracker was able to get a glimpse of him entering Sarah’s office before the lift door closed. He figured this guy must be a drama queen. Apart from a suicide, there nothing out of the ordinary on floor 216. 
Upon hitting the pavement, he unconsciously looked up, somehow expecting to see Sarah at her window waving at him, but his view was blocked by the layer of thick cloud and she was not waving at him. She was dealing with the suspicious looking character that just stepped off the service-lift and was presently sleezing around in her office. He was quite tall and seemed to impose himself in her office, rather than merely occupy it and was breathing loudly, too loudly to account for the short run from the elevator. His anxiety, even though he was clearly attempting to keep a lid on it, was nevertheless palpable as he fidgeted and scratched the dark black locks on his distorted angular head. 
“Can I help you?” She accused.
“I am looking for Mr. Lee. He has some files for me to sign.” The dark haired man did not sound convincing, not many document were actually signed anymore. Paper cost too much and it was an extremely inefficient method of data storage. 
“What kind of files?”
“Contracts, torts and legal things, Res ipsa loquitor.”
“Mmm.” She said gravely. “That does not sound very likely. This is the Cyber Security Division. We sometimes deal with legal matters, but never without company lawyers present and please don’t use legal terms you don’t understand. You are going to have to try a bit harder than that.” Contracts and torts and legal things? Res Ipsa Loquitor? How dumb does he think I am? She was always profoundly insulted when men made the mistake of assuming she was as dull as them. “Mr. Lee has passed away. His files will be re-allocated in due course.” She looked at the open doorway but the dark man did not budge.
“I need to see the files now.” He was clearly in a hurry and allowed a little hostility to creep out in an effort to underline that urgency.
“If, indeed, you do have business with this department you will be contacted in due course.” Sarah remained neutral and professional, but was concerned about the true nature of his business with the recently deceased Mr. Lee. “I do not have them and I cannot help you. I think you will find the door is behind you. Good day.”
Wave after wave of white hot rage washed over the dark-haired clown. His infantile ego had been dented and this could not go unanswered. The idea of his agenda being frustrated by some idiotic computer jockey infuriated him and an old mantra began repeating inside his head. “I am not human. I am the biological manifestation of the company’s will. In the name of stability I destabilize. In the name of order I deliver chaos. In the name of life I bring death.” He suddenly charged at her, quickly and malevolently, the nasty scowl upon his face a clear sign of his intent to do harm. She did not move, but stared him down, unflinching as a wry smile began to inch across of her face. As he approached with images of beating the life out of her dancing in his brain, he found he could not raise his arms and fists would not form. He tried for his gun, but it was just too heavy. He became most confused. Was this some sudden rush of morality flooding his body, washing clean the cankered crust of his licentious, black soul? He rejected that idea outright. He thought she must be just too good looking to hit, but that seemed unlikely too. He rather enjoyed messing up a beautiful face. They were just so damned punch-able and the looks of shock and surprise were just priceless. The truth, he seemed unable to recognise or refused to face, was she frightened him. He had, unconsciously, sensed the danger, the truth of this petite girl – that he did not stand a chance against her.
“What happened to all his stuff?” He said, retreating towards to the door.
“The office has been completely cleared out. Company property has been returned, his personal property has been sent home and the rest was thrown out.”
“The cleaners?”
“Well, a cleaner, yes.”
He left without a word, remembering the cleaner on the elevator not 10 minutes prior. Sarah reported the strange man to Security, but she knew that would do no good. Re-runs of “Random Camera L1 - L3” were showing on the link and box-headed security types loved that crap. She hacked into the security system and began downloading surveillance logs herself, but found she had to call on all her hacking magic to reconstitute the files, all damaged and jumbled into white noise. The security system had clearly been compromised and when she finally put Humpty together again, she could not find a trace of the man anywhere. The security footage of the corridor outside her office did not detect this man at all. At no time, it appeared, did this phantom enter her office, her vivid memory of their conversation notwithstanding. 
Normal people, average punters, cannot so easily circumvent high level security systems. He was clearly no ordinary man and the only people who could get their hands on that sort of technology were Intelligence Agents. Was that a gun he was packing? Tax me into poverty! I sent this loon after the cleaner. She knew she should not give a shit about the level 2 garbage collector, she’d only just met him, but she did care and did not know why. A quick check of the security logs revealing Cracker had not yet exited the business district, trebling her concern. Once he was in “The Pale” he would be hard to follow, but he had to get there first.
Cracker was still looking up at the layer, shaking his head and grumbling, “What a fuck-up.” The layer was constant reminder of the line drawn between the lows and upper society. He really hated the layer and what stuck in his craw was the fact that the very people that engineered this blunder were the same people that live above and beyond its rancid reality. Commit a crime against all humanity and receive thanks from a grateful nation. 
Still looking up and cursing the layer, Cracker didn’t notice the dead male on the grubby pitted cement. He tripped over the body of some poor sod, accidentally landing right on top. At close range Cracker noticed the man was wearing an expensive suit and it looked new, but upon second glance his eyes were drawn to a pair of grimy, calloused hands adorned with oddly bent fingers and filthy nails. This was no L.5. Others on street level chose not to notice the dead body in their midst and merely strode an elliptical path around the gristly scene. The crowd un-gathered remarkably fast and while nobody was looking Cracker pocketed anything he could find. 
- - -
Operational support guys, the underlings of the intelligence community, were always working on covert ops and difficult to locate. They were the guys that broke into houses to place covert listening devices, cameras and bombs - they undertook physical surveillance, they did all the jobs no one else wanted to do – in this case they were to collect the body of a blank. They kept an eyeball on their target and watched as he dropped like a stone. Initially they thought him to be shot, but did not see the discharge flare of a particle weapon nor was anyone close enough to have brought him down by other means. He just seemed to lose consciousness and fell where he stood. 
‘That’s our cue. Let’s pick him up” the driver had said. 
“No. Where’s the square head? We can’t pick him up without the green light from him.” The other shot back.
“We are here to pick up a dead body. Does he look dead to you?”
“He’s not moving. Wait a minute, there’s some good Samaritan trying to help.”
“The idiot in the busted overalls? He’s going to fuck this up for us. Let’s roll.”
The van was multi-purpose surveillance rig, currently disguised as a coronial pick-up unit. There were so many dead and dying on the street at any one time, it became necessary for these “meat-wagons” to permanently scour the thoroughfares of the city, scooping up the lifeless before they became too rancid. There were so many, that an extra van floating about would be noticed by no one. The driver kicked the small motor into life and the van slid silently to where the blank lay. 
As Cracker casually rose he was confronted by a Coroner’s meat-wagon driver and his body-snatching off-sider. 
“I was just about to call you guys. I don’t think he’s been dead for long – he’s still warm.” Cracker said in the most sycophantic tone he could muster.
 
“Yeah, yeah. Fuck off, pal” They said in unison.
Cracker took his cue and backed away, joining the throng of mindless automatons and marched towards the security gates. In the Primary Trade Dominion, security guards weren’t known for their high intelligence. Most realised they were going nowhere in life and were happy to get a gig sitting around doing fuck-all most of the time and the rest of the time you got to mess with people’s heads. They were all recruited from level 1 ranks and were nothing in the scheme of things, but when you stepped into their little bailiwick, they were the law. Even though, Cracker was a regular in the business district, businessmen certainly weren’t taking out their own trash, security always made him jump through all the hoops. 
“Place your bag, or sack, is it? On the scanner. Work Visa?” Like everyone else, Cracker did not like imitation cops looking down their noses at him. For that matter he didn’t like real cops acting superior either, but then again, they could shoot you just for the hell of it.
“Yep.”
“Retinal scan or implant?” 
“Tattoo.” Cracker thrust out his left arm, palm up.
“This is old technology. You must up-grade to retinal scanning or get an implant. Can I arrange an appointment for you?” The guard did his best impression of a desperate vacuum cleaner salesman.
“We have this discussion every time. It is way too expensive and I know you are just trying to sell product. Do you get kickbacks? Do you get more for the retinal tweaking or implant surgery?”
“Place your arm in the scanner, sir.” The guard replied with impertinence, managing to alter his inflection and tone so as to alter the meaning of ‘sir’ to ‘turd on the ground.’
“Its implants isn’t it?” Cracker quizzed as he waved his arm over the scanner. “Going under the knife every time there is a new model – the kick-backs must pile up, tax free, I bet.” The guard was tight lipped and more than a little upset. He did not like his integrity questioned, especially when it was plain he had questionable integrity. 
“You can go.” The guard pointed to the exit. “Now.”
“Is it true that they can force a customer to undergo surgery against their will?” Cracker was smiling widely. He knew the stop-go man was rattled.
“It’s a service contract, damn it! They know what they are getting into!” Retorted the red faced gate keeper.
Cracker laughed and wandered slowly through the gargantuan exit gates and into The Pale, a dark ghetto bounded on all sides by office blocks and high class apartment buildings, hundreds of stories high, rising through the layer and making a reasonable bid for outer-space. The Pale was the home of the unwanted and unnecessary human refuse of the great Primary Trade Dominion. The crime rate was unconscionably high and consequently the police rarely made a visit. Law enforcement was engaged in shielding the rich from the poor, not protecting the poor from each other. The poor could eat shit and die. In the Primary Trade Dominion, egalitarians were those who only ate eagle. 
* * *
Another check on the security records showed Cracker had finally made it out of the business district and into the Pale. Relieved, Sarah decided to do a little more digging and spent the rest of the morning interrogating the link and breaking into data-bases, gathering all known information about Tony Hardy. Unusually, however, she found that there was very little information stored about him at all. Most of the entries had been recently deleted, quite a few that very morning. No matter how hard she looked she could uncover almost no information pertaining to Tony (Cracker) Hardy. A birth certificate noting ‘Father Unknown’, some old school records, a business listing for Cracker Jack Cleaning and some fines for drunk and disorderly. That was about it. There were no expenditure analysis records, not one of the many thousands of advertising algorithms had ever sent him any spam and most unusually, there were no records of any malware attacks against him. 
Sarah sat still in silent contemplation, allowing her eyes to fade in and out of focus as she stared out the window, until she sported a blank thousand-yard stare. She mustered all of the processing power of her towering intellect, but drew no firm conclusions. On another day she might have shrugged off the creepy visit by the foul company agent, but he took off after cleaner with a hateful fervor. Something odd was afoot and this Cracker person seemed to be where the lines converged. She had, to this point, managed to elude the guilt of sending an armed intelligence agent after an unarmed cleaner, but it now began to advance in giant ripples of shame and dread. Outwardly, of course, she was ice cool. The legacy of a proper education and damned fine breeding, so she would have you believe. The truth, she never discussed for a number of reasons, was her mother managed to jag a rich boyfriend who had her safely squared away in boarding school the moment he moved in.
When 4.00pm approached she packed up for the day and took a hover-shuttle to the Elite-Management Medical Facility where she gave samples, underwent embarrassing and probing examinations and bombarded with radiation of all types from an army of scanners. She was in perfect health and was approved for promotion to Level 5. It amused her the results noted she was not pregnant. She had no intention of becoming a mother any time soon.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="6FDCB7F6-8387-487F-9AFE-F59C162A6753">
            <Title>Sarah Jacobson</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="8C8797E1-0AAC-468D-98A1-E77F829C444B">
            <Title>Pre-emptive Reciprocity</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="B6D19823-F51D-44BE-9D02-ADBFBC3929D8">
            <Title>Some Weeks After the Bombing</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="F3EF34C8-06C8-40C6-A05B-9427F8333653">
            <Title>The High Elvis</Title>
            <Synopsis>The Elvi pick a leader "The High Elvis" (in honour of the real Elvis' love of amphetamine) 
The High Elvis makes his inauguration speech and sings a song - The proposals in the speech are a first draft. I am sure you can think up something better.
5 Elvi have fits and they discover they are possessed by the jesus - I like this bit as there is a parody exorcism in a later chapter after which Kleo finds himself in heaven. We can put Kleo in heaven after the book ends. An epilog?</Synopsis>
            <Text>The High Elvis
3 weeks to 2 months after Bombing
The unprecedented speed of their success necessitated the Elvi to organise into the Federation of Elvisian Enterprises. Being genetically identical, the Elvi readily adopted the concept of the equality amongst their number, however, self-organisation necessitated a hierarchical structure that did not sit well with the rank and file and many an argument broke out, each side able to hijack quotes of The Dawkins to support their claims. Selection of the Elvi leader, was fast-tracked, the entire process taking only days of campaigning and deal making and few rounds of voting until, obvious front-runner Elvis 457, was proclaimed leader. He immediately set off another lengthy set of arguments and enthusiastic karate fights when he proposed that the leader, that he, be referred to as ‘The King’. Every Elvis had dibs on the title of “The King” and no individual would be permitted to appropriate it for themselves. They finally settled on “The High Elvis”, which was entirely accurate because Elvis 457 really loved his amphetamines. As did they all.
“Praise be to the Dawkins.” The first leader of the Elvi commenced his inaugural speech before a crowd of every Elvi in the Dominion. “As your first President, I am all shook up by the honour you bestow upon me. My hands are shaking and my knees are weak from the thought of a such a fool I, being gifted this pocket full of rainbows. I have a burning love for all of us in the ghetto. Suspicious minds may think I have taken this role to be the big boss man, but you are always on my mind and I love you so. As we travel along the long Jericho road, cross my heart and hope to die, we can make the morning and you’ll never walk alone. What we need is a little less conversation and a little more action so I washed my hands in muddy waters and they came out clean. It is no secret its a wonderful world and you’re the reason I’m living. If you give me all of your love, I’ll never stand in your way and there will be peace in the valley. I am pledging my love and let me be the one, but only the strong survive so please don’t stop loving me.” He paused and the adoration of the Elvi spilled over into deafening applause and cheers. 
“The blessed mother carries the great one as we speak. As the arrival of the one-after-the-one approaches, brothers and sisters, we must prepare. We must also ready ourselves for the arrival of many thousands, tens of thousands of our overseas cousins at the World Elvis Convention later this year. For both of these wondrous events I propose changing the face of The Quarter. We will make our home a place where families can visit without fear of robbery, rape or murder.” The vast mass of Elvi rose to their feet, clapping enthusiastically waving bright banners and whistling their new leader.
“We must take our corner of the Primary Trade Dominion and transform it into a shining example of what can be achieved when the Elvi put their heads together. Look around you my fellow Elvi, the ground you stand upon is a rotting sludge of dirt, garbage and shit. We are all forced to live in this festering cesspool of filth and crime. We have been served up nothing but contempt, but we will rise above it all.” A silent pause as the High Elvis looked over the crowd of adoring Elvi. “For the first time many decades we will revive communities, we will give people some pride and offer more than mere hope. The first proposal I have is for the beautification of the Pale.” A cacophony of laughter erupted. “Yes, my Elvi. We are going to clean this place up, starting with old fashioned collecting of rubbish. Then we will scrub the footpaths, hose down the streets, shovel up the shit and make this place gleam. We will fix the drainage, water pipes and sewers.” 
“You are joking.” Elvis 3000 yelled out. “We are musicians, not street sweepers.”
“We are much, much more. We are one hundred thousand strong and together in we can achieve anything. We will not need expensive machinery, our overwhelming numbers will effect change. How can anyone in The Pale ever get ahead when they live in the crushing poverty of this malevolent town? We cannot possibly evolve to our full potential if we are denied even the simplest of necessities. I submit that streets without clutter will give us all pride in our community and perhaps it will be the beginning of pride in ourselves. We will give ourselves the gift of dignity. It is a potent symbol of defiance. This is the way of The Dawkins.” The High Elvis had no problems in co-opting The Dawkins for his own political gain.
The crowd of Elvi erupted calling out in unison, “Praise be to the Dawkins.”
“Indeed, praise be to the Dawkins. I have written a song for the inauguration today. We are the lucky few who have been granted the gift of life and we must use this precious gift to effect positive change. Who are we to complain if we do nothing to improve our situation? Who are we to complain about the inevitability of death? What of all the humans that could have been? The one’s that were never born? Most have Never Stirred.” Elvis 457, the High Elvis, strummed his 12 string and effortlessly slipped into a heart-felt ballad for those who have never been.
Most have Never Stirred
We are going to die
That is what we must face
We are the lucky ones
Most never join the race.
Never will they perish
Never to falter and fall
Owning no life to cherish
They were never born at all.
The number of human beings
Who could be here in my place,
Outnumber all the sand grains
On the Sahara’s sandy face.
Untold unborn poets rhyming better than Keats
Countless bright minds Newton could not resist
The set of potential people and their possible feats
Exceeds the set of human beings that ever will exist.
In the teeth of these odds stupefying
You and I living banal and trite
Are the privileged few, there is no denying
Winning a grand lottery to breathe air here tonight.
How dare we find ourselves whining,
At the prospect of being interred
Returning to the prior state
From which most have never stirred.
The references, lifted directly from The Chronicles of The Dawkins, were unmistakable to the well-read gathering. The vast congestion of tearful Elvi clapped and cheered wildly, jumping and hugging each other at random, but the jubilation was soon replaced with panic. A group of five Elvi were lying flat, frothing at the mouth, in an apparent fit, each with ear-phones attached to a prehistoric music device. A small group gathered around them, one Elvis took the earphones from one of the Elvi, still jerking about on the ground and listened. His eyes widened and then threw the teeny speakers to the ground.
“Gospel music!!” He yelled and then vomited, stumbling as best he could for the nearest exit. Screams of panic filled the air as Elvi clamoured over each other in urgency and desperation, all heading in any direction away from the reviled tunes. The High Elvis rushed from the stage and stood before them in horror. They were not fitting, not like any epileptic he had ever seen. They were jerking about and talking some absolute gibberish as white foam spilled over their chins and the front of their jackets, running rivers of drool and bubbles between the rhinestones. Every so often, they would simultaneously break out into shouts of “Praise be to Jesus! The Lord and Saviour! Pledge now, pledge now.”
“Blasphemy! Get these men out of here immediately.” His inauguration reduced to farce, The High Elvis roared with anger . What the hell has brought this on? “Get them to a secure location and find out what they’ve been up to.” 
Upon hearing the name of the ancient deity, Jesus, the manic Elvi ran away in disgust and fear, tugging at their hair and reefing out handfuls of jet black locks. They too, in their revulsion, called out, “Blasphemy”, “Apostate” and “Unbeliever.”
* * *
“What has happened to them?” The High Elvis asked of the Minister for Internal Security. “That display has sent a shock wave though our fledgling nation.”
“They are suffering from an extreme case of Evangelism and we are all susceptible if exposure is protracted. According to what they have told us, they were researching old texts, ancient songs and music of the black discs when they stumbled across some hymns. It started out innocently enough, but they ended up with a dependency. The harmonies of Gospel are just too powerful for an Elvis to resist, they are all completely infected with Christianity and unresponsive to evidence based thought or rational argument.” The Minister for Internal Security, was completely exhausted from hours of reasoned debate being met with the deliberately obtuse. “It started with a small choir, but by the end they were conducting white masses, baptising each other and then got into sexual abstinence.”
“Celibacy? That’s just plain sick. Is there much hope for them?” The High Elvis hoped they would just snap out of it, but this sounded far too serious for a miracle cure. He had only been in the chair for five minutes when this dangerous meme popped up to spoil the show. If it spread his people could turn away from the true path and embrace blind faith. Something unthinkable.
“Personally, I am not hopeful, but none of us have any experience in these matters. Until today we were both low-rent entertainers.” The Minister for Internal Security was still getting used to his lofty new position. “We have the welfare of the entire Elvi to protect and need some expert advice.”
“It is good to see that you are also humbled by the burden of leadership. We must seek the counsel of the Wise Three. Send a delegation to the Travelator to locate Kleo, Silo and Chilo and invite them to walk among our number, spreading The Dawkins’ grace. They will know how to treat our diseased brothers.” The High Elvis did not know if they could help, he wanted to project strength and confidence. “We will have to do something about this gospel music. Find out if it is an isolated case or if there is a larger infection, Mr Minister for Health..”
“What? Yes, of course, High Elvis.” The new minister was still getting used to the title. “To ensure no further outbreaks, I would suggest a complete ban on Gospel music or, for that matter, any music with a religious context.”
“I really don’t wish to resort to censorship, there are always too many grey areas, but it may be the only way. What is the alternative? If there is an outbreak of this meme it could infect us all. We have to keep a lid on this and squash it down hard.” His fondest hope was for his beloved Elvi to practice their art in any way they deemed fit. Blocking an avenue of expression, as one of the first acts of his administration, did not augur well.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="596E291F-5D1F-47EC-BDAA-B397027BAD17">
            <Title>Finding Cracker</Title>
            <Text>Sarah jumped when she heard something like a metal bucket falling down stairs. Alarm planted her hand firmly on Cracker’s thigh. His burns made him wince.
“What was that?” Sarah hissed.
“A metal bucket falling down stairs.” Cracker took Sarah’s hand and was on his feet. “Someone is coming up the fire stairs, someone who doesn’t know to avoid the bucket. We gotta go.”
Cracker clunked the front door open and dragged Sarah down the narrow corridor. They heard the fire door open before they reached the elevator lobby. A huge, square-headed shadow blocked their escape.
“You!” the shadow spat.
“You!” Sarah spat back, recognising the agent from Travis Teh’s office. Now it was her turn to drag Cracker down a corridor, back to his apartment.
“What’s that ape want with you?” Cracker demanded once they were back in the apartment. He leant back against the front door, as if that would baricade it, and glared at Sarah.
“Me? He wants you, you idiot!” Sarah stomped around the living area hoping one of the miserable windows would open up and offer a rainbow bridge to freedom. “That’s the PAPER agent I warned you about.”
Cracker screwed his face up, confused.
Sarah stopped pacing. “Before the bombing! I came to warn you, remember?”
To Cracker, before the bombing was an age ago.
“Open up!” Three blunt, fist-sized thumps shook through the door. “You will return what you stole.”
Sarah pulled a tablet from her bag and went to the apartment’s security console in the kitchen. She made a series of frenzied erratic gestures at the tablet. “Come on!” she told the tablet. More gestures. “He’ll be requesting your door unlocks, which it will if I can’t subvert the command in time.”
Cracker, with heart belting at the cage of his ribs, stood at the kitchen doorway, and watched Sarah work.
The font door yielded an ominous click.
“Tax me dead!” Sarah swore.
Agent Jones barrelled in and, with the speed of an apex carnivore, grabbed Cracker by the throat. Momentum lifted Cracker of his feet and sent him—with Agent Jones’ gorilla-size hand stilling choking him—into the living room, over the coffee table and into the couch. The couch thudded against the wall.
“You will give me the disc,” Agent Jones said, menacing. 
Disc? Isn’t it stolen documents he’s looking for? Sarah rummaged the kitchen for something she could improvise as weapon. The were no pots or pans, or rolling pins, or quality flatware, or pepper mills, or candlesticks, or wine carafes, or even gravy boats. The few items of  cutlery were disposable. How do these people eat?
Agent Jones pinned Cracker to the couch with a knee. “Tell me where it is. Where is the disc?”
Cracker tried to gargle an answer. Agent Jones released his choke-hold.
“I have no idea what you’re talking about,” Cracker pleaded. 
Agent Jones clocked Cracker’s head with the butt of his pistol. “This is no time for lies.” He clocked Cracker again.
Cracker protested with cries of agony.
In the tiny pantry, Sarah found something she could use. She lifted it chin-high and stalked Agent Jones. She was almost there. She hefted the thing above her head like a battle axe.
“Stop,” Agent Jones said as his arm swung and the pistol cocked.
Sarah was looking straight down an uncaring barrel. On the couch, Cracker twisted his hips and shifted his weight just enough to lever Agent Jones, and his arm, and his hand, and the gun he was holding. Reflexively, Agent Jones pulled the trigger. The bullet struck. Gushing, frothing liquid exploded. Sarah cried out and brought her blunt instrument down upon Agent Jones’ head. He crumpled unconscious over Cracker, who made hard work of pushing him aside.
Sarah froze in the shock of the near miss. Everthing was sticky and wet.
Cracker smiled and warmed Sarah’s reanimation. “You know,” he said, “you’ll have to pay for that case of beer.”

They fled the apartment, ran down the fire stairs to the basement, and to Cracker’s van.
“Where can we go?” Cracker asked, expecting an Airborne like Sarah to have bodyguards or safe-houses, or judges in their pockets, or pockets of money to buy their way out of this mess.
“I don’t know!” Sarah said, exasperated.
They jumped in the van. Once out on the street, Cracker demonstrated his lack of speeding skills with screeching tyres, side drifts, and uncontrolled, teetering two-wheeling around corners.
“What the hell did you steal from these people?” Sarah said, agitated and frightened.
“Nothing,” Cracker said, as he botched a tight corner and almost cleaned up a parked vehicle.
“How about this disc? It’s obviously important. You truly know nothing about it?”
Cracker’s blood chilled. Coincidence. Sarah registered his hesitation. “It’s nothing!” Cracker defended.
“What’s nothing?” Sarah was becoming frantic.
“Just some old boot leather I picked up from a dead guy on a street. That nothing.”
Sarah blinked, trying to process. “You stole a dead guy’s boot?”
“No!” Cracker protested, but felt ashamed anyway. “Look, it’s just a piece of junk, ok? It’s still down there, I think, in the footwell.” Her nodded at her knees.
Sarah hesitated, wondering what kind of gunk she’d get on her hands. She grimaced and held her breath while she felt around the floor. She almost missed it; it felt like part of the floor mat until she realised it was loose. Tentatively, she picked at it, got a finger nail under, and retrieved it. By nighttime streetlight, she examined it, both sides.
“This can’t be what the fuss is about … Whoah!” The leathery disc in her hand shimmered a rainbow. “Did you see that?”
“What?” Cracker kept his eyes on the road and his hands doing manic things with the steering wheel.
“It … flashed.”
Cracker risked a glance, saw only the daggy circle of hide he’d tossed aside days ago. “Yeah. I thought it did that before. It’s just a trick of the light.”
Sarak flipped it over and over and tilted it at various angles, trying to catch the light again. She twisted it, just a little, testing its flexibility.
The van crashed and pivoted over its front wheel, landed on its passenger side, slid across the street and collided with a kebab cart. Tabouleh and sauces, red and white, smeared over the van’s windscreen.
Sarah, in the seat closest to the collision, didn’t get thrown much as the van toppled over. She would have suffered no injury at all if the Cracker’s flailing body hadn’t come down upon her.
“You hit a pot hole.” Sarah enunciated the accusation slowly and precisely, containing her fury.
“Nope. I travel these roads every day. There’s no pothole back there.”
“Then what did you hit?”
“I dunno. Wombat?” Cracker tried to grin but his jaw hurt. “C’mon. We gotta move.” He lifted himself off Sarah, climbed in back, stumbled over dislodged equipment and supplies, and threw the payload doors open.
Sarah followed. 
Around front, Cracker appraised the windscreen mess. “Tax! I hate garlic sauce!”
Sarah looked for damage, evidence of the original collision. There appeared no damage to the front wheel, the wheel arch, or the axel, as if some titan had put the van on its side as easily as a chess piece.
“C’mon.” Cracker strode away, already some metres down the road.
“Where are you going?”
“To listen to some karaoke.”</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="ECE979A6-D9B0-4A5A-93AE-D3CCECBB61E3">
            <Title>Tardegrade</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="503102BC-714E-4B14-ADA8-3267703A0D8B">
            <Title>Alexander arrives at the bomb site</Title>
            <Text>“Terrorist activity, eleven o’clock, three hundred metres,” the pilot called.
“Damn! The ambulances are already there.”
“Apparently delta squad’s roadblocks failed, sir,” a riot cop reported to base.
“Copy, alpha squad. You’re cleared to continue the operation around the ambulance crews.”
Alexander could contain his entitled agency no longer. His role, as ever, was to lead, and make a difference. He strode forward to the cruiser’s cockpit.
“Sir,” the co-pilot said. “You’re safer in the cabin.”
“I’m in charge here, captain,” Alexander told the co-pilot.
“Yes, you are,”said a voice of authority from the cabin. “For as long as you give no orders.”
Alexander puffed up with defiance until he saw the cop—the true leader of the operation—would call his bluff. “I want see what’s going on,” was all he could summon.
“Well, buckle up then,” the pilot ordered. “If we have to take evasive manoeuvres, I don’t want your pussy arse landing in my lap.”
Alexander took a second-row seat and tried his best to peer out the windows.
The face of a building burned and crumbled. Fire and smoke issued from the ground floor. The blast radius was substantial. Buildings across the street stood blackened and aghast. The bomb blew in windows for a hundred metres around, scorched the street and set fire to cars. A few stragglers limped or crawled from the explosion. Many lay writhing on street, burned and broken. From Alexander’s position, the motionless ones looked like crushed beetles. He saw very little blood, only ravaged clothing and injuries cauterised or caked in ash.
Less than a pace back from the ring scorching the street, a crowd of gawkers swelled.
“There’s nowhere to land,” the co-pilot reported. “Other than right on top of the injured. And ambulances.”
“Copy,” answered the cop-in-charge, and considered landing exactly where the fuck he wanted. If a few ambulances had to scatter, and he crushed a few soon-to-be-dead, so be it.
“Sir,” the cop with the sniper gun said. “I can cover it, sir.”

Constable Sman came top of his academy class in almost everything. But he didn’t come anywhere near as top-in-the-class as he came in Demographically Sensitive Culling. His scores were so good, upon graduation he could shoot and kill almost anyone he pleased, so long as he killed sufficient others to make his culling score demographically neutral. If, for example he wanted to kill an able-bodied Caucasian male in his thirties, he could calculate to sixteen points how many others he’d need to kill, across a statistically acceptable proportionate number of people, to balance the carnage across all demographics, so no one could sue prejudice against him for killing his original target. In the famous case of Purple Shirts versus the People of East Ulyssia, claims that Sman was colourist were summarily thrown out of court when his legal team provided detailed cop-cam evidence of the number of his kills on that expedition, which conclusively showed his killing spree was not prejudiced against wearers of purple, or any other colour for that matter. Further, they showed beyond a doubt, he was blameless of vilifying purple-wearing, tall, basketball-playing, balding women who abhorred mahjong and drinking tea in the afternoons.
When asked how he did it, he oft quoted a psychopath in an old movie: “I just have to kill a lot of people.”
Sman’s skills became highly sought by police agencies all over, and he enjoyed the demographic variations of regions around the world. Finding the final trains-potting Inuit in Surrey who enjoyed riding broken bicycles was a challenge he especially enjoyed rising to, but after a number of years and a stellar career of never blotting his spreadsheets, he grew homesick for East Ulyssia, for, as he would sometimes confess to his closest friends, “There are too many fuckin’ Cloudborne where I come from.”
Actually, he was almost found guilty, in one case where it was shown he’d fallen short of his quota in one significant demographic, which he easily rectified by blowing the head off the prosecutor. Case dismissed.
So, when the cop with the sniper gun says, “I can cover it, sir,” you know he can cover it.

The riots cops opened the forward port and starboard cabin doors as the pilot lowered the cruiser over the crush in the street. Pistols withdrew from holsters and aimed.
“Take out only enough to get them moving,” Corporal Sman ordered. “I don’t want to be here all night balancing the books.”
Corporal Sman’s colleagues, who often accused him of pleasuring himself to pivot tables (they didn’t know what they were), promptly emptied their clips into the crowd of gawkers. Sman swore.
The reaction was instant. Cries and screams went up as ripples in the crowd, like snakes under a rug, swelled one way then another. Eventually most of the crowd voted with their stamping feet to run away from the bomb site, and a grisly crop circle of trampled bodies bloomed under the cruiser.
The co-pilot set the cruiser down and all the riots cops except Sman jumped out, who parked his butt in the port doorway and dangled his legs out. The cruiser rose again.
“We need the right demographic spread of terrorists. Make sure you stick to the profiles I specified,” Sman ordered as he practiced his aim.
“Roger that,” the lead cop answered.
Alexander came and stood by Sman, and grabbed the doorway to keep balance as the cruiser slowly rotated. “How many more do you have to take out?”
Sman threw a panoramic hologram into the air outside the cruiser, where it would be easy for him to monitor. Statistical tables scrolled, charts and graphs materialised, and faces appeared one after the other. “More than I’d like,” he answered, aimed and shot into the crowd. A face popped up on his hologram with a green tick animating over it.
“So I’m just here to endorse the culling and the bystanders you’ve nominated to play terrorist,” Alexander said sullenly. It was not that he objected to the killing; it galled that he was not in charge of it.
Sman took another shot. The hologram produced another face and another green tick.
The cops punched through the crowd, looking for arrests that matched their profiles. The cruiser lazily rotated as Sman found targets and fired. On his hologram, a graph showed a series of lines that seems to be converging, and a pair of pie charts that were nearing becoming exact copies.
Suddenly, the graphs lost their cohesion. Sman swore. “What’s going on down there?” he demanded.
“We’ve had some Resist Arrests,” the lead cop reported. “Kramer shot a banker.”
Sman was furious. “Do you know how rare a Cloudborne banker is? Now the demographic analysis looks like the banker was our sole intended target! I’ll have to start over.” Sman twiddled and punched at the hologram and the whole of its panorama refreshed. Sman took aim and smiled at something in his scope. “Ah! White hip hop artist!” He pulled the trigger.
“They’re a demographic here?” Alexander asked.
“Not exactly. You can never take out too many white hip hop artists.”
“Aren’t you endangering the demographic balance of the whole operation.”
“Nah,” Sman said as he popped more targets and earned more green ticks. “Community service. See?” He paused and pointed to a floating graph. “Even the demographics don’t care.”
“All targets acquired,” the lead cop reported.
The pilot landed the cruiser. The cops loaded a restrained, gagged and blindfolded mess of terrorists into the cargo hold, and boarded the cruiser.
Once in the air, Sman resumed shooting.
“Almost there?” the lead cop quizzed him.
Sman shot a few times then consulted his charts and graphs. “Pretty much, yeah. I could do with one more, just to be safe. Have pilot hover over the blast site.”
“What are you looking for?” Alexander asked, hoping he could be helpful, so he could tells his friends later, his contribution was the clincher.
“I need an average Joe, preferably one of the bomb victims. White, thirties, manual labourer … you know, a gardener or a cleaner or something.” Sman scrolled through the casualty reports. the hologram went bing and produced a face.
To Alexander’s horror, it was Cracker’s. Jesus, they’d only been chatting last night, when Alexander called him in to clean up that suicide mess. With wide, panicked eyes, he scoured the blast zone for any sign of his … well, not a friend exactly—Cracker was Cloudborne, after all—more a compatriot in a loosely acquainted (had a beer with him once or twice), kind of way.
That’s when Alexander saw her. She was lying dead or unconscious in street, black and grey, cut and bruised. The way she lay was mesmerising. The flow of her long ashen hair spoke of the pure blonde beneath the caking dust. The soot could not disguise the architecture of her cheek bone, nor the artistry of the shape of her lips. Even her body lying at a misshapen angles could not disguise the elegance of her legs, the suppleness of her hips, the promise of her breasts.
Choirs of angels sang to Alexander, and he fell in love.
At which time the memory of Sman’s words intruded … white, thirties, manual labourer … a gardener or a cleaner or something.
Next to the object of Alexander’s adulation, a charcoaled lump of a man, a carcass, lay. Who is he, who lies so near my angel in tragedy? He was cut up and bruised, almost as much as the angel, but his hair had mostly frizzled away. Something about the angel’s companion poked at Alexander’s attention, and irritated it into recognition. Cracker?
“Found him!” Sman smiled with the satisfaction of the thrill of a chase nearing its fruition. He trained his rifle down and steadied his aim.
Alexander kicked the rifle. The particle beam wen wide and blew out an ambulance tyre. Alexander kicked again. The rifle clattered on the cruiser’s landing skids and fell to the street. In a second, the lead cop was standing chest-to-chest with Alexander. “What in the Alfred Deakin was that?”
“Sorry.” Alexander fumbled. “Sciatic tic. Old publications relations injury.”
“What the AF, Lieutenant?” the cop blustered, and handed Sman his pistol. “Take the shot, corporal.”
“Yes. sir.”
“You can’t shoot him.” Alexander was suddenly assertive.
“And just why the Edmund Barton not, Lieutenant?”
Alexander worked his jaw and compressed his lips. He couldn’t admit to knowing Cracker, certainly not in this situation. “Take them to a hospital,” Alexander said.
The cop’s eyes bulged. “I knew bringing you on this mission as a figurehead was a bad idea. Take the shot, Corporal.”
The corporal aimed the pistol. Alexander surprised the cop, and shoved him off his feet. Before Sman could steady his aim, Alexander planted his foot between Sman’s shoulder blade and assisted him out of the cruisers. Sman died with an arrested wail in his throat, his brains dashed out on smouldering asphalt.
The lead cop waved the others back from reacting, wiped his mouth and considered how he’d end this showtime impersonator.
“You know,” Alexander said, “for the sake of the demographics, given the way the corporal mopped up after you grunts, we could classify him as a cleaner, couldn’t we?”
A smile cracked the cop’s face. “Son of a sow,” he said, seeing Alexander as if for the first time.</Text>
        </Document>
