        <Document ID="34EA4225-16B4-4E0F-AE80-F3AFE73EC465">
            <Title>Title Page</Title>
            <Text>



&lt;$PROJECTTITLE&gt;
&lt;$fullname&gt;</Text>
            <Notes>These tags get replaced with the information set in Project &gt; Meta-Data Settings… &gt; Project Properties. You can edit those settings or just replace this text altogether.</Notes>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="761C66AA-8300-4EC2-BFC3-2B7249DC646D">
            <Title>Day 14</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="27D43395-5A67-44A2-BDE2-AF542F41DF0F">
            <Title>Sarah rescues Cracker</Title>
            <Text>As we have narrated earlier, Sarah found Cracker with his three drinking buddies, who looked like a pirate, a fence and a contract killer. We promise, the bombing is not far off now.
“Oh, tax!” the contract killer cursed. “It’s her. The one.”
OK, creepy, Sarah thought, then ignored the contract killer’s slack-jaw stare. “There you are, Mr Fairy Boxers,” she said, as she ambushed Cracker from behind.
Cracker ejected from his chair and attempted a mid-air half-turn, hoping the voice he remembered has heralding the Sarah he ached for. His clumsiness shoved the chair at Sarah and, for a moment, visions of the chair injuring her made him die inside. Sarah arrested it easily, spun it back and eased it under Cracker’s butt. “Sit, relax,” she said, as she stood behind him and rested her hands on his shoulders.
Cracker stifled an ecstatic moan.
Sarah’s polite-greeting-smile chilled as she locked eyes with Halo. “I’m Sarah. Cracker, introduce me to your friends.”
Cracker tried mouthing some words, then decided, trying not to mouth words would be a wiser option. Then he tried mouthing words and not mouthing words, both with equal paralysis. His three friends sprang to their feet and offered awkward hand-shakes with unsteady words.
Halo frightened Sarah. “What big eyes you have,” she said to him.
Halo’s nerves turned to metal. “All the better to see you with, my dear.”
“I wonder, Halo, if your friends—without looking—could tell me what colour your eyes are.”
“Green / Hazel / Lilac,” they clamoured to say.
“Well, they were lilac a moment ago,” Sarah said as she lightly massaged Cracker’s shoulders, and he stiffly crossed his legs. “Now they’re grey.”
Abe and Major examined Halo’s eyes. “They are grey,” Abe said.
“Cracker, honey.” Sarah kissed Cracker on the cheek. An affectionate, familiar kiss. “We’ll be late for dinner.”
Sarah helped Cracker stand, then put her arm around him in a way that beaconed, brand-new flourishing romance in progress. They made their way for the exit. Cracker, with weak knees, leant into Sarah’s embrace and shuffled alongside like he’d only recently discovered his legs. 
Abe whistled. Major nodded. “You’d crawl across 3 k’s of broken glass and—”
“Shut up!” Halo watched Sarah’s arm around Cracker all the way until he could see it no more. It wasn’t only that Sarah was beautiful. Halo searched for the right word. Sarah was healthy. She was the healthiest person Halo had ever met. He was sure of it. “Stacy bitch,” he cursed.

Not long now. The operative had already placed the bomb and was setting the timer.
Sarah and Cracker went out front. She took him by the hand and led him down a narrow hall by the cloak room.
“Were are we going to dinner?” Cracker asked, the blush of hope restoring his capacity for speech.
“We’re not,” Sarah said brusquely.
Cracker’s crankcase seized. The sump fell out of his belly. “Then—”
“When you were cleaning that office this morning …”
Cracker tightened his eyebrows with confusion.
“The suicide,” Sarah prompted.
Cracker remembered every detail of his first moments with Sarah. “What about it?”
“Did you, perhaps, take any documents from the office?”
“What?”
“I dunno,” Sarah said, “maybe there were documents that were, you know, spattered with … so you such assumed you should clean them up, throw them out with all the rest of … Travis …”
“Nah, I never took any documents.”
“You never took anything?”
“Nah.” But the guilt on his face betrayed him. No matter the guilt was over stealing from a dead man on the street. He was clearly a thief then, but all he got was a disc that looked like it had been cut from the sole of an old shoe. He would hardly need to confess that to a woman he was only beginning to have relations with. “What’s this about?” he asked, a little too defensively.
Sarah swallowed. “Not long after you left, a PAPER agent came in and rummaged around that office, looking for some documents. He seemed most desperate to find them. He even accused me of taking them, for tax sake.”
The mention of a PAPER agent chilled Cracker’s blood. “What does this have to do with me?” he asked, swallowing a dose of dread.
“He noticed the carpet had been freshly steam-cleaned and—well, he was clearly convinced the documents should have been there and, once he realised a cleaner had been in there not long before …”
“You set a PAPER agent on my arse?”
“I’m sorry,” Sarah mumbled, then, “No! I didn’t! He figured it out … a cleaner had been there. Look, I came to warn you, OK?”
“Oh, thank you very much.”
Sarah became indignant. “I spent most of the afternoon tracking you down. Do you know there is almost no data about you anywhere? But I still kept looking, and I’m here to warn you, you’re in danger. Just as well, too, because … I get here, and what do I see? Another operative calmly sipping beer with you!”
“Who?” Even with Cracker’s dim brain wattage, he easily discounted Abe and Major. “Halo?”
Sarah nodded. “He’s got cyborg implants.”
Cracker cackled, and almost wept. This beautiful angel was exposing herself as a conspiracy nut. “And how, pray tell, could you know that? You have a cyborg sniffer in your handbag? Tell me, do they smell more, or less, like chicken?”
“His eyes.”
“What?”
“I could tell by his eyes. He has empathy tech.”
“This doesn’t make any sense. You say a PAPER agent is on my trail as of this morning. But I’ve known Halo for years.” Cracker saw Sarah’s lip tremble as her confidence faltered. His dreams of being with this woman were slipping away. But maybe, if he could humour her for long enough, he could still wangle a night with her. “OK, tell me about this empathy stuff.”
Sarah decided to play a hunch. “I bet Halo’s a great pick-up artist, extraordinarily good at getting people to drop everything so they can go fuck.” Sarah saw the truth of that landed with Cracker.
“Well, we always wondered how he’d keep pulling it off. But, empathy tech? I’ve never heard of it.”
“Of course you’ve never heard of it. You’re Cloudborne. This is Airborne gear. The recreational consumer stuff allows the host to control their pupil sizes, and the colour and pattern of their irises. The sophisticated stuff monitors micro-muscle activity in a target’s eyes, so you can quickly adjust you eye colour and pupil dilation to trigger arousal in them.”
Cracker blinked and shook his head. “You said, recreational?”
Sarah pursed her lips. She shouldn’t be telling a Cloudborne this, but it probably wouldn’t matter. If Cracker repeated it, no Cloudborne would believe him. Perhaps Cracker wouldn’t believe her.
“You ever wonder about the all the Airborne do-gooders who set up schools to educate Cloudborne kids?”
“Not really. I just suppose there will always be a few Airborne with a social conscience.”
Sarah scoffed. “Every Airborne teacher in every school for the disadvantaged …”
Cracker took a second to finish her sentence. “… uses empathy tech.”
“You got it. There’s a whole consumer industry supplying Airborne with the hobby tech they need to exploit the Cloudborne how ever they want.”
Cracker replayed Sarah’s words in his head: … so they can quickly adjust their eye colour and pupil dilation to trigger arousal in their targets. “They target are our kids,” he said and set his jaw for vengeance. “I suppose it’s no more than a week-end pastime for them, like golf or big-game safari hunting.”
“I’m afraid so.”
“But what about Halo? He’s no—”
“His tastes may differ, but the game is the same.”
Cracker thought. “But how does this prove he’s some operative?”
“I knew the second I looked him in the eye. And my panties got wet. It took all my awareness to break rapport with him. The tech he has is much higher grade than the gear you need to manipulate a Cloudborne juvenile. It’s expensive, and hard to acquire. Only certain agencies have access.”
“If it’s so restricted, how’s a dreg like Halo come by it?” Cracker knew the answer before he asked. He wanted Sarah to say it.
“It’s unlikely a Cloudborne could engender such patronage. I’m afraid the logical conclusion is, your mate Halo is Airborne.”</Text>
