        <Document ID="AC94B329-E897-4EEA-A3BB-76C0B1409389">
            <Title>A blank walks into a bar</Title>
            <Text>Instead of being executed, Emil Kalheri decided he’d go to the pub.
It wasn’t that he’d developed an intention to squib on the final obligations of the contract—a Blank Contract, Special Agent Ajent Jones called it. He’d already performed the grisly task of mincing a hole in a man’s chest with a shotgun, and taken the most perfunctory steps to make it look a suicide. And he’d acquired the strange item Agent Jones said the man had stolen.
It went down exactly as Agent Jones said it would. Emil arrived at the office tower shortly after midnight and strode at the 2-storey glass doors as if he owned the place. To Emil’s impish glee, the office security system did recognise the brand and quality of the midnight blue suit he was wearing (supplied by Agent Jones) and granted him, the wearer, an all-access pass. In the lobby, doors opened on a luxurious Airborne-only elevator which exalted him more the two hundred floors into the air, and admitted him to an office suite dimly aglow with the navigation gear of passing hover-copts and lighting from other towers. Emil marvelled how clear the night air was above the Layer. He stopped at reception’s huge plate windows, transfixed for many seconds, as he tried to make sense of a golden, milky light high in the sky. “You, idiot, Emil,” he told himself. “That’s the moon.” He chuckled at the marvels the Airborne enjoyed as a matter of course.
Finding Travis Teh’s office was simple. As Agent Jones had predicted, Teh was the only one working this late, and his office light was on. When threatened with the shotgun, Teh was all guppy lips and jelly limbs, and eagerly handed over the item with shaky fingers. As per instructions, Emil shot him anyway, with the barrels of the gun shoved between Teh’s jaw and Adam’s apple. Teh even obliged Emil’s request to hold the gun’s grip as Emil’s finger caressed the trigger. Perhaps Teh thought it an opportunity to yank the gun away before Emil could fire. He was wrong.
Emil felt powerful. So powerful, he experienced an overwhelming urge to pee. He found the office’s opulent bathrooms, stood at the urinal and caressed himself for longer than functionally required. He felt so alive, even the pain of his testicular cancer was tamed.
“Tax you!” Emil cursed his balls. “You won’t get the chance to kill me!”
No, an unknown assassin would have that honour, once Emil delivered the item. Agent Jones promised it would be quick and clean. Emil cared little; any death would be quicker and cleaner than his own gonads eating him from the inside out. 
Emil triumphantly zipped the fly of his immaculate suit pants, and resolved against washing his hands.

Emil found a 24-hour bar a couple of blocks from the office tower. With the kind of satisfaction only a gambler could muster after a big win, Emil strode for a seat at the bar. He unbuttoned his coat with a flourish as he sat down.
“You sure, you’re in the right place, partner?” the bartender asked, eyeing Emil’s outfit.
“I’m exactly where I want to be,” Emile answered, and lifted his arm so the bartender could scan his armpit for his consumer account. “Your best tequila, barkeep, and keep them flowing.”
The bartender squinted at the bar’s Grogify tablet, reading. “Looks like your account runs out before … half a bottle.”
“Fit for purpose, my good soul,” Emil said. He’d drink his account dry, pickled-happy the contract he signed guaranteed his family would be provided for, for the rest of their lives. A momentary wave of sorrow took him. He missed his wife Peggy, and his kids, Becky and Roger. They’d be so much better off without him.
Emil downed the first shot of gold liquid and pushed the glass for replenishing. He grunted, remembered the item he still had to deliver to Special Agent Ajent Jones, and fished it from his breast pocket. He screwed his face into a pinch of unanswered questions. The disc, twice the width of his thumb, gave no clue it was worth killing for. It wasn’t even metal, as far as Emil could tell. It was dull and tough and bendable, although it sprang back to shape when he stopped squeezing it. He though it might be crocodile hide, though he’d never seen a crocodile, nor the genuine hide of any animal, for that matter.
Then it glinted. Or Emil thought it glinted. It could be the lighting in the bar, playing tricks. He downed another shot and waited for it to glint again. When it inertly refused, he bounced it in his palm, testing its weight. Then he tossed it up, like tossing a coin.
The stool under him trembled. The tide of his tequila splashed. Lights over the bar swayed. Glasses trembled and shook. Bottles leapt from shelves. Customers squealed.
Emil clasped the disc in his hand.
“What the tax was that?” the bartender blurted. “Earthquake? In Ulyssia?”
“I dunno,” Emil said. “I just thought how awesome this tequila is.” He drained the shot and slid the glass to the bartender for a refill. Emil was lucky; the tequila bottle was still intact.

With his account drained and his brain steeped in fermenting cactus juice, Emil staggered out into the toxic cold of the night. The air smelled like boiled cabbage, wet cats, and fried underwear. He’d have to contact Agent Jones, but then was not the best time. He was incoherent, and would need his wits about him when apologising for the delay. It would be better if Agent Jones never knew he got drunk.
“I’m sorry, Agent Jones,” he rehearsed, and got no farther before he giggled. “Hello, I’m Special Agent Ajent Jones,” he parodied. “I’m Ajent Jones … Agent Ajent …” He sputtered into laughter, then recomposed his impersonation. “I’m a stupid Special Agent, whose first name is also Ajent.” Racks of laughter seized him.
That’s when Emil dropped dead. It wasn’t as quick or clean as Special Agent Jones promised. An anonymous sniper hadn’t taken him out. Neither had his malignant sperm rocks. Emil died of a heart attack.</Text>
            <Notes>semen stones</Notes>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="332322CA-ADEE-4BCB-87F8-342E095FE26A">
            <Title>Cracker drinks with his mates</Title>
            <Synopsis>Why would Sarah bother?</Synopsis>
            <Text>The entire table turned as one and saw a tall blonde standing directly behind Cracker, the swarm of dancing bodies impolitely, but rhythmically, bumping her lightly. She looked over Cracker’s shoulder, directly into his eyes, faces only a few centimeters apart. “Hey, steam-boy. I’ve been looking for you.” Sarah glided gracefully into a chair and smiled, genuinely it seemed, at Cracker.
Cracker was completely dumbfounded. It was Sarah, she was seeking out his company on the same day his hand-brake had bailed. These sorts of things only happened in contrived vids. Now was his chance, all he had to do was not make a fool of himself. A tall order he silently admitted. When it came to women, it was only occasionally that he managed not to stick his foot in his mouth and even when he did have the odd victory, they quickly dropped off the map, never to return his calls. The uncomfortable silence at the table was becoming, well, uncomfortable. Think of something Cracker. Quickly. He thought excruciatingly slowly as beads of sweat rapidly formed rivulets across his brow, coursing a torrent down his temples.
“Nice conversation, boys.” Sarah decided to break the stalemate, the turning to Halo she stared him down, “Love is a defence mechanism created by women to alleviate the guilt produced when they wake up with people like you.”
Halo chuckled quietly. “You’re a thinker aren’t you? Please forgive my crudeness.” I will kill you one day soon, bitch.
“Consider it done.” A gentle smile returned to her face. She turned to Cracker, “Are you going to buy a lady a drink?” 
“Yes.” They all blurted in concert and as if choreographed, left the table and started for the bar, but stopped and turned toward her.
“Beer.” She stated sternly. “No lolly-water for me, thank you.” They all beamed enormous grins as it had long been established among their number, that beer drinking is the hallmark of any truly great woman. They eyed each other furtively and started for the bar, as if to race. She leaned out and grabbed Cracker by the shirttail. “You may as well stay. Three drinks at a time is my limit.”
He smiled as he returned to his seat. Wow, she does want to talk to me.
 “What’s with your friend?”
“Halo? He’s got anger issues. If he doesn’t get the woman every time, he gets upset.”
“The woman?”
“Yeah, he will sit for hours staring into the crowd and then decides on a target, as he calls it. Then after a few minutes, he’s off with them. You are truly a rare find.” It felt a bit odd having to explain the predatory behaviour of his acquaintances. “It is good for him to be sat on his backside every so often. I’m not like him, though, I’m more of ….”
“An aspiring comedian?” She laughed, recalling the shroud of steam earlier that morning. “You are definitely not like him. You’re nice.” She truly meant it. 
“Keep it down will ya? I don’t want it advertised. Being nice is not a virtue in this society.”
“Social commentary and I thought I had just come out for a couple of drinks.” Even with the odd intelligent comment, Sarah could not fathom why this amiable young man was so important. She enjoyed his company, but he was not likely to change the world or alter its direction, even slightly.
“Don’t get too excited. That’s about as deep as I get.”
“Don’t you want to get deeper?” Her eyes looking directly in his, releasing a flood of adrenalin into Cracker’s system. 
“Don’t do that to me.” He said, looking to his lap, fearing another embarrassing display. “I would like to retain some dignity.”
“What?” Feigning hurt feelings, but controlling the direction of the conversation precisely.
“Who’s acting dumb now, huh?” 
“You know Halo’s eyes are R.E.A. don’t you?” She said.
“What? Genetically enhanced? Bullshit, how could his parents afford that?”
“They are not really genetically enhanced, but R.E.A. is equally expensive. Each eye has what amounts to a miniature vid screen grafted onto the cornea which gives him control over the colour and pattern of his eyes. How do you think he gets all the women? I hate to say it, but some girls actually do have daddy issues.”
“I did notice some peculiarities with my last girlfriend.”
“This is how it works. Your friend can cause the corneas of his eyes to change colour and pattern randomly until he hits upon a pattern the woman unconsciously recognises. His eyes are set to default to a mirror image of hers. If she’s a narcissist she will fall for herself, but usually a after a few variations he’ll stumble across dear old dad’s corneal pattern or perhaps on old boyfriend’s or girlfriend’s for that matter. Once the unconscious recognition is made, she has little or no chance. She is blind-sided by her own neuroses.”
“No way.” Then after some thought, “I suppose it makes some sense. Those eyes are pretty weird and he is very intense”
“I saw him approaching me and I could tell. I have seen those exact eyes before when I was at school. The teachers abused half the student body before anyone cottoned on. I was lucky, apparently I don’t have any major issues, daddy or otherwise.” Which is amazing considering what happened with mum’s boyfriend. Gross! 
“He is going to be so pissed off when he finds out I know. This will be sweet.”
They both were giggling when the boys returned with the drinks sans Halo. Major held up his right hand as if to Hail Caesar, “Before you ask, I think Halo has left with his latest victim.” 
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="26F45DE5-6C08-4813-9799-9436D37FA753">
            <Title>A Big Bang for the Big Bang</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="51CCD1E0-C8BB-411F-BB7C-C0DF1B0DA814">
            <Title>Dr Logan</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="AB4E09F1-607A-404C-B48B-713B720D660B">
            <Title>A Shot out of the Blue</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="0AD0FFB2-8C95-484A-90FD-64CCB35B5EC9">
            <Title>Crack Protection Team</Title>
            <Synopsis>Cracker gets assaulted kidnapped by Haloren Smyth who is shitty about Cracker getting the credit as being the Nightclub Vampire.
Smyth makes admissions as being the serial killer. 
Smyth is then arrested - he was set up by Hayley and Cracker.
This is where Sythe gives the back story of why he hung out with Cracker. All of Cracker's friends were in on it.
Smyth is arrested.
</Synopsis>
            <Text>Crack Protection Team 
4 to 6 weeks after bombing - a week or so after the allocation.
Since the wedding, Cracker had been hiding out in his safe-house and had kept a low profile. Hayley and Kathy seemed to have dumped him, neither having the time to return his calls. He had been receiving regular reports of the tunneling efforts, but had not managed to get off the couch and show himself on site. He could not do much to help as progress had slowed, the excavating Elvi having now resorted to jack-hammers and hand tools, but the goal was in site. He had not received any notifications from Agent Jones and feared Proactive Reciprocity would win the race. He decided he had better chase it up.
He left the building and headed for the travelator. The Elvi had re-taken vast sections of the circle of carriages and it had become a popular, mostly safe mode of transport. As he wandered aimlessly towards the Management Zone border wall, he was struck in the head from behind and fell to the clean paving bricks. He attempted to stand up and protest, but he was struck several more times before finally losing consciousness.
“Wake up, wake up.” Haloren yelled in his face. “How fucking hard is your head? I am surprised you are still breathing!”
“How did you find me?” Cracker said rubbing his head ruefully.
“You didn’t think I would recognise you at the reception? That was the...”
“The worst disguise ever. I have been told. To be fair, it was only meant to fool Shakti. How was I supposed to know you were going to be there?”
“That is of no importance now. What it of importance is you. I saw you shot 3 times, point blank. How did you survive?” Halo thought there must have been some Hollywood trickery, but the footage was very convincing. He ripped open Cracker’s shirt and saw no ghastly blast wounds, virtually nothing. A few pink dots. Halo’s face bore testimony to a few adolescent zits that had left far worse scars. “Ha, I knew it. It was smoke and mirrors.”
“Yeah, you got me. Are you going to give me up to the cops? If they find out I am still alive, they will arrest or kill me.” Cracker didn’t like the direction of this conversation and knew he would have a better chance with the cops, but was still hopeful of a reprieve. He looked around for an exit but there were no windows and only one door. “Only one door? Where is this place? Everything looks old and decayed. The Pale?”
“We are in a safe location. That is all you need to know. Now what I need to know is simple. How did you get the credit for my work?” 
“Your work? What are you talking about?”
“They think you are the Nightclub Vampire.”
“Oh, shit.” Was all Cracker could think to say. Halo confirmed he was the most prolific serial killer in the history of the Primary Trade Dominion. Shit! Cracker did not believe it, even though he had just heard an unsolicited confession.
“I cannot let that stand. You don’t have the brain power to pull it off. Why would they think it was you?”
“The Commissioner set it all up. I was supposed to be charged and eliminated in my cell. I have no idea why. I have never met him and I was lucky I happened to be with Shakti the night of one of the killings” He prudently omitted Sarah’s name. He did not want her name ringing in the ears of a serial killer.
“James Marrin? That one was a real bonus.” Halo reflected back to the night of the bombing. He had been looking for James Marrin and he had just presented himself. Such a fortunate happenstance. “He had to be ticked off the list. He had tried to get close to you through Andrea Novetine. They both had to go.”
“That’s a pretty weird looking knife you got there. What is it?” Cracker asked, not really wanting to know the answer.
“My best friend.” Halo replied.
* * *
Hayley had heard enough and sent the team in. They blew the door, threw in concussion grenades and star-fire flares before they poured in. Haloren Smythe was out cold, but Cracker was wandering about coughing and spluttering. 
“What took you?” Cracker yelled as Detective Lanning entered the room. “Cut it a bit fine didn’t you? He could have killed me when I was unconscious and you never would have known.”
“I needed the confession for the Marrin murder otherwise you would not be off the hook.” She thought her judgment had been timely and divisive. 
“Come to think of it, you were pretty slow on the draw when Agent Jones was about to kill me, too.” He did not know if it were true, but the thought of being expendable did not sit well with him and if his concerns were valid, it exposed her as a stone-cold bitch. 
“You are only imaging things. After today you can get your old life back.” She appeared cheerful but inwardly she was, momentarily, second guessing her operational acumen. Was I too slow? It doesn’t matter, he is only level 2. “I have to get this one back to headquarters.”
“No, wait. You can interview me here, but don’t take me anywhere.” Halo coughed out as the uniforms in flack jackets finalised trussing him up. Hayley had no problem speaking with him there and then. There will be that much more to talk about when she had him strapped in the interrogation suite. “You have got this all wrong. I have been protecting Cracker for years.”
“Can you tell me about that?” Sarah was unconvinced. This low-life will say anything to sleaze his way out of this one.
“Yes, but I need certain incentives.” Halo thought to press for concessions. As a member of the upper elite he simply could not help himself to negotiate for more and more. If he could gain some concessions from her, it followed that he could gain more.
“You will be offered neither promises or threats. I am investigating multiple murders and you will receive no favours from me.” She shut him down quickly and without hesitation.
“I at least need protection.”
“You will be afforded every protection under the law.” She was genuine in her promise to protect her prisoner. She had a duty of care and would not shirk her responsibility.
“You may find what I am about to say difficult to believe. Anthony Hardy, Cleaner, L.2 is the great-grandson of Jameson Hardy, Corporate Magnate. Not only that, as far as I can tell, he is the only living direct descendant.” 
“Oh, come on!” Cracker burst out laughing . “You can do better than that.”
 “It is a fact, Anthony. Your grandfather, may the market be merciful to his memory, would fuck the hair on a barber shop floor. He had quite a number of children, all outside of wedlock, scattered throughout Ulyssia to various different mothers. When he was declared dead, his relatives and his heirs, were hunted down and murdered. A small group, my father being one, all prodigies of Jamison Hardy were tasked to protect the last few survivors.”
“Why would you do that? His fortune must have evaporated by now.” Hayley did not sound impressed.
* * *
“Not all of the accounts. There exists off-shore accounts, vast sums in various tax havens, that cannot be accessed by anyone other than Jameson Hardy or his heir. The next closest relative is determined by examination of the applicants DNA, not fancy documentation and no-one, to this day, has managed to lay claim to the money and live long enough to spend it. On each and every occasion the banks have been approached by a claimant, they are at once cut down by a coparcener or the enemies of Jamison Hardy. The wealthiest in the world have hatched schemes for decades trying to access the money hidden away, but they have always failed. There are trillions waiting to be liberated and this lump of human refuse sitting here before you is the only person in the world who can access the accounts.”
“If you were protecting Cracker, why did you kill so many innocent girls?” Hayley refused to buy this story. 
“Innocent girls? There is no such thing! Anyone that came close to Cracker was taken out. Most of them were genuine targets, obviously agents sent to kill him, but perhaps a few were collateral damage.” He knew there was more that just a few, but fuck them, they had got in his way, a crime sufficient to attract summary execution. “Who do you think the target was for the Stick-it-Inn bombing?”
“Me?” Cracker was still in denial. “Am I so important that hundreds of other are killed just to get to me? No, it is not possible.”
“But the murders were so vicious, so brutal. The violence displayed and the damage inflicted on your victims was more, much more, than required to repel an attack. The level of hate towards women was hideous ” Hayley brought the conversation back to the topic at hand.
“What is the difference between killing a man and killing a woman? They bleed, feel pain and die in pretty much the same fashion as men. There is nothing so special about woman kind that affords them any protection from me. Are you suggesting that killing men is preferable? Or desirable? That the human condition of the male is so objectionable, it is simply okay to kill them on that basis alone?” Halo was an equal opportunity killer. “You will probably never know or appreciate the precision, the skill and dedication required to snuff out life, time and time again. It becomes quite addictive and for better or worse, I embraced it, reveled in it. I will kill anyone that gets in my way.” He was glad he could get some of this off his chest. He did not feel guilty, not at all. He had been busting to share his love of killing with someone, anyone. He had never expected, however, he would divulge the truth of his heartfelt passion to a cop. He had considered the possibility that he might, one day, be caught and interviewed, but was sure his social status would afford protection. Failing that, he dreamed of being brought down in a hail of police plasma fire in a brave and spectacular last stand. The concussion grenades Hayley’s team had used knocked him out cold, denied him that glory.
“How many heirs were under protection?” The number of relatives of Jameson Hardy must run into the hundreds, she thought.
“I don’t know exactly. There were numerous cells that operate independently, but have diminished over the years as each heir was murdered. Jamison was perfectly aware his progeny were under threat and even while he was still alive, plots were being hatched and alliances forged. All for the one purpose of wrestling away his hidden wealth. Publicly acknowledging any of his numerous descendants would have placed them in mortal danger. He concluded it was safer to leave them to their own devices, offering up no overt assistance, just covert protection teams.”
“You were never my friend were you? Not like the others, they were real friends.”
“No, I was never your friend.” Haloren replied with no shame. He had a job to do and emotional attachments would prove counter-productive. 
“My real friends are all dead now.”
“No, you never had friends. Major, Abe and any other acquaintances you have ever had, worked for me. You have been under observation and our protection for most of your life. The only reason you are still alive is because of me and my company of dedicated defenders.”
“Bullshit.” Cracker found it hard to believe that his shit-filled existence was, in reality, charmed and he had reason to be grateful. By any objective measure his life had been one of hardship and deprivation and he firmly believed he had solid grounds for complaint. 
 “Who do you think brought you up?” Halo looked Cracker up and down and then smiled at Detective Lanning.
“My uncle, Chester. At least that’s what he told me.”
“Indeed, that is what he told you. He was not your uncle, but one of the first to join the team.”
“But he abandoned me when I was 16 years old. Fucking prick went out one day and never came back.”
“That fucking prick, as you call him, was tortured and killed that day and the fact that you are still alive means he kept his mouth shut. You have much to be grateful for.”
“I didn’t know that and could not know that, but I was left high and dry while not yet a man. I didn’t know when I was to be kicked out onto the street and have spent all the years since frightened at the prospect of being homeless. I have never paid rent, I don’t know where or who to pay. You are level 6 and could never imagine the creeping sickness of that constant, unrelenting anxiety, not knowing what would happen from to me one day to the next.”
“No one knows that, you idiot. There is no such thing as certainty, just the promise of it. Don’t you know that?” The great teacher needed an education. “You lazy minded, useless level 2 turd. Have you ever bothered to read the documents Chester left for you. They were sent shortly after of he died. Did you read them?”
“No. I couldn’t be bothered. As far as I was concerned he abandoned me and I wasn’t interested in anything he had to say. (????) He could go fuck himself.”
“Idiot. You own the apartment. I could never understand why you didn’t sell up and improve your station. I guess being a complete moron slowed you down.” Haloren was incensed. I have been protecting this imbecile for years. What a fucking waste of time, unless of course I can get hold of some of that cash. The ultimate prize.
“Haloren Smythe, Chief Executive Officer, L.6?” Hayley had sat back and allowed the conversation to unfold naturally and was pleasantly surprised by her suspects candor. “You are under arrest for the murders of James Marrin and Andrea Novetine.”
“No, I cannot be taken to custody. A member of the Upper Executive Class locked up for murder? One of two things will happen. I will either be seen as an embarrassment to my peers, in which case I will be killed in short shrift or I will be protected, no-matter what you throw at me. I will never spend one minute in gaol. Which scenario do you favour? Neither will further the interests of justice and I know you are a true servant of the law.”
“I have in the recent past, bent the rules, but that was a very special case and I do not propose repeating that again. Your case is less than exceptional and on this occasion I will stick to procedure. You will be charged and processed like any other prisoner. What will happen to you will be out of my control.” She did not care if he died in custody or his benefactors busted him out, just as long as her reputation remained unsullied. The Nightclub Vampire was finally in custody and she was taking him in.
“Well, fuck you then. I will say nothing further to you.” He said not another word.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="E2F8323A-F910-428B-9989-91872850DBDD">
            <Title>Morning After the night before</Title>
