        <Document ID="9AB7A0B4-ADCF-4CF2-B671-41B6D0C2D54C">
            <Title>Meet the Boss</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="0F62E5C2-41F2-41F7-B09A-AF381C34B686">
            <Title>Template Sheets</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="1973C6C2-71DF-4F53-A4C8-6FED58885369">
            <Title>Day 1</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="32669DF4-F10A-409A-9D95-1DC0961C0302">
            <Title>Fight and the end of the tunnel.</Title>
            <Text>Fight at the end of the Tunnel
2 months after bombing
The vid phone screamed, demanding Cracker’s attention. The Elvi had urgent news for the Great Teacher, but were unable to get access to the management zone to speak to him in person. 
“Hello? This is…actually I don’t know who I am supposed to be, who is calling?” Cracker said without confidence or authority.
“It’s Elvis, Great Teacher.”
“Ahh, you are going to have to be a bit more specific.” 
“Elvis-in-charge of the tunneling team, sir, we have hit a bit of a snag. We have cracked a sewer main and it is leaking badly, threatening to burst at any moment.”
 “How far from the completion are you?”
“We have already hit the underside of the basement. We only have to crack the floor, about 1 metre of steel reinforced concrete. It should not take that long, but we are concerned about flooding. The tunnel is slowly filling and the stench is unbearable, but the smell is not the biggest problem. We are not only concerned with the possibility of suffocation, but when we crack the floor we will be endangered by the bio-agents stored there in the lab. We have been screaming out for the bio-suits for the last week, but it has fallen upon deaf ears, Great Teacher. We need you to get the suits here fast.”
 
“Have no concern other than the task at hand. Dig in short shifts, say 1 hour for each team. Shorten the length of time if necessary, but do not crack the floor. I will chase up the suits and deliver them myself. Consider it done, my Elvi brother.” Cracker was feeling very guilty. He had taken on the supervisory role with the promise that no Elvi would be harmed, but had been so pre-occupied with the capture of Haloren Smythe and he had not visited the work-site for nearly ten days. He had ordered the suits weeks ago and had been assured that the treasury would release the funds forthwith. He was weighed down by the shame of failing his brothers and was determined to promptly resolve the issue, giving the treasurer a kick the the backside for good measure. 
“Thank you, Great Teacher.”
Cracker ended the conversation and contacted Sarah with some urgency. “Hey, Mrs Shakti, what’s up”
“Cracker, good to see you. How can I help the Great Teacher today?” Sarah was jovial, precisely as Cracker expected.
“Is your line secure at your end?”
“Yes, I am yet to move out of my apartment.”
“The Elvi are just about to break into the lab, but have no protective gear. There are no bio-suits, emergency showers, radiation gel or even the incendiary grenades. We need to see the High Elvis and his ministers. I was told everything was under control, but someone has dropped the ball and placed my digging team in danger.”
“They have made terrific progress, but it will be for naught unless we can access the lab safely. I will contact the High Elvis and get him to shake some coin out of the treasurer. I get the impression the treasurer thinks the money is his and is trying to save the Elvi nation on a shoestring. I will call you back shortly. Great Teacher? Good to hear from you.” Sarah had also been pre-occupied since her allocation ceremony. 
Cracker only had to wait a few minutes for the return call from Sarah. “Yes, Cracker we have a major problem. The suits were never ordered and the explosives cannot be obtained until next week. The Minister for the Treasury says you never ordered the suits and he knows nothing about it. There will be no suits, no safety showers and worst of all, no explosives.”
“That’s complete crap. I spoke to him personally, weeks ago.” Cracker was aghast.
“I guessed as much. He’s a politician now and will point the finger in any direction except his own. I will meet you at the work site straight away. Bring your tunneling gear, Great Teacher.”
“See you soon, Blessed Mother.”
* * *
Cracker had only been on site a few minutes when Sarah arrived, her protruding belly was round and taught. Far too distracted by the impasse the present circumstances had created, she did not notice the approach of Cracker until he was upon her.
“Oh,Cracker, you got here fast.” She knew the time had come to tell him of the T-virus and had been practicing what she would say all the way to the site, but now all those well rehearsed words had sublimated into the ether. “I have something to confess.”
“This doesn’t sound promising.” He knew coming clean, at least from the very few times he had given it a lash, was a dirty business and rarely went well for either party.
“Please don’t interrupt. This is going to be difficult enough as it is. Do you remember your remarkable transformation the night you removed the disc from under my skin?”
“It’s on my highlights reel.”
“I did not need to know that, thank you. I told you I thought the disc had done something to you, but that was a lie.” She baulked for a second or two, second guessing the decision to purge her conscience. “Oh dear, it is all so tawdry. When I was about 12 years old my mother was in a relationship with a very nice, virologist. They were in love, at least that what it seemed like to me. He was always sending her flowers and presents, but he never thought to send me or my sister anything, not even an e-card when my twelfth birthday came along. A few weeks later, I was at home when a package arrived for my mother. Another gift! I knew it was wrong, but I so wanted a present for myself. Mother was not home, so I opened it up and found a short note and a vial containing a clear liquid. The note read drink the vial, which I did without hesitation, not being bothered to read the rest of the message. My immature taste buds salivated with anticipation of a sugary treat, but it was near tasteless and I dismissed it as water. Reading on I was sickened to discover the vial contained the three doses of the T-virus, a contagion transmitted by blood or other bodily fluids such as semen. I knew the vial did not contain blood, so naturally I thought I had swallowed a vial of semen and really freaked out. I remember with absolute clarity the disgust I felt. He had given me a STD! I was a 12 year old virgin with a social disease!”
“Ah, hence the virginity thing.” The Dawkins would weep! 
“The virginity thing? Can you imagine how I felt?” 
“Well, if this conversation is going in the direction I think it is, I know exactly how you felt. What did you tell your mother?”
“I never said a word. I was too frightened to tell her. Her boyfriend never called again and she never spoke of him again.”
“So I got the virus from you when I removed the disc?”
“There is no doubt.”
“Well worth it.” He smiled. Well worth it. 
“I am so sorry.”
“Are you kidding? Since then I have never felt better. I’ve got a six pack now and I don’t do any exercise. I mean, none at all. I got shot three times and survived! Sarah, you did me one big favour. Am I correct in assuming the virus is behind all of this?”
“Yes. Everything in my body operates at a significantly more efficient rate than the average person.”
“Understatement. But why are you telling me this now? We have to work out how we are going to get those Elvi into that lab safely.” Cracker wanted his Elvi safe and sound.
“We don’t. None of the Elvi are going in. It is just too dangerous. You and I have to go in, no suits, no protection.”
“I can’t let you go in there, it’s suicide.”
“I don’t think so. I think we are the only two that can enter the lab safely. I am willing to bet the T-virus will give us sufficient protection.”
“You are willing to bet our lives and the life of the little Dawkins?”
“Either that or you get to pick which Elvi get to go in our place. Is that a decision you want to make?”
“No, I can’t face that.” Picking who gets to die, while he gets to live, was far too cowardly for Cracker to consider. “If we get infected by anything nasty, we could be in there for quite a while before we get the all clear. If we were ever let out.”
“Yes, I have considered that, but who is going to stop us?” 
“No one, I guess.” He did not like the idea of being stuck indefinitely in a bio-weapons bunker any better than Sarah. “Site supervisor, I need to speak with the site supervisor.” Cracker asked generally of the Elvi present.
“Of course Great Teacher, he is in the tunnel”
“The Blessed Mother and I need to speak with him now.” Cracker commanded and the one of the labourers headed off into the tunnel returning a few minutes later with the digging crew. The Site Manager Elvis in a dull blue safety vest approached and stood reverently, safety helmet politely removed and held to his stomach in both hands  He reeked terribly of sweat and sewage and was completely soaked by both.
“Blessed Mother and the Great Teacher. You honour me.” He bowed humbly to his venerated leaders.
“Elvis, we do not have the safety equipment I promised. The funds were never released and the gear was never delivered. I am so very sorry.” Cracker, the Great Teacher, was genuinely and profoundly contrite. 
“We will carry on regardless. All my Elvi are prepared for whatever is in there. We are all prepared to die to protect our people.” The site manager was deadly serious. Each man and women in the digging team had contemplated the horrors awaiting inside the laboratory and volunteered anyway, knowing that every other Elvi would do the same without question.
“No, it was my responsibility. I made the arrangements, but failed to follow up and this is the result. I cannot and will not allow anyone to place themselves in danger because I of my neglect.” Cracker was mortified that he had let this situation arise. “The Blessed Mother has a plan that will place none of you in danger.” 
“The Great Teacher and I, alone, will be present when the basement floor of the lab is cracked. We will take the risk and we will bear the brunt of anything that befalls us.” Sarah explained, cloaked in an invisible aura radiating confidence and authority that was difficult to resist.
“Blessed mother, no. You are pregnant. You cannot place yourself or the one-after-the-one in danger. That is precisely why we are here. We are the cannon fodder in this cold war and eminently expendable. You are far more important than any one individual Elvis.”
“This is not a request, Elvis. You are instructed to removed all your men and women from the tunnel and seal the entrance.” The Great Teacher decided the debate was over.
“There is no need to seal the tunnel. As you ordered, Great Teacher, it was dug at a slight upward grade for about 50 metres before we had to dig down and under a mass of cables, pipes and conduits. Essentially we have created a giant “U ” bend that is now filled with sewer and waste water. Contaminated air is blocked by the water and cannot get out. To get in there you will have to swim through about 20 metres of raw sewage. The air on the other side is toxic, we are pumping in fresh air through a hose, but it has had limited effect. You will be forced to retreat before succumbing to the fumes.”
“We will have to take our chances. Is all the digging equipment ready to go?” Cracker tried to be all business, but in reality he was just plain terrified. 
“Every hand tool, jack hammer, grinder and drill we have is sitting there idle at the moment. At least allow me to accompany you, Blessed mother. I fear I will be branded a coward if I do otherwise.”
“I cannot allow that, Elvis.” The Blessed Mother hugged him gently and caring not for the fetor rising from his bedraggled frame, she placed her hands on each of his cheeks, “No one could ever accuse the Elvi of cowardice. You humble me with your fealty, something I do not deserve.” Elvis began to weep. Hands had been placed upon him by the Blessed mother and had felt her grace.
They made their way toward the entrance of the tunnel, the Elvi present exchanged perplexed looks of shock and amazement as the two great leaders of their movement navigated toward dangers untold. Having just made the return journey themselves, they were acutely aware of the repugnant, nauseating swim their leaders were about to embark upon, faces silently pleading for their pregnant leader not to enter. None of them wanted to be a bystander at the death of the Blessed Mother and they desperately wanted to be somewhere far, far away. 
The easy stroll up the slight grade, belied the horror that awaited just over the rise where the floor angled sharply downward to a pool of bacteria and filth. Cracker’s eye’s felt the sting of the vapours and was already gagging when he entered the sludge. He tried to draw in a deep breath, but coughed and spluttered as tussive miasma attacked his delicate lung tissue. He managed, with some difficultly, to hold down a second deep breath and waded in, trying desperately to think of something else, anything else and hoping he would not suffer a coughing fit while fully submerged. Eyes and mouth firmly shut he swam like a blackened slime toad, through the molasses thick filth, bumping into walls and the bottoming out on the floor before finding his way, in the darkness, to the surface on the far side. The air was thick with putrid particles, methane vapours and stink of every kind. He thought the air on the other end to be sickening, but it was far worse in the little pocket, a mere 2 metre high and 5 metres long. He pulled on the rope tied around his slippery torso, drawing it hand over hand until it was tight. 
“Sarah! Sarah?” He called out but he heard no reply. “If you can hear me I made it. I will pull you through.” The rope fell slack and he placed the shit-dribbling plasti-cable over his shoulder and walked towards dead-end of the tunnel. Sarah appeared out of the ooze looking as beautiful as ever. The sludge fell away onto the ground, her skin and hair purging itself of pathogens and dirt. 
 
“Well, that was not pleasant.” Sarah smiled, teeth gleaming white against her darkened, stained skin.
“An interesting taste sensation to be sure.” Cracker’s eyes made a decent attempt at popping out of his head as he gagged and dry wretched for several minutes before squatting on his haunches, spitting up watery saliva and drooling on his chin. 
“Let’s get to work, we have a nation to save.” Sarah gently caressed her stomach, a wave of warmth and contentment pulsed through her hands and throughout her body. Well, at least he seems to be happy I am here. 
“They have really outdone themselves. I never thought we would get this far. I am so proud of my Elvi.” Cracker beamed.
“Let’s not get too excited, we have to get through a metre on concrete and then contrive a way of destroying the labs and everything stored therein, without explosives.”
The roof of the tunnel was the lumpy concrete underside of the laboratory basement floor, angry chunks of sharp aggregate jutting out into the airspace awaiting a tender scalp. At least they hoped they were under the laboratory in question. No doubt the maps and blueprints they had gathered together for this dig contained minor inaccuracies that could lead to major errors in the field. Having no other option than to trust the Elvi had excavated accurately, they scraped away the remaining clay and began drilling into the hard slab above. This concrete had been poured decades prior and had continued to harden as the years ticked by and even with giant impact drills and tungsten tipped bits the going was tediously slow.
Breathing was difficult, nigh on impossible, but somehow their bodies managed to scrape together sufficient oxygen from the creeping yellow fog to support bodily functions. Their skin began firstly to tickle, then itch and burn as the smallest of scratches allowed gaseous acid to sear into their flesh, setting it ablaze. Pregnant or not, Sarah was determined to pull her own weight, easily keeping up with her muscular male counterpart, but unlike him, she never complained. 
To minimise effort and save time it was decided that they would make the smallest access hole possible sufficient to allow Cracker entry, but insufficient to accommodate Sarah and her tumescent belly. Eight hours solid work was rewarded with full penetration into the bowels of the laboratory complex. Cracker took a torch and climbed up through the concrete slab and disappeared. 
Sarah sat quietly with the remaining torch insipidly glowing in the rank vapours that swirled about as she wheezed each lungful of needles out. The access hole lit up and Cracker’s head poked down out of the roof as she heard muffled plasma bursts and screams from behind him.
“They beat us here. The agents have taken over the facility. There are a few people in here we need to evacuate.”
“How did they beat us? Why didn’t Agent Jones warn us?”
“Ask him yourself. He’s coming with us.” Cracker jumped down, followed by an armed Agent Jones. “Hurry Up! We have to get out of here.” Sarah dived into the stench and disappeared, sliming her way to the other side where Elvi were waiting with buckets of warm water and fresh clothing. 
Professor Hermann dropped down from the tunnel roof with athletic ease as did the rest of his nimble team of subterranean survivalists Dr Anstey, Dr Millicent and Dr Ye. 
“There is no time to explain. Everyone has to jump into that raw sewage, dive 2 metres down and swim 15 metres straight ahead then two metres to the surface. It’s a giant U-bend and not unlike me, its full of shit. Can you guys hold your breath that long?”
“Easily.” Professor Hermann smugly scoffed. “We just want to get out of here.” Without delay he and his colleagues entered the mire and happily dived down into the unknown.
“They won’t be too far behind us. I’ve got one grenade and that’s all.” Agent Jones coughed deeply from the bottom of his lungs, spitting up white chunks of tissue as his legs buckled under his weight. He gasped and wheezed for breath, but could not maintain consciousness. Shuffling feet above alerted Cracker to the imminent arrival of heavily armed company agents, ordered to clear the facility with extreme prejudice. He rolled Agent Jones on his back and unclipped the small grenade hanging from his belt and tied a loop of rope around his waist. The unconscious agent was dragged, supine into the swamp of effluent, while Cracker waited until the first agents dropped out of the ceiling. He tossed the grenade and dived under, dragging Agent Jones by the rope. The size of the explosion, combined with the the tiny space the expanding gas found itself in, insisted that the pressure be released by the path of least resistance. The floor of the basement above blew upward, slamming into the underside of the floor above, killing every agent in the room, but it did not stop there. Unrelieved, the compression wave pressed upon the pool of sewage with such force that the entire reservoir of liquid shit blew around the U-bend. Out the far end of the tunnel, Cracker, Agent Jones and a few hapless Elvi along with twenty thousand litres of liquid diarrhea shot out, horizontal to the ground where human sized nuggets slopped about in the black slush.
“In the name of the Dawkins, hose them down.” The team leader ordered. “Hurry up!”
“Cracker? Which one is Cracker?” Through the black paint, Sarah could not tell who was who.
“I am the Great Teacher, Blessed Mother.” Cracker stood up, fumes visibly rising off his frame in the dimly lit building site.
“What happened in there?”
“I found survivors. They’ve been there nearly 20 years! I let them tell you how they survived, but they told me the team of agents broke into the labs a few days ago. They are systematically clearing out the lab of everything, samples, files and equipment. I found the survivors hiding out, but luckily Agent Jones found us, he helped us all get back to the tunnel, he killed a couple of his own in the process.”
“It’s over then. They’ve won. They have all they need to kill the off the Elvi.” Resignation was not something Sarah ordinarily resorted to, but she felt she had no option other than to acknowledge a dead-end.
* * *
“Hello? I am told you two are in charge here.” Professor Hermann apologetically interrupted as he continued to wiped the dark stains off his face. “Thank you for releasing us. We’ve been locked away five decades. These are my colleagues Dr Anstey, Dr Millicent and Dr Ye. And I am Professor… ” 
“Hermann? Is that you? It can’t be. He would be at least..”
“60 years old?”
“Yes.”
“I am Professor Andale Hermann, but how do you know me?”
“I am Sarah Jacobson, Alana’s daughter. Do you remember me?”
“Of course. But you would be nearly, 30 years old by now, wouldn’t you? And aren’t you just glowing”
“Not too bad for 32, don’t you think?”
“And your mother?”
“She died in the great viral cleansing, 20 years ago, along with my sister.”
“She died? I don’t understand.” He was deeply shocked by this news. He had truly expected her to see her again. “You opened the package?” 
“And drank the hemlock, Professor. I read part of the note, but I threw the rest out.”
“Why would you do something like that?” 
“I was 12 and from what I read, I took to the understanding that I had swallowed a vial full of semen. It was just inoculated water was it not?” Please just be water.
“Yes, inoculated saline solution. If you had read the whole thing you all would have been fine. Oh Alana.” The news of Alana’s demise cut him deeply, perhaps permanently. “You killed her, you know that don’t you?” 
“It is something I had not realised until very recently, Professor. Self-loathing is difficult to deflect and even more difficult to endure.” 
“You could not have known.”
“I swore if I ever saw you again I would beat you senseless. I spent years training in multiple martial arts disciplines, just in case I happened across you one day. I could not conceive of any story you could concoct that would ever excuse you for breaking my mother’s heart so cruelly.”
“Have you infected many people? By the looks of you there must be at lease one” He noted her taught little belly.
“No, believe it or not, I have remained a virgin. However, I have infected one other. Cracker here, known to some as The Great Teacher, has also been infected. I think there may be one other, but I am not sure. I think she got it off Cracker.”
“I haven’t touched anyone” Cracker protested.
“Kathy hugged you and kissed you in the morgue.”
“Ah, yes. Kissing in the morgue. Good times.” Cracker tried on sarcasm and it seemed to fit.
“She examined your injuries too and dipped her hand in your blood.”
“She has been looking mighty good lately, yeah.” Cracker finally came around. “I can’t be blamed for that, I had just come back from the dead.”
“The Hybrid T-virus brought you back from the dead?” Professor Hermann was surprised. “I never thought the virus worked that well. That is amazing.”
“I don’t think he was actually dead, just moribund.” At no stage had Sarah actually thought Cracker had died. He was dragged back from the brink, not resurrected. “The point is I have remained a virgin for 32 years because I had no idea what I had contracted. From my personal experience it is entirely positive, but is that the case for everyone?”
“The T-virus supercharges every system in the body. It slows and then reverses aging. It is a panacea for all human pathogens, no microbe can make you ill and there would be no need for any more expensive medicines. Jamison Hardy locked us in there because we would have destroyed the pharmaceuticals industry, so we have been working on other less damaging versions of the virus. We have had plenty of time to tinker and even made a virus to inoculate against the T-virus.”
“I don’t want that” Cracker protested. He was perfectly happy the way he was. “Keep it well away from me.”
“It is not a cure, once you are infected you are infected for life.”
“How are you not all insane? 20 years locked in a bunker? I wouldn’t last a week before I went nuts.” Cracker, along with everyone else, found it difficult to understand how four people could live in in such proximity for so long with succumbing to neuroses of one sort or another.
“It’s the T-virus, it has to be. I believe that its effect upon our physiology is so profound and our brain tissue is so sturdy, so perfect, that it shores up our psyche as well. Marvelous!” Dr Hermann could offer up no other explanation. According to his knowledge of psychology they should have killed each other years ago.
“What about food and water?” Sarah was still unconvinced. “20 years without food or water?”
“Each lab had its own separate air and water filtration and recirculation systems, we were never going to suffocate or die of thirst. Our biggest issue was food, but we had anticipated Mr Hardy’s reaction and had in place sufficient raw materials to grow proteins and vegetable matter in the lab. It is not something I would recommend eating. In fact, I would kill for a steak just about now.”
“Elvis?” Sarah called out. Ten or more tripped and pushed each other over as they ran to be the first to stand before the Blessed Mother. The losers of the race formed a small rabble behind the winner, still pushing and shoving. “Take Professor Hermann and his team to the High Elvis. They are to be treated as honoured guests. Get them cleaned up and fed, probably no steaks Professor, but it should be better than lab grown protein. The Great Teacher and I will meet you in Parliament House.”
“What’s going on?” Dr Anstey protested but didn’t fight, knowing she could destroy any ten of these identical drones at a whim. “Are you clones?”
“Yes, ma’am. Elvis Clones.”
“How very interesting.” She said as she took him by the arm and slowly walked away. “What exactly is an Elvis?”
“Much has changed in 20 years, professor. We were here, tonight, for a reason and we have failed in that important mission. We have to re-group and I think you may be able to provide some insight. You will be treated well and have access to the link. You can track down any relatives, but you cannot let it be known that you are alive. You will put yourselves and your family in grave danger.” Sarah did not want these people wandering off and alerting the company to their existence. “Company agents are raping your facility for the purpose of developing a viral agent to kill 100,000 people, the entire Elvi nation.” 
“I don’t know what an Elvi is, but killing a whole nation of them sounds a little excessive.” Professor Hermann was happy to help and walked off slowly with a group of Elvis helpers, looking up and stretching his arms to the sky, still not quite believing he was free.
“Morality is not a big thing, these days.” Sarah sighed.
“32 years old, eh?” Cracker called out the elephant in the room. “Do you need a hand, grandma?” 
“Very funny. See if you look so good in 10 years.” 
“I probably won’t be pregnant.” 
“No you won’t. What about him?” Sarah pointed to Agent Jones who was coughing and vomiting up black ooze through his nose and mouth. “Of those who came out of the lab complex, he is the only one not protected by the T-virus. What should we do with him. He could be infected with anything. Why didn’t they give him a bio-suit?”
“He had to take it off to fit through the hole we dug out. We will get two Elvi to take him back to their medical centre. All three will have to go into isolation until we know if they have anything nasty. Volunteers only for this one.” 
“We need to get hold of Hayley and Kathy and meet at the Elvi Parliament. Whatever we do, we have to act fast.”
“I’ll get these jokers to clean up as best they can and fix that pipe. There will be shit pouring out of the tunnel soon, unless I can get the boys here to dodgy-up a repair. Even if we are lucky, a repair like that won’t last long. I’ll meet you at parliament house as soon as I can.” Cracker pointed at the open end of the tunnel, still seeping filth.
“Make sure you have a few showers before you get there.” Sarah said holding her nose. “Take as many as you have to.”
“You don’t smell too good either.” Cracker returned fire.
* * *</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="F54E1FB1-F61E-4541-AEBF-69D2BD4BEF28">
            <Title>Hangover</Title>
            <Synopsis>Standard hangover scenario.  Some of it may be worthwhile, but not necessary to the story. 
A small description of Cracker's premises.  
Good description of Crackers face after the bombing - must stay in.  "His blackened left eye was firmly closed shut and continually seeped a dilute blood-ooze that trickled over the collection of lumps, cuts, scratches and bruises that together contrived to construct something not too dissimilar to a face."
Cracker and Sarah discover that Cracker must have picked up the gyges disc from inside the office because he is seen by surveillance video to enter the office, but not to exit.
They rifle through Crackers "spoils: of the day and find ID for Emil Kalheri and a general access chip - access to all electronic doors in the PTD.  The general access chip becomes important at a later stage.

They find the disc and decide that they should speak to someone with computer engineering/hardware expert.  Luckily Sarah knows just the guy.  However, Cracker cannot go with her as her friend is airborne and he cannot know she has been hanging out with the cloudborne.  </Synopsis>
            <Text>Hangover
Morning after Bombing -Year Zero
He awoke at home, in bed, alone. The all too familiar alcohol haze pounded an entirely unpleasant African-style beat in his head that became exponentially louder (and proportionately unpleasant) when he opened his eyes, but this proved as pointless as it was painful. One eye was almost entirely swollen over and the good one refused to focus. Eyes firmly shut he carefully untangled himself from the sheets.
He gingerly placed his feet on the carpet, but somehow the gentle clash of calloused flesh and cushioned floor was far too loud and another tribe of drummers were awoken in his head. After an earnest trial and error session he presently found a combination of moaning a lot and crawling was his best method of transport on the epic journey to the bathroom. He set up base camp at the foot of the bed and with half a bottle of water and a wet towel to slide on so he didn’t get carpet burn on his dick, he set forth to bivouac at the doorway. After only two water stops the brown, brackish liquid in the bottle was gone, but he was at the door and made the brave decision to push on and make the final assault on the bathroom. In any case, he could stay no longer, there was a real chance of his tongue would remain permanently stuck to the roof of his mouth. His choice of a wet towel proved to be a winner as the floor to the bathroom was covered in a cheap-shit-plastic faux-linoleum that managed to be both offensively ugly and dangerously slippery, dry or wet, but especially wet. He slid along, burnt and battered, inch-worming and moaning in a low mournful base tone that would have sounded really cool with an African beat. 
“Is that a weird sex thing or are you cleaning the floor?” Sarah laughed with delight, but all too loudly for Cracker. “Please tell me it’s a weird sex thing.” 
“Can you give me a bit of a hand?” Cracker managed between moans. “I think I might be dying.”
“Men such wimps. C’mon let’s get you up.” She rolled her eyes but helped him to his feet and they both made the final few steps to the bathroom where she left him to his misery. He vomited a voluminous and vile mound of something he could not readily identify and then endured an attack of the beer splatters, but before long he was showered and almost human. Well, not really too close to human. Most of the hair on the left side of his head had been burnt to black stubble and the scalp was raw and heavily blistered. His blackened left eye was firmly closed shut and continually seeped a dilute blood-ooze that trickled over the collection of lumps, cuts, scratches and bruises that together contrived to construct something not too dissimilar to a face.
As he exited the bathroom he said, “I have a couple of questions. Firstly, what the fuck happened last night? I remember the Elvis bar, but not much after that. Did I really try to eat my own face?”
Cracker found Sarah serenely curled up on the lounge, sipping a hot beverage and reading her personal com-screen. She had no injuries at all, in fact she was positively glowing and did not seem to be in any pain or discomfort, from either the expected hangover or from the unexpected explosion. 
“Yes, you bet Elvine you could eat your own face. You said it was already half cooked so it would be easy. One of the funniest things I have ever seen. In fact I have never laughed so much in my life.”
“I hate to be a little indelicate, but did we?”
“No. I am not that sort of girl.” She said with grin.
“A bit below your station perhaps?”
“Well that is a bit of a problem, but no. I am just a little old fashioned, that’s all.”
“Old fashioned? What does that mean? You’re not a virgin are you?” He joked, laughing loudly. “I don’t think I have ever met a member of your tribe.” He continued to laugh as he truly had never met an adult virgin before, certainly no-one that would lay claim to it.
“Yes I am.” She was serious.
“No-one has ever?”
“No.”
“You’ve never ridden the baloney pony?”
“No. I would suggest, however, that we focus on what happened last night rather than what did not happen. The bomb at the bar and the flooding of half the city, perhaps?”
“Are you waiting for a brave prince on a white steed?” He hoped she would laugh.
“NO!!” This time, very loudly. She then smiled, her face a picture of serenity.
“Okay. I give up.” He looked at the small computer device in her hand. “Been surfing the link, eh? What’s in the news?”
“We are, of course.” She seemed pleased. “Last night’s events have not gone unnoticed.”
 “What does it say?”
“They say the bomb at the bar was part of a coordinated attack, involving a cyber-vandalism on the water infra-structure that resulted in water being diverted away from the fire” She looked dead serious.
“But we diverted the water.”
“They are blaming it on the gentleman who got stuck the drain.”
“A stroke of luck, but he didn’t strike me as the terrorist type. Will they believe it?” Cracker hoped so.
“I think so. I accessed his file and spent all morning padding it with radical, terrorist literature and links to all sorts of nasty organisations. Last night he was a rather boring, run of the mill manic-depressive, now he’s a king-pin of world terrorism.”
“I’d call that a promotion, but I am guessing that does not solve all our problems.” He seemed as genuinely concerned as, indeed, he should have been. They were in serious shit.
“No, I don’t think so. Is this Shakti fellow trustworthy?”
“Shakti is an idiot, but he won’t name me. Not because he gives a shit about me. He is not capable of honourable sacrifice. He will keep his mouth shut because he will not admit he got help from a cleaner. He would be embarrassed and his mother would be pitiless.”
“Seriously? He’s scared of his mother?”
“Deathly. He will give them some ludicrous bullshit story and they will scratch their heads for a while assuming it is just another example of his astounding stupidity. He will stick to the story, no matter how absurd and they will finally give up. They will have to execute him or let him go and they will let him go. He is not the only person afraid of Eloise Shakti.”
“Even if he doesn’t name you, it is a fair bet that you will be spoken to at some stage. They will throw out a wide net and you are bound to be hauled on board. What are you going to say?”
“I don’t know. What are you going to say?”
“What am I going to say? Listen to me, very carefully. If I appear on their radar, it is all over for me! Management tangled up in a terrorist plot with a cleaner? Even if I could prove I had no involvement, the muck would stick and I would be ruined. More likely, however, they will execute me alongside you.” She was a serious as the plague and twice as black.
“On Shakti’s show no less. Maybe if I act dumb.”
“Yeah, you might be able to pull that off.” She was now smiling brightly, not a trace of anger. “I think we may have another problem.”
“Oh, yeah? How so?” questioned Cracker, intrigued and a little concerned. He didn’t think he could get into any more trouble.
“Just after you left my office, a company agent came in and demanded to see Lee’s files. He was quite insistent and was more than a little threatening.” She showed little concern that the man had obviously meant her serious harm. “I don’t think he was after files, though.”
“A company agent? Bullshit. They lurk in the shadows don’t they? I don’t reckon an agent would be that obvious.”
“I’ve downloaded the surveillance logs from work. He does not appear on the vid-files at all. I’ve tried reconstructing the data and I can’t seem to find a trace of him. Look.” She handed him her small device and replayed a video file. “Images from outside my office. Shuffle through it. There I am, but he never appears anywhere.”
“How?”
“He must have had some device that corrupted the surveillance devices.” She explained, in as simple terms as she could, but the technology behind this device, if it existed, was infinitely more nuanced and incomprehensible.
“I’m not seeing me leave your office.”
“Let me see that.” Sarah snatched the screen and scrolled the video back and forth. She shuffled right back to when Cracker had originally arrived. There he was in his finery. She noted his pants were yet to split and had a quiet chuckle. Indeed he had entered her office, but according to the surveillance vid, he had never left. The lift doors had opened, but no one got in or got out. “You know what this means?”
“Yep. I noticed something you didn’t.”
“Okay, there is that.” She was kind of hoping he wouldn’t mention that. It was pretty obvious when she thought about it. Maybe this guy wasn’t such an idiot. She looked up and saw that he seemed to be doing some sort of victory dance.
“I’m smarter than you!” As he spoke his body suddenly reminded him that he had, last night, survived an explosion and was currently suffering the worst effects of a night with the Elvi. He crumpled onto the floor and let out a mournful cry. “And I did it with a hangover.” He managed to wheeze before defaulting to the foetal position. 
“Real smart, yes. What it means is you picked up the device when you were cleaning my office! Where did you put it?”
“I haven’t gone through yesterday’s spoils yet. They’re in the bag over there.” He looked around, but there was no bag. “Or is it there? Nope. I put it somewhere. Maybe Andrea took it.”
“You must find it.”
“Try the kitchen…..under the sink.” He closed his eye and slept for what could have been two minutes or two hours. He awoke in a panic, fearing she would be gone, but to his amazement she was still there hovering over the kitchen table, all manner of refuse neatly separated, categorized into piles.
“Hey there, back with the living I see. That was a long snooze, I’ve been home and back while you’ve been out.” She seemed cheerful as she pointed to a small unremarkable circular disc, no bigger than a thumbnail and wafer thin. Its smooth silver surface glistened enticingly, begging to be picked up for a closer examination. “It is amazing. There is no power source, it must pick up and amplify ambient electro-magnetic fields.” She guessed.
“It is certainly compact. What do we do with it?” Cracker wanted to keep it himself. “They will be looking for it, won’t they?”
“The company will move hell and earth to get it back.” She then lobbed a battered black wallet on the table. “What other mischief have you been up to? This belongs to a labourer name Emil. Where on earth did you get this?”
“I found it on a dead body. He was lying on the footpath at the base of your building. He was fair game.” He thought to make up a convoluted story, but he didn’t want things getting any more complicated.
“You stole it off him?” She was disgusted by the thought.
“I don’t have the luxury of ignoring opportunities like that.’ He was not sorry he took the wallet or anything else he had taken in his time. Stealing from the dead was not one of his finest moments, but it was far from his worst.
“A stroke of luck, as it turns out. It contained this genuine Termocorp general access chip! Can you believe it?”
“Shit. General access. That’ll open every door in the Dominion.” 
“Maybe, but they will be looking for that too. It may have already expired, but the only way to know is to use it.”  
“What’s a labourer doing with it?” He found that a little peculiar.
“That is the question. While you were passed out I did a bit of digging. Emil Kalheri died yesterday of testicular cancer. He spent his entire life working as a labourer, he had a wife and two offspring. You say you saw him dead at the foot of my building? That is interesting, because according to this he died quietly at home among family. A labourer could not possibly have gotten a visa for the business district without a work permit. Don’t ask, I have already checked – no work permit. So how did he get there and why was he there?”
“Can you give that access chip a top up?”
“No, it’s hard wired. Once it has expired it’s useless, but it can’t be cancelled. I can check where it has been and something tells me it was used to enter my office building.”
“What does?”
“Do you remember what Shakti said last night?”
“I don’t recall too much.” He fibbed as he tried to work up a laugh, but couldn’t. 
“The Commissioner told Shakti that Lee was murdered. Why was the Commissioner think it was murder? Why was he involved at all? It has got to be well below his rank .”
“The Commissioner wouldn’t ordinarily involve himself with operational matters. If he did, why would he send Shakti? He must have known he would fuck it up.” Added Cracker, finding it amusing to be involved in an adult conversation for once. “Yes, he must have known.”
“It’s all a bit strange and we both seemed to be wrapped up in it.” She was perplexed but didn’t seem concerned at all. When she began to consider the tangled mess she found herself in, she felt thrilled, light headed. Before meeting Cracker her world was one of commerce and number crunching and now she was immersed in an alternate reality of intrigue and murder. 
“What are we doing with the disc?” He already had all sorts of little capers planned. Electronic invisibility would open up many lucrative opportunities for Cracker.
“I had better keep it.” Sarah heard a grunt of disapproval. “So you can forget any wicked plans you’ve got cooked up”. She picked up the disc and continued. “Yes, I can see this thing has lit up the dark vaults in that slimy alcohol sponge you call a brain.”
“It’s such a temptation, just to test it out.”
“I will get it looked at, I know a tech who would love to get his hands on one of these.” She was smiling widely. “This is all very subversive isn’t it?” She giggled and made a vid-call, while she tried to place the disc on the table, but it stuck to her finger. She fruitlessly tried to gently flick it away, but it merely swapped from finger to thumb and back again. Cracker took it from her an momentarily stared at the prize, but placed it down when Sarah glared.
“Hello?” The bald head with a fat face on the screen said. “Oh, hey, Sarah. What’s up?”
“Johnny. I would like to see you sometime today. What are your plans?” Sarah said in a friendly, but business-like tone. 
“You know me, I always have a full dance card, but I think I can fit you in.”
“You have no plans do you?” Sarah said with certainty.
“None whatsoever. Just tell me where and when, my girl.” Johnny happily admitted.
“It will have to be very discreet.” She then muted the call and turned to Cracker, “What’s the name of that bar we went to last night?”
“Graceland.” Cracker said, but he was shaking his head vigorously. “The Memphis Mafia Bar and Grill is better and hidden away.”
“Have you heard of the Memphis Mafia Bar and Grill?” She said, now looking at the phone. 
“No, but I will find it. What Sector?” Johnny asked. 
“It’s in the Entertainment District – Street Level.” 
“Street Level? You can’t be serious. I have never been to street level and have no intentions of going there today, even for you Sarah.” Johnny was not one to hide his outrage. He had heard the inhabitants at street level had covered the ground with a stinking, sludgy substance called mud. Why would they do that?
“Okay, where then? There can be no security or surveillance.” 
“What? Is there anywhere in the dominion that has no surveillance?” replied Johnny, quite curious now.
“Yes, in the entertainment district, street level!” Sarah snapped back. “Memphis Mafia Bar and Grill, one o’clock.”
“Okay, okay. This better be good. How do you get to street level anyway?” Johnny really did not know.
“You’ll work it out.” Sarah ended the call.
“Was that your boyfriend?” Cracker asked, with incredulity.
“Johnny? God’s of bankruptcy, no!! He’s just a friend. There will never be romance there. I can’t tell you where he works, but he’s a hardware specialist. He could make a computer from a pile of sand and some wire.”
Cracker was immensely relieved to hear she was not romantically linked this man. He knew Sarah had no interest in a mere cleaner, but he didn’t like the thought of someone else having her. “What sort of a grown man calls himself Johnny?” He made no attempt to hide the contempt.
“He is a very smart man and if I can concoct a decent enough story he may be of some assistance.”
“But Johnny?” He let out a loud quite obviously false laugh. “The Johnny.” 
“That is the very definition of the pot calling the kettle black.” She was smiling, but didn’t seem happy.
“What are you going to tell him?” Asked Cracker, making a mental note not to ridicule her fag mates again.
“I don’t know yet, but definitely not the full truth.”
“If he knows too much he will get himself or us killed.” Cracker wanted tell her he had no problem with Johnny-boy getting himself killed, but managed to control the impulse. He had already decided to hate him.
“You cannot meet Johnny. It will be difficult enough to justify meeting in a street-level dive, but there is no way he will understand a level 5 socialising with the working class. He would be disgusted and would not keep his mouth shut.” 
“Sounds trustworthy.” Cracker noted, words dripping in sarcasm.
“In my world he is about as trustworthy as they get.” This was not something Sarah was happy to admit. No matter what circle of friends or colleagues she found herself with, she could trust no one, ever. Friend or otherwise, it was rare to find anyone who would fail to report even a mere whiff of moral unorthodoxy. She recalled a lesson from her youth - A friend is someone who will shit on you sometime in the future, generally the near future. Words to live by.
“Okay, so you don’t want me to go. I am too much of an embarrassment.” The truth tasted bitter.
“Yes.” Sarah knew she had wounded him, but his feelings would have to wait. She was too preoccupied with the prospect of meeting with Johnny and the risk it represented. She would work on the assumption that he was untrustworthy and hoped to have in place a method of manipulating him, a species of blackmail perhaps, but at the present moment she could not think of thing.</Text>
            <Notes>Fun hangover description
Cracker is named as a terroist
They mention Shakti
Stuff about Eloise
Stuff about the disc has already been handled
Will the Termocorp general access chip be required later?</Notes>
