        <Document ID="9456A39D-3811-43F5-A5F4-A6ED4E75283C">
            <Title>A hot time in the ol</Title>
            <Synopsis>The start chapter is pretty woeful.  Very poor exposition of a couple breaking up.  This bit can probably be dumped, but Crackers (then) girlfriend is later murdered by the "Nightclub Vampire" and he is arrested for the murder.  (Later when Cracker is being interviewed for the murder by Shakti, it turns out that Shakti is his alibi).
The important thing here is that Sarah tracks down Cracker at a nightclub (The Stick it Inn - must have a name change) which is then bombed.  In the nightclub you get to meet some of his friends including "Halo"  (Later found to be Haloren Smythe - Airborne operative of upper management that picks up girls and kills them for fun - The Nightclub Vampire)  He searches for a girl, approaches them and is then out the door with them, quick smart) Halo says, "Love? Love is the stuff that jumps out the end of your cock."
After the bombing they have to escape the area, otherwise risk automatic arrest.  They have a little adventure walking through some dangerous areas of town.  

They are burnt and injured and decide they will be easy for the cops to ID so they have to get cleaned up and use a public bathing facility.  Pretty Gross.  
There are gays and lesbians harrassing people at the entrance of the  - this should be removed - too cliched and silly.
The storyline here is not set in stone.  I need some major event that will require a response by the cops.  The flooding of the public bathing area results in water being diverted away from the fire and the airborne were drinking recycled water.  It is a bit silly and when you read it you will roll your eyes at certain points (or at all points).  I was always worried about this bit.  A great deal of this can be removed or replaced.
</Synopsis>
            <Text>
A hot time in the ol’ town 
4 hours to time of bombing
If he was expecting rest when he finally got home, Cracker was sadly mistaken. Andrea, the live-in pain in the backside, went straight for the throat. “You fucking low-life sack of shit, lying son of…….” Uncharacteristically, she ran out of verbal abuse quickly and opted for extreme violence delivered in the form of a knee to the groin. Considering this insufficient to gain Cracker’s attention, she followed up with a sharp kick to the face. Reeling backwards and again ruing his decision to pursue this relationship, his pained voice groaned, “Okay, I give up. What have I done this time?” 
“It’s what you haven’t done. You said you were going to put our names up for allocation. I need some stability and I need commitment.” Punctuating her need for stability by kicking the antique hat-stand, snapping the main shaft clean in half. 
“Jesus Christ, that’s real wood. Fuckin’ actual wood, for fuck's sake.” He had never been so completely aghast in his life. “Why would you want to break a piece of real wood – genuine cellulose fibre grown from a seed?” He knew she could be violent and vitriolic, but not wantonly destructive, not like this. It wasn’t the first time he had been hit by her, but his furniture had always survived unscathed. “Okay, I’ll put our names down tomorrow if you want, but if we are allocated now, we will never make level 3.” 
“Too late. I decided on an insurance policy this time and applied for random allocation to level three.” She beamed. 
“That’ll take forever, if it happens at all.” He didn’t really know how long it would take, but it was the only thing that came to mind. 
This was a moment for Andrea to savour. A pregnant pause and then she flicked on the holo-screen and scrolled down to the latest random allocation approvals. There was her name, Andrea Novetine, allocated to James Marrin BComSc L.3, Information Integrity Manager. “I’m moving up, boy. A Cleaner? What was I thinking?” She laughed to herself and then added the ex-girlfriend’s obligatory parting cliche, “Oh, yeah. I faked them all.” 
“That is childish and clichéd.” Cracker shot back, but her barb had hit the mark. 
“It also happens to be true. At least that’s what I’ll be posting on the link. Tony Hardy is a dud.” She laughed and having already prepared her bags, walked out the door with not another word said. Satisfied that she had struck a blow for females everywhere and the sisterhood would be pleased. 
Just in case she came back, Cracker waited a reasonable period before arranging celebratory drinks with his three best friends. He would have copped a knee in the balls long ago if he knew that was all it took for her to leave. He was not the first to arrive at “Stick it Inn” and hoped they had got a good table near to the bar and away from the gaming machines. Cracker didn’t mind a bit of a flutter, but he wanted to be out of earshot of the gaming so as to concentrate on celebrating or, more accurately, drinking. He was busting to tell his mates about Sarah. Just thinking about her made his heart palpitate in a dangerously irregular fashion. 
The "Stick it Inn" was an ‘open’ premises and allowed entry to all and sundry, no matter what level. It was nice in theory, but dress regulations were so strict that it was virtually impossible for levels one and two to get in, but Cracker knew the security staff, all corrupt ex-cops and could get in without too many problems. They were all waiting for him when he got inside. The table wasn’t too bad, close enough to the dance floor to gawk and far enough away, so you could talk over the music. Halo was already in position, looking straight out, unblinking and continually scanning, the crowd of revelers with his maniacal 1000 yard stare.
“Found her yet?” Cracker asked of Halo who did not acknowledge his presence. 
“He’s in search and destroy mode. He won’t be talking for a while.” Major interjected. “He’s trying to spot his next ex-missus.” 
“C’mon Halo, say something,” pleaded Abe. “We’re here to support poor Cracker in his hour of need.” 
“No, it doesn’t matter. I met the perfect girl today. You’d crawl across 3 k’s of broken...” Cracker tried to say but was cut off by a cacophony of protest.
“Why is it that every time you meet a girl you want to crawl across broken glass and suck a dick?” Abe managed to say above the din.
“Yeah, it’s too fucking old.” Major said with unrestrained frustration.
“What do you think Halo?” Abe said, trying to deflect the unwanted attention.
Halo dropped his eyes to the table and then transfixed his icy stare upon Abe. “All level ones must die.” He growled in a gravel-monotone. Dead silence, then hysterical laughter from all but Halo. 
“Fuck you pal, I made it to level 2 a while ago. We’re all the same now.” Abe shot back with glee.
“How depressing.” The deadpan Halo sneered, rolling his eyes and returned to his surveillance duties. Halo was not the sort of bloke to be bothered with small talk or much talk at all for that matter. He said as little as possible, but he was clearly a formidable intellect and what he said was generally worth hearing. His pale blue eyes sported pinpricks for pupils and the constant back and forth of his head left him resembling a psychotic radar array. His gaze cut through the crowd until he located tonight’s object of his affections. 
“2 o’clock” Halo announced and he was off into the throng, leaving Cracker, Abe and Major at the table. Halo rarely missed out when he spotted ‘the one’ for the night. It was usually a matter of approaching the target, banal getting to know you talk and then they were out the door so fast that, on occasion, she would leave her coat and handbag. His target selection methodology was a complete mystery to his friends. They had asked a million times what he said to get them to go home with him, but he would never divulge the secret. He wanted to be the only one that knew the short cut to a woman’s heart. However, on this occasion Halo’s radar had led him astray, his target selection proved imprecise and he returned to the table, tail between his legs in double quick time.
“Dykes. They’re all fucking dykes.” He complained as he resumed his seat, noting that, being shot down so quickly, the it was still warm. 
“What happened big fella? Get knocked back for once?” Cracker was not entirely sympathetic, finding himself glad that some women had taste. 
“The dyke reckons she is looking for someone.”
“A woman is not a lesbian because she doesn’t want to dance with you.” Cracker shot back.
“I never mentioned dancing. Have you ever seen me dance?”
“No, can’t say that I have. What did you say then?”
“She shut me down before I said a word.” Said Halo, openly shocked and saddened. 
“How are you ever going to find love?” Cracker said with a large helping of sarcasm, knowing Halo had no interest in such a trivial fixation.
“Love? Love is the stuff that jumps out the end of your cock,” said Halo with absolute sincerity. “Oh fuck, it’s her.”
The entire table turned as one and saw a tall blonde standing directly behind Cracker, the swarm of dancing bodies impolitely, but rhythmically, bumping her lightly. She looked over Cracker’s shoulder, directly into his eyes, faces only a few centimeters apart. “Hey, steam-boy. I’ve been looking for you.” Sarah glided gracefully into a chair and smiled, genuinely it seemed, at Cracker.
Cracker was completely dumbfounded. It was Sarah, she was seeking out his company on the same day his hand-brake had bailed. These sorts of things only happened in contrived vids. Now was his chance, all he had to do was not make a fool of himself. A tall order he silently admitted. When it came to women, it was only occasionally that he managed not to stick his foot in his mouth and even when he did have the odd victory, they quickly dropped off the map, never to return his calls. The uncomfortable silence at the table was becoming, well, uncomfortable. Think of something Cracker. Quickly. He thought excruciatingly slowly as beads of sweat rapidly formed rivulets across his brow, coursing a torrent down his temples. 
“Nice conversation, boys.” Sarah decided to break the stalemate, the turning to Halo she stared him down, “Love is a defence mechanism created by women to alleviate the guilt produced when they wake up with people like you.”
Halo chuckled quietly. “You’re a thinker aren’t you? Please forgive my crudeness.” I will kill you one day soon, bitch.
“Consider it done.” A gentle smile returned to her face. She turned to Cracker, “Are you going to buy a lady a drink?” 
“Yes.” They all blurted in concert and as if choreographed, left the table and started for the bar, but stopped and turned toward her.
“Beer.” She stated sternly. “No lolly-water for me, thank you.” They all beamed enormous grins as it had long been established among their number, that beer drinking is the hallmark of any truly great woman. They eyed each other furtively and started for the bar, as if to race. She leaned out and grabbed Cracker by the shirttail. “You may as well stay. Three drinks at a time is my limit.”
He smiled as he returned to his seat. Wow, she does want to talk to me.
 “What’s with your friend?”
“Halo? He’s got anger issues. If he doesn’t get the woman every time, he gets upset.”
“The woman?”
“Yeah, he will sit for hours staring into the crowd and then decides on a target, as he calls it. Then after a few minutes, he’s off with them. You are truly a rare find.” It felt a bit odd having to explain the predatory behaviour of his acquaintances. “It is good for him to be sat on his backside every so often. I’m not like him, though, I’m more of ….”
“An aspiring comedian?” She laughed, recalling the shroud of steam earlier that morning. “You are definitely not like him. You’re nice.” She truly meant it. 
“Keep it down will ya? I don’t want it advertised. Being nice is not a virtue in this society.”
“Social commentary and I thought I had just come out for a couple of drinks.” Even with the odd intelligent comment, Sarah could not fathom why this amiable young man was so important. She enjoyed his company, but he was not likely to change the world or alter its direction, even slightly.
“Don’t get too excited. That’s about as deep as I get.”
“Don’t you want to get deeper?” Her eyes looking directly in his, releasing a flood of adrenalin into Cracker’s system. 
“Don’t do that to me.” He said, looking to his lap, fearing another embarrassing display. “I would like to retain some dignity.”
“What?” Feigning hurt feelings, but controlling the direction of the conversation precisely.
“Who’s acting dumb now, huh?” 
“You know Halo’s eyes are R.E.A. don’t you?” She said.
“What? Genetically enhanced? Bullshit, how could his parents afford that?”
“They are not really genetically enhanced, but R.E.A. is equally expensive. Each eye has what amounts to a miniature vid screen grafted onto the cornea which gives him control over the colour and pattern of his eyes. How do you think he gets all the women? I hate to say it, but some girls actually do have daddy issues.”
“I did notice some peculiarities with my last girlfriend.”
“This is how it works. Your friend can cause the corneas of his eyes to change colour and pattern randomly until he hits upon a pattern the woman unconsciously recognises. His eyes are set to default to a mirror image of hers. If she’s a narcissist she will fall for herself, but usually a after a few variations he’ll stumble across dear old dad’s corneal pattern or perhaps on old boyfriend’s or girlfriend’s for that matter. Once the unconscious recognition is made, she has little or no chance. She is blind-sided by her own neuroses.”
“No way.” Then after some thought, “I suppose it makes some sense. Those eyes are pretty weird and he is very intense”
“I saw him approaching me and I could tell. I have seen those exact eyes before when I was at school. The teachers abused half the student body before anyone cottoned on. I was lucky, apparently I don’t have any major issues, daddy or otherwise.” Which is amazing considering what happened with mum’s boyfriend. Gross! 
“He is going to be so pissed off when he finds out I know. This will be sweet.”
They both were giggling when the boys returned with the drinks sans Halo. Major held up his right hand as if to Hail Caesar, “Before you ask, I think Halo has left with his latest victim.” 
They all looked towards the bar when it happened. They were later told the magnitude of the explosion, but at the time, it felt like a snap of the fingers and then silent black. The moans of the dying did not commence for an extraordinary length of time. Some in the dark silence feared they were already dead and found comfort in any noise. Sarah had not been out for long when dragged from the rubble by the blackened figure. He had clearly been inside when it happened, a sooty smoldering and bloody mess. 
 “Have you seen Cracker? You have to find Cracker. He’s important….” Her calm facade temporarily replaced by panic as her head and eyes darted about, desperately looking for Cracker.
“Hey, it’s me.” Cracker coughed. “Don’t worry about anything. I’m not letting anything happen to you. Let’s just get out of here.” 
“What about the others?”
“They are gone.”
“Good. Let’s follow.”
“No, they are gone.” 
“What? Oh, Oh dear.” She said, realizing where they had gone.
Locking arms, they steered through the acrid smoke, blazing wreckage and black faced zombies before finally making it to the street, hobbling and wincing.
“We can’t stay here. There’ll be mass arrests as soon as the cops arrive.” Cracker wisely suggested.
“They won’t dare arrested me, I’m level 5.” She snapped with indignant ignorance.
“Good for you. You can wave me goodbye when I’m whisked away. They’ll arrest anyone found at a terrorist incident. Trust me, we don’t want to be here.”
“Okay, but you need medical treatment. Holy tax audit! Just look at you” His hair had been burnt to his skull, his bloody face was blackened and skin already peeling.
He was going to fire back a comment on her appearance too, but even with filthy blood matted hair and sooty makeup she was still stunning. How can it be? Profoundly distracted by her radiance, it took a few seconds to compose himself. “No, the infirmaries are the first place they will look. I’ve got a medical kit at home….unless you want to go to yours?”
“I think you would be closer, besides, you can’t visit me, you’re you know…..level 2.” She tried desperately to soften the blow, but in reality, he could not get within 1000 metres of her district. He did not have a valid visa and even if he did make it through security, it would only take one false comment, one moment of ignorance for him to betray himself as an outsider – a low. If she was caught bringing him into the Management District she would lose her job, her apartment and her standing in society. This was not something she, or anyone else, would contemplate doing. Ordinarily this truth would have burrowed directly through Cracker’s thin skin, piercing his soft sensitive core, however, the mere presence of this woman caused him to forget his irrational abhorrence of the natural order. Instead, Cracker smiled warmly and led the way, both lightly slapping each other’s smoldering apparel as they went. 
Within minutes of the blast, the entire district was swarming with uniforms, all seething at the news of yet another terrorist attack. Anyone unlucky enough or dim-witted enough to be in the area were immediately detained and, injuries notwithstanding, dragged off for interrogation. Healthy detainees rarely survived the rigors of interrogation, making the likelihood of the injured surviving, some horribly disfigured, virtually zero. It was okay though, deaths were easily attributed to injuries received during the blast. Interrogators could really go to town on the injured - club three colours of shit out of them and they were legally covered. A win–win situation, all things considered. 
As a rule, walking the streets at night was an extraordinarily dangerous escapade, but with the the cops out to lock up all and sundry, the danger trebled. Cops created havoc wherever they went and it was always wise to give them a wide berth. With no taxis allowed to enter the destruction zone, Sarah and Cracker would have to leg it and take their chances. On the street, robbery, rape and murder were the principal activity for unemployed and uneducated and if they thought escaping a terrorist’s bomb was an incredible feat, plotting a course through this maze would be a spectacular deed of skill, bravery and dumb luck. 
Darting from shadow to shadow, occasionally tripping over the homeless (or were they dead?) they made it to relative safety. They were safe from the cops here, but so was everyone else including the gangs. Luckily, local holigans, attracted by carnage, gravitated towards the bomb-site and were locked up as terrorists. Compared to the insane outbreak of human activity they had just eluded, the streets here were dead. Sarah had spent less than 1 hour at street level in her entire life and, although the smell was appalling, she found herself thrilled at the prospect of running from the cops through dangerous gang territory. She could not remember being so….Alive was the only word she could come up with. Alive. That’s it. It was very strange, she absently thought, that the Moral Compliance Directorate never picked upon this element of her psyche. Risk seeking was fun and she wanted more.
“What?” Cracker asked, apparently confused.
“What?”
“You said – Alive.” 
“Oh, yes. Glad to be alive. Thank you for getting me out. I am not sure I would have made it.” She was still slightly lost in thought. 
“I was not going to let you die in that appalling mess” Cracker was close to sobbing, thoughts of his friends flashing in his head and was about the break out into an embarrassing display when she slapped him on the back, hard. Knocked sideways, Cracker gasped for breath and as he regained his footing, he also gathered his composure. He did not feel like crying anymore. She is fantastic. She knows exactly what to do. 
“You were still on fire.” She said, concocting a face-saving lie for Cracker. Gazing into his dirty, blood-spattered face, she knew he had appreciated the gesture and that pleased her greatly. She slipped her hand in his and, momentarily, they both gripped tightly.
 “We have got to get cleaned up somehow. We’re never going to make it looking like this but we can’t get into the public baths around here. We don’t have the access.” Cracker said sounding rather urgent.
“I think I can get us in” She interrupted and raised her free hand, as she pointed to the sleazy public dorm complex opposite. Disengaging her hand from his, she walked swiftly to the entry panel and entered a code. Immediately the doors opened and she smiled and wandered in. Cracker remained dead still, astonished she could bypass security so easily. She motioned him to the door and they both slipped inside. Although the dorm’s interior was teeming with hundreds, perhaps thousands, of people Cracker slid along the nearest wall, trying desperately to remain in surveillance blind spots. Sarah was not concerned at all and said to the covert Cracker, “I’ve disabled the security video so you can walk with me if you like.” 
“How the hell did you do that?” 
“I have a certain expertise in cyber-security. The security has facial recognition capabilities, I can’t be seen in here. They’re would be too many questions”
In the stark light of the dorm’s interior, their scorched hair and blackened faces caused the throngs of residents to ogle as they meandered through the maze of corridors and atriums until they hobbled to the public hygiene facility. 
“Make sure you block the drain before you start showering. The recyc-units filter out and analyse contaminants – they will notice any explosive residue. Don’t unplug it - let the next guy explain” Sarah, said somberly. 
“Okay. Block the drain. Anything else?” How am I going to manage that? 
“Yes. I think you had better watch your backside. Have a look at the reception committee.” Sarah pointed ahead to the group of middle-aged overweight gentlemen dressed in only fluorescent man-kinis, indicating they had found the entrance to the male facilities. 
One of the men placed his hand between his sagging man-boobs and over his heart, “Look girls, he’s smoking. That is smoke, isn’t it?” There was no doubt Cracker was about to become the centre of some unwanted and possibly painful attention. Cracker hesitated at entering the doorway – suddenly getting clean had lost appeal.
“I am not too sure about this.” Cracker knew that sexual assault was an unpleasant fact of life in the public dorms, but not nearly as prevalent as it had been when private security had been employed to protect the residents. Like priests released among altar boys, the security guards were merciless in satisfying increasingly sick desires - raping and abusing their charges with impunity. “I’m not too sure about this at all.”
“Don’t be a baby.” said an unsympathetic Sarah. “Are you a virgin?” 
“Yes, I am and it is my fondest hope that I remain that way. I’ve even got a No-Entry sign tattooed back there.” Then grinning he pointed to a group of women who were all presently undressing Sarah with their eyes. “Looks like you have a fan club too.”
The group of agitated females blocking the entrance to the Ladies were equally as frightening as the men. Very large women, all of whom had a crew cut and permanent scowl etched in crows feet. Sarah was not at all intimidated, walking towards her fate with dignity and authority, head held high. The pack grinned knowingly, shooting elated glances between each other and then parted, allowing Sarah unhindered access. Two of the biggest and ugliest followed quickly, giving each other high-fives as they entered. 
Cracker still had not worked up the courage to approach the men who were now beckoning him to enter. He was now debating whether to try to save Sarah from the girls, even though it was certain they would beat him senseless. Nevertheless, he began to sprint to Sarah’s aid, but was stopped in his tracks when both female predators flew horizontally out of the door, landing supine and unconscious. Sarah followed close and looking gravely at the rest of the crew. Her stare easily dispersed them. She then approached the fluoro-men, glowering and simply said, “He is not to be touched. Do you understand?” A few replied unenthusiastically. 
“Do you understand?” She said, this time, yelling so unbearably loud, all ears in a 100-metre radius were left ringing. It was inconceivable that such a roar could emanate from so tiny a vessel, but it did.
“Yes, Yes.” was the synchronized reply. All nodding their heads in, seemingly, choreographed unison.
“Good. I think you should go home now.” She said. This time she was quite reserved, her voice even and softly spoken and more disturbing than the deafening rage she had just liberated. Her dominion over emotion was astonishing, almost machine-like in precision. The fluorescent gang retreated in shame and with no decorum. Cracker laughed hysterically as they ran in concentric insanity, trying desperately to get away, bumping and shoving to facilitate escape. 
“Is there a collective noun for gay men?” Cracker managed to ask through the chuckles.
“A wad?” Sarah immediately shot back.
“What about lesbians?”
“I don’t know. I’ll get back to you on that one. Now where were we? Oh yes – showers.” She motioned to the now empty entrances.
“Okay see you in five.”
“Five? I’ll be longer than that.”
“You can only consume a maximum of 20 litres and that costs a fortune, unless you take the bottom rung,” Cracker said.
“Bottom rung?”
“You’ll find out.” He said with a devious smirk.
 Although the facilities were reasonably clean, Sarah was aghast at what she saw. The lack of tiles and adornments did not faze her. It was the showers. Hundreds of batteries of tiny shower cubicles, not unlike cages, stacked ten high, clung to the walls. Water fell from giant elongated shower-heads above the top tier only and then spilled down through the grates to the cubicles below. The top tier showers received clean water direct from recyc, but by the time it reached the bottom rung it was a cold opaque brown sludge of dirt and urine. Showering up top was by far the most attractive option, but the cost was so prohibitive most could only partake on special occasions. Before taking the ladder to the top rung - there was no other option - Sarah had to locate and block the massive floor waste drain on the main floor. It wasn’t too hard to follow the rivers of mire to where they merged and disappeared, but the drain was massive. Far too big to block off and even if she could the room would flood in minutes. She had not predicted the enormity of the facilities or the vast amounts of water circulating through the filtration system. She had envisaged blocking up the floor waste with a face washer, just as she had done as a child when she had made shallow, tepid little baths in the shower recess. This is certainly very different from the en-suite at home.
She now needed to redirect the water away from filtration. The control room was unmanned and may as well have been unlocked as, at least for Sarah, the codes were child’s play. Redirecting the water was disturbingly easy, but due to ignorance of the water infrastructure she had no idea where the water went. Although there was no time to drag her feet, she made a few entries, cranked the water temperature up 5 degrees and treated everyone to a 200-litre shower.
Sighs and groans of enjoyment emanated from the shower cubicles, heralding the arrival of genuinely hot water. There were probably only a couple of hundred showering at the time, but each was having the most extravagant wash of their lives and were pleasantly surprised when the water did not automatically switch off after the standard 2 minutes. Sarah climbed to the top and undressed, piling her smoky, scorched apparel on the floor. She stepped into the cubicle and entered bogus identification codes causing water to descend from the massive shower outlet above. I hope Cracker remembers the code, there can no trace. After about 10 minutes, the water shut off and a blast of warm air shot at her from above, within a minute she was completely dry, but with outrageously unkempt hair that would later take a tub of gel to tame. She considered the pile of malodorous clothes and with some artful ripping and tearing, she managed to remove most of the blackened material and then dressed, garments inside out.
When she emerged Cracker was waiting, his coat discarded, the shirt beneath had been protected from the flames, showing little sign of the trauma occasioned to the rest of his attire. Although her hair was a bit out of shape, Cracker saw she was back to her stunning best, even without the aid of any cosmetics and her clothes seemed to be laundered. She was truly marvelous – seriously intelligent, beautiful beyond belief, great sense of humour and resourceful. Her injuries seemed to have almost healed - grazes and scorches had nearly gone and the deeper wounds were no more than rapidly evaporating thin pink lines, whereas his injuries were still swelling up, oozing blood and pulsating. The only fissure he could find in her perfect veneer was the fact that she seemed to like hanging around with him, a level 2 lowlife. He had always found difficulty in respecting women dumb enough to like him. What could she be playing at?

“A clam” She said cryptically.
“Huh?”
“A clam of lesbians. What do you think?”
“Wouldn’t it be a bearded clam?” 
“No, too low-brow” She said with abrupt certainty.
“Ouch, so much for wit.” He said, faintly disappointment. 
“You’re much better at slap-stick.” She said, casually reaching out and brushing Cracker’s disheveled dark locks to one side. The touch of her delicate, manicured fingertips was a tantalizing torment to Cracker, unleashing waves of warm pleasure and shrill pain. She would not love him; she had a real life and a real career ahead of her. He was beneath her. The thought that she would only ever see him as a hick-cousin or a retarded sibling was immediately confirmed when she fastened down a disorderly tress with a salivary thumb. 
“You don’t know me that well.” He said, smiling and gently taking the offending hand and looking deeply into her eyes searching for the smallest hint of interest, but there was nothing. She seemed completely neutral and, in fact, more concerned with gluing down his disobedient fringe. Still smiling, he returned the hand to its owner who immediately licked it and recommenced sliming his hair.
“No, no. I must protest,” he said, smile gone. “On behalf of every boy who has gone to school with his mother’s spit in his hair, for every child who discovered the price of Christmas is having to kiss mustachioed aunts and goateed grandmothers. For kids who have endured endless uncomfortable afternoons on the lap of bachelor uncles or imbibed the special odour of stale cigarette and beer fumes produced in the arms of dear old granddad, I protest.” 
Not knowing what to say Sarah remained mute, mouth slightly agape. 
His voice now rose, slightly and with his chest puffed out, he continued the rant. “No-one, alive or dead has ever wanted someone to spit in their hair. Most consider it an insult. Don’t get me wrong, I have no objection to the exchange of bodily fluids. Just when it’s consensual.” Unable to maintain the façade a sardonic smile snaked across his face. 
“I actually thought you were serious for a second.” She said slapping him, lightly for a change. Then, after a moment of thought, she said, “You’ve been practicing that, haven’t you?”
“You have no idea how long I have waited for the opportunity”
“You’re a fruit-loop.”
Changing topic quickly he said, “Thanks for saving me, by the way. Those G-String boys don’t usually take no for an answer.” What’s a fruit-loop?
“Consider it pay back for getting me out of the nightclub.”
“Good, we’re even, then.”
“No, not quite. You saved my life. I only saved your backside.” Thinking this was hysterical; she set free a colossal unladylike belly laugh and slapped Cracker on his right butt cheek, a bright but fleeting glint of desire in her eyes. Cracker didn’t notice, though, he was too busy rubbing his stinging rump. 
“Trust me, you saved my life. I would rather die than be raped. You don’t owe me a thing.” He said, profoundly grateful he did not go 12 rounds with the bathroom bowel bandits.
“Well, I think I at least owe you a drink. I boorishly dropped the last ale you gave me. One second I had a drink and the next my hair was on fire!” 
“How clumsy of you.”
“Exactly. After that embarrassing faux-pas, I feel obliged to buy you a drink.” Sarah said enjoying the silly banter far more than any dialogue with colleagues or friends. Her mind drifted the distinction between colleagues and friends, she acknowledged, was rather vague as she never socialised outside of business circles. She was unable to nail down one conversation she had had in adulthood that did not involve commerce. That’s all they ever think about, making money. This mere thought, in and of itself, was seditious and Sarah immediately purged it from her mind. Where had that come from? Money is everything, remember? 
“We should be safe in the quarter. It’s not too far out of our way.” He said happily, a huge smile exposing his unusually straight and white teeth, all his own. No mean feat considering the diet and hygiene of the lows. “I had a mongrel of a time blocking off that drain. How the hell did you manage it?”
“You blocked it off? I can’t believe you did it.” Her eyes nervously glancing at the doorway, a mere few metres behind them, but there was no sign of flooding.
“You didn’t? You were very specific, ‘Make sure you block the drains’.” Cracker used the high-pitch whiny tone that all men employ when lampooning a female voice.
“I diverted the water instead.”
“Thanks for the heads up”
“How did you manage it?”
“Chucked in anything I could find. Towels, chairs, mops and buckets and finally some crazy fat guy jumped on top of it all, shouting ‘Power to the people, comrades.’ I left when the crowd gathered.”
“A communist rally.” She said nodding knowingly. “If he gets reported, he’s as good as dead.” Impromptu or not, a communist rally was a communist rally.
“Same for us, I fear”
“A good time to make haste.”</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="9C445A89-2119-4D75-BC17-431090785A41">
            <Title>Nuclear fallout</Title>
            <Text>For fission of uranium-235, the predominant radioactive fission products include isotopes of iodine, caesium, strontium, xenon and barium. ... Many of the fission products decay through very short-lived isotopes to form stable isotopes, but a considerable number of the radioisotopes have half-lives longer than a day.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="F753051B-E1F7-4056-B155-E047B48342EA">
            <Title>The Elvi</Title>
            <Synopsis> Introduction to the Elvi.  Cracker and Sarah go to "The Quarter" to listen to see an Elvis show.  This should stay.
</Synopsis>
            <Text>The Elvi
3 hours after bombing - Year Zero

As the hover-copt took off, the three giant fans flailed about madly in effort to remain stable and in the air. These triple-blade egg-beaters had sophisticated computer assisted flight systems, without which the vessel would plummet to the ground in double quick time – assuming of course that you could get it airborne in the first place. Just like helicopters of old, these craft were actively trying to crash every airborne microsecond – even with computer assistance, flying these suckers was still a bit hit and miss. 
“Where to?” Asked the pilot. “Shakti said anywhere you want to go.” 
“Still up for that drink?” Sarah said quietly to Cracker. “We cannot let this guy know where we live.”
Cracker turned to the pilot and with the best upper crust accent he could muster requested, “To ‘The Quarter’ driver and don’t spare the horses.” He knew it wasn’t funny, but found he could not stop himself. Why do I say these things?
“The Quarter? Are you guys feeling okay?” asked the pilot incredulously. “That place is crime central.”
“Yes, we are certain. The Quarter. Where the Elvi live.” Cracker reiterated.
 “The Elvi?” Sarah was most confused.
“Not Elvy, ELV-EYE. The plural of Elvis.” He just got a blank stare. “Never heard of the Elvi? Jesus Christ, where have you been?”
“Enjoying the fruits of social inequality, of course. What do you think? I have only been at street level a few times before and never after a terrorist attack. I have no idea what you people get up to.” Sarah said as a ‘matter-of-factly’ as she could. 
“What ‘you people’ get up to? Well, Lady Posenby, we die. We die horribly. We work, so you can play and we suffer for your amusement. When we are not doing that, we get drunk and listen to music.” 
“Music?” She said, deliberately ignoring Cracker’s pain-and-suffering diatribe. “Music. That is something we have in common then.”
“If you like old time, you are in for a treat. The Elvi are the best, they can do any old song and their voices are amazing, just amazing.” He said, deciding to leave the social inequality debate for another day.
“Who are these Elvi?” She asked, having never heard of them.
* * * </Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="8E99DEFD-C9F3-4410-B0DA-F258620E7A24">
            <Title>Just a Few Questions</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="8DBE1A85-6C39-41AD-9FC5-44A5B810C0A4">
            <Title>Pre-Emptive Reciprocity</Title>
            <Text>Pre-emptive Reciprocity
For the dark man the 216 floor trip to street level seemed to take forever. He was in the starting blocks and was sprinting even before the doors were fully open, a particle pulse pistol was now in his hand as he charged out to the street, but there was no-one there. Not the meat wagon and not the blank. Economy be damned! The blank was supposed to be waiting. He really wished that just one of these jobs wasn’t a cluster fuck, but they always were. Always. He wasn’t looking forward to filing this report, the Director was not going to be happy.
* * *
“Let me get this straight, Agent Jones.” said the incredulous Director of Pre-Emptive Reciprocity and Pro-active Euthanasia. (P.R.P.E.) “The blank lost the Gyges disc and you lost the blank. Does that about sum it up?”
“I wouldn’t chose to describe it in those terms” replied the soon to be demoted field operative. “The blank was really unwell. He could barely stand, but insisted on being juiced up with AMT to complete the contract. He made it look like suicide, but he somehow dropped the disc. I went up to retrieve it, but the office had been sanitized and a new tenant had moved in.”
“You idiot. You could be identified.”
“No, I had my disc. Security records would have been scrambled.”
“I mean by people in the building. Did you speak to anyone?” The director could not believe a fully trained agent could be this obtuse.
“No…..No I didn’t.” He figured he had better start lying. This truth telling bullshit was getting him nowhere. He decided to keep the cleaner out of it, at least until he knew what this street sweeper had pinched from Mr. Lee’s office.
“What happened with the blank?” The Director inquired.
“He had finished the job, there was a company meat-wagon around the corner and I was about to pop him off, when we discovered the cloak (disc) was missing. I went to fetch it and when I got back he was gone and so was the van.”
“So you lost the blank, a cloak and a van. A good days work and it’s not even midday.” The director stared him down. “This was a baby-sitting job, Agent Jones. He kills them, you kill him. Easy. You had better find that blank and silence him.”
“And the cloak?”
“Find that too. We can’t have just anyone waltzing through secure zones at will. Violating the privacy of citizens is our job.”
“Yes, sir.” Agent Jones said, very glad this conversation was over.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="EDBE66B7-AEF4-47CD-BB29-BFFD8DD2855A">
            <Title>Dawkins Quotes</Title>
            <Text>‘Follow your reason as far as it will take you, without regard for any other consideration. Do not pretend that conclusions are certain when they are not demonstrated.’

</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="03DAB0BF-6B46-44BE-A6E3-5639C57D69DD">
            <Title>The layer</Title>
            <Text>‘The layer’ was a permanent thick cloud of condensation, emissions and miscellaneous airborne particulate matter that barely allowed rays of sunlight to penetrate and strike the ground 216 floors below. Decade upon decade of fossil fuel emissions, chemical misuse, pesticides and cow farts had pushed the atmosphere well beyond the point of no return. No meaningful attempt was made to reverse the degradation of the environment. There was no profit in that. The natural and according to the company, rational course of action, was to spew yet more noxious chemicals into the atmosphere. Unprecedented amounts of “air-cleaner” over extended periods. The sales pitch to the world was “total atmospheric clarification”. Clean air, clean living and, with “Marlborough Country” ads once again possible, a surge in cigarette sales. 
Sales went beyond anyone’s wildest expectations. Financially, the company and the Primary Trade Dominion really cleaned up, unfortunately however, the atmosphere did not. Rather than clarifying” the air, all pollutants condensed together at about 200 metres above sea level. The floating pea-soup acid-mist represented the sum of all the filth and foul muck ever spewed out of a smoke stack, exhaust pipe, chimney or cow’s bum. Pure concentrated evil. 
Governments from all over the world protested, sent envoys and some even threatened military action. The CEO of the PTD laughed at that prospect. No one would attack from the west, it was a nuclear wasteland and PTD now manufactured most of the world’s armaments and all of the high-tech bio-weapons. Very few had the balls to attack and even fewer had sufficient armaments to sustain such unwise undertaking. The company explained to their disgruntled customers that the process had not failed, it had merely worked in an unexpected way. The final desired result would merely take a little longer and require the purchase of more product. With the addition of a little dye in the mixture, “the layer” was changed from spew green to a pleasant silvery white hue. Apart from the aesthetic improvement, the extra purchases did nothing. 
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="B675504F-E7A3-48D6-8A11-32D61F3C534A">
            <Title>Baseline</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="8A4A6BF7-968C-47DF-8A94-DC2963993EFA">
            <Title>Exorcism</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="E4563FDC-E0EC-4FDA-B1B4-562C43E7837F">
            <Title>Death a la carte</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="BDED8326-49F0-498C-BDBB-9DAC915D477C">
            <Title>General</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="EE4D7D80-96AE-40A4-87E8-4452F04A00F7">
            <Title>Honouring Dishonour.</Title>
            <Text>

Honouring Dishonour

Shielding Richard from the glare of the formidable security and surveillance capacity of the Primary Trade Dominion was going to be next to impossible. To take the Commissioner’s chair Hayley had to deliver unto the CEO a bona fide Elvi Saviour and she could not just substitute some random child in the place of Richard. Apart from the stomach churning amorality, she knew would not get it past the CEO. He was just too shrewed. Does it have to be a child? She thought vacantly. She then thought to contact the High Elvis. 
- - -
“Thank you, your Elvisness.” Agent Jones said, so happy his contribution had been noticed. “This is the greatest honour of my life.”
“Nothing you do not deserve, Agent Jones. I assure you.” The High Elvis said, grim. “Let’s take our places.”
Every member of the Elvi Parliament was present in the chamber, splendid in ornate official parliamentary red and gold regalia, silent and dignified. The public gallery was packed with chattering Elvises and Elvines, cheerfully waiting for the pageantry to begin. They were trusted the family members of the parliamentarians below, but still considered themselves lucky to being a part of history.
Agent Jones, draped in a floor length hooded cape of glimmering blue satin, entered the Chamber from the speaker’s side entrance to uproarious applause. Smiling and nodding, he slipped across the chamber slowly and deliberately, taking in the long sought after applause. Members of parliament and spectators alike were standing and cheering as he silently centred himself in the chamber.
“Our saviour!” An enthusiastic spectator called out. 
“Not our saviour!” The High Elvis took the podium to silence. “He is The Saviour!” The chamber roared with approval. “Let me tell you what he has done for the Elvi nation. He was an assassin was sworn to uphold the security of the PTD, but he saw the righteousness of our cause and he pledged himself to us!”
“Praise be unto him.” Cried out the Minister of Defence, precisely as the script demanded. The parliament erupted into ecstatic applause taking quite a few minutes to die down.
“Yes, praise be unto him.” The High Elvis nearly choked on his words. “He went on a secret mission for us, to deliver a contagion and wiping out the threat from those who conspire against us.” The crown hissed on cue. “He lead a team of agents on a suicide mission strike at the heart of their conspiracy. The bodies of those agents were left for us to see, but the mission was a success. With all of their viral agents destroyed we can look to the future with some confidence, thanks to you Agent Jones.”
“I thank you and my Elvi friends everywhere.” Agent Jones bowed deeply, pausing reverently and with unbending back, gracefully raised himself upright. “I am pledged only to the Elvi.”
“I put a motion, the only motion tonight, to this very special sitting of parliament. I ask that the chamber to declare today and for all time that our friend Agent Jones be not only born to us a new member of the Elvi Nation, but born to us as the Saviour of the Elvi. Great Saviour” H.E bowed.
The gallery went crazy, jumping up and down calling out “Great Saviour” over and over, a few feinting with ecstasy, others sobbed and hugged. 
“I call upon parliament to vote on the motion.” H.E. “Mr Speaker, you have the floor.”
Members in the chamber quietly recorded their votes in the recently installed voting system. Nothing too fancy, votes were generally a yes or no proposition, so all you had to do was press the red or the green button. The Speaker had the results in minutes and stood up before the awaiting mob.
“On the single proposition before us tonight, for the affirmative 99, for the negative 1. The motion is passed. Agent Jones is born to us new member of the Elvi Nation and declared The Great Saviour!” The speaker managed to get out most of it before being drowned out by the crowd. Who the fuck voted against the motion?
“Come forward Great Saviour” H.E. motioned Agent Jones before him. “Bow your head. I hereby crown you Great Saviour of the Elvi.” H.E. wanted to place a grass crown upon Agent Jones’ head, but since only the ultra rich had the coin to maintain a lawn, they made do with an intricately woven, thorny vine.

- - -
“Sarah?” Hayley asked over the encrypted channel. “I think they are about to move. Are you secure?”
“Not at this point in time. I will see what I can arrange.” Sarah replied. “This is it isn’t it?”
“Good luck, Holy Mother.” Hayley’s anxiety hung in the air before she cut call. From the few fragments of intel she had scraped together, it seemed Haloren Smythe had identified Richard and was closing in. The CEO had approved unlimited resources and he had syphoned units from everywhere including, most recently, her surveillance and tactical teams. She sent all the gung-ho, jarheads she could gather in the they would fuck it up. Good luck keeping those boys under control Mr Smythe.
Surveillance operatives had followed Sarah, Richard and the Elvi Security team to a very exclusive part of the city. Although the Elvi had set up multiple decoy operations, they felt it was more probable that not, they were now following the right baby-saviour. 
“Sir, they are at 12896 Goldstream. High Security, but we have tanks and are confident of a fast entry.” Radioed in the lead surveillance operative to his boss Haloren Smythe. Are you sure we have got the right kid? They all look the fucken same to me.”
“We cannot be 100% certain, but I don’t have the luxury of time.” Haloren wanted to win this competition quickly and all of the intelligence pointed to this kid. Even his name was a bit obvious, but he was not certain. Fuck, this operational management thing is harder than I thought. “Tell the entry team it’s a green light. Hard entry, extreme prejudice.” No use having witnesses. 12896 Goldstream? The address seemed familiar.
The target premises was a massive and very rare free-standing dwelling, set on on entire acre of manicured lawn and surrounded by a 5 metre high security barrier of concrete and steel, virtually impenetrable and most likely electrified. The gate was was the obvious point of entry and the first attempt was made using a small tank that just ploughed in a top speed. The dent in the gate was minimal, but such was the impact the crew were rendered unconscious, having to be dragged out and replaced before another attempt could be made. So much for a quick entry.
“What the fuck is that gate made from ?” The leader of the entry team inquired loudly. Shoot the fucken thing off!”
The tank was reversed away from the gate and then it let out a massive plasma blast from the fat, stubby barrel poking out of the turret. The gate blew off it’s hinges and spun wildly along the slender driveway, digging up chunks of bitumen and dirt before hacking massive furrows in the lawn, exposing dark, rich soil. 
Two small tanks and an APC entered the gate and raced toward the house, one tank took to the rear and the other the front. 20 operatives in black entered the gate and fanned out, waiting for the breach. 
Elvi Security begged to secure Richard in some hidden vault, but Eloise had assured them the house was a vault itself. Unconvinced, they readied to engage the intruders, taking up defensive positions throughout the sprawling mansion. 
“Your men don’t have to worry.” Eloise Shakti calmly informed Sarah from the comfort of her sofa. A pale, fragile tea set took centre place before her on the coffee table, the pot’s spout steaming enticingly. “This will be all over soon. Tea dear?”
“What about the team of killers outside?” Sarah was beside herself with thoughts of losing her son, but Richard was happily playing on the floor, completely aware of his situation.
“We are perfectly safe.” Eloise insisted. “I am Eliose Shakti and my house is fortress, built of thick tungsten reinforced poly-crete and ballistic glass. It has automated defence systems and a squadron of drones. You will be safe and my grandson will be safe.” It had only taken one look at Richard for her to see that this kid was special and placing him in danger was unfathomable. ”In the unlikely event that any of these people gain entry, we will need more than the pop-guns your security are carrying. She stood up, tea cup daintily in hand and traversed the room to an innocuous panel in the wall and placed her hand on a sensor pad. The panel clicked open to reveal a cache of serious firepower. “This is the Primary Trade Dominion.”
“By the power of finance! I’ve have never seen weapons of this type.” Sarah was a daughter of the PTD and could not help but be impressed by the state of art weaponry.
“There’s a cabinet like this in every room and I have unlocked them all.” Eloise smiled. “These are advanced prototypes of pulse rifles and these are pulse cannons. Be careful of the PPP’s are extremely powerful. My companies manufacture them, of course.” Eloise was a proud business woman. “There is a variety of smart-grenades - incendiary, electro-magnetic, sonic pulse and good old-fashioned fragment grenades.” Mrs Shakti, the elder, was always prepared - in this case, perhaps, more than prepared.
The tanks kept their distance, waiting for the order to open fire as the APC slowly approached the massive portico at front of the house, mounting a set of six wide creamy-white onyx stairs, cracking the expensive masonry and crushing it to rubble. 
“They cost a fortune!” Eloise was incensed. Even for the ultra rich, stone of that quality was rare and she had only just had it installed. She opened the blinds of the huge front windows giving her a perfect view of the entry team and the response her defence systems were about to make. “Don’t worry dear, this will be over quickly.” She took a sip of tea.
Sarah noted the the glass was impossibly thick, but did not seem to distort the light or the view. Upon seeing the police closing in, Sarah instinctively picked up Richard, cradling him close. She saw a solid square bollard, about one metre thick rise out of the grass, 3 metres in front of the APC and unleashed a blast from a pulse cannon, splitting it entirely up the centre. Through a wet pink mist, molten fragments of armour star burst in every direction. The shock wave knocked out anyone in a 30 metre radius, largely neutralising entry team at the front of the house. In response, the two tank commanders opened fire, striking the front and rear simultaneously, but did not seem to scratch the surface. Exterior Holo-emitters had kicked in, instantly covering the damage and deflating the egos of the tank commanders. Immediately, more bollards popped up and the tanks were instantly hit from all sides with a barrage of particle pulse fire. Elvis Security opened fire with PPP’s from inside the house, exploding black uniforms with their turbo charged pistols. 
“You were not kidding when you said it would be over quickly. How long did they last? Two minutes?” Sarah was in awe of her mother-in-law and was glad she was close-by when she she go the word from Hayley. 
“Mr Shakti and I always thought it would be a mob from the Pale that would try to over-run us, but the Police? Get out there and mop up the rest...” Eloise barked at the Elvi. They all stood in place until Sarah gave them a nod. “…but take one alive, I need to find out who sent them.”
The Elvis’ were merciless, blowing massive chunks out of anything in a black uniform, discriminating not between the unconscious and the upright. The lucky single prisoner was dragged before Eloise, Sarah and Richard, who was surrounded by a company of black haired guards. 
“You and your little band made a big mistake coming here. Did you not think I would be ready?” Eloise was incensed. “Why did you come here here you stupid, stupid man?”
“The child must die.”
“What? Why?”
“He’s some sort of Messiah for the Elvi.” He managed to squeak out before the lead security kicked him in the side. “He is a symbol of hope.”
“A messiah? A symbol of hope? He is my grandson and he will be afforded all of the protection my position provides.” Grandma Shakti took a deep breath. “Now, who sent you?”
“We are on a CEO’s Directive to track down and neutralise the saviour of the Elvi. My commander is Haloren Smythe.”
Taking our her personal coms device, she called the CEO. 
“Eloise Shakti. Haloren Smythe ordered a raid on my house to murder my grandson, they think he is the Messiah ….. No, they are mostly dead …..You will have to pay for the clean-up… They broke my set of Onyx stairs!….Yes those ones! ………….Even if he is the Messiah, I don’t care. He is my grandson and my protection falls upon him. An attack upon him is an attack upon me. I hope you comprehend what I am saying. Goodbye.” 
- - -
The CEO was frozen in place, momentarily catatonic, wanting to go back a few minutes in time, back to the good old days when he had not pissed off Eloise Shakti. Coming back to the present he turned to Hayley Lanning, seated smugly on the other side of the desk.
“This vid evidence looks pretty solid. The ceremony in their parliament seems credible to me, but everything pointed towards a birth of a child. This is entirely unexpected.”
“You heard the High Elvis himself sir, Agent Jones was ‘born to the Elvi’ and they called him the great Saviour.” Hayley decided to pile even on even more. “Look, I’ve identified the Elvis Messiah, I have identified who breached the security at your not-so-secret virus lab and I have ballistics that identify him for the murders of the family of a man named Emile Kalhari. A most brutal crime.” I told you I would get you for that Agent Jones.
“He is a rogue agent of the worst kind and he will be dealt with by The Directorate. They will interrogate and dispose of him.”
“They won’t interrogate him. It will be murder pure and simple.” Hayley feigned protest.
“It will be a sanctioned contract termination on my orders!” The CEO was feeling a little better after that shock call from Eloise.
“Yes, Sir.”
“You may leave, Commissioner Lanning.” He gave her a wry smile. “The job is yours.”
“What about Haloren Smythe? What do I do with him?” Immediately acting the Commissioner.
“Nothing. He will be retiring shortly.” He shuddered at the thought of what Grammy Shakti would do to the poor bastard. He will have no friends now and nowhere to hide. “He has done something very reckless. He tried to raid Eloise Shakti’s mansion.”
“My goodness! How many casualties?” She knew it wasn’t going to be pretty.
“35 dead, 2 tanks and an ACP and two tanks destroyed.”
“Two tanks and and APC? They cost a fortune!”
“And they cracked her onyx stairs out the front, too.” The CEO added, horrified.
“My goodness. Can you even get that anymore?” 
“I don’t know, but she said we are paying for the clean-up and by that I mean your department.” 
“Yes, sir.” The damage bill notwithstanding, she was happy the Directorate would deal with Agent Jones. She did not want to try to effect an arrest of The Great Saviour, it would be most unbecoming for one of the Holy Aunts.
- - -
The next order of business was to drag Haloren Smythe into his office for a dressing down. The debacle would not and could not be forgiven. Within half an hour of being summonsed, Haloren Smythe, former candidate for Commissioner of Police, was standing on before him. 
“How is the investigation progressing, Haloren?” He was keen to hear the bullshit Haloren was about to spin.
“Well sir, I have identified the child, but we had a set back. We mounted an arrest operation on an address on Goldstream, but we were repelled by automated defence systems.” Haloren stuttered.
“What intell did you have on the address itself?” The CEO inquired.
“It was a fluid situation, sir and I had to act quickly. We had multiple decoy targets and had not identified all of the players, but we were certain we had zeroed in on the correct group of people.”
“So you made a bold decision and went in.”
“It seemed correct at the time.”
“Any losses?”
“Well,…yes. We lost an APC, two TT’s and 35 men.”
“Two tanks, you say?” The CEO decided it was time to lower the boom. “You idiot! You imbecile! Raiding the home of Eloise Shakti, the PTD’s most prominent arms manufacturer, with zero intel. Do you know what you have done?”
“Eloise Shakti?” Oh dear, what a monumental fuck up. “I was in the process of analysing our tactical alternative.” 
“Did you personally lay eyes on the property? Any idiot could tell the house belonged to someone of substance.”
“Am I to try and extract the child again?” Haloren figured he already knew the answer.
“You will do nothing of the sort. You will stand down, immediately. Authorisation for resources is hereby rescinded. This farce is to end. Eloise Shankti has already been in my ear and she is as angry as she is rich.” He paused for effect. ”Do you really think the son of Alexander Shakti is the Saviour of the Elvi?”
“If you say it like that it sounds a little absurd, but there is a timing anomaly with the pregnancy. Shakti may not be the father.” 
“Even if it is true, it does not matter. You have struck at the heart of the elite, your people. The same people who, when you were looking at a death sentence, plucked from your cell and set you free. The child is now protected by God herself and he is untouchable. In any case we have already identified the Saviour and he is not the son of Alexander Shakti.” The CEO scoffed.
“Impossible, my information is quite compelling. You have to listen, please.” He knew he was finished.
“Look at this video” The CEO raised the holoscreen and the presentation began and they silently watched. “Quite compelling, isn’t it?”
“Where did you get it?” Haloren asked, knowing it must be a ruse.
“You under estimated the abilities of Commissioner Lanning, Mr Smythe. She managed to get hi-qual-vid of this naming ceremony for ‘The Great Saviour.’ It happened just last night. According to the High Elvis, can you believe the name? our very own Agent Jones, is now the most revered soul in the Elvi nation. They even gave him a crown.”
“That’s absurd, the messiah is a newborn.” Haloren said. Commissioner Lanning?
“Agent Jones was “born to the Elvi” and named their Saviour because he lead a team of Elvi who raided one of my facilities, destroying decades of viral research.” The CEO took a deep breath and suppressed a smile. The Elvi think they have destroyed my killer virus! They think they are safe! A surprise awaits those pompous fools. “I am yet to alert the Directorate of Agent Jones’ treason and only because you are of executive stock I will give you a final opportunity to partially redeem yourself.”
“Anything, sir.” 
“I want you to terminate Agent Jones’ contract. Do you think you can manage that?” The CEO liked to mock. “The books have to be tallied up by the end of business, tomorrow. You will then disappear, but you will not be free. Your friends will spurn you and your portfolio will wither. When I need you again I will contact.” 
“Thank you, I won’t let you down.” Haloren Smythe. He felt like killing someone and slipping back into the underworld was something he looked forwards to.
The CEO sat contemplating his morning and his little scrape with Eloise. Her little grandson may not be related her at all. He tucked that little gem away for future reference. 
- - -
The new Commissioner was glad to leave to CEO’s office so quickly and with so few questions. She could not believe he had swallowed that ‘born again’ crap so easily and she also couldn’t believe her first act as Commissioner was to place a listening device in the CEO’s office. She still didn’t know the fate of Sarah and Richard and had an urgent call to make but she couldn’t call Sarah on her personal comms device and had make it back to her new office before she could make encrypted contact.
“Oh Hayley, it was both terrifying and thrilling. Eloise says the defence system had hardly kicked into gear before wiping out the entry team. She was disappointed I didn’t get to see the drones in action. Richard seemed to know everything was fine and really enjoyed the show. But what about the repercussions, quite a few of your colleagues died here today. There will be calls for payback.”
“Anyone dumb enough to try to a direct assault on the Shakti mansion gets what they deserve. Such a lack of respect for the rest and repose of the upper executive class.” Hayley was just busting to tell Sarah the news and managed to bring the conversation around. “I would not worry too much about police retaliation, after all, you are speaking to Commissioner Lanning.” Hayley winced as Sarah screamed with delight.
“Congratulations! It worked out pretty much as you hoped.” Sarah was thrilled. “Your competition is out of the picture?”
“Yes and he’s is in big trouble for the raid.” Hayley laughed.Victory felt good. “We have to meet tonight. I am in the mood for a celebration.”
“Okay. That’s if I can get Grandma to hand over my son.”
- - - 
Holy Aunt Hayley, the most powerful law officer in the land, made sure her wig was on straight and she was obscured from security vid as she made her way to The Quarter. Any electronic contraption that could trace her location was cast aside and left in her office. She was never dumb enough to get cyber-implant, so there had been no need for home neurosurgery.
The Elvi had spent most of their working lives in hospitality and arranging an extravaganza at a moment’s notice presented few obstacles. There was only one place the all of the Elvi could congregate at once, the old sports stadium. The upgrades since Kleo’s funeral had been progressing steadily. The bathroom facilities were second to none and it was considered an honour to jag the position of bathroom attendant. 
As Hayley made her way inside the vast stadium, towards the executive boxes, she side-stepped her way through small bands of Elvis cleaners scuttling about polishing the floors and windows, all the while belting out tune after tune in perfect harmony. Elvis Security had been tight, but they had been smart enough to get the Dawks on board for some of the catering and at each entrance couple of Dawks would hand everyone a complimentary bottle of water reminding the Elvi, “Keep hydrated.” Haley was was greeted at the door of the Royal Box by a very correct Elvis matre’d, 
“We are honoured by your presence Holy Aunt Hayley. You are the last of the honoured guests to arrive. Follow me, honoured Aunt.” He said, perfectly poised.
“Thank you, but I would like to make my own way.” Hayley said as politely as she could.
“Of course. As my honoured guest wishes.” Replied the matr’d, nose slightly bent out of place. “Make way, Holy Aunt coming through. She weaved her way through the crowd, to sighs of delighted recognition. 
The Blessed Mother, The Great Teacher and Holy Aunt Kathy were outside with the High Elvis, each holding a glass of wine, except for The Great Teacher who had a bottle of beer in each hand and one sticking out of his pants pocket. Chilo and Silo, dressed in their standard Druid attire, were standing to the side, speaking, no doubt, of things important as they leaned against the chunky handrail of a huge newly constructed deck. Jutting out into the stadium about 20 or 30 metres it could easily hold two hundred. Beyond the handrail and about 5 metres below was the playing field surface, quickly filling with Elvi. In the dimly lit stadium Holy Aunt Haley could see the ground was bright green and thick with precisely cut lawn. The effort that went in keeping the grass alive must have stupendous, she thought as she stared vacantly at the starless, pitch black sky. 
The stadium seating was almost full now, the buzz radiated outward and washed across Hayley like a warm, full body caress. She could see row after row of happy faces, enthusiastically engaged in chatter with their neighbour. Breathing in her success she turned to greet her friends.
“Commissioner!” The High Elvi called out, arms raised and waving about madly. Hayley strolled confidently to the group “Please join us. I have just heard of your promotion. Congratulations!” They hugged and then Sarah, Cracker and Kathy joined in for a group hug.
“Thank you, Thank you all.” She beamed a white smile. “You can let go if you like, Cracker.”
“I am just so happy, Holy Aunt Commissioner.” Cracker laughed as he disengaged.
“Not funny.” Smiling back at him. 
“This is a very big night for the Elvi too.” H.E.said. “We will finally be introducing Richard to his people. The plans have been gathering dust for months and now it is finally here. All thanks to you, Hayley”
“We all had a role to play and you played yours perfectly your Highness.” Hayley replied, keen to return the complement. “We may have one more act to play out tonight, I fear.”
“We are ready.” The High Elivs whispered.
“Waiter.” Cracker called out to a passing Elvis. “Can we get another round? Hayley what are we drinking tonight?”
“Everything, I think, but I will start with champagne if there is any.” Hayley was ready to cut loose.
“You heard the lady” Cracker said. “Champagne and keep them coming.”
“Yes, Great Teacher, the Holy Aunt’s glass will never be empty.” The waiter backed away 4 paces and turned towards the bar. 
“I got a spare beer, if you can’t wait.” Cracker offered.
“Please.” She said and grabbed the neck of the bottle, poking out of Cracker’s pants pocket. “Still cold, excellent.” She twisted the top and took a dainty sip before turning to Kathy. “I have not seen you for weeks. How are you?” Hayley gazed upon Kathy’s slender arms and noted she was patch-less.
“I’ve been busy, far too busy even, to organise a decent patching regimen.” Kathy looked a bit glum. Sobriety clearly did not suit. “I spend most of the time arguing with Sarah’s friend Johnny. Everything is a battle with him and it never stops. He has a brilliant analytical mind, but he has not learned to share.”
“I am reconsidering the entire venture, but carry on until next week. Schedule a meeting and you can brief me, but for now we are here to have some fun.” Hayley decided business was off the agenda. “Where are your patches? This is most unusual.”
“Don’t worry, I’m stocked up for a long night, but I wanted to ease into it for a change.” Kathy, forced sober by ‘Johnny management duties’ had actually began to enjoy sobriety, in moderation. She tilted her glass toward her mouth and took small sip. “Mmm, not bad.” The talk of patching cracked Kathy’s resolve and she nodded to Cracker who slipped his hand into his jacket pocket, stood to her side and imperceptibly slipped a patch in to her palm. Unceremoniously and for anyone to see, she slapped the random patch on the inside of her thigh, just above the hem-line. She had no idea whether it was an upper of a downer or a combo, but she was keen to find out. 
“Here’s to us!” Sarah raised her glass and let out the standard “Wooooo!” Thus announcing to the crowd there is a bunch of girls here and they are drinking champagne.
“Wooo!” said Hayley, Kathy, Sarah in synchronised cheer and ‘clinked’ glasses high, hugged, giggled and drained their glasses. Holding them, again high, they hailed down a waiter. “Wooo! Ha, Ha, Ha!”
“Where is Richard?” Hayley asked Sarah.
“In the security area, downstairs. They will bring him up when then time comes.” Sarah took a swig of her champagne flute. “My security team now have state of the art PPP’s. Eloise decided to give them an upgrade.”
“Give?” Hayley would have believed ‘sold’, but ‘give’? 
“She wanted overwhelming firepower protecting my boy.” They clinked glasses and drank a silent toast. “Do you think he is actually safe now? We still have that problem” Sarah pointed to the bar area. Agent Jones had made his entrance, dressed in his hooded cape and ‘grass’ crown. The crowd became silent very quickly, even the band stopped playing.
“Oh, dear.” Hayley said as her hand covered her mouth.
“We had hoped he would have been taken care of by now.” Said the High Elvis. “It looks like it will happen here.”
“We have that covered don’t we?” Sarah asked, suddenly fearing for Richard. “Perhaps I will check in on Richard.”
“We have it covered.” The High Elvis assured her. “Great Saviour! Most revered one.” He called out across the length of the deck. The Elvis-elite remained silent but parted, allowing free passage directly to the the High Elvis. 
“I am well my leader, born again, as it were.” Agent Jones was sickeningly cheerful.
“Come and enjoy the hospitality of ALL YOUR ELVIS BROTHERS AND SISTERS.” H.E. said to the entire mob. The Elvi returned to their conversations and the band burst back into life. 
- - -
Haloren Smythe only took one gun on his suicide mission. Intel-chatter indicated major Elvi activity at the stadium which meant it was very likely Agent Jones, The Great Saviour of the Elvi, would be there. He had no real plan, but he knew if he could be close he would succeed. Getting away alive was not an option. He had no life now anyway. All bridges burnt and all debts called in. 
Approaching the stadium the fabulous sounds of the Elvis concert belting out became louder and louder. He followed the din to a fence erected around a condemned section of the stadium. He gained entry through one of the old rickety grandstands and before skulking from shadow to shadow, he was within striking distance of the main party deck. All security in his path, mysteriously absent at every turn. Even with no discernible opposition it took an eternity navigate through a maze of corridors and walkways but he was now on final approach. He resolved to move quickly and with purpose, making no further attempt at hiding and hoped luck would be with him. He slowed to a confident stroll as he approached the maitre’d (?). 
“Good evening, I am…” but he was cut off.
“You would be the assassin. We have been expecting you.” The maitr’d said bluntly to a stunned killer. “This way, sir.”
He entered the front bar area and was met no resistance. Apart from a few sideways glances, the elites gathered there barely seemed to notice him until he had made his way into the centre of the vast deck. For the second time that night, the gathering slowly parted causing a straight path to the High Elvis and his party of honoured guests.
“You are earlier than expected, assassin, but we are ready.” The High Elvis challenged the lone gunman.
“I am here for Agent Jones.” Haloren stuttered. “Stand aside.”
“Okay.” The High Elvis and his friends stood aside, leaving the Great Saviour a lone figure.
“What is going on your highness?” Agent Jones was confused.
“Standing before you, my Elvi.” The High Elvis, now broadcasting over the PA, drew a deep long breath. “…is Agent Jones, a most dishonourable man. He tortured his own team of Elvi to ingratiate himself with the enemy. He bit their ears off!! We all saw the bodies of our brave brothers.” A massive collective sigh of shock and disgust arose from the crowd below.
“I did nothing of the sort.” Denial was all he had.
“Holy Aunt Hayley.” The High Elvis took a step back.
Hayley was momentarily mute, not expecting to make this speech in front of her rival, Haloren Smythe. She knew he was dead no matter what he did, so he may as well accept this one last mission. 
“Thank you High Elvis. The bite marks on the ears of the victims exactly matches the bite pattern of Agent Jones. His DNA was found in the wounds. Furthermore, he tortured and attempted to kill the Great Teacher with a weapon that was identified in the murder of a mother and two children. Children he killed just to cover his own incompetence. Proved by ballistics. It is also rumoured he tortured and killed two surveillance operatives, again in an attempt to cover his own incompetence. He is untrustworthy and will turn on you all as soon as it is convenient. This is a sanctioned termination of a contract and there is no reason to interfere.” 
“Oh, come on! You were in on all this?” Haloren stupidly blurted out. “That video was all bullshit?” I was right, I was fucking right. He could get word to the CEO but he knew it would do no good. He now realised the magnitude of his defeat. She had crushed him.
“Silence! No one owes you any explanations. This man, this assassin, was behind the plot to kill the One-after-the-One!” She took two steps back again exposing Agent Jones to his fate. “You are here for a reason, get on with it and then do the right thing.”
Each Elvi took out a gleaming PPP and watched carefully as Haloren raised his pistol and walked toward his target first slowly before breaking into a trot. 
“No, No. I can tell you about the discs. I can tell you all of their plans. Please.” Agent Jones managed to blurt out before his head exploded. Doing the right thing, Haloren wedged the gun under his chin and pulled the trigger.
“Clean up this mess” The High Elvis called out. “Now the real party can begin.”
The Elvi erupted into cheer and all began to sing a the Elvis classic ‘Unlucky Brain’. First recorded a few months earlier, as a parody of Alexander Shakti, they had changed the lyrics for this special occasion and gave a short rendition.
	Unlucky brain keeps oozing out,
	From the head of a clown, 
	That was stalking our crew,
	Now there’s brain on my shoes, (brain on my shoes)
	Assassin shot you 
	Through that Old Unlucky Brain,
	(Old Unlucky brain) 
	Through that Old Unlucky Brain. 
As they sang, Sarah and Cracker looked at each other and began to laugh. Both were splattered in Agent-brains and picked chunks from one another’s face and hair. 
“What was that slogan again?” Cracker said directly into Sarah’s ear.
“Brains, but no stains!” They laughed in unison, recalling their very first encounter. “We all need to get cleaned up.”
The others in their elite group had not faired any better and were each wiping their faces with handkerchiefs, serviettes and anything else they could lay their hands on. Holy Aunt Kathy shook her head of dark hair, dislodging a couple of bone fragments and a few bloody teeth which bounced and lightly clattered on the deck. 
A wave of Elvis cleaners appeared out of the crowd and set to whisking away the bodies, mopping the deck and chasing down blood splatters. The Holy Aunts, Blessed Mother and The Great Teacher were spirited away to the private chambers of the High Elvis to take advantage of a quick shower and a set of new clothes. They returned to the party, resplendent in glittering dinner jackets and flared pants, to a resounding cheer from the Elvi, all similarly dressed.
“It’s time.” The High Elvis couldn’t wait and turned to the interior of the stadium, now full and walked to the centre of the long handrail at the edge of the deck. He raised his hand high above his head. “My Elvis brothers and sisters! Welcome!” A single ancient flood light, long thought extinct, burst into life, illuminating the highest ranking Elvis. “We have waited for this night for a very long time and now the waiting is over. We walk the path of science and reason, the way of the Dawkins!” He paused as the deafening cheer slapped him in the face and dislodged his bouffant. “The one-after-the-one, made in the image of the Dawkins, is born to us and we are blessed with his presence tonight!” Again the entire stadium erupted.
“We are honoured to have with us tonight, to help us celebrate this historic event, The Great Teacher, Holy Aunt Haley and Holy Aunt Kathy.” Each was greeted with thundering cheers and applause as they were illuminated by a personal spot light, their jackets glimmering madly as they waved to the crowd, 100,000 strong. 
“Tonight we honour our dear departed Kleoboulos. We honour his wisdom and a his vision that this day would come. We honour the vision of his disciples, Chilo and Silo. This day we welcome our saviour, the one-after-the-one, King Richard I of the Elvi!” It was fortunate that the massive stadium did not also include a roof, as it would have been launched into orbit, such was the cacophony of joy that erupted. The band kicked-in in a effort to drown out the crowd and after a lengthy battle the band seemed to win out, finally allowing the High Elvis to continue. 
A glass bounded commentary box, high above in the old media centre, exploded into angelic light as the band again kicked into high gear, belting out the tune of a yet to be released song of praise. The High Elvis himself stepped forward and pointing directly above, the crowd saw the Holy Mother standing behind the glass holding Richard high above her head for all to see. For a moment there was absolute silence. The king looked down from on high and raised a pudgy little hand and waved regally to the adoring throngs. It was both the cutest thing any had seen and the most spiritually uplifting experience of their lives, forever binding them as one. All at once the Elvi on the playing field fell to their knees and held there hands aloft, staring upward with adoring eyes. Just as quickly and in time with the music they all stood up, arms still raised, shaking their hands wilding they now began to scream to the sky, “He has come, he has come…”
“It’s looking up people, it’s looking up.” The High Elvis, without breaking eye contact with Richard began to sing. No Elvis standing before him had ever heard this song, but they knew implicitly the correct dance moves, pulling off some impressive instant choreography, raising the heads and hands high at the end of each verse.
	Well, blessed one, long wait for thee, 	Been pitching your story like a prophecy,  	My friends came with, we’re all in the club, 	He is born, let’s all look up.  	He’s the one, after, the-one.
	Well, my heart was aching and please repeat,  	I see the truth, my life is complete  	It is you that I think of when I’m not at the 			pub
	He is born, let’s all look up,  	He’s the one, after, the-one 
	Well, let me just tell you what’s one my mind 	You have fixed my soul in an act so kind 
	It’s the one-after-the-one that I love best 	Your words are a cure, there be no protest 
	Well, you touched my heart a thrill I must admit 	It misses beats and flutters more than a bit 	I'm glad to say that he's the one for us 
	He is born, it’s lookin’ up
	He’s the one, after, the-one
	Well, my tongue don’t lie when I try to speak 	My insides are making like a thief on a spree 	You’re the one so pure for this soul of mine 	You are so good we will build you a shrine
	Well, you touched my heart a thrill I must admit 	It misses beats and flutters more than a bit 	I'm glad to say that he's the one for us 
	He is born, Let’s all look up
	Uh huh huh, oh, hey, yeah, yeah 	Uh huh huh, oh, hey, yeah, yeah 	He’s the one, after, the one,
	It’s looking up!

</Text>
