        <Document ID="14457040-3E15-40DC-AD2B-62B83482105F">
            <Title>The Gyges Disc</Title>
            <Synopsis>We have had a few discussions about this bit.  As I said before I don't care if the McGoober is a disc, a program, an evil leprechaun or a maltese falcon.  It just has to be something that is of some significant value and has the ability to end the world.  

I like the mantra of Agent Jones "I am not human.  I am the biological manifestation of the company's will.  In the name of stability, I destabilise. In the name of order, I deliver chaos.  In the name of life I bring death."  He says it a couple of times during the book.

The interaction with Agent Jones is just meant to set up the fact that the disc is wanted and Sarah has sent an armed intelligence agent after a cleaner.  She has to have some reason to chase Cracker up.  She notes that Cracker's records are in the process of being deleted and she think he is going to be erased.  This sets up the meeting the in the nightclub.

"The blank" lying around for hours and hours is a bit of a problem.  May need a bit of a re-write.  Also the bit about the blank was going to be important, but it wasn't really followed up.  The death of the blank and his family is later mentioned as one of the reasons Agent Jones must die, but the whole blank thing could be rewritten or removed without too much difficulty.

The part about the operational support guys might easily be removed too.  They are later both tortured to death by Agent Jones to cover his incompetence.  This could be removed too.   

Crackers interaction at the security gates with a security guard is not of great importance.  Just showing everyone is a scammer and getting an implant in your brain is not a great idea due to clauses in the service contract  - they can operate on you, against your will, to implant the latest tech.

I do like the bit where I say "Law enforcement was engaged in shielding the rich from the poor, not protecting the poor from each other.  The poor could eat shit and die.  In the Primary Trade Dominion egalitarians were those who only ate eagle."
</Synopsis>
            <Text>THE GYGES DISC
14 hours before the bombing
9 months BTD
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="C4FEEF9D-6643-4B5C-BBAC-17E9A83C9C91">
            <Title>Honouring Dishonour</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="0EFCFCE4-5815-4630-AD77-D44ED2E2A3E7">
            <Title>Sarah celebrates her promotion</Title>
            <Text>You’d think when Sarah celebrated, she‘d celebrate in lavish fashion, high above the clouds of the Layer. As was her Airborne entitlement. Surely, she’d commission an events empresario, who’d ensure the drinks were fashionable, the finger-food risked no stains on lavish evening wear, the music acts were the hardest to book, and the views evoked gasps among even the most over-sated dandies of the most over-dandied salons. Even if Sarah found some delight in such excess, she’d draw the line at opening the purging booths. She’d have no A-list guests gorging on her catering till the sun came up.
Instead, when Sarah celebrated she randomly selected a friend, and dragged them to a particular karaoke bar called The Blue Shade in The Grotto quarter of The Pale, the seediest district in the seediest of regions, where not only angels feared tread, but most of the unfortunate Cloudborne as well. 
Sarah was running out of friends; none ever agreed to a second celebration. Low on options, this time she invited Johnny.
Sarah savoured Johnny’s discomfort.
“Seriously?” Johnny was exasperated. “An ELVIS bar?” Dread and hope (hope this was all a joke) competed and made a caricature of his face.
“They prefer being called Elvi,” Sarah corrected. “Show some respect.”
Sarah ignored the waiting line of ducktail and pompadour haircuts, the leather jackets and the jumpsuits. She dismissed the protests of the clip-boarded hostess (phoenix jumpsuit in pinks and blues) and the muscle-corded bouncer (all leather), and promenaded inside, towing Johnny in her persuasive wake. His alternating expressions of horror and disgust made his thoughts easy-reading. He was dwelling on the skin peels, hair treatments, detox regimens, new clothes, new glasses, new liver, and possibly even the new teeth he feared he’d need after visiting such a place.
Sarah didn’t care. She was, after all, invincible.

Sarah took a seat at the bar. Johnny regarded the one adjacent as if it was some bacteria-incubating, medieval birthing stool. Reluctantly, he pecked a few napkins from a hopper on the bar, dabbed pathetically at the seat, then dragged it aside (shielding his hands with napkins) so he could stand alongside Sarah. For a moment, he leaned casually on the bar, then recoiled and, instead, stood casually bolt-upright, hoping he might minimise infection.
A bartender wiped down the bar and replenished complimentary bowls of peanuts and bacon. Sarah was almost certain the mixologist’s Mink Brown pompadour was a wig that also incorporated the sideburns.
“What’ll it be?” the bartender drawled. “The house special is the Hunk of Burning Coal.”
Sarah flipped a cocktail menu over. “Which one is that again?”
“Rum, hazelnut liqueur, crème de banana, pineapple juice, banana, and bitters. Garnished with honey and peanut butter. For an authentic experience, crumble in some bacon.”
Johnny gulped hard. “I’ll just have water.”
“We got Mountain Valley spring water, sugar,” the bartender offered enthusiastically.
“Is it imported?”
Sarah screwed her face at Johnny’s stupidity.
“Imported?” the bartender asked. “Now, where would it be imported from, honey?”
“Never mind,” Sarah cut in. “We’ll both have my usual.”
The bartender smiled and winked at Sarah, and headed out back.
An arrested round of applause choked up, as a punter—dressed in pompadour, sun glasses and an orange-pleated jump suit—took the stage and grabbed the mic. She punched a number into the machine and a tune washed away the noise of the hushing crowd, a tune which, in a suspiciously high number of universes more stable than this, would be instantly recognisable as The Wonder of You. In the universe of this Sarah and this Johnny, the songs The Elvi enjoyed attracted controversy and contention. Their titles and lyrics were a bizarre creole of mondegreens and quasi-political activist slogans. Everyone knew there must be better, truer words, but no one could agree on what they were. Needless to say, in this universe, such songs enjoyed little popularity. Except in The Grotto.
Sarah perversely enjoyed these songs. They were earthier and somehow truer than Airborne music, which she regarded as hairdresser, style-over-substance consumer fizz. Music from above the clouds was inescapably in the clouds, ethereal and unemotional, and struck no chords on the strings of Sarah’s heart.
The bartender returned with a pair of tall, coarse glasses, and a bottle. She popped the cork; the bottle frothed over its Airborne label.
“Oh, thank God,” Johnny said as the waitress poured, relieved he might escape this whole ordeal without a dose of Cloudborne dysentery.
Sarah clinked glasses with him as the karaoke tune reached a chorus crescendo and the punter sang, “The Chunder and Spew.”
As a murmur of dissent snaked through the audience, Sarah laughed, clapped with delight and bellowed at the stage, “I come from a land down under!”
Johnny choked on his bubbles. “How long do you plan on torturing me with this?”
“Oh, we’re here for the marathon,” she said, hoping he’d hear the wickedness in her voice. “I’m drinking you under the bar.”
“Tax!” Johnny swore. “This again?” He moaned with dismay, held his sweaty, bald head in his bratwurst-fingered hands, and contemplated how little health leave he had left. “Aren’t you starting your new gig in the morning?”
“I am, and I’ll be fine.”
She’ll be pristine, Johnny conceded. Sarah could, would, and often did drink all-comers under the table. As others fell unconscious at her feet, to his knowledge, she was never the slightest shot tipsy. She never had hangovers, and never had bags under her eyes. In the years he had known her, she somehow kept herself in perfect health, never so much as a pimple. Her ever-sunny, porcelain skin and and deep-lake eyes were aphrodisiac to him, and her loved her, which was the only motivation for putting himself through this again. 
“Let’s make it fast then,” he said and gulped his glass dry.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="1EFBF571-99A9-42C0-B0B1-389BFAF52A73">
            <Title>The Three Sages</Title>
            <Text>We just need Sarah to be pregnant with the clone of Richard Dawkins (or whatever prophet is chosen).  How that is achieved is not that important.

I do have one of the Sages conducting an exorcism.  A number of the Elvi are infected by "the Jesus" and are totally unresponsive to reasoned argument.  I think the exorcism should remain in the book.

It is only the intervention and interaction with "The Three Sages" (or other literary device) and the Elvi that either are able to make a difference.


Kleoboulos Windus
Principal Prophecy Facilitator
Seven Sages Corp.
“Don’t call us—heroes are called”

It’s been a quiet few decades — generations, to be truthful
I have a side project 
time is of the essence … winter is coming … by which I mean Christmas
but we’re in the southern hemisphere
Are we? I’m sorry, I must be suffering from Travelator lag. That old thing moves so slow, you never know where you are, mostly because you can’t believe you’re still in the same place as when you got on.
Oh, god. Your offices are on the Travelator.
They are, but let me assure you, we occupy one of the more functional cars.

If you think monorails were bad (assuming you live in a universe where monorails existed for a time), the Travelator …
Video killed the radio star
radio killed the non-fiction author
which pretty much killed most critical thinking in all but a fractional pifteenth of a percentage of all currently banging universes.

a megagalactic shitstorm tidalwave fuck-quake that’s about cave your civilisation’s head in … even megagalcactic is too small. this is trans-galactic, trans-universal, perhaps even trans-multiversal.
for the sake of little stars everywhere that are yet to be born, it’s time you wake up and think

we are the 3 sages of the Travelator
I thought there were 7
… shrugs … “We had artistic differences. The others are on hiatus to pursue personal projects”</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="6F74D195-3B39-4B26-9A9A-30DF62BB4422">
            <Title>Dark Forces</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="3742B1D7-436B-4577-86B3-610130F779F9">
            <Title>Changing Brooms</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="7DED0F43-DFE7-4595-9CF4-504E94DD5AFD">
            <Title>The Gyges Disc</Title>
            <Synopsis>We have had a few discussions about this bit.  As I said before I don't care if the McGoober is a disc, a program, an evil leprechaun or a maltese falcon.  It just has to be something that is of some significant value and has the ability to end the world.  

I like the mantra of Agent Jones "I am not human.  I am the biological manifestation of the company's will.  In the name of stability, I destabilise. In the name of order, I deliver chaos.  In the name of life I bring death."  He says it a couple of times during the book.

The interaction with Agent Jones is just meant to set up the fact that the disc is wanted and Sarah has sent an armed intelligence agent after a cleaner.  She has to have some reason to chase Cracker up.  She notes that Cracker's records are in the process of being deleted and she think he is going to be erased.  This sets up the meeting the in the nightclub.

"The blank" lying around for hours and hours is a bit of a problem.  May need a bit of a re-write.  Also the bit about the blank was going to be important, but it wasn't really followed up.  The death of the blank and his family is later mentioned as one of the reasons Agent Jones must die, but the whole blank thing could be rewritten or removed without too much difficulty.

The part about the operational support guys might easily be removed too.  They are later both tortured to death by Agent Jones to cover his incompetence.  This could be removed too.   

Crackers interaction at the security gates with a security guard is not of great importance.  Just showing everyone is a scammer and getting an implant in your brain is not a great idea due to clauses in the service contract  - they can operate on you, against your will, to implant the latest tech.

I do like the bit where I say "Law enforcement was engaged in shielding the rich from the poor, not protecting the poor from each other.  The poor could eat shit and die.  In the Primary Trade Dominion egalitarians were those who only ate eagle."
</Synopsis>
            <Text>THE GYGES DISC
14 hours before the bombing
9 months BTD
It took Cracker 35 minutes to get down to the ground floor, some 216 floors below Sarah’s office and 100 floors below the layer. There had been a major delay when an L.5. suit decided to slum it with the workers and got in the service elevator at level 100. Without a word he entered his code and the lift began ascending again, straight back to floor 216. Cracker could smell a mild scent of something burning, hair or skin perhaps, but it dissipated as soon as the L.5 scurried off the lift. Cracker was able to get a glimpse of him entering Sarah’s office before the lift door closed. He figured this guy must be a drama queen. Apart from a suicide, there nothing out of the ordinary on floor 216. 
Upon hitting the pavement, he unconsciously looked up, somehow expecting to see Sarah at her window waving at him, but his view was blocked by the layer of thick cloud and she was not waving at him. She was dealing with the suspicious looking character that just stepped off the service-lift and was presently sleezing around in her office. He was quite tall and seemed to impose himself in her office, rather than merely occupy it and was breathing loudly, too loudly to account for the short run from the elevator. His anxiety, even though he was clearly attempting to keep a lid on it, was nevertheless palpable as he fidgeted and scratched the dark black locks on his distorted angular head. 
“Can I help you?” She accused.
“I am looking for Mr. Lee. He has some files for me to sign.” The dark haired man did not sound convincing, not many document were actually signed anymore. Paper cost too much and it was an extremely inefficient method of data storage. 
“What kind of files?”
“Contracts, torts and legal things, Res ipsa loquitor.”
“Mmm.” She said gravely. “That does not sound very likely. This is the Cyber Security Division. We sometimes deal with legal matters, but never without company lawyers present and please don’t use legal terms you don’t understand. You are going to have to try a bit harder than that.” Contracts and torts and legal things? Res Ipsa Loquitor? How dumb does he think I am? She was always profoundly insulted when men made the mistake of assuming she was as dull as them. “Mr. Lee has passed away. His files will be re-allocated in due course.” She looked at the open doorway but the dark man did not budge.
“I need to see the files now.” He was clearly in a hurry and allowed a little hostility to creep out in an effort to underline that urgency.
“If, indeed, you do have business with this department you will be contacted in due course.” Sarah remained neutral and professional, but was concerned about the true nature of his business with the recently deceased Mr. Lee. “I do not have them and I cannot help you. I think you will find the door is behind you. Good day.”
Wave after wave of white hot rage washed over the dark-haired clown. His infantile ego had been dented and this could not go unanswered. The idea of his agenda being frustrated by some idiotic computer jockey infuriated him and an old mantra began repeating inside his head. “I am not human. I am the biological manifestation of the company’s will. In the name of stability I destabilize. In the name of order I deliver chaos. In the name of life I bring death.” He suddenly charged at her, quickly and malevolently, the nasty scowl upon his face a clear sign of his intent to do harm. She did not move, but stared him down, unflinching as a wry smile began to inch across of her face. As he approached with images of beating the life out of her dancing in his brain, he found he could not raise his arms and fists would not form. He tried for his gun, but it was just too heavy. He became most confused. Was this some sudden rush of morality flooding his body, washing clean the cankered crust of his licentious, black soul? He rejected that idea outright. He thought she must be just too good looking to hit, but that seemed unlikely too. He rather enjoyed messing up a beautiful face. They were just so damned punch-able and the looks of shock and surprise were just priceless. The truth, he seemed unable to recognise or refused to face, was she frightened him. He had, unconsciously, sensed the danger, the truth of this petite girl – that he did not stand a chance against her.
“What happened to all his stuff?” He said, retreating towards to the door.
“The office has been completely cleared out. Company property has been returned, his personal property has been sent home and the rest was thrown out.”
“The cleaners?”
“Well, a cleaner, yes.”
He left without a word, remembering the cleaner on the elevator not 10 minutes prior. Sarah reported the strange man to Security, but she knew that would do no good. Re-runs of “Random Camera L1 - L3” were showing on the link and box-headed security types loved that crap. She hacked into the security system and began downloading surveillance logs herself, but found she had to call on all her hacking magic to reconstitute the files, all damaged and jumbled into white noise. The security system had clearly been compromised and when she finally put Humpty together again, she could not find a trace of the man anywhere. The security footage of the corridor outside her office did not detect this man at all. At no time, it appeared, did this phantom enter her office, her vivid memory of their conversation notwithstanding. 
Normal people, average punters, cannot so easily circumvent high level security systems. He was clearly no ordinary man and the only people who could get their hands on that sort of technology were Intelligence Agents. Was that a gun he was packing? Tax me into poverty! I sent this loon after the cleaner. She knew she should not give a shit about the level 2 garbage collector, she’d only just met him, but she did care and did not know why. A quick check of the security logs revealing Cracker had not yet exited the business district, trebling her concern. Once he was in “The Pale” he would be hard to follow, but he had to get there first.
Cracker was still looking up at the layer, shaking his head and grumbling, “What a fuck-up.” The layer was constant reminder of the line drawn between the lows and upper society. He really hated the layer and what stuck in his craw was the fact that the very people that engineered this blunder were the same people that live above and beyond its rancid reality. Commit a crime against all humanity and receive thanks from a grateful nation. 
Still looking up and cursing the layer, Cracker didn’t notice the dead male on the grubby pitted cement. He tripped over the body of some poor sod, accidentally landing right on top. At close range Cracker noticed the man was wearing an expensive suit and it looked new, but upon second glance his eyes were drawn to a pair of grimy, calloused hands adorned with oddly bent fingers and filthy nails. This was no L.5. Others on street level chose not to notice the dead body in their midst and merely strode an elliptical path around the gristly scene. The crowd un-gathered remarkably fast and while nobody was looking Cracker pocketed anything he could find. 
- - -
Operational support guys, the underlings of the intelligence community, were always working on covert ops and difficult to locate. They were the guys that broke into houses to place covert listening devices, cameras and bombs - they undertook physical surveillance, they did all the jobs no one else wanted to do – in this case they were to collect the body of a blank. They kept an eyeball on their target and watched as he dropped like a stone. Initially they thought him to be shot, but did not see the discharge flare of a particle weapon nor was anyone close enough to have brought him down by other means. He just seemed to lose consciousness and fell where he stood. 
‘That’s our cue. Let’s pick him up” the driver had said. 
“No. Where’s the square head? We can’t pick him up without the green light from him.” The other shot back.
“We are here to pick up a dead body. Does he look dead to you?”
“He’s not moving. Wait a minute, there’s some good Samaritan trying to help.”
“The idiot in the busted overalls? He’s going to fuck this up for us. Let’s roll.”
The van was multi-purpose surveillance rig, currently disguised as a coronial pick-up unit. There were so many dead and dying on the street at any one time, it became necessary for these “meat-wagons” to permanently scour the thoroughfares of the city, scooping up the lifeless before they became too rancid. There were so many, that an extra van floating about would be noticed by no one. The driver kicked the small motor into life and the van slid silently to where the blank lay. 
As Cracker casually rose he was confronted by a Coroner’s meat-wagon driver and his body-snatching off-sider. 
“I was just about to call you guys. I don’t think he’s been dead for long – he’s still warm.” Cracker said in the most sycophantic tone he could muster.
 
“Yeah, yeah. Fuck off, pal” They said in unison.
Cracker took his cue and backed away, joining the throng of mindless automatons and marched towards the security gates. In the Primary Trade Dominion, security guards weren’t known for their high intelligence. Most realised they were going nowhere in life and were happy to get a gig sitting around doing fuck-all most of the time and the rest of the time you got to mess with people’s heads. They were all recruited from level 1 ranks and were nothing in the scheme of things, but when you stepped into their little bailiwick, they were the law. Even though, Cracker was a regular in the business district, businessmen certainly weren’t taking out their own trash, security always made him jump through all the hoops. 
“Place your bag, or sack, is it? On the scanner. Work Visa?” Like everyone else, Cracker did not like imitation cops looking down their noses at him. For that matter he didn’t like real cops acting superior either, but then again, they could shoot you just for the hell of it.
“Yep.”
“Retinal scan or implant?” 
“Tattoo.” Cracker thrust out his left arm, palm up.
“This is old technology. You must up-grade to retinal scanning or get an implant. Can I arrange an appointment for you?” The guard did his best impression of a desperate vacuum cleaner salesman.
“We have this discussion every time. It is way too expensive and I know you are just trying to sell product. Do you get kickbacks? Do you get more for the retinal tweaking or implant surgery?”
“Place your arm in the scanner, sir.” The guard replied with impertinence, managing to alter his inflection and tone so as to alter the meaning of ‘sir’ to ‘turd on the ground.’
“Its implants isn’t it?” Cracker quizzed as he waved his arm over the scanner. “Going under the knife every time there is a new model – the kick-backs must pile up, tax free, I bet.” The guard was tight lipped and more than a little upset. He did not like his integrity questioned, especially when it was plain he had questionable integrity. 
“You can go.” The guard pointed to the exit. “Now.”
“Is it true that they can force a customer to undergo surgery against their will?” Cracker was smiling widely. He knew the stop-go man was rattled.
“It’s a service contract, damn it! They know what they are getting into!” Retorted the red faced gate keeper.
Cracker laughed and wandered slowly through the gargantuan exit gates and into The Pale, a dark ghetto bounded on all sides by office blocks and high class apartment buildings, hundreds of stories high, rising through the layer and making a reasonable bid for outer-space. The Pale was the home of the unwanted and unnecessary human refuse of the great Primary Trade Dominion. The crime rate was unconscionably high and consequently the police rarely made a visit. Law enforcement was engaged in shielding the rich from the poor, not protecting the poor from each other. The poor could eat shit and die. In the Primary Trade Dominion, egalitarians were those who only ate eagle. 
* * *
Another check on the security records showed Cracker had finally made it out of the business district and into the Pale. Relieved, Sarah decided to do a little more digging and spent the rest of the morning interrogating the link and breaking into data-bases, gathering all known information about Tony Hardy. Unusually, however, she found that there was very little information stored about him at all. Most of the entries had been recently deleted, quite a few that very morning. No matter how hard she looked she could uncover almost no information pertaining to Tony (Cracker) Hardy. A birth certificate noting ‘Father Unknown’, some old school records, a business listing for Cracker Jack Cleaning and some fines for drunk and disorderly. That was about it. There were no expenditure analysis records, not one of the many thousands of advertising algorithms had ever sent him any spam and most unusually, there were no records of any malware attacks against him. 
Sarah sat still in silent contemplation, allowing her eyes to fade in and out of focus as she stared out the window, until she sported a blank thousand-yard stare. She mustered all of the processing power of her towering intellect, but drew no firm conclusions. On another day she might have shrugged off the creepy visit by the foul company agent, but he took off after cleaner with a hateful fervor. Something odd was afoot and this Cracker person seemed to be where the lines converged. She had, to this point, managed to elude the guilt of sending an armed intelligence agent after an unarmed cleaner, but it now began to advance in giant ripples of shame and dread. Outwardly, of course, she was ice cool. The legacy of a proper education and damned fine breeding, so she would have you believe. The truth, she never discussed for a number of reasons, was her mother managed to jag a rich boyfriend who had her safely squared away in boarding school the moment he moved in.
When 4.00pm approached she packed up for the day and took a hover-shuttle to the Elite-Management Medical Facility where she gave samples, underwent embarrassing and probing examinations and bombarded with radiation of all types from an army of scanners. She was in perfect health and was approved for promotion to Level 5. It amused her the results noted she was not pregnant. She had no intention of becoming a mother any time soon.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="A16B3850-ACE8-41EC-8782-24BC96C63417">
            <Title>Just a Few Questions</Title>
            <Synopsis>Agent Jones tortures his old colleagues and finds out the Cleaner had "Cracker Jack Cleaning Service" on his overalls. Agent Jones kills them, just for the hell of it.</Synopsis>
            <Text>
Just a few Questions
2 to 3 weeks after the bombing
Agent Jones had the techs were separated and tied into interrogation chairs in adjoining interview suites. All upholstery and bindings were made from uncomfortable scratchy plasti-carbon fibre both for strength and ease of cleaning. Blood and chunks of tissue easily hosed off the fabric, facilitating a speedy turnover of prisoners during busy times. Most interrogators did not carry a set of medieval instruments of torture, a pharmacy of pain amplifying drugs or some cliched psychology text book. The method of choice was the time honoured belting the crap out of the prisoner until he confesses. Agent Jones however, carried a chef’s knife roll filled with gleaming stainless steel knives, curved saws, drills and diabolical mechanical devices with spinning cutting wheels and pointy bits. He loved a good trope.
“What the fuck? There is no need for this.” Screamed Tech idiot 1. “What do you want to know?”
“Tell me about the cleaner.” Agent Jones approached with a slender probe and slipped it into the webbing of his fingers, drawing out a scream of fear and extreme pain.
“I don’t know. He stopped to help the blank and we told him to fuck off. That’s it.”
“What did he look like?” The second probe introduced to the webbing of his other hand.
“A Eurasian male, about 25 years old, dark hair and dressed in a pair of busted-ass overalls. I think he was a cleaner because of the logo.”
“Logo? Why didn’t you tell me this before?”
“You didn’t ask. You’re the fucking intelligence agent. I am just a tech. We love gadgets. We love slinking around in the dark, planting surveillance devices and the odd bomb. Investigation is your gig.”
“What did it say?” Agent Jones was excited, he might live to the end of the day yet. “The Logo?”
“I can’t remember. Jack’s cleaning? Maybe.”
“Let me assist your memory.” The probes sticking out of either hand were connected to a transformer and a current was sent into his body. He screamed and screamed, but no relief came. “Do you remember now?” The current was increased and the screams intensified, his steaming body jerking and jumping in spastic spasms. “How about now, fuck-face? Huh? Put my contract on the line will you?” Agent Jones allowed the current to flow for another minute or more before it was interrupted and the screams died down. 
“Let me think! Please, just let me think!” The tech was panting and frothing at the mouth, his sweat soaked torso squirming. “Jack, Jack’s Crack, Jack’s Crackers Cleaning? No, No. Cracker Jack Cleaning. That’s it. Cracker Jack Cleaning.” 
“There, that wasn’t so hard.” Agent Jones was very happy and decided to celebrate by torturing his ex-colleague for several more hours. Confirmation of “Cracker Jack Cleaning” was gained when the process was repeated with Tech No 2. They were then killed. He did not have to kill them and he probably shouldn’t have, but they were witnesses to his incompetence and they had to be eliminated. His career was far more important than their two lousy lives.
The interlink yielded very little information to Agent Jones, but after a few hours of on-line inquiries he managed to nail down a couple of addresses that looked promising. </Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="8A739941-A5B1-449B-8851-333716ADC717">
            <Title>Anthony "Cracker" Hardy</Title>
            <Text>Anthony “Cracker” Hardy
		Role in Story:	Central Male Character
		Occupation:	Contract Cleaner 
At the start of the book Cracker is a mere cleaner and neither happy nor unhappy with his existence.  But meeting Sarah makes him want more - he wants to be treated like the Airborne or at least something along the lines of equal treatment.  As a cloudborne individual he does not have the capacity to challenge the structures of society so he needs assistance.
   	
		Physical Description:	 appearance, 183cm, medium build, dark straight hair, pale complexion, dark slightly hooded eyes, straight-slender nose, bright smile with straight white teeth. Hands calloused, cracked and grimy. 
		Personality:	Poorly educated, but intelligent, street-wise and wise cracking. He has a childish and crude sense of humour and regularly makes coarse and inappropriate comments. He will steal anything if there is a prospect of getting away with it, but he is fiercely loyal and would never double-cross a friend. Blend of amoral criminality and Victorian decency.
		Habits/Mannerisms:	Thief, crude comments. Awkward attempts at humour. Loves to High five.
    	
		Background:	He was brought up by an uncle who disappeared/abandoned him while he was in his mid teens. He remained in the apartment he was brought up in, expecting eviction that never came. He fell into contract cleaning and started his own company. He uses his contacts in law enforcement to obtain contracts/jobs cleaning crime scenes.
		Internal Conflicts:	He hates his station in life and knows he cannot change it. He desperately wants to be loved, but the impersonal nature of the society has made a search for love impossible. He always desires women that are higher born and completely out of reach.
		External Conflicts:	 	
    	
		Notes:	</Text>
            <Notes>First appears with: Sarah</Notes>
        </Document>
