        <Document ID="EF5B25DA-CD68-4E3B-9005-29125509EAC4">
            <Title>Sarah Jacobson</Title>
            <Text>Sarah Jacobson
		Role in Story:		Central Female Character
		Occupation:		Mathematics and Cyber-Security Expert 
Sarah is airborne, but after meeting Cracker and their first silly adventure, she realises that she stands on the shoulder of many and has a bit of a re-think.  She is infected with the virus, but as it can only be transmitted through blood and semen etc she has not infected others.  She does, however, eventually infect the disc and Cracker.

… she was ice cool. The legacy of a proper education and damned fine breeding, so she would have you believe. The truth, she never discussed for a number of reasons, was her mother managed to jag a rich boyfriend who had her safely squared away in boarding school the moment he moved in.

    	
		Physical Description:	Caucasian female, 175cm, slim build, straight long blonde hair, perfect teeth, stunningly beautiful and possesses a towering intellect. 
		Personality:	She does not believe any man is her equal either intellectually, morally or physically, probably because it is true. She has a PhD in Cyber Security and is an upper level manager. Naive to the difficulties faced by the working class.
		Habits/Mannerisms:	Well spoken 
    	
		Background:	When she was young she was infected by the “Panacea virus” and has remarkable physical and intellectual attributes above and beyond most others. 
		Internal Conflicts:	Upon seeing th squaller most of the PTD citizens live in causes her to re-evaluate her lifestyle and her career.
		External Conflicts:	 Contracted as a Cyber-Security Expert to th Police Department, she is torn by the fact that the work is satisfying, but not upwardly mobile. She questions the basic ethos of the society - money is everything.	
    	
		Notes:	</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="6D68F8A2-5679-47BF-9C1A-A0A2A187FC88">
            <Title>Haloron Smythe</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="E4837D55-D3A8-4846-A801-B2ADFBDAC0CF">
            <Title>Private Contractor</Title>
            <Synopsis>Emil Kalheri's home - Hayley finds the blank's family - all murdered.
The chapter is where Hayley Lanning decides she required a Cyber Security Expert from outside the organisation.  She cannot trust the computer dudes already employed by the Police Department.  She goes to interview the guy only to find out she is being allocated a woman, Sarah.
There is a dirty dark haired man outside the house.  I was going to make that person Agent Jones, but that didn't go anywhere either.
Hayley finds out the body of Emile Kalheri had been picked up from the morgue by a woman claiming to be Mrs Kalheri - definitely not her.
I think the chronology of events may be out of whack here.  Yeah I am pretty sure some things are not in the right order.  </Synopsis>
            <Text>Private Contractor
3 days after bombing - year zero.

Although she had spent her career unraveling mysteries far more puzzling and inexplicable than her present mundane missing persons case. As with every other inquiry, she would attack with finesse, bringing to bear the adroit artifices born of experience and skill. Missing persons investigations necessarily involve interviewing relatives of the missing as, more often than not, a relative or friend was involved at some level. Guilty family members are always eager to lend a hand, but Hayley Lanning was never fooled, her legendary interviewing expertise would always get to the truth, no matter how awful. 
A lone visit to Emil’s family home in The Pale was a frightening prospect. Her interviewing skills didn’t mean shit down there and she would be in mortal danger. The Pale was filled with the refuse of the Primary Trade Dominion who would have no problem in raping and murdering an unaccompanied female, police officer or not. 
Hayley approached the door of the Kalheri hovel with trepidation, stepping over a dirty, dark haired man sleeping on the cold, filthy concrete. The door was a soiled translucent polymer with a small clear plastic window allowing the occupant an opportunity to identify assailants before the door is kicked in. On this occasion, however, this visitor did not force entry, but stood quietly at the door and politely waited. 
“Hello? Mrs. Kalheri? I am Det. Supt Lanning. Can I come in?” She did not want to enter the domicile. It was impossible to tell what sort of disgusting vermin riddled the premises and she was not keen to find out. She had burned many an outfit after visiting the pale, as it was near impossible to get rid of either the lice or the odour. She pushed and the door creaked open to revealed garbage and refuse piled high with a small pathway dug through to each room.
She entered and fully imbibed the fragrance of rotting food, mildew and sundry filth causing her breakfast to quickly rise and fill her mouth. She looked left and right and then just emptied her stomach contents onto one of the piles and then on the floor. Breathing through her mouth she noticed, with some disgust, that she could still unmistakably taste the unpleasant air of the squalid house. She stepped carefully through the refuse to what might be described as a lounge room, it was bigger that the other rooms and seemed to have some chairs that were not totally smothered. It was in the kitchen where she made the gristly discovery, an adult female and two female children, dead on the floor, with massive sealed wounds, characteristic of a particle weapon used at close range. 
Signs of lividity indicated the bodies had not been moved after death and they had been there for at least twelve hours. Det Lanning confirmed the estimate by comparing the ambient temperature with that of the bodies. She called it in and after waiting an eternity, a couple of uniforms wandered through the door. The death of an L.1 housewife was not important to the average cop, even an obvious murder such as this didn’t seem to raise too much interest in these guys. These two examples of law enforcement’s best were typical of many of her colleagues - cynical and lazy. They assume a world weary persona, having seen it all, but in reality they achieved fuck-all and complained at every opportunity. In particular they complained the law made their job too damned difficult and only corrupt sycophants get promoted. They were not wrong on either count, but what they could not or would not acknowledge is difficulty creates expertise and suck-ups always get promoted.
   
“I am Detectective Supt Lanning and I want this matter given the highest priority.” She directed. “Full forensics, no short-cuts.”
“Why? She’s just some low life. I mean just look at this shit-hole.” The senior of the two said. “We don’t have the resource to investigate every murder.”
“I am acting on a Commissioner’s Direction. Do not get in my way little men.”
“Yes, ma’am. I’ll call in the Cavalry.” He knew when to about-face.
“I expect all information to be sent to me. You are now in charge of this crime scene. Don’t stuff it up, boys. I will be watching you.”
“Whatever you want, Superintendent.” The youngest patrolman was now is full suck-up mode and accompanied her to the front door and watched as she disappeared into The Pale. He turned back to his partner, “What a fucken bitch! Who the fuck does she this she is? Huh?”
“Yeah, fuck her.” The older one agreed, but after some contemplation, “Make the call. We had better keep the Commissioner happy.”
* * *
Hayley returned to her desk in the Commissioners Office and was very glad to be back in a clean environment. She did not like leg work, especially when it took her to the Pale. It was simply a disgusting place. For the modern detective, investigations are mostly talking on the vid-phone to interview witnesses or even suspects. How on earth anyone conducted an investigation before the invention of vid-phones Hayley could not understand. She found it impossible to comprehend how prehistoric investigators did their work. Imagine speaking to every witness face to face. It must have taken forever!
She then commenced her list of calls and was shortly speaking to Kathy of the Morgue. “Do you have any results on the samples?”
“Hi Hayley. Wow, it was so lovely to meet you today! I have to report that the computer records are now missing. There is no record of any samples taken, not even the biopsy I took this morning.” Kathy did not sound happy, but was sporting a gormless grin. 
“Can’t you get more samples?”
“No, his wife collected him about an hour ago. The records disappeared a few minutes later.” She let that sit for a second. “I did keep a small section of the tissue sample I took this morning, cool huh?” She giggled. “I will conduct the tests myself, but it will take a while.” 
“His wife took him? I just left his wife about half an hour ago and she was in no condition to collect anyone.” Recalling the dreadful scene she recently stumbled upon. She could still smell the stench that had greeted her at the Kalheri residence and this thought had her abdomen lightly jerking as she suppressed nausea.
“I’ve had our computer-techs take a look and they can’t find any evidence of tampering. Whoever has hacked our system here really knows what they are doing. This is no teenager on a joy ride through the link, this is serious and well organised. If you want to find your man Kalheri, you will have to sort out our data corruption problem.”
“Get the techs to send me all the security files and vids since yesterday. In the meantime I will see if can get hold of someone who can help and I will get back to you.” 
“Whoever it is they will have to be very good, Superintendent.” Dr Logan’s hologram smiled brightly. “The techs at my end are no fools, but they are stumped.”
“I will track down the best man for the job.” She terminated the call and then raised the Commissioner’s secretary. “Janice, is this big man in?”
“Yes.”
“Good. Can you tell him I need to see him?”
“What about? He is a busy man.” She saw her job as protecting the commissioner from any distractions and garrisoned the terrain leading to his door.
“None of your business, L2. Tell him I am on the way.” 
Several minutes later she blew past the secretary, gave a cursory knock on the door and burst into the Commissioner’s Private Office. He was seated behind his desk reading text from his vid screen.
“At least you knocked.” Said the Commissioner, not unhappy. “What do you want?”
“I am here to report that I have located Emil Kalheri, sir.” Hayley announced in a grey monotone.
“Good, good. Where is he?”
“When I left him, he was lying on a slab in the Health, Relaxation and Respite Zone Morgue.”
“Excellent work.”
 
“He has disappeared again. According to the Coroner’s Office, his wife collected his body about ten minutes ago.”
“He has not disappeared then, he is with his family.”
“No. I found Mrs. Kalheri and daughters dead on the floor of their home about an 2 hours ago. They had been dead for quite a while and there is no way she collected Emil’s body today. On top of that all of his records have been deleted.”
“Like I said before, what do you want?” Commissioner was getting a little testy. I asked her to find a missing person not bitch about the computer network.
“I need a computer geek, preferably an expert in cyber-security.” Hayley announced. “We will have to put him on contract.”
“We’ve got plenty of them in this department.” Commissioner did not like bringing in outsiders, they were alway way too expensive and could never keep their expert mouths shut. “What’s wrong with our experts?” 
“They are not good enough and they are, without exception, too lazy and too indiscreet. I need someone independent of this department.” Detective Lanning did not like shining a light of truth on her department. She adored investigative work, but despised the lazy jury-fuckers she called colleagues, those who, either deliberately or by abject incompetence, made her job impossible. There were always some people who did not like to work, but law enforcement attracted more than their fair share, far more. “I need someone who not only knows what they are doing, but also cares about what they are doing. The Police Department is not exactly over run with these types.”
“Someone you can trust?” He joked. He trusted no-one, why should she?
“I don’t think I can trust any outsider, just more than the members of this department. I need your permission for funding. He won’t come cheap.” 
“Yes, but he will have to be operational. I am not paying contract rates for a fucking desk-jockey.” The Commissioner chuckled, an untrained geek wouldn’t last too long in a critical incident and the department would not have to pay the contract out. He loved the idea of dropping a bucket of shit on an uninitiated outsider. “Go to it.”
- - -</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="F472C924-96A2-4C67-85B0-4D4B9F5A8109">
            <Title>Extra DNA</Title>
            <Synopsis>Cracker (even though he is still smarting from being shot) and Sarah decide to confront Kleo regarding the pregnancy. 
</Synopsis>
            <Text>Extra DNA
2 to 3 weeks after bombing
Cracker’s wounds were healing and closing up nicely but, since he was a good 37 or so degrees warmer that the rest of the inhabitants of the morgue, he could not stay. Now that he was dead, the future was replete with opportunity and he could leave his old life behind him. He did not think that it was likely that he would suddenly become rich and powerful, but he was hoping to jump a few levels in this new incarnation. After all, I am the decendant of a dead rich cunt. Or is it rich dead cunt?
“I will get back to headquarters, see how the dust has settled and and file my report. I will be fine, the Commissioner thinks he got his way and will be satisfied he has forced his will upon me. I expect a Cheshire cat sitting behind his desk when I walk in, but that will disappear when I demand the promotion he had on offer. Detective Chief Superintendent sounds good to me.” In preparation for her showdown, however, Hayley would be packing as many weapons as she could reasonably hide on her person. “Take Cracker to the safe house and I will meet you there this evening.”
“I’ll get all the documentation up to speed and Cracker will be officially dead.” Dr Logan, joyful as ever, set to the task. “Keep in touch and good luck.”
Out on the street Cracker and Sarah trudged towards the Immigration Gates and were waved through without delay. Security cared not for who left the HRRZ, their only concern was keeping people out.
“I’ve got the disc, safely wrapped in plastic, so we should have no problems with facial recognition software. The last thing we need is you being caught on tape wandering around a couple of hours after you died.” 
“Being dead will take a little getting used to.” 
“I would like to take a detour, if you feel up to it.” She thought it may be pushing him a bit far, but she desperately wanted to confront Kleoboulos and his culpable apprentices. She knew the confrontation would serve very little purpose, other than affording the opportunity to vent her disgust at the atrocity they had committed. She had seriously considered reporting the crime to the police, but several weeks as a departmental contractor had convinced her that involving the police in anything other than, perhaps, a noise complaint, was pointless. The Primary Trade Dominion Department of Public Safety did nothing to keep the public (or each other) safe, but actively undermined any efforts to suppress crime in order to prosecute their own private agendas. If there was nothing in it for them, they were not interested and in fact, they were the leading perpetrators of crime in most categories. 
“Not really, I got shot three times and died two hours ago.” He rubbed his chest and belly gingerly, the pain still pulsing away quietly. “I think I need a day or two. What did you have in mind?” 
“I want to talk to the sages, but it can wait a couple of days.” Her need to speak to to them was urgent, but after shooting Cracker she thought she should afford him a little consideration. “Let’s get you to your safe-house.”
“No, that sounds like fun. Let’s go.”

</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="E2571F9B-549F-4787-9C23-911289FA706E">
