        <Document ID="CC974F8D-64EF-49B2-9060-F1132BD9836A">
            <Title>Sarah meets Cracker</Title>
            <Synopsis>It was her first day in new position as Cyber Security Manager


He already knew what he was going to tell his drinking buddies – You should have seen this girl. You’d crawl across 3 k’s of broken glass just to suck the cock that fucked her!  He knew it wasn’t exactly original and could not remember where he had first heard it, but she was literally that beautiful. Now all he had to do was locate 3 kilometres of broken glass and a bottle of mouthwash.

  It was not that she was totally devoid of human sympathy, it was that these type things were not considered important by the company and, consequentially, by anyone else. If the company did not worry about these things, the individual should not concern themselves either. If his death brought more profit, then the death was worthwhile, even necessary. Everyone knew that.</Synopsis>
            <Text>
Recruitment policy rarely titillated the attention of Tony “Cracker” Hardy. Why he was offered work, and why he took it, remained untested mysteries. During the endless hours of his monotonous labours, he made no cause to reflect on whether his was a dog’s life, whether the grass paid him mind, whether a bullfighter could be a lady, whether he could fall in love, whether his heart was wooden, or what colour suede shoes one could safely step on.
Indeed, he didn’t think twice. Thinking was neither for the millionth nor last time for Cracker. Or the first, for that matter. Nothing was always on his mind, other than somehow, someone was usually trying to tax him over.
Jagging some unexpected work for The Airborne had obvious advantages, especially in an office above the Layer, which afforded an actual view out its windows. Cracker could see clear across the floating, pea-soup acid-mist to the next building, and hundreds of metres beyond.
Between monolithic business and residential towers, swarms of hover-copts flitted between landing pads that sprouted from the sides of buildings like mushrooms from a tall tree. When a child, he once saw two hover-copts. They fell from the clouds, their burning wrecks crashing into the neighbouring apartment block. Without forethought of danger, he and his fellow hooligans went playing among the cooling carnage. They laughed at the passengers’ lifeless grimaces, their lips burned away exposing gleaming, full-toothed Colgate grins. Gawking adults also mocked the gruesomely barbecued Airborne folks. Their mirth filled the hearts of Cracker and his mates with righteous comfort.
A hot, smoky flash brought Cracker back from his reverie and into his present-day eyes. Two hover-copts collided. As their burning, entangled carcasses broke up, they fell out of sight under the Layer. A fiery ring expanded and burned a pit in the fluffy poison. It persisted only moments until the Layer closed over.
As with most of life’s experiences, Cracker savoured this hover-copt crash with less satisfaction than his first.

The Airborne never descended to the cloud-borne ground or streets. Instead, they sought less grimy options in the sun above the Layer, and flew in hover-copts like gluttonous insects, between honey pots, greedy for social opportunities in the right restaurants with the right people. The frenzied frequency of such flitting about ensured mid-air collisions were plentiful. A fact of life, and the reasonable price of avoiding the Cloudborne. The Airborne would rather roast in a fireball than breath the same oxygen-starved air as their unworthy, under-layer underlings.
As the Layer swallowed the hover-copt wrecks, Cracker dropped his mop. It slapped moistly and woodenly on the durable office floor. “Did you see that?” he said on impulse, and received the disapproval of thin office lips and superior office eyebrows. Presently, without apparent care for the implacably deadly accident on the other side of their windows, most of the condescending facial features returned to business. Others remained fixed on the boorish cleaner, and gave their heads to shaking. Cracker took this as an invitation to piss off somewhere else, which he did, shuffling to the main job in the office next door. 
He hoped no one would report him for breaching work-place etiquette. He needed jobs like this, and the opportunities they furnished for gazing upon the tidier, lovelier women such workplaces selected, attracted and nourished. If Cracker did have thoughts, he had them of women, especially office-entitled women. He industriously catalogued them all. Finance bred large breasts. Cyber Security engendered tall blondes. In Publishing, girls with endearing lisps flourished. Biotech enhanced lab-coat-wearing waifs with irresistible come-fuck-me eyes. Events Management produced women who could organise the end of the world, yet were incapable of cleaning their own toilets. Of all the vocations, Cracker shied away only from police and People &amp; Culture consultants, whose cultured, medicinal beauty Cracker felt concealed an insatiable enmity against men such as himself. 
Cracker went a-cleaning, a deep-clean in the office of another executive who opted for the suicide retirement package (please select between gun in the mouth, neck-tie of computer cable, or overdose with your choice of restricted substance). Such retirements were on the rise, and no one thought much about them. Certainly, police departments though not much about them, and generally assigned investigations to the most junior officers they could get away with.
Cracker resented the visceral mess of the executive’s resignation. Who used firearms these days? When particle-pulse weapons were quieter, more accurate, and far cleaner, fusing and sealing all wounds? No bothersome rivers of blood, just drag the body out, give the floor a quick mop, and you’re open again for business. But Travis Teh (the name was still on the door and the desk plate) used a truly messy and antiquated shotgun. His brains were on every surface.
When Cracker was within finishing, a tall, stunning blonde (and therefore, in Cyber Security) strolled off the elevator, attracting a wake of slack-jawed gawkers of all genders from offices, meeting rooms, and water coolers. At once, her blue eyes pierced Cracker to the core, and excited his heart to palpitate. Her high cheek bones, unblemished skin, and faultless teeth, drew the air from his lungs and knocked him aback. Her hair was a cascade of pure white sand, alight with random prisms of colour. Her legs, hips and breasts (insufficiently large for a role in Finance) inspired his atheist soul to desire rapturous reconciliation with God, and to bless the hapless Travis Teh who splattered himself in the service of keeping Cracker busy until she arrived. With a melancholy sigh, Cracker knew he would never lay his Cloudborne hands anywhere near her. A short conversation was his highest expectation.
In the mirror behind the office door, he saw his white overalls bearing the name CRACKER JACK CLEANING SERVICE, and the large rip in them—a legacy of poor tailoring and an expanding butt—which yielded an unflattering view of his boxers, branding him among the most objectionable of grunts, even if he was wearing his good underwear.
 Cracker’s mind dredged for something witty and original to say. Something to set him apart from the crowd. Something whimsical, yet pithy. As she entered, he almost tripped over a chair as he backed into.
“How long will you be?” Her voice was joyously authoritarian to his ears, as he tried to hide the rend in his overalls. “I was told the office would be ready by now.”
“Shotgun,” was all he could cough.
“What?” 
He tried again, but nothing came out. Then he wheezed, and that was worse.

Sarah Jacobson knew the effect she had on men, but she would not make this easy for him. Men ain’t nothin’ but dicks and hormones, her sister used to tell her. No brains … Sarah lost her train of thought, distracted by the cleaner rubbing his tush against the wall. Was he buffing away one last suicide smudge before she saw it?
“Do you speak English?”
The cleaner gulped and settled. “Yes, English. Mr. Teh … used a shotgun, ma’am, and … well their was much of him to retrieve.” He smiled with the achievement of completing an entire sentence.
“Shotgun?”
“Yes ma’am.”
“Are you hiding something nasty?” she asked the cleaner.
The question seemed to shame him. “Ma’am?”
“On the wall. Step aside and let me see the wall.” She flicked her hand. The cleaner jumped aside and collided with the glass office partion.
Sarah stifled a laugh as she ran her fingers over the desk, and the executive chair’s arms and seat.
“I would recommend replacing that,” he said, sounding more relaxed. “Brains are a bastard to get out.” He flushed with embarrassment.
Sarah held out her hand and circled an index finger like she was slowly stirring a beverage. Cracker took her meaning and rotated for her. Sarah laughed when she saw the rip in his overalls, exposing his boxer shorts. They were pink with little cartoon fairies. In other, more less mutant universes, the fairy would be recognised as Tinker Bell.
“They were fifty percent off  in a six-pack in Floormart,” Cracker explained, red-faced.
“Are those places as bad as the streams I’ve seen?” Sarah asked.
“Worse. I was amazed I found a pack that was still sealed.”
Sarah straightened her smile and her spine. “Well, next time I encounter splattered frontal lobes, I’ll know who to call. You should use that in advertisements.” 
“Yes, I see it now: Cracker Jack Cleaning Service—Splattered Brains? No Stains,” he quipped, surprised at the speed of his reply. “I have your permission to use it then, ma’am?”
“Please, I’m Sarah. Sarah Jacobson. When will you be finished here?” 
“I just have to steam-clean the carpet. You should be fine to settle in then.”
“Settle in? Oh, I’m not working in this slaughterhouse. It smells of cleaning fluids and diarrhea in here. And …” Sarah sniffs the air as if for potentially toxic gases. “… what’s that aftershave you’re wearing? Is that aftershave you’re wearing?”
“It’s, uh, vodka,” the cleaner said and scratched a particularly nasty shaving rash under his chin.
“Cheap vodka,” Sarah said, curling her nose. “As for my office, I’m at the other end of the floor. I’m thinking I might knock a few walls out and turn this space into a lunch room. Then there’ll be no concerns if you leave some dead meat lying around.”

Sarah sat at her pristine desk, in her pristine high-back chair, in her gleaming new office, and stroked the fine-grained, polished timber desktop with such satisfaction, you’d think she’d inherited a gelato factory. Squaring her shoulders, she readied herself for business, and fired up her quirky little holoscreen. It was an antique device, but the peace she felt when using it … well, that’s another story.
Its voice and display reported a table of messages, mostly relating to her previous position. One was about her promotional med-exam, which was at 1:30pm that day. Though a formality, promotions were never confirmed without a clean bill of health. Sarah had never been sick in her life, never so much as a cold. Viruses never affected her. Sarah never sniffled, except in the aftermath of shotgun suicides.
She gestured at the holoscreen, and it served her the latest watchlists of available properties. She scanned, and quickly found Travis Teh’s apartment already up for lease. He’s only been dead 7 hours. It’s in a good building, above the Layer’s cloud level too. I’ll have to look into it, in fact I’ll take it. Without thought or consideration for whether Shotgun Dude was a family guy or what fates the family might meet, she made a bid, and the property was hers.

Dead meat? You’ll be fucking dead meat, you …
But then Cracker’s thoughts dissolved, as a cascade of other fantasies of meat and fucking came to mind. All he could feel was her absence, as pains in his chest and groin. He should finish steam-cleaning. Instead, he found himself stalking down a hallway in search of her. He didn’t even feel his legs move. If only he could think of some blithering thing to say.

“Ah, Mr Fairy Boxers,” Sarah said as the cleaner appeared at her office door, with his hands clasped at his navel. “The scent of your vocation preceded you down the hallway by some seconds.”
The cleaner cast about for something he might give voice to, and came up with, “Sticking with holoscreens are you?” He chuckled. “Where’s your beta-max?”
Sarah rolled her eyes. Her expression posted a message any woman could read, and which every man gormlessly missed: why do men think insults are good pick-up lines? No one dissed her holoscreen.
“What’s your name, Mr Fairy Boxers?” she challenged, while still feeling chuffed about her new apartment.
“My name’s Tony Hardy, but everyone calls me Cracker.”
“Good. Cracker Fairy Boxers it is then.”
 
Cracker hoped Sarah might be a nice person, but she only made him feel ashamed. He thought, Typical Airborne. With their obscene riches, they were no better than anyone else, but they acted as if their bowels made birthday cake. While I’m taxed for life. Flourishing Cloudborne businesses rarely lived for long. Instead they were absorbed in hostile takeovers or crushed under-foot. Every morning he woke with the fear he would be evicted from his home and stripped of every meagre thing he owned. “You know, I got the call for this gig at 4.00am. I was told to ready the office for some new computer virtuoso, and what thanks do I get? Some blonde Cyber Security clone looking down her perfect nose at me. Maybe I should squirt Shotgun Dude’s guts back into that room.”
Sarah blinked when she realised the cleaner came up with the same nickname for the deceased Mr Teh. Then she shrugged. “I’m sorry if you took my behaviour as rude, Cracker Fairy Boxers. Will there be anything else?”
Cracker set his jaw and inflated his chest. “You’ve been marked for bigger things, eh?”
“Who told you that?” she blurted out.
“Ma’am. I’m not at liberty to discuss it,” he said, swelling with the point he’d scored.
Sarah leaned forward enough for her blouse to gape and present a seductively safe glimpse of her lingerie d’mamaries. “Tell me how a cleaner would know anything about me.”
WIth the pleasure of his brief win souring, Cracker swallowed and froze. Sarah stared and waited him out with an excoriating stare. “Okay, okay,” he said, to kick-start his lungs. The remainder came in a single blurt. “I have a friend—well, more of an acquaintance. Actually, not so much an acquaintance as … never mind. He’s in public relations—”
“Which makes him a liar.”
“—of the Department of Traffic Movement and Public Security—”
“A colossal liar then.”
“He’s in Homicide.”
“A colossal, homicidal liar.”
“I think calling him homicidal is a bit purple. Anyway, whenever there are late night incidents of a people-dying-horribly nature, he directs the cleaning work my way. That’s it, I swear. He heard the new exec was a high-flyer, that’s all.”
Sarah’s eyes narrowed. “Do you scrub evidence as poorly as you scrub away gore?”
“No? … No! Never,” Cracker said, trying to keep his tells from telling. “I never mess with any evidence. I’m never late. I get the job done quickly, so everyone can keep up with the making money.”
“Yes, and I should be on with it. Good day, Mr Fairy Boxers.” 
A deflated Cracker left. 
Sarah checked her door was opaqued and locked, then raised her arms triumphantly above her head. She was on her way up, however this gig evolved.

Before we finish with this, their first encounter, there remains one detail we should report: the thought of Cracker’s boxer shorts made Sarah smile.</Text>
            <Notes>What design is on Cracker’s boxer shorts that endears him to Sarah? “They were 50% off in a 6-pack” … Agent Jones must buy them at the same sale.


14 hours before bombing - 9 months BTD
“The reformer has enemies in all who profit by the old order and only lukewarm defenders in all those who would profit by the new order” Machiavelli, The Prince, 1513.

, Cleaner, Level 2, or any of his low-born peers

, rising sharply and dropping suddenly to rest gently, but fleetingly, on the pin-pong bat landing pads jutting out willy-nilly into the cluttered airspace. </Notes>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="D90C144C-70D5-4269-AEAB-7D7C08F50B23">
            <Title>Honouring Dishonour.</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="CDE651E8-B243-4C62-9E8A-A1DA47537BA2">
            <Title>Elvis of the Travelator</Title>
            <Synopsis>Cracker and Sarah make it to the travelator to speak with the sages, only to find Elvises. The Elvi fall to their knees to worship the Blessed Mother. Sarah is not happy with the adoration (and worship of the unborn child) and tells them she doesn't know if she is going to full term.  The Elvises are not happy - they are all sterile and cannot understand the concept of abortion.  (Later, after the Humpening, the Elvi are infected by the virus and become fertile.)
</Synopsis>
            <Text>Elvis of the Travelator
2 to 3 Weeks after bombing  
Somehow, Sarah identified the correct car when she saw it slowly wobbling towards them and they both hopped on with no troubles. Expecting to see the three wise-rapists, they were very surprised to be met by a group of well-dressed Elvi, all in stances of different martial arts styles, ready to fight.
“Who are you? What do you want?” Tae Kwan Do Elvis had a suspicious mind.
“Hey boys, what’s up?” Cracker was cheerful and always got on well with the Elvi “I am Anthony Hardy. I guess you guys are all called Elvis?” He smiled widely.
“The Great Teacher!” Wing Chung Elvis knelt and bowed low, his forehead touching the floor. 
“No need for that, Elvis. This is Sarah.”
“Oh, the Blessed Mother!” Karate Elvis dropped to his knees. “We are truly honoured.”
 “Get up.” Sarah was presently obsessed with tracking Kleo and his band of assailants and found Elvi adoration repellant. “Where are the sages, Elvis?”
“They have been summoned by the High Elvis on a mission of great importance and we have been given the task of defending their keep, Blessed Mother.” Karate was now standing erect and staring ahead.
“Don’t call me that. I still don’t know if I going to full term yet.” Sarah came from the ‘my body, my choice’ school of thought and knew she had not said anything controversial. 
“No, No. Please Blessed Mother.” Karate again dropped to his knees. “You must deliver to us the one-after-the-one. I fear my brothers and sisters would become lost and would risk being infected by the religion.” Karate Elvis’ hands were shaking and lips quivering, eyes welling up. He came from the ‘my Saviour, my business’ school of thought. “We cannot be denied him.”
“That is probably not a discussion you want to have with the Blessed Mother, Elvis.” Cracker didn’t know much about women, but he knew men had no lines in that play. 
“Forgive me, great teacher.” Unconvinced, tears cascaded down his face. 
“Tell me, Elvi, where have the sages gone?” Sarah was not moved by the embarrassing display.
“They are in the quarter, five of our number been possessed by the Jesus.” Karate managed to get out through the tears.
“The protection of the Dawkins be upon them.” Tae Kwan Do and Wing Chung parroted.
“Then we shall go to the quarter and find the sages.” Sarah announced as if the travelator had a steering wheel and had expected the helmsmen to change course.
“It will take about 6 hours to get back around there.” Karate grabbed his guitar and started playing, the two others joining in for an impromptu concert.
The Blessed mother spoke to me today
Her smile kept my troubles at bay,
To her I wish to say
Abort and we’ll blow you away.
Yeah, abort and we’ll blow you away.
“It’s safe to say all of the Elvi will have a similar reaction and if you expect support from the Elvine, you will be very disappointed. They are all sterile and will not understand the destruction of such a precious gift.” The ways of the Elvi were complex, but also childlike. Cracker had been dealing with them for many years and had only recently been able to navigate the nuanced social structure of the clone nation.
“Their view seems unequivocal, but it is none of their business. I will be careful not to to speak of this again, certainly not while we are in the quarter.”
“Only five hours and fifty-nine minutes to go.” Cracker said as the concert continued.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="1FF31E79-3AE0-4F7C-AD2C-1F75547C61C8">
            <Title>Co-opted Allocation</Title>
            <Synopsis>Shakti immediately contacts his mother, so if Sarah ever puts herself up for allocation (random marriages arranged by the government to improve productivity) his mother will rig the allocation. </Synopsis>
            <Text>Co-opted Allocation
2 to 3 weeks after bombing

Shakti didn’t care too much for interrogation techniques, it seem all so aggressive and manipulative. “If only they would just tell the truth, it would really make thing a whole lot easier.” He had often said, quite stupidly, out loud. When the acclaimed Detective Superintendent Lanning volunteered to take the lead on the interview, he snapped up the chance. To prepare she needed an hour or so to review the file and Shakti took the chance to call his mother.
“Alex, what do you want?” His mother was in the middle of entertaining important people and had no time to indulge her child.
“Mother, I don’t have much time. I have just arrested a serial killer and I am about to interview him.”
“Oh, simply ghastly. Please cease reminding me that you are a public servant. What do you want? Your father and I have guests.”
“Can you add another to the list?” Shakti 
“Yes, dear.”
“Sarah Jacobson, PhD, L.5.”
“I hope she’s better than the rest. At least she is level 5.”
“Thank you, mother.”
“Go back to your sordid criminals.” She disconnected quickly. She had guests to attend to.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="1F2E8303-0183-4629-B103-F9F87C171343">
            <Title>New Folder</Title>
