        <Document ID="919D2CF0-5507-4484-8FBD-D91FC4E6F9C9">
            <Title>Leiutenant Shakti</Title>
            <Synopsis>Quick introduction to Eloise Shakti - one fierce operator.
Introduction to the incompetent Lieutenant Shakti - I like the idea of this character bumbling his way through life, always trying but never quite getting there.  Another example of a privileged person being granted the keys to the kingdom.  His brain does not function correctly it seems, as his brain would not accept brain-dumps - "Monkeys retain information better".  He eventually joins the police - a complete humiliation for his mother and father.  Later you find out that the rank of "Lieutenant" does not even exist and it was created just for him so he would not be taking orders from the 'cloudborn'.

Shakti is only good at reading off a cue card and is placed in the public affairs division - and then has his own TV show - Random Camera.  All this is a bit of a wank and didn't really go anywhere.  So it can be re-cycled or dumped.  There are a couple of further mentions of Random Camera later in the book.  Random Camera is a trope-fest.</Synopsis>
            <Text>
LIEUTENANT SHAKTI – PEOPLE’S HERO
9 months BTD
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="C540D38F-F90F-4347-9A8B-959BB0E497A9">
            <Title>Fin</Title>
            <Text>Fin
Earth 1
Many Universes Ago

The fallibility of any advanced technology is inversely proportional to the intellect of the user, but even the most brilliant are prone to moments of colossal idiocy. It is the smartest, the highly educated and the best of breed who determine the ascent, decline or end of a civilisation.
 * * * 
Thousands of transparent go-cubes, perfectly centred in their lanes and precisely one metre apart, silently careened above the broad bio-asphalt transit-way snaking a path through the lush urban rainforest. Dr Anthony Hardy squirmed uncomfortably in the synth-leather seat, frantically examining the holographic simulation floating in the air before him. According to reports, today’s test-run would proceed without catastrophe. The team had been running simulations for months and predicted a stable stasis field except, of course, for one annoying minority report. 
“A black hole?” He finally said. 
“Don’t worry, Tony.” Dr Sarah Jacobson reassured with a flash of white enamel. “The probability is infinitesimal. I’ll bring up the numbers.”
“No need. I’ve seen them, checked them, and checked them again.” He terminated the projection and took his wife’s hand. “So where does this black hole come from?”
“A localised gravity well.” She replied. “It is unexpected, but hardly catastrophic.”
“A black hole is a black hole, you can’t dismiss my concerns so easily.” He replied. “Exit ramp!” 
The cube jagged and took the off-ramp which decended sharply under the road-deck and into the heart of Marrickville Forest.
“How is it that no-one can make a go-cube with a smooth ride?” Sarah squeaked, face smooched against the side window.
“Old cars had wheels that actually touched the ground, amplifying every bump.” Anthony grabbed her arm, reefed her upright and pecked her on the cheek. “No anti-grav back then.” 
“The world has you to thank for that.” She beamed, although her contribution to dark-energy research was just as praise worthy. “We make greater strides today, do we not?”
“Unless…”
“… Unless earth gets sucked into a big black hole.” She rolled her eyes. “But we do have the fail-safe protocol.”
“That is of little comfort” He countered. “We don’t even know if the information will be preserved.”
“Or if our transmissions will ever be heard.” 
“Whatever happens, we will make history.”
“Or end it.” 
Suddenly decelerating the go-cube whipped 90 degrees and ducked into a narrow, un-signposted driveway snugly wedged between two straight rows of ancient, red mahoganies. The cube slid along the skinny boulevard, gliding into a thicket of eucalypts, pine trees and cedars gradually thinning out to a clearing of manicured lawn. The cube spun and paused. Two transparent gull-wing doors yawned open, allowing the two scientists to disembark. 
The pathway from the drop zone hugged the bank of a shallow meandering stream. Pristine water babbled quietly across flat pebbles, as the morning sunlight manically bounced off the rippling surface. A mob of grey kangaroos had gathered at the water’s edge, supervising their young who were jumping about in rough-house play, sorting out the junior pecking order. The largest of the males looked up and considered the two primates approaching, leaned back on his tail and displayed his broad chest which he scratch rhythmically with black paws.
 
 “Gotta pay the ferryman.” Dr Hardy said as he produced a sandwich from his pocket. 
“Is peanut butter even good for them?” Asked Sarah. By now the animal was conditioned and bounded over, presenting himself for payment.
“Does it matter? These things are everywhere,” he said, offering up alms to the kangaroo king of Marrickville. “Not to mention the bloody koalas. How long did it take us to get here today? Stopping every five minutes for a fucking marsupial to cross the road.” The kangaroo took the sandwich, a paw on both sides, took a bite and chewed happily.
“Language, Dr Hardy!” Sarah protested. “It makes you sound so dull.”
“We wouldn’t want to be wedded to a dullard, would we?” He replied, sarcasm front and centre.
“You know what I mean. I would love you if you were….a cleaner. But this is not about cleaners or koalas, is it?” Sarah took her husband’s hand and they watched the kangaroo hop away. “The test will be fine.” She kissed him gently on the cheek.
“You’re right, of course.” He grinned, absently taking a draught of the cool morning air, imbibing the vapors of eucalyptus and pine. “It’s good to be alive.” 
“Let’s get to work then!” Sarah replied.

* * * 
As they approached the research facility, two broad doors silently rolled open, granting access to a brightly lit atrium of marble and glass. Uncluttered by security gates, metal detectors or people, Dr’s Hardy and Jacobson quickly ambled across the gleaming marble towards the elevators. Dr Hardy pressed B10 and waited momentarily for an elevator to present itself and both climbed on board descended 100 metres below the ground to the test facility. 
The state of the art facility was where wild scientific theories clashed, sometime violently, with the laws of physics. Theoretical physicists could contrive any theory at a whim, but it fell to the experimental physicist to do the heavy lifting. 
Theoretically, it was expected that today’s test would form a stable stasis field, inside which time would slow almost, but not quite, to the point of cessation. Time could never stop moving forward, but it could be slowed down with a recipe of gravity waves, dark matter and dark energy. It all seemed so simple. 
“Boss?” John ??? Said. “What music for today?” 
“I think it’s Kathy’s call today, isn’t it? Kathy?” Dr Hardy asked.
“I have something very special.” Kathy’s clear eyes sparkled brightly. “The Presley Brothers, Elvis and Jesse.”
“Never heard of them” Dr Hardy said.
“They were one of the first rock n’ roll gospel singers, about 100 years ago.” She replied. “I can take or leave the songs, but theirs voice are spectacular.”
‘Okay, The Presley Brothers it is.”



A lab is testing some sort of device 
2 small discs spinning
A gravity well forms
Gets larger and larger.
Starts to consume objects
The Hardy and Jacobson freak out
“We can’t shut it down!” “It’s expanding exponentially!” “Abort!” “Abort! Abort!” “There’s nothing we can do. Initiate sucession protocols.” “Is it all recorded?” “Every data base we could find” “Let’s fuckin hope we haven’t been looking at a reflection of our own galaxy. “That would put a crimp in our multi-verse theory.” “And this would be all for nothing.” “It has been an honour working with you.” “I love you too Anthony.”
Aerial view of the compound crumbling in on itself and into a hole in the ground that grows, consuming the car park other buildings, trees, a helicopter trying to make a desperate escape. The hole widens and consumes the surrounding landscape then swiftly sucks the entire planet into a black hole. It widens and widens, one of the spiral arms of the milky way slows down and begins to swirl and spins into the ever widening expanse. Other spiral arms are knocked out of orbit, crash into one another and spin   into the black hole. The gravity shift causes the super giant black hole at the centre of the milky way to consume the inner spiral arms until there are just two black holes that move together and form as one.
 






