        <Document ID="9944DB6B-732D-4950-B9E3-9C119A1EDA42">
            <Title>An afternoon at Cracker's</Title>
            <Synopsis>This is where firstly Agent Jones lobs up to Cracker's and tortures him to get info on the disc.  Cracker is infected by the virus so the blows don't do too much damage and allows him to be a smart-arse whilst getting bashed.  Sarah and Hayley turn up and scare him off. (he convinces them not to kill him by telling them of Damien Hardman/CEO's black operation of digging up the old bio-lab. He is their operative now, maybe)  
</Synopsis>
            <Text>
An afternoon at Cracker’s
14 days after bombing
Hayley and Sarah discussed a no-knock entry with all the trimmings. A suited up entry team bristling with firepower blowing the door clean off its plastic hinges, but decided on a discreet visit to Mr Hardy’s tiny apartment, just the two of them. Sarah thought to contact Cracker somehow, but dismissed the notion. It would be extremely suspicious if the only raid she had ever been on was compromised in such a fashion. She crossed her fingers and placed trust his discretion if, indeed, he had any.
As they approached, Sarah noted Cracker’s door ajar and the plastic jamb was shattered. Hayley drew her pistol and motioned Sarah behind her as she gently prodded the door open. Muffled voices from within drew them to the living area where a dark haired man was standing was holding a pistol to the head of Cracker, kneeling silently before him. Hayley took careful aim, but remained motionless, index finger lightly touching the trigger.
“Where’s the disc? Where is the disc?” Agent Jones was livid and struggled with his ever present internal demons, but managed so far not to kill Cracker. “Do you know what I am?”
“Some fuck-wit with a weird shaped head?” Cracker knew he was pretty much dead, but there was always the possibility of salvation and a few laughs. All he had to do was stretch this out as long as possible and hope for a miracle.
“I am not human. I am the biological manifestation of the company’s will. In the name of stability I destabilize. In the name of order I deliver chaos. In the name of life I bring death.” Jones could not stop the smirk forming and broadening across his face. He loved scaring people, it was deeply and profoundly fulfilling. Killing, although necessary when certain criteria had been satisfied, was not his ultimate goal. Once dead they could feel neither pain nor fear and could no longer amuse him. “Where is the disc?”
“What’s it look like again?” Cracker’s memory was fading quickly as the moments slipped by. 
“It is a thin metal disc, about the size of a thumbnail.” 
“Nah, nothing’s coming to mind.” As he said this he was struck hard across the face with the butt of the pistol, but no blood appeared. He became light headed and he thought of Sarah and how he would never betray her. He would die first, he silently hoped. “Um, small disc you say?” What’s the big deal anyway?”
“It is a highly sophisticated piece of technology and I want it back.” 
“Did you lose it yourself? Are you in trouble with your boss? No wonder you are so upset. Look, if I find a disc, you will be the first person I call. There is no need for this.” 
“Shut up.” Agent man smashed the butt of the pistol into Cracker’s face, but he did not budge and he did not bleed. “I am asking the questions here. I am going to torture you and you are going to tell me what you know.”
“Okay, I’ll talk.” Cracker was highly skilled at the art of bullshitting off-the-cuff and had no intention of saying anything that resembled the truth.
Recognising the dark haired man and a little surprised that Hayley had not yet opened fire, Sarah sprinted across the room in a blur of speed and intent, driving her shoulder into his rib cage, audibly smashing bone. Agent Jones was airborne for three or four metres, striking the filthy kitchen wall, but before he struck the ground Sarah had removed the pistol from his hand and had it pressed up against his forehead. 
“Wow, Sarah I didn’t know you had it in you.” Hayley was impressed, but confused. She had figured Sarah a desk jockey, but she was quite clearly mistaken. 
“I have seen this man before. He paid me a visit at work recently. He is damned assassin!” Sarah did not like curse words, but they were very handy for conveying anger and outrage when the occasion arose. 
“You know this guy?” Hayley began searching Agent Jones, paying extra attention to the rib cage causing him to recoil in pain when firmly frisked. Concealed in various locations on his body were four knives, two more pistols and a small shiny disc. He was cuffed and trussed before they turned their attention to Cracker who was sitting in slack-jawed astonishment at what he had just seen. 
“Thanks, Sarah” Cracker smiled, but there was no smile in return. Shook her head and turned to Hayley, expressionless.
“Do you know him, too?” Hayley was not amused. 
“No, I heard you say her name.” Cracker finally cottoned on.
There was a long long silence as Cracker and Sarah glanced at each other furtively, silently trying convey what they wanted the other to say, but of course this did not work, but with fortuitous timing Agent Jones ended their absurd pantomime.
“Let me go! You will regret fucking with me! I will bring the sky down upon you all. You will all die screaming if you don’t free me, now!” Agent Jones was already working on what method of death he would visit upon these inconsequential fools. Most certainly torture would be involved.
“Listen here, agent man, you will not be walking out of here alive unless you are truthful and forthcoming. I am a Superintendent of Police and I will fuck your life.” She stared him down with dead eyes and poker face.
“A lousy cop? You think you possess power, but you are nothing. You are a paper shuffler, a street sweeper. A public servant.” Being called a public servant was the ultimate insult.
“Sarah, do you have any problems with torture?” Hayley did not like being called a public servant, even though it was a fundamentally accurate description.
“Not under the present circumstances.” Sarah did not smile. She knew that this was crossing a well-marked delineation, but this guy was a murdering piece of crap and proud of it. “In fact, let me have a quick chat to him.” She dragged him across the room and began. It did not take long for Sarah to reduce the pompous buffoon into a sobbing, flaccid sack of misery.
“Agent Jones. Where is Emil Kalheri?” Sarah had the blubbering agent in the sights of the particle pistol. 
“He was transferred to our med-facilities for examination and then cremated.” He slobbered blood and saliva through a set of loose teeth.
“Why is the Department of Proactive Euthenasisa interested in an L.1 labourer?” Hayley’s interest was more than professional. She was certain she was staring at the maggot who killed Mrs Kalheri and her daughters in a cold blood.
“He was a blank.” Jones expected that would be sufficient to answer the question but his reply was met with a wall of impassive stares and decided to clarify in the hope of avoiding further bodily damage. “A blank is a person, usually L.1 or L.2 suffering a terminal illness. We use them for low level contract terminations. Their families are quite well compensated.”
“What of his family? The Kalheri’s?” The neutral stare she had cultivated over many years did not give her away. Hayley felt a seed of rage taking hold, deep inside her and it was quietly burning, heat building upon itself exponentially. The anger began to spread across her body, but she pressed a lid down and clamped upon it hard. Permitting herself to remove the lid would allow oxygen to rush in and her rage would bloom into an inferno. Not yet, not yet.
“They got their payment and they have moved to a nice apartment outside the Pale. They are very happy.” Agent Jones was always economical with the truth. “I checked in on them yesterday.”
“You lie!” Hayley stepped closer to the quivering lump. “You executed them all! I found their bodies.” She raised her pistol and rested it against his right temple. 
“Okay, Okay. Yes I did. He had breached the contract by losing his disc.” Pained staccato breaths and a racing heart made hearing almost impossible above the pounding in his ears. “My contract would be terminated if I did not follow scorched earth protocol.”
“The disc? Like this one?” Hayley picked up the innocuous piece of metal.
“Give me that. You don’t know what you are dealing with. They are dangerous in the wrong hands. I will be executed if I lose another one.” This was the first answer he had given that was entirely true.
“Looks harmless enough. What does it do?” Hayley examined the thin, innocuous piece of metal.
“It does many things. We currently used them to thwart electronic surveillance. I don’t know how it works, but I understand it uses gravity waves to create some sort of a field that, among other things, effects surveillance devices. You are invisible to video and audio surveillance. Two or more disks cannot come into close proximity, the two fields interact to create a gravity-well.” Agent Jones.
“A black hole?” Sarah did not hold back on her alarm.
“We were told there was a danger of creating a gravity-well or something, they said nothing about a black hole.”
“How close are we talking?” Sarah was now very glad she had asked Johnny to take the other disc elsewhere. 
“Within 25 millimetres, give or take. You can tell when there is another one is nearby. They vibrate in your jacket and get uncomfortably warm.” An image flashed into Hayley’s brain, the burn she had seen on Emil Kalheri’s leg. Perfectly circular and no bigger than a thumb nail.
“What are we going to do with you? I can’t arrest you, can I? Your department will have you released within days and then quietly dispose of you, won’t they? What do you think Sarah? I vote shoot him.” Hayley was the straightest cop in all of the Dominion, but even for her, popping off a dirty company assassin had some considerable appeal.
“Well, he doesn’t seem to be of any more use does he?” Sarah hoped she was joking, but she found herself not at all perturbed at the prospect of seeing this man die. 
“This is it Agent man.” Hayley pressed the pistol firmly into his forehead.
“Wait, wait! Please don’t. There’s something else. I have been put on security for a black operation. The new director, Damien Hardman is planning to dig up the old bio-weapons lab in the security zone. I don’t know what for yet, but….”
“But you will find out, won’t you?” Her pistol remained in vigilant contact with his slippery skin. “It looks like you are useful after-all.” She withdrew the pistol and wiped the frightened-agent-sweat from the barrel across the front of his shirt. The tiny seed of rage inside her was left to smolder quietly, awaiting the proper time for release.
“Is this man in further danger?” Sarah placed her hand gently on her cleaning buddy’s shoulder.
“No. No one else knew who I was targeting. I made a few link inquiries, but I can delete my log when I get back to the office.” 
“No, you can do it now.” Sarah picked up his palm device, buried under a pile of his weapons. “Password or code?”
“Password - Kill Kill Kill.” He and the boys at work thought it was pretty hard-core, but under his present circumstances he thought to revisit that decision.
“You colossal tool!” Cracker had remained remarkably quiet, however he found he was unable to remain silent. Kill Kill Kill, what a dick.
“And done. I have also linked you brain implant and your device to my server and I will know where you go and what you do. Everything.” Sarah smiled and dumped the device back on the table, this time on top of the pile. “If you get up to any shenanigans I will rattle your your implant and turn your brain to pudding.” 
“How did you do that?” Agent Jones was impressed. He had seen some pretty tricky tech-freaks before, but none that would match the blonde standing before him. 
“Listen up. I am going to let you go on the understanding that you now work for me.” Hayley laid down the law. “You will report to me, daily and you will do what I say. If you betray our confidence I will expose you for the fool that you are. You have lost two of these discs now and I am sure your controllers will not be forgive you.” 
“Yes, Yes. Of course.”
“This man is not to be harmed in any way.” Sarah confirmed Cracker’s immunity.
“He won’t be touched.”
“After we let you go, when you are no longer under my control, your vain-glorious male pride might compel you to think of revenge. Your chest might fill with hubris and you may want to break our deal. That would be Olympic standard foolishness. You are presently and quite rightly frightened of me. Retain that fear. It will save your life.” Hayley began to loosen his bindings. “I think I will keep the weapons.” I bet I’ll find a match for the Kalheri family killings. 
“I still vote to shoot the cunt.” Cracker said deadpan.
“Language!” Sarah shouted, feigning offence.
“I think you are right.” Hayley again pointed the gun the agent’s face. “You cannot be trusted. Close your eyes.” She stood for a few seconds of ear-splitting silence and loudly yelled, “Bang!” The agent crumpled to the floor and began to sob, any thoughts of retaliation purged from his broken spirit. He was now an agent turned.
“Now get the fuck out of my house!” Cracker yelled in his face and dragged him to his feet, before booting him in the backside as he stumbled in the general direction of the front door. “That was way cool, Detective..?”
“Detective Superintendent Hayley Lanning, PhD, L.5 and in case you don’t already know this is Sarah Jacobson, PhD, L.5.” 
“Nice to meet you both. Anthony Hardy, L.2.” Cracker did not look at Sarah. 
“I think you should be kept under protective custody for a while Mr Hardy.” Sarah was now very formal, hoping Mr Hardy would also keep up the pretence.
“No. I won’t allow that, but I am happy to keep in touch with your office. That’s if you can handle having my details appearing in your comms records.”
“I think that would be okay, Mr Hardy.” Sarah did not mind a bit. 
“Mr Hardy.” Hayley was also being quite formal. “Do you know anything about the disc that agent was looking for?”
“Nah. I have no idea what he was talking about.” He looked at Sarah, but her eyes glanced away.
“I have a feeling we will be seeing you again, Mr Hardy.” 
“I look forward to that.” He really did, just as long as she brought along Sarah he would be happy to catch up with her again. “ I hope it is very soon.”
</Text>
        </Document>
