        <Document ID="8A837F6A-7AA4-4A2C-A6D7-05564AB9B15A">
            <Title>Damien gets a Promotion</Title>
            <Synopsis>He has to justify himself before the director.
The CEO decides the director knows too much and kills him and promotes Damien Hardman to Director.
The death of the former director is also a giant trope (secretary kills him and then kills herself)  Another manner of death may work.
The death of the director later becomes important -(Knowing that he will fuck it up, Shakti is given the investigation.)</Synopsis>
            <Text>
Damien gets a Promotion
Day after Bombing - Year Zero
Damien had not even made it to the elevators when he heard the commotion. People were coming from every direction and heading for the Director’s Office in a mad panic. By the time he entered the office it was full of gawkers and office geeks, some of whom already had their hand-devices out and recording for a social media download. 
“All of you put the devices down down and get the fuck out of here!” A couple of them looked around, but kept filming. “This means you fuck-face. Give me that phone.” The reluctant office-dweeb failed to hand over the phone in a timely enough fashion and was thrown on his head by Damien in a serious attempt to break his neck. The phone dropped and was immediately crushed under foot. “Anyone else want to record this? Hand them over people.” At that 10 devices hit the deck and were all subject the same fate as the first. “Get the fuck out. This is a crime scene.”
Now alone he made a quick assessment of the scene. A was quite obviously a murder-suicide, but he could not fathom it. He had just been speaking to Janine not two minutes before and she seemed fine. She said she was looking forward to her son’s birthday, not more than a week away. Something was definitely wrong and decided he had better secure the room and let the cops work it out. This was way too big for an internal cover-up, the news would get out quickly.
At that moment a hologram appeared on the Director’s desk, “Director? What happened? What’s going on there?” The CEO inquired innocently.
“The director has been shot by one of the secretaries. They are both dead.” Damien said.
“Who is that?”
“Agent Damien Hardman, sir.”
“I was just speaking to the director when we were cut off.”
“It only just happened, sir. She seemed fine to me, but I can’t see how it could have happened any other way. She just walked in, shot him and then shot herself.”
“Is there any way of keeping this in house?” the CEO inquired, knowing full well that the news was already out.
“No. I don’t think so. Half the admin-staff were taking vids when I walked in.”
“Those recordings cannot get out.” The CEO insisted.
“It’s already sorted. Every device has been smashed, along with one of the camera men.” He smiled proudly. It was yet another example of the problem solving properties of personal violence. 
“Good work. In fact I have heard nothing but good reports about you, Agent Hardman. I am promoting you. Clear out your desk, you are now the Director.” 
 
“Sir, I am amazed you have heard about me at all. I’d be jumping five pay grades. Are you certain you have the right man?”
“Do you want the job or not, son? I don’t want to hear any of this false modesty nonsense. Take the job or risk disappointing me.” The CEO knew that no one dared disappoint him.
“Of course, sir. I can’t wait to see the looks of the Deputy Directors.” Damien was now grinning wildly. The enormity of what had just happened was sinking in. He was the Director, the fuckin Director! Oh, Yeah!…….Oh, dear.
“They’ll have their knives out.”
“I’ll just sack them.”
“You have the reigns. Do whatever you think is prudent to establish stability.” The CEO was jubilant. He thought Damien must have been reading his mind. He had been planning a bit of a purge for a while now and his new director would bring the timetable forward. The Deputy Directors, each of whom commanded an army of operatives, would not go without a fight and there was the potential for civil war to erupt within Pre-emptive Reciprocity and Pro-active Euthanasia Directorate. He figured Damien would only last a week and any knowledge he had of the bombing would die with him. Whoever was left standing after the dust settled would be too shell-shocked to pose much of a threat to him and he or she would be his puppet.
* * *</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="86D29188-2AD7-4D2E-89BF-E22A72945C01">
            <Title>Majority Report</Title>
            <Synopsis>This is just some backstory to set up the glass dome room at the top of Hardy Tower (No 1 I think).  (The entire building is used by Jameson Hardy and he has his "central control room hidden there)
Also sets up the entrance-way to the Control Room area.  It looks like a stone wall, but as Jameson Hardy approaches, a portion of the wall shimmers and and a door appears.  When he goes through the door shimmers and returns to solid stone.  I have not described the control centre - it is the hub of his world wide conglomeration of companies - he can see everything.  I don't really know what that would look like. 
The door opens for certain people and reads their DNA prior to opening.
The main reason for this is to show why he makes the decision to de-commission all of the underground research labs and cover them with a thick layer of concrete.</Synopsis>
            <Text>Majority Report
20 years Before The Dawkins 
	Jamison scurried out of the laboratory, headed straight to the security manager’s office. Hayden Miles, Level 3 Manager (Security) quite literally fell off his chair when the richest and most powerful man in the Dominion strolled, alone, into his office. Miles was a typical hardworking family man who loved his job. His proudest moment was being baptized “L3” by his delighted wife following his promotion to Level 3 Manger (Security). He loved introducing himself as L3, a meme that quickly took off among the management classes.
“Get up, Mr Miles, we have a bio-security breach.” Jamison announced. “Labs 3 through 10 must be quarantined immediately.”
“Call me L.3, sir. Full barrier closure?” Miles L.3 asked confidently.
“Yes, doors sealed shut, concrete poured.” CEO Hardy sounded grave and took a deep breath. “There can be no leak, we could lose everything.”
L.3. was, by this time, on his feet and already making a vid-call. “Yeah, John. Could you execute a “Secure and Smother” on Labs 3 through 10?”
“No problems.” Said the disembodied head. “We can seal the doors now and pour concrete when it arrives.”
L.3. Miles looked to the great Jamison Hardy. “All done. The steel blast doors are sealed and cannot be opened from either side. The concrete can be delivered within hours, but it would be better at night, if you want to keep it quiet.”
“No, get it done immediately, L.3. I’ll worry about damage control.” He knew a night delivery was a much better idea, but time was a factor and a CEO doesn’t take suggestions from a Security Manager, no matter how good the notion. He was, however, generous enough to offer some support, “Good work, Miles.”
“Thank you, Mr Hardy.” By the rites of tax avoidance! Jamison Hardy in my office. L.3. Miles was star-struck and was kicking himself he had not asked for a selfie. His wife would never believe Jamison Hardy had appeared unannounced in his office.
Returning to his headquarters, the great Jamison Hardy gathered together his best up and coming accountants, finance guys and economists in his private conference pavilion, the Blue Sky room, high on the top of Hardy Tower One. The soaring steel and glass erection was one of many such projects, scattered across the dominion, commissioned by Jamison to provide low-cost accommodation to the bulging lower classes. He did, however, reserve for himself this one tower, 200 levels of self-indulgence, decreeing a stately glass dome to adorn the summit. Forty-seven metres in breadth, the breathtaking hemisphere of curved glass, set with thick laminated lead crystal, was centred high above the conference hall and allowed strange diffuse light to safely enter, filtering out colossal quantities of radiation the planet no longer deflected. The nature of the highly filtered light, violet and indigo now cast aside, contrived with the curvature of the dome to focus, reflect and refract the remaining beams until they took on a pink and green diffuse MDMA hue, swirling and dancing to a haphazard, silent melody. 
The flickering aurora awed first time visitors into a daze of open-mouthed silence, eliciting cautious inspection of the cavernous glowing pavilion, each step in the dimness judiciously measured. Acclimatisation varied from person to person, but only ten minutes elapsed when Jamison’s panel of accountants and finance advisers were seated and ready to hear the great man speak. He sat silent for an age, carefully considering each man and woman seated before him. Satisfied they were sufficiently overwhelmed by his presence he swiped a holograph icon, triggering a burst of bright white light, killing every shadow and instantly painting the giant conference room stark alabaster.
The participants of this unusual gathering, all now furiously blinking and rubbing their eyes, were seated at two long, thin plasti-fibre tables placed parallel and facing off across 2 metres of gleaming hardwood floor. At one end of the configuration the tables curve desperately toward either side of Jamison’s separate oak wood desk, stopping close to the antique edge, daring not to touch divinity.
“Good afternoon ladies and gentlemen. Thank you all for coming on short notice.” Jamison was curt. The assembly of proto-bootlickers all replied, at once, with variations of “Of course, Mr Hardy” and deteriorated into a stomach churning cacophony of sucking-up. “Okay. Thanks for the support. I don’t know any of your names and before you start blurting out the noise your parents used to distinguish you from your over-indulged siblings, I don’t want to know. You have been brought here precisely because I don’t know you.” Jamison stood up and began to pace slowly and deliberately, commencing a circuit of the tables, contemplating something of gravity. “I want a report on the effect to the economy if a drug was created curing all diseases, sicknesses, ailments, illnesses……you get what I mean. A world without the need for doctors or medicine. I want to see some blue-sky solutioneering. I want a comprehensive report with conclusions for positive action. I want it in 7 hours.” 
The deadline was set purely by the length of time it took to knock up the special concrete mix required to safely smother a leak of weapons grade bio-agents. Jamison reasoned that he would probably need to pour, but he at least wanted to see if anyone could construct a viable argument supporting the notion that a world without disease was a good thing. 
“Well you seem to appreciate the gravity of the situation.” Mr Hardy ordered. “You are to remain in this room until the task is complete. No messages - in or out. You will have access to any data-base you think fit and order any refreshments you like, just as long as you complete the task. I will not be happy if the task is incomplete.” He left it at that, knowing no-one wanted an unhappy Jamison Hardy, the consequences were just too grave. Not one of the bright minds sitting before him was brave enough to break the silence. Each mutely contemplating the horror of a world without sick people to suck dry. Jamison sprightly walked towards a blank wall and as he approached a door appeared and creaked open, swallowed him up, before closing and melting back into the wall, leaving no sign.
“All of our careers ride on this ladies and gentlemen. Let’s get to it. I suggest we compile a report from the perspective of our particular fields and merge it into one report. Then throw it into cyber-editor to highlight any conflicting ideas and merge writing styles. Then make our recommendations. Any objections?” No-one replied to the self-nominated leader and they all got to it. 
* * *
One copy hard copy of the report was printed and delivered to Jamison Hardy by the hand of one of his most trusted friends, Germaine Lanning. All electronic records were deleted, the hardware irradiated and shredded. 
“Why all the cloak and dagger, big man?” Germaine was one of the few allowed the luxury of talking plain to the wealthiest man in Ulyssia.
“I can’t trust the link anymore. There are too many ways for it to be compromised. Snail mail is superior if you can trust the courier.” Jamison hoped he did not have to explain himself any further. He opened the envelope, noting the seal seemed intact and went straight to the conclusions and recommendations, he had no intention of reading all that financial double speak. 
“You can trust me, you know that, Jamison.” Germaine slid her hand onto his upper thigh, trying to drag his attention away from the document.
“What? Yes, of course I trust you.” 
“I’d pour the concrete if I were you.” She laughed, her hand now high enough on his inner thigh as to be regarded as groin. 
“Reading my mail?”
“Of course. Someone has to take care of you.” She kissed him lightly on the cheek.
 He studied the final page of the document. “Yes, it is just as I thought.” He kissed her in return, this time on the lips. “I need you to go back to the authors of this little gem and tell them I am very impressed and let them go home.” 
“Of course, Mr Hardy, but don’t think this little conversation is over.” They kissed again, long and passionately. She stood and quickly exited, high heels clicking on the hardwood surface.
Jamison waved through a holographic icon floating above the emitter on his desk. The icon quickly disappeared and was replaced with a male head wearing a security guard’s cap. “Germaine will be visiting the panel in the Blue-sky room. Please lock the door behind her and open the dome.”
“Boiling them inside out? Consider it done.” He opened the dome flooding the Blue Sky Room with radiation and transforming it into a giant microwave. “Haven’t had a cook-up in a while, sir.”
“Have them removed and disposed of. Replace the air and clear the room of radiation.” 
He terminated the call before the guard could reply and set to making another call. “Miles L.3.?”
“Yes sir. The concrete has arrived and we are ready for your instructions, Mr Hardy.”
“Pour it. Shut the labs down.” He knew, as the words were spoken, he was condemning scores of highly educated and hard-working scientists to suffocation. He was especially sad for his loyal employee and good friend Professor Herman. “Good-bye Professor.” 
He estimated hundreds of work-years of research would be lost forever. The amount of money he was tossing away did not bare thinking about, however, the report was unequivocal - the economy could not suffer the shock of a healthy population.Hundreds of thousands, perhaps millions would suddenly be out of a job. Treatment of chronically ill patients (the best kind) would dry up quickly and medicines would be worthless. Pharma-stocks would free-fall and the domino effect through other industries was, according to his little panel of experts, was nothing short of catastrophic. They only made one recommendation – 
“The miracle drug, if it exists or ever comes into existence, must be destroyed. All research must be destroyed and all related research staff must be eliminated.”
Jamison reflected that had just saved the entire world and no-one would ever know. The curse of the true hero.
* * *
A chubby, jovial looking chap knocked on the door noting the name, Miles L.3. Security Manager, proudly displayed for all to see. Chuckling quietly and grinning widely he opened the door silently and confidently walked in.
“Who are you? How did you get in here?” Miles, L.3 was far too busy following Mr Hardy’s instructions to be disturbed by the fat interloper. “I am on important business for Jamison Hardy.” To infer some sort of familiarity and to impress, he dropped the first name of the big-boss. 
“Sorry to give you a start, Mr Miles. I have a message from Mr Hardy. He is very impressed with the way you get things done.”
“What’s the message?”
“The message is - Sorry.” He pulled out his old Glock and sent three bullets in tight formation into the centre of the Security Manager’s chest and then one into his forehead. Without ceremony, he grabbed the body by one leg and dragged him out to a pile of dead security guards who were unlucky enough to be working that day. These boys and girls would remain on duty, forever vigilant, encased in concrete. 
* * *

</Text>
        </Document>
