	62.	^ Jump up to: a b Cowen, Ron (2015-01-30). "Gravitational waves discovery now officially dead". nature. doi:10.1038/nature.2015.16830. 
	63.	Jump up ^ Relativistic Binary Pulsar B1913+16: Thirty Years of Observations and Analysis
	64.	Jump up ^ "1974: the discovery of the first binary pulsar". arXiv:1411.3930 . doi:10.1088/0264-9381/32/12/124009. 
	65.	Jump up ^ Crashing Black Holes
	66.	Jump up ^ Binary and Millisecond Pulsars
	67.	Jump up ^ "Noise and Sensitivity". gwoptics: Gravitational wave E-book. University of Birmingham. Retrieved 10 December 2015. 
	68.	Jump up ^ Thorne, Kip S. (1995). "Gravitational Waves". Bibcode:1995pnac.conf..160T. arXiv:gr-qc/9506086 . 
	69.	Jump up ^ David G. Blair (Ed.) (1991). The detection of gravitational waves. Cambridge University Press. CS1 maint: Extra text: authors list (link)
	70.	Jump up ^ For a review of early experiments using Weber bars, see Levine, J. (April 2004). "Early Gravity-Wave Detection Experiments, 1960–1975". Physics in Perspective. 6 (1): 42–75. Bibcode:2004PhP.....6...42L. doi:10.1007/s00016-003-0179-6. 
