        <Document ID="F62B93DE-8649-4E01-B061-7CD8597B0BC2">
            <Title>Sarah checks on Cracker</Title>
            <Synopsis>A quick check of the security logs revealing Cracker had not yet exited the business district, trebling her concern. Once he was in “The Pale” he would be hard to follow, but he had to get there first.</Synopsis>
            <Text>Sarah locked her office door and sat so fast at her desk, you’d think it was a sports car. She raced to warn Mr Fairy Boxers before the dark man with a gun and ominous present-tense sentences, found him. She knew she should care nothing for a Cloudborne, especially a manual labourer, but there was no shaking the feeling she may have, in some indirect way, set the dark man on his trail. What is this feeling? Is it guiltiness? No, she decided. It’s worse. It’s embarrassment.
She stroked her holoscreen and smiled. Let others deride it, dismiss it, and never suspect its capabilities. She made it throw a dizzying forest of 3-dimensionally rendered business documents around her, then blinked a small console alive in the shade of them. From a drawer she produced a keyboard—yes, a keyboard!—and discreetly typed. Faster than the executive overviews around her could render an insomniac unconscious, she was inside office security, downloading surveillance logs and reconstituting half-overwritten image streams. Glee, however, dissolved to pursed-lipped disappointment when she found no trace of the dark man among the compromised data. There was no evidence of him in reception, no record of his awkward marching in the hallway, no account of his reptilian slithering in Suicide Dude’s insufficiently sterile office. No gun. No menace. Sarah ran the logs through image processors looking for editing artefacts. Nothing. The spaces where the dark man had been became infused, instead, with Sarah’s fear.
Scrambling security footage like this required rare sophistication. That the dark man had such support, marked him as a probable PAPER agent. Tax me into poverty! I sent this loon after Mr Fairy Boxers.

Sarah spent the rest of the morning gathering everything known about Anthony Hardy. Everything, however, amounted to very little. The spaces where data had once been were as obvious as toothache to her. Much was recently deleted, as recent as this morning. No matter how deep she dug, all she uncovered regarding Anthony (Tony, Cracker) Hardy was: a birth certificate (father unknown), patchy school records (noting his obstreperousness in the face of poor grades), a listing for Cracker Jack Cleaning (no business address), and some fines for drunk and disorderly. There were no consumer records. There was no evidence of any marketing advertising algorithms apprising him worthy of spamming. Sarah raised her eyebrow when she searched for malware attacks against him and found none.
Was Mr Fairy Boxers too dull to surveil? 
Sarah stared out the window with a blank thousand-metre stare. On another day, she might have shrugged off a visit from a creepy agent. She could handle those, but the cleaner couldn’t mend his own overalls. He’d be no match for Agent Jones. Sarah would have to warn him.
When 1:30pm approached, she took a hover-copt to the clinic where she gave samples, underwent embarrassing and probing examinations. A colony of scanners bombarded her with a broad spectrum of radiation. As expected, she was in perfect health, and therefore approved for promotion.
One result gave her cause for reflection.

PREGNANCY: negative

It was more than a decade since she resolved she would never—could never—fall pregnant.

Sarah went home and resumed searching for Mr Fairy Boxers—a home address, or even a regular watering hole. On an impulse borne of desperation, she gathered a cloak, tied her hair back, and called a hover-copt for The Ghetto, where she did the rounds of bars where vanilla Cloudborne mixed with the Elvi. A nagging hunch—or perhaps the only drive she could raise enthusiasm for—suggested Mr Fairy Boxers might enjoy karaoke.
Her dim hunch ignited into a solid lead at the Viva Vegas Club where a waiter named Elvine knew the names ‘Cracker Jack Cleaning’ and ‘Cracker Hardy’, and was familiar with the tear in his overalls.
“Go check out the Stick it Inn, sugar,” Elvine told Sarah. “That’s where his bodyguards usually hole him up.”

Sarah found Cracker at the Stick it Inn, flanked by three scruffs who looked like a pirate, a fence and a contract killer. Bodyguards indeed. Not long after, the bomb exploded.
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="6C7DD647-7F59-442E-AB84-1F16E0F56768">
            <Title>Cutting Room Floor</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="9BF7C57D-99E4-4193-97B1-9EED7FF55881">
            <Title>Changing Brooms</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="BE9C3B45-AE39-48A8-8AC9-32499F0EC135">
            <Title>Sacrificial Elvis</Title>
            <Text>
Sacrificial Elvis
Agent Jones, the only agent to survive the raid on the bio-weapons bunker, sat opposite Director Harding and listened. Covert agents were a suspicious lot and he figured his remarkable survival would be the subject of a great deal of scrutiny from his punctilious boss.
“How is it, Agent Jones, that you are the only one to survive? How is it, that you ended up 100 metres away in a pool of excrement?” Director Hardman had gone over it in his head, again and again, it did not make sense.
“I have no memory of what happened after I entered the bunker, but I can’t imagine being covered in shit was by choice. I woke up in a med-facility, with three nurses hosing me down.” The shit bit was true, but the medical staff at the Elvi Infirmary were professional and had cleansed him down with great care. “I was told where I was found, but I have no idea how I got there.” He crossed his fingers and prayed to the Dawkins that Hardman would swallow his lie. The Dawkins? Man, I have been hanging with the Elvi far too much.
“I find it difficult to believe electro-pulse grenades caused such a peculiar pattern of destruction. According to the reports, it undermined a path beneath several buildings and blew out the side of a deep excavation in a vacant lot. To top it all off we are being sued by the lease holder. He wants the cost of the clean-up to come from us! That will be coming out of your pay, Agent Jones.” 
“C’mon boss, it will take me years to pay it off.” Agent Jones was jubilant, if he was planning to dock his pay, he wasn’t planning to kill him. He was safe, at least for the immediate future. A cursory examination of the blast site, however, would quickly reveal, rather than the hole being blasted under the buildings, a tunnel had been dug. 
“The loss of the other agents was not unexpected and we must continue our mission.” With only one agent left at his disposal, Hardman could do nothing else but ignore his suspicions until he had proof of treachery. He was under instructions to kill him at the conclusion of the operation and he would use him until then. “We are in the hands of our scientists at the moment, but while they are cooking up their potions, we can snatch a few Elvi off the street.”
“Why don’t we just book a 5 or 6 piece Elvis show? I can arrange that if you want.” Agent Jones confidently suggested.
“That will save a whole heap of trouble. Go to it.” Director Hardman was amused that Agent Jones had finally come up with a good idea.
* * *
The Elvi war cabinet consisted of the High Elvis, his parliamentary cabinet plus the Blessed Mother, the Great Teacher, the Holy Aunts and Professor Hermann. The double agent, Agent Jones, reported to the war cabinet, when he was able, but was not permitted to opine on matters of policy. Meetings were scheduled for each morning at 6.00am and in the evenings at 6.00pm, when matters of urgency were debated and matters of strategy devised. The most urgent issue, of course, was the imminent threat from the PTD to the existence of the Elvi.
“Why don’t we just infect all of the Elvi with the T-virus? They can throw what they like at us and it will not matter.” The Minister for Health saw the issue, quite simplistically, as being a medical issue that would be resolved by inoculation.
“That would not reduce the threat, just alter it. If they think we have immunity to their viral agent, then they will just change strategy. In any case there is not enough of the virus at hand to inoculate all 100,000 of us. We have to make them commit to the plan they have. Do we know when they are planning the abduction?” The High Elvis was trying to think three moves ahead of the company agents he was up against. 
“How are we going to keep an eye on all of the Elvi?” The Great Teacher could see, from the blank expressions before him, that the cupboard was bare on the other side of the table too. 
“Agent Jones made contact, High Elvis.” Holy Aunt Hayley, police duties making her late as usual, drifted into the committee room holding her notepad and read the message. “He says, ‘Director Hardman will require a 6 piece Elvis band to play a gig at the Gravity Well Lounge, Penthouse Level Hardy Tower One. Thursday 25 June at 7.00pm.’ Agent Jones has, in effect, asked us to send in some sacrificial lambs. Now we decide who we offer up.”
“We now have a crack team of operatives that could intercede.” The Minister for Graceland Security proudly announced. 
“Director Hardman has arranged the gig in The Gravity Well for a reason. It’s deep in L.5. territory and the security is too tight. I guarantee they will be there in numbers and geared up for a slaughter. We will never be able to sneak in enough back-up to take them all. I do not think we have to intercede, in fact I suggest that we do nothing at all.” As she spoke the faces of the cabinet became ashen, but as she continued the cabinet became stolid and unified with resolve. “They must have their lab rats to experiment on. I would therefore suggest we ready ourselves to innoculate with the T-virus, but only do so after we have our volunteers.”
“Only you could think of such a plan, Detective.” The Great Teacher added, memories of the Haloren Smythe arrest jumped to his mind.
“Dispassionate would be an understatement.” The High Elvis finally said after several minutes of silence. “You and Holy Aunt Kathy should be innoculated with the T-virus immediately.”
“I already have the virus in my system, High Elvis.” Holy Aunt Kathy knew the morning after The Great Teacher’s revival that something was very different. She had woken up energetic and clear headed, something she had not experienced since childhood. She had to stick three patches to her arm to make the strange feelings go away.
“My team and I will have to get to work. Is there a suitable space available?” Professor Hermann was keen to start manufacturing huge quantities of virus from the samples he had managed to grab on the way out of the bunker. 
“You and I have to be at the gig.” Cracker whispered to Sarah. “I want to show you something. I can use my contacts to gain access, but you will have to arrange a booking for that night.”
“A booking at the Gravity Well? That’s near impossible at such short notice, even for me!” Sarah did not like sucking up to lowly receptionists at fashionable restaurants and she especially disliked feeling grateful if she managed to get a table on the night of her choice. 
“Yeah, well you are Eloise Shakti’s daughter-in-contract. Get her on the case.” Cracker was not taking no for an answer. “Tell her you want to take Alex for a romantic dinner.”
“A romantic dinner with Alex?” She also did not like the idea of being seen in public with the husband. “Eloise would never believe it. I will have to come up with something better that that.” 
  * * *
Three male and three female Elvi, dressed in their finest on-stage outfits of shiny gold suits with black lapels and matching reflective flares, were ready for their mission. Their glistening pitch black hair was combed directly upwards about 10 centimetres from the forehead into a bulbous, protuberant coiffure and slicked down over the crown and either side of their heads. The males had wide, bushy, but neat, sideburns that spread into black mutton-chops before tapering off to a point, just shy of the chin. They stood at attention before the war cabinet as the High Elvis stepped forward, ceremonially hugging and kissing each on both cheeks.
“Each have you have volunteered for this mission and you are all aware of the dangers. If any of you want to reconsider, now is the time.” The High Elvis waited for a few moments and was about to continued when he saw one of the volunteers lift his right foot to step forward.
“Stand fast!” The band leader, indistinguishable from the others, yelled loudly. The wavering foot of the volunteer was re-planted firmly back on the ground, heel wedged against heel. Proud to see his colleague back in line he called out, “Liberia Elvi superbia!”
“Libera Elvi superbia!” Replied the line with musical intonation and precision. “Libera Elvi superbia! Libera Elvi superbia!.”
“Do you have the sample of the virus?” The High Elvis asked of the band leader.
“Yes, your highness.” Band leader Elvis replied reverently. “Once we are inside their lab complex, we will release it.”
“The location you release will be critical. Only you can make the decision, Elvis.” Holy Aunt Hayley was very concerned the virus would be released too early and before it can deliver the results desired. “Ensure that at least one of their scientists is present, all of them preferably.”
“Yes, Holy Aunt.” His chest puffed out. “We will do our duty or die trying.” They about faced and marched out of the committee room and were off to their gig. 
“Did you even tell them?” The Great Teacher, like everyone else in the room was having second thoughts. “That it is a suicide mission?”
“No, they will fight better if they don’t know.” Holy Aunt Hayley countered. 
“For fuck’s sake. You could have got the same results if you sent me!” 
* * *
The band, simply called ‘Elvis’, arrived early knowing the security checks required to get to the top floor of Hardy Tower 1 would take an age. Suspiciously, but not surprisingly, they were ushered through security and were up on the top floor in no time. The Gravity Well Lounge, a pick-up joint for the ultra rich and desperate, was located directly adjacent to the restaurant of the same name. All ready to fight to the death, they didn’t make it three paces off the elevators before all were silently taken out by darts and surreptitiously spirited away. 
 A well groomed, bi-spectacled waiter, head adorned with a brand new and very expensive wig, caught the well practiced moves of the agents in the corner of his eye, quickly re-entered Gravity Well, heading for the tables at the rear. In his haste he bumped a table, causing a large glass of Shiraz to teeter and threaten to spill the contents, but it was snatched out of the air by the diner who, without pause, gracefully brought glass to her lips and took a sip.
“A lovely vintage.” Sarah said.
“They have already been taken, without so much as a shot fired.” Cracker blurted out, not caring that Shakti was on the other side of the small table and could hear everything. “The wheels are already in motion.”
“Can I help you?” Shakti inquired. “I think you have the wrong table.” 
“It’s me Alex.” Cracker took off his glasses, but his was insufficient for Shakti to make the connection, requiring him to take off his new wig. 
“Cracker, is that really you?” Shakti always had to ask to stupid questions. “Oh, that’s right you are alive again and everything forgiven.”
“Is there are problem here?” The head waiter had seen the fuss and wanted to minimise the discomfort to other diners. 
“No. There is no problem, I told the the waiter I loved the wine, but my husband…” She coughed daintily. “… disagreed with me and I sought a third opinion. He was about to give that opinion when you interrupted. I would like to hear it, if you do not mind or would you rather I call over the maitre ’d.”
“Of course not, madam.” Peering over the top of his glasses he gazed upon Cracker with suspicion. “I will leave you to it.”
“Alex, do you remember when I said I was going to ask you for a favour? A favour that you had to grant no questions asked?”
“Yes.” 
“I need to borrow Sarah for the night. No shenanigans I promise, I just needs her to look at something.” He was sure that sounded better in his head. 
“Can’t we finish dinner first?” Shakti was having a very pleasant evening and he was looking forward to the crab. “It takes the lab years to grow my main course and I do not want it to go to waste.”
“That isn’t exactly no questions asked, but if you insist. As soon as you are finished.” He looked over his shoulder and saw the head-waiter, standing at the bar, arms crossed with one foot ferociously tapping the floor. “I think I have outstayed my welcome here.” Cracker turned and walked toward the bar leaving the Shakti’s to their homogenized, bio-tech crab-like-things.
“What the fuck do you think you are doing, Cracker?” The head waiter brought Cracker in close so he could yell in his ear quietly. “This is Gravity Well. This is the premier restaurant in the world. This is the last time. The favour paid and we are even.”
“Okay, tonight is the last time.” Cracker thought he was telling the truth.
* * *
For the next hour Cracker made sure he kept his head down and kept out of sight behind the set of giant drapes adorning the eastern internal wall. The head waiter was on the war path and would throw him out at any further disruption to the diners. When he saw the last of Shakti’s desert disappear he waved Sarah over. She gave Shakti a kiss on the cheek and she headed to the drapes and disappeared behind them.
“This is a bit strange, Cracker.” Sarah swept a few crumbs from the front of her shirt with the back of her fingers as she looked upon at the non-descript stone wall. “There does not appear to be anything to see here.”
Cracker raised his eyebrows, “Is that right?”. He then sidled along the wall for several metres where the stone began to shimmer, a door appeared and opened inward and Cracker slipped inside. Sarah followed quickly and as the door silently closed behind her, Cracker lit up the room to reveal the long dormant operations centre. 
“I found it on your allocation night. I showed Kathy, but there was not enough time to work out what the hell this is.”
“Well, the hardware in here is about 20 years out of fashion, but would have been state of the art when it was manufactured.” She looked over the bevy of clunky buttons and knobs and after some consideration, powered the ancient computers up, which in turn switch on a miscellaneous array of old 2-D monitors, surveillance systems, environmental controls and strange humming machines. “The processing power of this system is really quite good. It is comparable to most small systems we have in place today and…my goodness, free lunch for everyone! The specifications say this was manufactured twenty three years ago and has a quantum processor. I would have thought that impossible. The idea of quantum processors has been around for a long time, but we are yet to perfect the technology.”
“What’s this thing?” Cracker opened a the door on the big white machine, an interior light revealed a white space with four empty glass shelves and empty storage compartments on the inside of the door. “It stinks a bit.”
“That is a refrigerator. They were used before stasis cupboards.” Sarah was taken back to her childhood and the many times she was scolded by her mother, ‘Shut the door, you are letting the cold out!’ It course it was the heat getting in not the other way around, she had told her mother a thousand times. “Are we able to stay long? This is going to take a while. I should be able to access most of the systems, given enough time.”
- - -
The invention of the stasis cupboard was one of the greatest leaps forward in food hygiene. The technology behind the storage bins was a highly protected secret that the PTD would not allow into the public domain. Industrial spies had been regularly executed to ensure the secret remained safe. No one person possessed all of the knowledge and stasis cupboard technicians were the highest paid maintenance personnel in the world. Joining the ranks of the Stasis Technicians Association was by invitation only and akin to joining the Illuminati. An invitation to become a Stasis Technician was one of the highest honours and no-one ever refused. Theirs was a secretive society and once joined you could never leave.
Precisely why you had to be superman to repair a fridge, was a conundrum that no-one outside the association could answer. It was also a question that no-one inside the association would answer. These people were paid obscene amounts of money to perform, what appeared to the uninitiated observer, menial tasks. While it was reasonably clear what the stasis cupboards, preserve food, it was infinitely more difficult to divine how that was achieved. Perishable items placed inside the cupboard would not deteriorate in any way, no matter how long they were in there. It was as if, on the inside of the cupboards, time had stopped.
- - -
“Kathy thought this place had not been used in years, so I think we are here alone, but I’ll take a look around if you like.” 
Cracker commenced his reconnoiter, wandering off along a brightly lit corridor to nowhere. Just a dead end and a blank wall, however, as he hoped, the stonewall shimmered and another glistening metallic door appeared and opened into a palatial and luxuriously appointed apartment. The floor gleamed throughout with white marble, complementing ostentatious fittings and furnishings of brocade silk and gilded trim. The opulent decor, heavily layered detail upon decadent design, for no other reason than an ostentatious display of wealth. Massive tapestries and full length portraits hung from the walls and from the ceiling of each room immense crystal chandeliers. His astonishment grew as he wandered from room to room, mouth agape. He counted no less that six bedrooms and seven bathrooms, a sauna, steam room, lap pool and an arboretum bulging with overgrown trees and bushes, some pressing upon the the glass walls and ceiling. 
Past the arboretum lay a fully appointed medical facility, filled with stainless steel apparatus of varying kinds, but upon the examination table was a body of a male, dessicated and flaking after decades in the dry, environmentally controlled air. The hair atop the man’s head had fallen onto the floor, in a pile of brittle brown strands and the examination table was stained with bodily juices that had oozed out in rivulets and then, over time, dried up into brown organic refuse. Cracker had seen plenty of bodies before and was more fascinated than disgusted by the mummified remains lying there before him. He flicked a few switches on the nearby machines, but he could not get them fire-up and decided this was a job for Sarah Shakti, pointy-headed computer-geek.
Sarah was seated in front of an antiquated QWERTY keyboard, working furiously on a peculiar program she had stumbled across. From what she could gather, this application had been silently operating for decades, while the rest of the server remained all but dormant. No matter how she attacked the problem, she could not get around the security system that demanded DNA identification. Having hit a dead end, she decided to find Cracker, who had been gone an eternity. She she followed the only corridor to a blank stone wall, but could go not further. 
“Cracker!” She yelled out. “Cracker? Hello? Where are you?” The stone facade in front of her shimmered and a door opened, allowing Cracker to re-enter the corridor. ‘And I didn’t have to say open-sesame!”
“You should see what’s in there. It must be the most expensive apartment in all of the Primary Trade Dominion.” Cracker was wide eyed and excited. “Even you will be impressed, I think.”
“Just a quick look.” She never found inspecting real estate all that compelling, having bought most of her properties sight-unseen, but thought a few minutes away from the cyber-puzzle would not be too much of a distraction. “Anyway, I’ve hit a bit of an impasse with the security system, but given enough time, I can work it out.” The entry door shimmered into existence, Cracker stood to the side, gentlemanly granting Sarah first access. Sarah had grown used to level 5 splendor, but even she had never seen sickening luxuriance and edifying magnificence blended so perfectly. “Well, I must say I have never seen a property where the relation between form and function blurs under the weight of layer upon layer of detail. This is not a place not of pragmatism, but beauty purely for the sake of beauty.”
“Huh?”
“I like it.” She decided the overstated nature of the decor required an understated reaction.
‘Okay. Come through here to the med-lab.” Cracker pointed beyond the arboretum and the lap pool to a glass doorway beyond. “I couldn’t get the machines on line.”
“You probably should have flicked the power switch.” Sarah promptly caused the machines to started beeping and buzzing. “That’s a surprise. Readouts say this is the body of Jameson Hardy and he has been dead for 20 years. Well, I guess we have solved that mystery. According to the records he died of a viral infection around the same time as the great viral cleansing.”
“Money could not save him?” 
“No, it never does.” Looking upon the dried husk, she took a moment and then smiled. Her mind was now ablaze, complex algorithms flashing through neurons, subconsciously assessing possible solutions and rejecting all but one. She picked up a skeletal arm and scraped away a chunk of dessicated skin and dusted into a specimen jar. “Let’s go.”
“Why do you need the dandruff?” Cracker had encountered many gristly scenes cleaning up after homicides, but even he found himself a little disgusted. 
“I have located a system that requires DNA identification to pass security. This should suffice.” She quickly shuffled out of the apartment and returned to the control room with Cracker having to play catch up. 
Sarah sat before the array of monitors and dumped the skin dust on the security scanner. The central monitor, sitting quietly before Sarah immediately announced the system was unlocked and she had access to everything. “Double my tax cheque! This is not to be believed.” She said as she continued to scroll through record after record, jumping from vast spreadsheets to bank statements, her fears confirmed with every entry she scanned. She was unable to sit still, becoming more and more animated by the surprising data appearing before her eyes, now close to popping out of their sockets.
“Well, out with it.” Cracker was staring at the screen, but did not understand the significance of the data. “What are you so excited about?”
“This room is the control centre for the entire empire of Jameson Hardy. Everything he did and everything he had planned is recorded in here. The system we just opened is the portal to his financial records and institutions. A few of the foreign interests appear to be hyper-portable and multi-jurisdictional, regularly changing locations of legal and quasi-legal entities.”
“In English?”
“Jameson Hardy’s foreign banking institutions, manage hidden wealth with indifference or outright defiance of the interests and laws of multiple nation states. He owned private banks for ultra rich clients specialising in tax evasion.”
“A tax haven?”
 “He had majority interests in three banks, but he had also set up his own investment concerns in various places around the world, but all of those institutions were controlled from here, by this system. I have only taken a small sample, but it appears every wealthy family and most corporations in the Primary Trade Dominion hid trillions in those tax havens. Most of the money was deposited about two decades ago and none of it has ever been withdrawn.”
“Why not?” Cracker wanted to withdraw some cash there and then. 
“My guess is Jameson Hardy was the only person with the access. When he disappeared, all access to that money was cut off. They still get vast dividends from their investment, but they can’t touch the principal investment. She pondered the horror of losing access so much money. “That must have sent shock waves through the executive classes, many heads would have rolled.”
“Can we access the money?” Cracker thought the direct approach was best.
“I am not certain, but what do you have in mind?” She knew she could access the money, but did not want to let Cracker dream, not yet anyway.
“I would like to give a fat cheque to all of the poor.”
“No, that is admirable, but also dangerous. The economy would react quickly to such vast amounts of money being dumped into the market at once.” She saw Cracker didn’t seem convinced. “If you made everyone a millionaire one week, the price of bread would be 1000 a loaf the following week.”
“And all of the money would end up back where it began?”
“Exactly. The structures in place would ensure the money would be soaked back up by the rich. It would, in fact, make the wealthy, wealthier.”
“Not the result I was looking for.” Cracker scratched his head. This economics stuff is too hard. “I will have to give it a bit of thought, but can I make a bit of a withdrawal?”
“Not, now. I will have to get familiar with the system, first.” She got back to work, fingers a blur as she tapped at the clunky keyboard. 
“Why do the doors open for me?” 
“To my amazement, you must actually be related to Jameson Hardy. I think the doors are set to allow through relatives and maybe a few honoured colleagues. Your genetics must be sufficiently similar to qualify you as a relative.” She did not look up from her work as she continued. “I doubt if you could access the finance data-base, but perhaps we can change that. He must have had some sort of succession plan in place.” Sarah continued for hours.
The passage of time, relative to the observer, was largely dependent upon the activity the time was wasted on. Cracker sat on the floor for hours before finally being drawn back to the sumptuous apartment. For Sarah, however, it only seemed like a couple of minutes before she once again raised her head and saw a gilded edged plate sitting before her containing most perfect examples of fresh fruit she had seen since since her childhood. She reached out to touch the brightly coloured temptations, but baulked, momentarily thinking it was a still-life hologram.
“I found them hanging from the trees in there.” Proudly announced Cracker, hunter-gatherer. “Can you eat them?”
“Are you kidding? Look at this pear.” She brought the light green tear-drop to her mouth and bit into the white juicy flesh with vigor. “Mmm, that is so good. Try that, it’s an orange” She said as pear nectar ran to her elbows, but she did not care and dove back in, audibly slurping. “Peel it, first.”
“I know.” Cracker tore the sphere in two, releasing sweet juice onto the floor. He had seen oranges on the link and had keenly gulped down orange-like fruit drink (with faux-pulp added) his entire youth. He had a good idea of what to expect, but gingerly took a bite and instantly his mouth was filled with a sweet pure taste explosion of natural fruit, but it was also acidic, wonderfully so! His delight and surprise saw Sarah laugh, gratified she had introduced him to such a simple pleasure. “I have no words. What’s that?”
“A guava, maybe.” She thought that pears and guavas should not grow in the same environment, but maybe they were special varieties. “Watch for little seeds.”
“That is even better.” He then began to taking one bite from each offering. “All so good!”
“Was that your first time?”
“I will alway remember this moment, when I lost my virginity to a banana.” He inserted the pointy end of a banana in his mouth and bit down hard. “Mmm. Love that creamy texture.” They both had a bit of a giggle, but cut it short, unable to help themselves finishing off the plate. “How much money are we talking about?”
“At least trillions, maybe tens of trillions. The system has been operating for 20 years and has been trading the whole time. From what I have seen it is programmed to spread millions of low risk, modest investments, over many markets and industries. Everything low risk. Eeverything short term. The return on each investment is small and generally assured, but the accumulation of these investments over 20 years is stratospheric.” She could see that Cracker wanted a number. “Let’s put it this way. If you are entitled to Jameson Hardy’s investments and offshore accounts, you are the richest person that has ever lived.”
“Cool. Who should I kill first?” Cracker pulled his best poker face but, when Sarah burst out, he could not help but let out in a hearty guffaw. 
“There is much for thought, Emperor.” Sarah bowed. 
* * *
Over the next several weeks Cracker and Sarah managed to gained access to the hidden control room irregularly, but finally located a hidden access point at ground level leading to a private elevator. Twenty four hour access soon morphed into Cracker moving into the apartment full time. The L.5. Apartment of Sarah’s just did not make the grade anymore.
He and Sarah quickly formed a team of appropriately trained Elvi who could conduct some initial grunt-work research on the computer system and vast data-base.
 * *
The kidnapped Elvi had been transferred to a brand new clandestine laboratory well outside the city limits. There were no roads to this facility and only a chosen few agency pilots were aware of it’s existence. Still unconscious they had been transferred to the testing chambers, all in a row, with no fuss. Each were strapped into an interrogation seat in the centre of a sealed chamber where they were to be subjected to multiple indignities and exposed to bio-agents of fiendish design. 
Agent Jones was the most experienced at enhanced interrogations and was the obvious choice to lead the interviews. Before they gave these Elvi their latest version of Ebola, the Director wanted to be certain that the Elvi were unaware of their plans. The chambers were massive cubes of ballistic rated poly-glass and the interrogations had attracted a great deal of morbid interest from those who had never had the chance to see a talented interrogator in action. Agent Jones unsealed the door of the first cube and entered, a small chefs roll in his right hand.
“I know you!” Drummer Elvis called out. 
“You will know me even better before the end of the day.” Agent Jones smiled and looked around at his audience all filled with good humour and blood lust. He knew he had to be merciless with this Elvis and the other Elvi prisoners. He leaned in and whispered in the drummer’s right ear. “I am with you Elvis, I am here to ensure you play out your role. We have an audience, so I will have to make this look believable. You will be tortured and you will die, but I assure you the mission will be carried out. Do you understand?” 
“I understand, thank you.” The drummer said quietly. “The sample is in a small patch behind my ear.”
“Thank you for delivering it to me. We have been in lock down for weeks. I am going to bite your ear off now.” Agent Jones sank his teeth into the gristly flesh of the ear and tore at it until a sizable lump ripped free, along with a tiny plastic patch. The observers outside the cube erupted into applause as the Agent spat the ear into the air with theatrical flair. Initially, the crowd of scientists and sundry agents didn’t know why he was whispering in Elvis’ ear, but they had got their answer. The Elvis screamed, but he and the rest of his troupe knew they were on a one way mission and their fate mattered not.
Agent Jones had at each Elvi with a clinical ferocity, maximising pain, but never endangering their lives. Every one of them, man and woman alike, kept to their story, that they were merely entertainers out to do a gig at the Gravity Well lounge. They all assured Agent Jones he had the wrong Elvis. They were pummeled into wads of bruised and bleeding flesh, but they never broke and Agent Jones admired them immensely. 
“They know nothing.” Agent Jones offered up to the Director. “This lot are just singers and dancers.”
“Good. We will commence testing in a few days, after they have rested up.” Director Hardman was pleased. “I want them to die healthy.” The operation seemed to being chugging along nicely and any suspicions about Agent Jones had evaporated when he spat out that first piece of Elvis ear. 
“How is the security on our samples?” Agent Jones hoped he wasn’t too obvious.
“I think we are all secure, but go and check if you wish.” His suspicions about Agent Jones again leaped back into life. He hooked into the security feed and followed Agent Jones, by camera, down each corridor and through every check-point, until he had entered the storage facility. He saw him chat with the scientists, check a few things and then leave. He did not see him drop the tiny flat vial, down the inside of his trouser and onto the floor of main storage, nor did he notice him crush it underfoot. At the end of his shift, Agent Jones sleazed out of the facility, hitched a ride on an outgoing flight, hoping never to return.
The following morning Director Hardman was in his office, waiting for the arrival of Agent Jones when the security alarm from the bio-lab began oscillating loudly. The holo-phone screamed, demanding some attention of it’s own and the disembodied head of the senior bio-engineer appeared above the emitter. “What’s up Dr?”
“We have lost most of our samples.” He let the terrible news settle before carrying on. “Every sample in main storage has died. I don’t know how, but the entire lab has a pleasant lemony fresh scent.”
“Sounds like sabotage to me.” The Director was certain. Where the hell is Jones.
* * *
“High Elvis, I have not been able to culture sufficient T-virus to infect the entire Elvi.” Professor Hermann announced to the war-cabinet. “Um,… the only solution I can see is we inoculate as many Elvis’ as we can and then let them, um, you know, inoculate each other.”
“You mean fuck themselves stupid?” The Great Teacher had to get in first. “Now, that’s what I call science!” He didn’t bother to raise his right palm for a high-five, he was sick of the rejection. Don’t any of these idiots have a sense of humour?
“Not the way I would put it, but essentially, yes.” The professor smiled, but was not impressed with Cracker’s attempt at wit.
“Well, Professor start the inoculations and let the infections begin!” The High Elvis was suddenly in good spirits.
“I would suggest we organise this a little better. Is there anywhere we can assemble all of the Elvi?” Professor Hermann wanted to apply a little scientific method to this fuck-fest. “Firstly we should have an even spread of male and female Elvi. They must all be prepared to infect multiple Elvi, not necessarily all at once, but that would certainly speed up the process somewhat.”
“We shall meet at the Kleoboulos Memorial Stadium in two days. I don’t think it will be too hard to arrange volunteers.” The High Elvis could not wait.
Two days later the entire Elvi nation assembled in the same stadium Kleo, the Great Sage, had been cremated only a few months prior. The High Elvis called them to order and told of the threat to their nation which filled the crowd with fear and disquiet until he told them of the solution - The Humpening. The cheers took an age to die down. The volunteers, including the entire cabinet, lined up and were, male and female in turn, infected with the T-virus. After an hour or so each of them were contagious and ready to infect. They lined up across the southern end of the old sporting field and removed their clothing to reveal Elvi bodies in various stages of maturity and decay.
“Now, not everyone can be inoculated at once. Wait your turn and no line jumping.” The High Elvis wanted an orderly orgy, at least until he was finished. “Let the humpening begin!” A deafening cheer was let out by the crowd and they began.
The Great Teacher was there mainly out of interest and to watch the carnage unfold from the high in the stands. He thought he might lend a hand if necessary, but after a quiet start, a frenzy of fear, lust and urgency took over. Many Elvi, male and female alike, were burdened with multiple partners attacking from different angles, all at once. The Elvine’s and the Elvis’s were troopers, taking far more than just one for the team. Male Elvi searched the giant white stack of writhing cloned flesh for any orifice that might reasonably accept penetration. Quite a few ears, eyes, nose and throat specialists emerged from the pile that day.
Disturbed beyond words, the Great Teacher quietly slid out of the stadium, ensuring his backside was firmly wedged up against the wall as he slowly made his way home, regretting dreadfully that he could never erase those memories. 
 The humpening lasted for several days of shocking debauchery, before it was certain all were infected with the T-virus and plumb tuckered out. Some suggested they make it a yearly event, but they were probably the lucky few who hadn’t been arse-fucked for three days solid.
* * *
The water treatment and filtration facility for management and executive level professionals was a completely separate system to the rest of the primary trade dominion. The water was filtered, sterilised, oxygenated and piped to every executive and management level home and business to ensure the elite had the cleanest drinking water in the world. The facility was high security, but a company of Elvis operatives penetrated security and dropped the ‘Anti T-virus innoculant’ into the water and left before anyone even noticed.
The result was profound, but again unnoticed. The executive and management elite, level 5 and above, were immune to the Hybrid T-Virus.
* * * 
“How did this happen?” Director Hardman screamed at Professor ???
“We have been the victim of a biological attack, but of a most benign kind.” Prof ???
“Benign?” The Director was incredulous. “The operation is in ruins.”
“Sir, I have a sample of the organism used. It only attacks human pathogens, but in the external environment. It does not tolerate heat above 35 degrees and cannot survive inside the human body. It was released on the floor of main storage and then it spread out across the floor and transferred by foot traffic everywhere, including inside the cool room and the entire inventory stored there was destroyed. They wanted to kill our pathogens, Mr Director, not us.”
“Benign, huh? Anything else?”
“Not all of our inventory was in the cool room. Dr Stroman was working on a promising variety of a synthetic bio-agent in his lab last night. His lab has been quarantined until we clear the facility of the contaminant.”
“How much of a setback is it then?”
“All not lost. It may take a month to get back to speed, but Stroman thinks he is close to a breakthrough.”
“I want testing to commence on the Elvi within two weeks.”
* * * 
“Agent Jones, the Elvi nation owes you a debt of gratitude. You have served us well and will hereby be known as the Great Protector.” The Parliament erupted in applause, stomping their feet so hard the plaster ceiling began to crumble. The High Elvis raised his right hand and the cacophony ceased. 
“This Parliament is in recess.” The speaker of the house banged his gavel and the representatives shuffled out.
“Great Protector, I expect to see you at the War Cabinet meeting this evening.” The High Elvis said over his shoulder as he left the chamber.
“Yes, sir.” Agent Jones did not feel like much of a protector, certainly not a great one.
</Text>
