            <Title>Manuscript Format</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="11451FC7-C8DF-4737-ADE7-E3EB486EA733">
            <Title>Death A la carte</Title>
            <Synopsis>Intro to character Damien Hardman - an executive within the "Proactive Euthenasia and Prementive Recipricity" Directorate.  He has just been promoted, and has to answer for the night club bombinb.  The Directorate had ordered the bombing, but it had somehow gone wrong with the water being diverted. 

I like the paragraph starting with "Avi had not reported in..."
He finds the body of AVI and an unknown woman (Cracker's Ex) and gets rid of them. 

</Synopsis>
            <Text> 
Death A la carte
Day after bombing (Morning)
The foyer of the Pre-emptive Reciprocity and Pro-active Euthanasia Division of Termocorp was quiet and dignified as usual. An ever-changing hologram of the CEO stood vigil over company crest etched into the floor of the entrance hall, while dark suits and sensibly cut skirts scurried about busily. The only outward acknowledgment of each other’s existence was the nifty footwork employed to evade collisions, thereby avoiding arguments and violent recriminations. 
Damien Hardman had moved through middle management briskly and his present job description elevated him to a prestigious position, but his current office displeased him immensely. Promotion meant an office on the upper floors and he had hoped to leapfrog the layer, but instead it had landed him smack bang in the centre of it. He had no view, the white haze of the layer rendering his window nothing more than a necrotic mirror that slowly turned green as the fetid, septic air tarnished the thick glass. Even though he knew turning out the lights and squashing his nose against the pane made little difference, adding only a few centimeters of visibility, he tried it again anyway. No matter how many times he did it, the view did not change. He tramped back to his desk and illuminated the room. 
Avi had not reported in, but Damien wasn’t too concerned. He knew her little quirk of getting laid after finishing a job and he figured she was still hosing herself down after an all-nighter. He thought it was a little ghoulish to fuck so soon after killing someone, but opined that it was something to do with the interconnectedness of life and death – the circle of life bullshit. He wondered if death made women want to fuck, it would certainly explain why he never went home alone from a funeral, but he rejected the notion that killing aroused Avi. Her psych profile stated precisely the opposite was true, killing neither excited her nor caused her anxiety; she was emotion-neutral. She was probably just molested as a child, he concluded. 
The device she planted had gone off without a hitch but, he could not work out why the water had been diverted. It seemed very likely the events were connected – an explosion and fire immediately followed by an attack on the water supply. How had Avi managed both? The CEO had apparently consumed half a litre of recyc and was screaming blue murder; luckily, some bonehead had drowned himself in public baths and been erroneously labelled the terrorist. This was quite a stroke of luck in an otherwise sticky state of affairs. Scapegoat was his favourite meat and this fat bloater had been laid squarely at his feet. He would still have a few curly questions to answer, so he needed to act quickly. 
He touched an icon and the plain face of his secretary popped up on his holo-screen. “Have you managed to rouse Avi yet?” 
“No answer, sir. I’ve tried her communicator, hard line, link line and I’ve rattled her implant so many times her frontal lobes should be jelly. She doesn’t answer.”
“Where is she?”
“Satellite tracking says she’s at home.”
“Have you sent anyone around?”
“No. I had not thought of that.”
“Do it now.” He ordered abruptly. Technology really made people lazy and stupid. No-one, it seemed, could be bother to get off there ever-widening backsides and physically do something, relying almost exclusively on surveillance devices and electronic trickery. He sighed and spun around on his chair to check out the view. He sighed again. How could I forget? No fucking view. He touched the screen again. “Harmony, who have you got going?” 
“No-one yet.”
“Don’t worry, I’ll go.” He said, scantly disguising exasperation.
Half an hour later he entered Avi’s flat. All seemed fine; carpets recently vacuumed, cushions neatly placed on the sofa, kitchen sparkling and an annoyingly pleasant view out of all windows. The bedroom was similarly spotless; bed made up with fresh crisp sheets, hospital corners perfectly folded. As he entered the bathroom, however, he caught the minute scent of something very familiar – blood commencing to rot. His pistol was out in an instant, index finger resting lightly on the trigger guard, his eyes scanning the room in quadrants. Avi and some unknown woman were in the bath, no pulse and cold, congealing blood pooled around them. Looks like you brought the wrong one home this time, Avi. Who the fuck is this other one?
He called it in and within minutes, a sanitising team arrived to remove all evidence of Avi ever having been there. The concealed armoury cabinet and the tiny makeshift workshop was carefully cleared of booby traps, weapons, explosives and all manner of electronic gadgets, some of which would warrant later study. It looked like Avi had manufactured a few ingenious improvised devices. The bodies were carved into pieces, removed in several duffel bags before the entire premises was steamed, disinfected and fumigated. They were deposited in a furnace along with all of Avi’s belongings, everything. The entire operation taking less than 1 hour. The company assassin name Avi and her unknown friend never existed.
Damien had barely planted his behind in his office chair when summoned to the top floor. Very rarely is it a good thing to get an invitation to see the Director and he seemed none too pleased when Damien entered the cavernous office. 
“I want a report on the nightclub debacle.” The Director blurted out.
“Well, sir, as directed by your office we arranged for a device to be planted in the Stick it Inn, a nightclub in the inner west. Agent Avi was dispatched and planted the device. Somehow the water infrastructure was compromised and water meant for the lows was redirected to the north side.”
“How was the water compromised?”
“That is unknown, but according to reports, Alexander Shakti sorted out the problem and got the water running in the right direction.” He said, waiting for the shock to hit the Director. The legend of Shakti was known far and wide; he created problems, he did not solve them.
“Shakti? That idiot doesn’t know how to wipe his own arse.”
“It seems unlikely he could have fixed it, some engineers are taking the credit. We haven’t been able to identify all of them yet.”
“Identify them and report back to me. Do not contact them. Do not terminate them. This is a Chairman’s Directive.”
“Yes, sir.”
“I want Avi’s contract terminated.” The director said gravely.
“I have already taken care of that. She’s been wrapped and roasted.” He said, hoping this would please the boss. He thought better of complicating the issue by telling him about the other girl they found. If she ever mattered, she didn’t anymore.
“Good. That should satisfy the CEO. Now get out of my sight.” The director was impressed. Before the young upstart entered, he had half-considered terminating his contract. Clearly, they had promoted the right man – the operation had gone awry, but he had acted decisively. He immediately entered a few key strokes in his comms device and a hologram of the CEO appeared, illuminating the desk with a gentle glow. 
“What happened?” The CEO demanded. “It was a simple job. How could it go so badly?”
“I have just spoken to the agent in charge, Damien Hardman. The worker he dispatched was one of our best operatives, Avi and she had never failed us before. She seems to have placed the device correctly, the explosion was on time and devastated the entire building. They are going to have to demolish and rebuild. We are still in the early stages, but we have not identified the cleaner among the dead. He may have been blown to pieces or he may have escaped.” The Director was in full damage control mode. “As directed, sir, neither Damien nor Avi knew the identity of the target. That still remains between you and I.” 
“Keep it that way Mr Director. How did the water get switched off? You know level sevens were drinking fucking recyc don’t you?” The CEO never used coarse language unless he was livid. “Could this Avi woman be involved?”
“I don’t think so, she was meticulous and a true professional. She loved killing, I mean she really loved it. It’s unlikely she would do anything to upset the apple-cart. She took pride in her work and did everything by the numbers. Nevertheless, I had her contract terminated. If she was a liability, she is no longer.” He hoped that sounded sufficiently managerial for the CEO. 
“Good. At least some action has been taken. She won’t be a problem now.”
“Agent Hardman is looking into the source of the vandalism at the public baths near Erskintown. It seems that it was tampered with on-site, but with some really tricky algorithms, something the IT boys had not encountered before. There are vast reams of data missing, including all security footage for several blocks in every direction. Whoever these terrorists are, they are very good.”
“You will have to be better, Mr. Director.” The CEO always looked for opportunities to slap the director down. “I hope Agent Harman is up to the challenge. I expect results and I expect them fast.”
“What about the target, Sir. Should we go looking for him?”
“No. If he is alive, just let him be. Do not approach him and do not arrest.” The CEO was most adamant. 
“Apart from me, no one knows who he is.” The director said with relief. He knew he would not blab, so nobody under his command will have any reason to speak to the poor sap. For whatever reason, a cleaner had come under the adverse attention of the CEO and that meant his life expectancy was now quite short.
“Yes. You are the only one.” At that very moment the CEO decided the Director had to go. He was an intolerable security risk. He was kicking himself, though, he should have used a blank for this job, but he had erroneously figured his army of assassins would be able to handle a simple bombing. Yes, the director definitely had to go. He picked up a small device and depressed the button. Immediately he saw the Director’s Office Manager enter the room with a Particle Pulse Pistol, she leveled it at the director’s head and fired without hesitation. The Director slumped dead on his desk and she then put the gun to her temple and fired.
“Excellent.” The hologram said and then disappeared. He did not wish to burn such a reliable asset as Janine Wildsmith, she had provided high quality intelligence for years, but she knew what she was getting into when she agreed to be a sleeper agent and was well aware that she would have to make this sacrifice when called upon. She was loyal to her Director and it had taken him years to turn her, but the birth of her intellectually impaired son found her swimming in debt. To protect the health system from undue costs, when identified, the retarded and deformed were destroyed in utero, but somehow the tests had not picked anything up. She came to him begging for financial relief, precisely as he had predicted when he had her medical records altered. </Text>
            <Notes>What is Damien’s job?

Who is the Director?
Who is Avi?
The CEO?</Notes>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="4626AB59-367A-4358-A058-D4628D46F17B">
            <Title>Postcards from a failed Universe</Title>
            <Text>Postcards from a failed universe
After destroying his old lab, The Johnny set about blaming his team for the calamity and terminated their contracts before the wreckage had ceased to glow. He settled into a short campaign of finger-pointing and self aggrandizing, which yielded him a new contract and a new, remotely located research facility from which he would set in motion the destruction of the world.  
He settled into his new accommodations and was soon ordering around his inferiors with disdain. He loved his new lab, but he despised underlings of any kind. His last team had seen him at his incompetent worst and he simply would not tolerate the survival of that knowledge. He hated when reality intruded into his manufactured world, undermining his confidence and shaking his fanciful claims of genius. This time, with a clean slate, circumstance would not conspire against him and he would prove he is the master of his domain. 
“Attention, attention. This is test number 3296 on disk A. Gamma Radiation.” The Johnny announced over the intercom, from behind 15 cm of ballistic lead crystal. 
His minions gently placed disk A on a broad white polymer bench, located in the centre of the room and directly below the emitter and then casually made their way to the exit, but Johnny had already sealed the lab tight. 
“5 second bursts at 500 GeV.” The Johnny announced and entered the code. “Unless you want to cook from the inside out, clear the floor. You have 10 seconds to remove yourselves.”
“Wait a minute, sir!” The junior lab tech called out, fingers stabbing at the keypad. “The door is stuck fast.”
 “Hurry, little man. 7, 6, 5..” The Johnny smiled. 
“Come on, sir.” He jerked desperately upon the interlock , but it did not budge. “It’s fucking stuck! Stop the test. Stop!”
“3, 2..” The Johnny counted down with delight, but the techs somehow struck upon some luck, the door suddenly changed it’s mind and they shot out to safety. “Did you seal it properly?”
“Fuck you.” The lab techs screamed. Johnny laughed heartily.
The humming of the emitter escalated into high pitched squeal as the disk was bombarded with gamma rays from above. After 20 cycles the emitter was shut down and the disk was examined microscopically.
“Nothing! Gamma radiation has no effect whatsoever. What else can we try?” The Johnny asked of his crew, but none answered, assuming it was one of his frequent questions asked in the rhetoric. “Well? Answer me if you want to keep your jobs.”
“Sir, we have tried chemicals, electric current, magnetism, heat, cold, all manner of radiation, MRI, Ultrasound, Tactile imaging, Positron emission tomography, x-rays and neutrino showers just to name a few. We have even tried bashing it with a hammer, with no effect. The only thing that makes a difference, as you report, is spinning.” A brave minion managed to get out.
“That is the only thing we cannot do!” The Johnny raised his voice louder again, but upon further contemplation he concluded they had run out of options. “Yes, I think we should spin the discs. We have applied all manner of external stimuli and I can’t think of anywhere else to go. Spinning it is, agreed?” Johnny had known all along he was doing nothing but busy work, wasting time until he was left with the only option, spin the discs. 
“Yes, sir.” The three lab-techs said. The Johnny loved being called sir. He had recruited the right brand of sycophant, but despised them nevertheless.
“Let’s get to work.” The Johnny bravely announced. “We should begin work on preparing the bunker.” In the inevitable event of an explosion, at least the lab would remain intact. He knew his career would be terminated if he presided over another disaster. One more cluster-fuck would not be tolerated and even his protected level 5 backside would be spanked raw.
“The bunker is a complete disaster, it will take us a month to clear it out.”
“You have a week. Hard work has never harmed anyone.” The Johnny loved the irony. He had never worked hard for more than 10 minutes at a time his whole life and he was not about to start now. Supervising the heavy lifting was about as close he was going to come to breaking into a sweat. He had the burden of command, something the imbeciles standing before him would never know, not if he had anything to do with it. “Get to work!”
The three labs techs gave each other that familiar look. They each hated the Johnny in equal measure and would like to see him suffer and die.
* * *
Precisely at that same moment, Sarah felt what she thought to be mild abdominal cramps. The contractions were not painful, rather a noticeable inconvenience accompanied by a disconcerting anxiety radiating from the child. She had channeled his feelings for month and had felt them coalesce from blurred recollections of primordial instinct into identifiable and unique emotion of growing individuality. Mostly he broadcast love and pure joy, which caused her to wonder whether every mother experienced this same connection. More than once, in fact on every occasion, she had concluded her link to her child was special. 
On this occasion she divined her baby wanted out and he wanted out now! She did not know why, but she knew it was his conscious decision to be born and he was not going wait a second longer. The message she was receiving was not coherent, but a transmission of pure thought and emotion absent of language or formal design. It was both beautiful and frightening. She was then blasted with an image of a face, blurred, but familiar. As the image gained clarity, she saw a vision of The Johnny, in his lab and the accompanying emotions were of contempt and fear. Was this link with Richard, she thought, a reanimation of ancient, long forgotten instincts? Was it evolution on steroids? It did not really matter why it had manifested, to her the communication seemed perfectly natural. Directly, she found herself making a holo-call to Kathy Logan, the competant, but permanently wasted medical practitioner.
“Sarah?” Kathy was only on ketamine, a drug to which she had acquired a massive tolerance, making her quite lucid. “What is the time? Actually, what day is it? It think I am supposed to have the day off, if it’s Saturday.”
“It is late Saturday, Christmas eve if you didn’t know. Kathy and I think the baby is coming.” Sarah was calm and could feel a warm glow inside her. It seemed the child was pleased Dr Logan would be involved in his delivery. “I am not in pain, but I am certain the child is anxious. In any case, he’s done gestating.”
“Merry Christmas, then!! Well then, let’s implement plan A.” The birth plan was now set in motion. Having a plan A, inferred, perhaps, that there was a plan B or even C, but that was not the case. Dr Logan was confident enough in her abilities as a medical practitioner (and party planner) to keep the process as simple as possible. The most complicated task, it seemed to her, was getting Shakti to the birthing centre without him freaking out.
 Early the following morning, on the 25th of December, exactly 283 days after she met the three great sages, the virgin Sarah gave birth to a healthy 3.9 kg male child and promptly named him Richard-Dawkins Jacobson-Shakti or Richard-Dawkins for short or Richard for shorter. The name would not be shortened any further as Sarah did not like Dick. Kathy had never before seen a birth so quick and silent. So speedily did Richard shoot out Kathy’s patches had hardly anytime to kick-in and she managed to get through the delivery completely sober. Something that surprised all. 
Sarah had not known what to expect, except pain, but she felt hardly a twinge of discomfort and passed the child into the world with ease. Quite remarkably, Sarah’s body returned to a pre-pregnancy state within hours. Her skin, musculature and internal tissue tissue were perfectly elastic and any subsequent medical examination would conclude she had never given birth and was, in fact, virgo-intacto. The unwanted presence of Alexander Shakti in the birthing room, bothered her greatly, but it was infinitely preferable to Eloise Shakti’s presence under the pretence of support. She didn’t want her husband to see her exposed flesh and insisted he stand by her head and hold her hand. Even when she had ‘consummated’ their relationship, she recalled with disgust, it had been in total darkness.
The visit to her private suite by the High Elvis and the remaining two great Sages was a highlight. Silo and Chilo were expecting Sarah would still harbour some significant anger towards them, but were surprised by her welcome.
“What is this?” Sarah smiled with delight at the boy in her arms. “Look Richard, the men who are responsible for your birth.” 
“The One-after-the-One!” The High Elvis proudly announced as he, Silo and Chilo bent to their knees and bowed in reverence. “To be alive in the time of the Saviour is truly an honour.” He offered up a three small gifts.
“Are you three what passes for wise men these days? ” Sarah giggled and brought each gift to her ear before giving them a little shake. “Let me guess Frankincense? Myrrh?”
“Praise be to the Dawkins.” Silo and Chilo were never out of character. 
“Well if you can keep this a secret for a while I would be very grateful.” Sarah didn’t need a stampede of Elvi in her room. 
“Are we forgiven Blessed Mother?” Silo asked, quietly expecting rejection. 
“Richard is the best thing that has ever happened to me! You are forgiven.” Sarah could not believe she was forgiving the unforgivable, but the wonder of her son rendered her euphoric. 
“We will have to make an official announcement to the Elvi, but I am also concerned about the security implications.” The High Elvis brought down the mood. “He is our Saviour and must be protected at all costs.”
“Labeling him Saviour will probably draw unwanted attention.” Sarah returned fire. “We are talking about danger from the elites of the dominion are we not? My people do not tolerate hope. If they do not know his name, they cannot target him. Although I think it will be impossible to hide his existence, the Elvi will surely notice that I am not pregnant. Can all the Elvi keep a secret?”
“They will have to. The safety of the One-after-the-One is paramount. You will have 24 hour Elvi protection. I will not take no for an answer.”
The following days and weeks were a shear delight for her, apart from having to share Richard-Dawkins with her husband. She resented his claim of right over a child she knew was not his and had to fight the overwhelming desire to tell him the truth. She feared she could only exercise restraint for the short time. She had been relieved when Dr Kathy had urged Shakti to “avoid intercourse” for the first few months following the birth. A three month reprieve from the horror of a Shakti-fuck was as good as she had hoped for and she now turned her thoughts to allocation-exit-strategies.
Shakti had not even questioned why Sarah had suddenly felt the need to hire six Elvi servants. Two Butlers, two maids and two child care specialist, all carrying guns for reasons Shakti could not fathom. They loved him as their own and insisted that Richard was never, ever left alone, always watched over by a benevolent Elvis. 
The connection Sarah felt with her precious Richard was far more profound than any other mother had ever felt. It was a link unbreakable, Sarah knew it the moment she looked upon him. Losing herself in his eyes set off a deluge of endorphins and waves of joy before unknown to Sarah or, she had concluded, any previous mother. Her delight had periodically been interrupted by images of Johnny appearing in her mind, accompanied now, with feelings of sorrow and impending doom. Kathy had told her she would experience some fluctuating emotions, but there was no one mentioned strange images of Johnny tinkering away in his lab or feeling like she was mourning the dead. Sarah had dismissed the thought of post natal depression. Not me! Compared to the extreme unlikelihood that she would fall victim to something as commonplace as a chemical imbalance, the possibility that Richard was the source of the imagery was now compelling. Although, Sarah had to admit, Richard had never even seen Johnny and could not know what he looked like. What it meant, if anything, Sarah could only guess.
Sarah decided to visit the office, catch up with her colleagues and show off what she had squeezed out of her vagina. She had a terrible time convincing the Elvi security she would be safe inside police headquarters, but she managed to persuade them to stay in the foyer. She always wondered what possessed mothers to drag their newborns to the office. It only served to interrupt work and annoy the boss. She now knew why and cared not of petty disruptions, she was in possession of the greatest child that had ever been born and she wanted some validation. Friendships would be made and lost on the strength of the admiration Richard received. She had already suffered through one excruciating mother’s group meeting where a bunch of silly women trotted out their pathetic excuses for offspring, each claiming they had birthed greatness. The thought was simply ludicrous! It was clear to any objective observer, Richard was a child of a superior kind and those mothers were just plain delusional.
 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -
The office of the Commissioner had suddenly become vacant and was being advertised to level 5’s and above. The previous Commissioner had not survived the “Nightclub Vampire” scandal and had been forced into retirement by an unfortunate fall out of his office window. The most competent level 4 administrator in the country had no chance of jagging the level 5 position of commissioner. It mattered not that incompetence occupied the seat, only that the bloated buttocks squished into the chair possessed the correct social standing. The applications had clogged the inbox of HR and, rumour had it, even a level 6 senior executive had applied for the job! Everyone in HR was greatly amused and intrigued. Wow a level 6 Commissioner, that would be amazing!
 Naturally the front runner for the job was Detective Chief Superintendent Hayley Lanning. She had, single-handedly some claimed, caught the real “Nightclub Vampire” in the act of torturing a victim and somehow, video of the arrest had been leaked to the media. The author of the security leak had never been tracked down, but Superintendent Lanning was pretty sure it had been someone named Hayley. She was loved by the elite and scumbags alike, she was a star. If the position was up for election, she would easily jump through all the hoops HR placed in her way and straight into the Commissioner’s Office, but democracy and populism would not enter into the equation.  She had not allowed herself the luxury of thinking the job was in the bag and did not dare occupy the big office, but remained in her tiny cube, a few doors down. 
“Sarah! Or should I say Blessed Mother?” Hayley was pleased to see her.
“Just as long as I can call you Holy Aunt Commissioner.” They both forced a chuckle. 
“Not yet. And who is this beautiful boy? Richard? You are gorgeous, just look at you.” The senior cop’s heart had melted in seconds and she urgently raised both arms, a silent request for a hold. “Oh, he smells so good. It should be bottled.” 
“He is perfect isn’t he?” Sarah felt Richard awash with happiness and a little embarrassment.
“I heard you had a pretty easy time of it” Hayley knew she should engage in baby-talk before taking this unexpected opportunity to send Sarah on an errand most urgent.
“Yes, Richard was obliging.” Sarah said, resting her hands upon her flat, muscular stomach. “He was in a big hurry to see the world.”
“What brings you in today, Sarah?” Hayley knew it could not just be motherly pride dragging Sarah into the office within a week of childbirth.
“It is a little embarrassing. I am not sure exactly what is happening, but it seems certain Richard and I are telepathically connected.” She felt a glow of relief emanating from her child which calmed her greatly. “I have never given this phenomenon much credence, but I can’t explain it any other way.”
“What do you mean?” Hayley didn’t believe in that crap either. “”Like what?’
“During the pregnancy I felt he was sending messages, just raw feelings, unyoked thoughts and images. Like the image of the beta-mode indicator pattern. The image wasn’t just something that I made up in my sleep, but it was real. Since the birth it has become much more noticeable. He has been sending me, at least I think it is him, images of Johnny in his lab. The first time I saw these images was just before I went into labour. I don’t know what it means, but afterwards I always feel terrible. Like someone has died.” Richard beamed happiness to Sarah. She was finally getting the message.
“Both of those images are linked to Johnny. Which is both interesting and timely. I have received a couple of disturbing reports from the techs working with your arrogant friend.” Hayley disliked The Johnny as much as she despised Cracker. She could not work out why Sarah tolerated either of them, or Shakti for that matter. What is she? An idiot magnet? Then again, isn’t every woman? It is our lot to endure oafs of every variety. 
“Interesting? What is he up to?”
“Members of his team report that he tried to kill them, but worse, far worse, he is planning on testing those blasted discs under centrifugal force.”
“He’s going to spin the discs again?” Sarah was hit with a blast of dread from her Richard who began to cry Hayley’s arms. 
“I think you are right.” Hayley said. “Richard understands us.”
“If Johnny continues this course of action, his contract will be terminated, with extreme prejudice.” Hayley was firm. Johnny was planning to disobey orders, her orders no less.
“I don’t see the need for that.” Sarah countered. “I am happy to speak with him if you like.”
“Yes, it will have to be you.” Hayley was pleased. She had planned to ask Sarah to do just that. Wasn’t that just serendipitous? “For your information, the CEO has directed me to conduct a clandestine investigation into reports that an Elvi Saviour has been born.”
“I should be able to give you a hand when I get back to work.” Sarah managed to hold back a belly laugh only by gluing a smirk across her face. 
“You may have some insights.” Said deadpan Hayley.
‘I will take Richard with me. You know, make out that I am showing off the baby. Johnny will hate it, but will assume it is a woman thing and bite his tongue.” Richard began laughing and squirming. It seemed he was looking forward to his adventure.
“Take care with the young one, that lab is doing some dangerous work.” Hayley didn’t really care, but thought it was the right thing to a new mother. New mothers loved that shit. 
“Okay.” Sarah didn’t say she planned to take Cracker and Dr Logan with her. Cracker annoyed the Johnny beyond distraction and his presence would ensure logic and reason be replaced by arrogance and antipathy. She will be able to manipulate his anger riddled brain and bend his will in any manner she liked. Men are so easy to bamboozle.
“You have your instructions Sarah. Make sure he doesn’t blow us all up.” She implored. “It will not look good on my resume and if I miss out on the top job because of the pride of that privileged fool, he will pay.” A serious and deadly threat.
She left the Commissioner-in-waiting and contacted Cracker and Kathy to arrange a rendezvous. Cracker, still unemployed and waiting for that big payday, was immediately available, but Kathy had a long day ahead, carving up corpses and hallucinating on a new patch she was just dying to try. 
***
There was only one bunker in the facility, a large excavation dug originally for a subway station and later earmarked as a bomb shelter for a nuclear conflict that never came. The control room and labs were situated close by, but sufficiently distant, from the bunker to be safely outside any blast zone. With only one suitable location, he was compelled to test both discs in close proximity, a fundamental breach of agreed upon protocols, but necessary, to properly complete the task at hand. So he believed. 
The Johnny had had several purpose built centrifuges manufactured for this eventuality, which he would have his techs set up in the bunker. The bunker was about 30 metres below ground level, built of concrete many metres thick and after his team had cleared it out completely, was cavernous. The ceiling was 10 metres thick, comprising of heavily reinforced concrete and the walls were lined from floor to ceiling with sandbags in the hope they would absorb the enormous discharges of energy that, no doubt, would be released. Johnny thought, momentarily, to contact his handlers in the police department, but envisaged some dumb-fuck public servant would try to tell him what to do. The very idea! A uneducated dullard issuing me instructions! Never!
He decided that keeping the public servants out of the loop was the wisest course of action, given their limited ability to understand complex scientific concepts. No explosions for him. No Way. Not a chance.

***
The following morning, just before sun up, Sarah took Richard, Kathy and Cracker took an Elvi piloted hover-copt to the laboratory complex they were hoping to save from destruction. Upon landing they were greeted by a lab-coat who was desperate to leave as soon as possible and did not mind who he told.
“That Johnny idiot is going to kill us all! He tried to irradiated us. It was only pure luck I managed to get out of the lab in time. He is reckless, far too reckless, to be a serious scientist.” Johnny’s assistant was glad to see someone had taken heed of his warnings.
“What’s your name, mate?” Cracker thrust out his hand for a handshake which was gladly accepted. 
“Orion Contolan, PhD, Physicist, L.4.”
“Cracker Hardy, Cleaner, L.2.”
“L.2? What are you doing here? You can’t help me.” Orion did his best Johnny impersonation. “I sent out a plea for help and get sent a scumbag. What is wrong with you people? My toilet doesn’t need snaking out, my life needs saving. Fuck!!”
“Sarah Davidson, PhD, L.5. And this is Dr Kathy Logan, PhD, L.5. We are here to help, including Cracker. If you don’t want him here, we will leave you to it.” Sarah turned to leave.
“No, No. Please stay.” He put his arm around Cracker’s shoulders with genuine feigned affection. “I love this guy.”
“Any enemy of The Johnny is a friend of mine.” Cracker was alway able to forgive quickly and hugged him back.
“This little man here is Richard.” Sarah completed the introductions. “As far as Johnny knows, we are all here to show off my son.”
“You’re on a first name basis with the imbecile? I had not considered the possibility that he would have friends.” Orion was surprised to say the least. 
“Well then, take me to your leader.” Cracker laughed, but again he was the only one amused. Gotta hire better writers.
The Johnny was found alone in the control room, calibrating and re-calibrating the array of instruments and scanners wired to the two centrifuges. He knew he was just procrastinating by doing the busy work that his team of geeks had already completed. They had done a great job, but he resolved not to tell them, just in case it all turned to shit and he needed to point the finger.
“Johnny!” Sarah called out. “How is the progress?”
“Sarah?” He was happy to see her, but not the child. “Is he yours?”
“Yeah, I’m hers.” Cracker interjected.
“Not you, moron. The child.” The Johnny dismissed Cracker as expected.
“Yes.” Sarah said. “This is Richard, my son. Isn’t he just gorgeous?”
“You know I hate children, but I must say, he is a lovely little bastard.” In the face of such a beautiful, serene child, he could say little else. “And if he keeps quiet I will let him stay.” 
“Very charitable of you.” Sarah felt a blast of hate pulsating out of Richard’s tiny little chest and was shocked at the intensity.
“I did not think you would have the chance to visit Richard, so I brought him to you.”
“Very thoughtful, but what do you want?” To Johnny, it was obvious there was another reason for her presence. “Who sent you?” He had given up on the idea of being with Sarah, she was now tainted by Shakti-jiz and would be forever unclean.
“Any plans on say, spinning the discs?” Sarah quietly slipped out.
“Who told you that?”
“Richard did.” It was kind of true. He was warning her about something the Johnny was about to do and the techs had just filled in the blanks. “He fears you are going to do something catastrophic. So what are your plans with the discs?”
“I have done everything I can, but they are completely inert, except when spinning. I have been working on this for months and have completed all standard simulation modeling. Spinning is the next logical step. It’s the only thing left to do.” Johnny was pleading, he did not like being second-guessed.
“Have you submitted the modeling results or a proposal?” Sarah asked, ignoring the disdain Richard felt for The Johnny, “At least tell me you have something written up?”
“No. NO! They would not understand the science!” Johnny screamed. “This is my gig. This is my job. This is my life. Tell your people to leave me to do my job in peace.”
“My people are your people and you are as obliged to obey as I am.” Sarah’s voice started to raise as she unconsciously began to feed off Richard’s rage. “You will at least make a presentation.”
“No, I am ready to go and I cannot countenance any interference.” The Johnny was now in full voice and he was booming. “Look at the screen.”
Johnny waved his hand over an emitter and a giant holo-screen appeared in front of them, floating in the control room. The image was appeared to be a shot, from above, of two small shiny discs, sitting upright on their edges, each held fast on spindles of a converted centrifuge.
“The way I figure it, if the discs they are spun in opposite directions at the same time and speed, they will cancel each other out.” The Johnny said with pride.
“That’s just a guess, right?” Cracker decided he should start annoying The Johnny.
“You are assuming a great deal.” It was Dr Logan’s turn. She was, again, surprisingly lucid for midday. “Sounds like trial and error to me.”
“Well fuck you all.” Johnny waved his hand over the console and set the discs spinning.
Sarah could immediately feel an exponential escalation in Richard’s rage. He began squirming and crying loudly, making holding on near impossible. Sarah handed him to Kathy who struggled to keep him under control until he settled when she approached the control centre console. He desperately tried to escape Kathy’s clutches and make a bid for the console, but she was too strong.
“Turn it off, Johnny.” Sarah was loud and serious. “I am not kidding. Switch it off.”
“It has to cycle through, there is no turning it off.” The Johnny was terrified, but unrepentant. His idiotic pride, again had gotten the better of his reason and he had gambled his future.
“I was sent here because they are considering terminating your contract. You had better hope this works out.” Sarah was furious and so was Richard, still squirming here and there, trying to get near the control centre console.
As the discs spun at increasing speed, slender light blue tendrils arced between the discs and became darker and thicker. Sparks were now shooting in all directions, smashing the roof and exploding sand bags and setting the crystals on fire. Sandbags, hundreds of kilograms in weight flew around the room like autumn leaves in a windstorm, spinning and slapping into one another before crashing into the gap between the discs and disappearing into the ether. 
“Gravity well!” Cried out one of the techs. “Holy shit, you can feel it from here.” He threw a notebook vertically into the air, but fell at a 45 degree angle to the ground and slid across the floor, slamming into the wall.  
“We have to stop this.” Dr Logan said. “There must be some way. There must be.” She gave Richard to Cracker and moved closer to the console where Sarah was already working hard, desperately pressing buttons and entering instructions. The Johnny was in the foetal position in the corner, watching his career spiral out of control, again. 
“I am trying, but the security on this system is ridiculous.” Sarah was having no luck. The shooting sparks were now lightening and cracking loudly, desperate to bust out of the bunker, some 100 metres away and 30 metres below. “I need more time, a lot more time to crack this.”
Suddenly the system was silent and the gravity well disappeared. The panicked crew looked about the control room in amazement, only to see Cracker holding a thick power cable, recently pulled out of a wall, in one hand and Richard-Dawkins in the other. “Don’t look at me, it was Richard’s idea. I looked in his eyes and the next thing I was out the cable.”
“Thank God for Richard-Dawkins.” Dr Logan said as she snatched him away from Cracker and cuddled him with genuine affection. “You are the smartest little man in the world, aren’t you?” Richard giggled in agreement, then pointed his little right index finger at The Johnny and started crying. Sarah feeling precisely the anguish in her little man’s soul. 
“Saved by a child, Johnny.” Sarah said, shaking her head slowly in disbelief. “Saved by my child.”
“I don’t understand.” The Johnny said, removing his thumb from his mouth. “Spinning in the opposite direction should have worked.”
“Well,” Cracker commenced. “The discs are not opposite are they? If my memory serves me correctly, one is infected with the Hyrbrid-T virus and one is not.” These elites continue to disappoint, he thought.
“Are you suggesting I infect them both with the Hybrid T virus?” The Johnny countered.
“No, not at all. I am suggesting you make the discs opposite. One is infected with the T virus. I would suggest the opposite of that is infecting the second discs with the Hyrbid anti-T virus innoculant. Then try spinning them in opposite directions.”
“Is Richard telling you that?” Dr Kathy asked, as Richard began to laugh and point at Cracker. 
“I do have ideas once in a while.” Cracker said as Richard continued pointing a laughing. “It’s rude to point kid.” 
“This just gets worse. Saved by a baby and given instructions by a dullard.” The Johnny was again unhappy. “Can I stoop any lower than this?” All present thought yes.
“We will have to get back to the city, Johnny. Can you promise you will do nothing more until we we come back?” Sarah asked nicely. She did not want to end him, but knew that was a possibility. 
“Okay, no more experiments for a while. We will have to clean up the bunker and take sample data, do a bit of analysis. That should take a couple of months of study. Shall we meet next week?”
“One week from now.”
***
At that moment every stasis cupboard in the Primary Trade Dominion malfunctioned. Food instantaneously spoiled and turned to dust. At Food and Drug Administration Headquarters, urgent meetings at the highest level were convened. For the first time in it’s history something had seriously gone wrong with stasis technology. Sure, a few cupboards had blown fuses and had other minor issues over the years, but never such a catastrophe. 
“I don’t believe this is some sort of accident or natural event,” opined Halverson Tentries III, L.5. Maintenance Technician. “Someone is fucking with us.”
“They clearly don’t understand the technology and could do some real damage.” 
“That is an understatement. We are talking about the end of time.”
“Listen to Mr Worse-case-Scenario” nbnbnbn “It would take some seriously reckless action to mess with space-time.”
“We can’t assume no danger and hope for the best.”
“Just as long as they are not dumb enough to centrifuge the stasis circuitry.”
“No-one would be that moronic.”
“You know we will have to examine every stasis cupboard, bin and pantry in the PTD, don’t you?
“Yep.”

* * *
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="153A9602-7823-42E2-8DE7-3CDBF7BE87C1">
            <Title>A Big Crush</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="DEDFED21-7F75-4CA2-A7F2-934F6DFEC279">
            <Title>Leiutenant Shakti</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="5D6E10A4-3484-4FF5-BF75-F3348FAF54A3">
