        <Document ID="13DEB42B-4D71-436E-BC07-8187610E3AA9">
            <Title>Genesis of Genecide</Title>
            <Synopsis>Sort of a job performance review for Agent Jones.  The new director of "Proactive Euthenasia and Pre-emptive Reciprocity" Damien Hardman flexing his muscles a bit.  This meeting shows how tenuous an agent's employment (and life expectancy) is.  (Then sets up Agent Jones to interrogate the operatives (who picked up the Emil Kalheri's body) to assist in identifying Cracker.  Which in turn sets up Agent Jones visiting Cracker at home and torturing him.  Then Sarah and Hayley turn up to speak with Cracker and then Shakti turns up to arrest Cracker.  It is meant to be farcical - Spys, Cops and More cops turn up to speak with Cracker, all for different reasons, but all pretty much at the same time.)</Synopsis>
            <Text>Genesis of Genocide
10 to 14  days after bombing
Agent Jones marched up and down trying to work out why the new Director had summoned him to his office. He had fucked up a few times in the last two days and he knew he was a prime candidate for contract termination. The first order of business for most new directors was to clear out the deadwood and he was feeling dry and combustible. He walked the last few metres to Damien’s Office as a condemned prisoner, his wrists unconsciously drawn together by invisible bindings, head low and back hunched. 
“Agent Jones, I have been reviewing your performance of late and I think we have a few issues. Your blank disappeared on a simple termination and you lost a Gyges Disc. Have you any idea what you have done? In an effort to cover your pathetic backside you have left a trail of destruction and misery. I cannot see any purpose for the extreme prejudice displayed in killing the entire Kalheri family. It has blown up in to a major police investigation! I am struggling to find a reason not to terminate your contract here and now.” He opened his draw and removed a particle pistol, placing it gently on the desk in front of him.
“Sir, I have been through all this with your predecessor. The techs took the blank to the wrong morgue without notifying me. He was located and transferred to our labs. In any case, we got nothing from our examination. There is just one more termination to effect and the matter will be finalised.” Agent Jones’ eyes were affixed on the tiny red lights of the gun, flashing in regular pattern. It was charged and the safety was off. His worst case scenario unfolding.
“You want to kill more people?” He picked up the pistol and casually pointed it at the frightened agent.
“Just one, sir, but I have been unable to identify him. I think he is a cleaner and he’s got the disc, but that’s as far as the trail has lead so far.” He failed to tell the Director that he had stood no more than one metre away from that very cleaner during a 116 floor elevator trip and failed to notice anything, apart from the annoying trolley. He was already in enough shit and he knew from painful experience that revealing too much of the truth will fuck you up every time.
“Who else was on this with you?” Damien was insistent
“Apart from the techs, no-one.”
“What did they see?” 
“A cleaner is all I got from them.”
“Did they record anything?” For the purposes of finding out who fucked up during covert operations, it was standard procedure to record everything possible on audio and video.
“No point. There was a gyges disc in play.” Agent Jones, just managing to stifle a smug grin, was very grateful there was no possibility of any electronic record of his fuck-up surfacing. It was bad enough to make mistakes, but far worse for those errors to be captured for posterity. Enduring the ridicule of his murderous contemporaries was more than Agent Jones’ ego could tolerate. 
“You need to re-interview the techs. Use advanced interview protocols if you think it necessary.”
“Yes, sir.” He could not wait to torture those pair of clowns.
“After that I have a very special job for you. This will be your last chance, your very last chance to impress me, Agent Jones. We will be digging up the old Bio-Tech research facility of Jameson Hardy. It is a level 10 operation and you will head the security team.” The Director did not tell him the likelihood of death was very high. “Shakti and his team are still here, taking statements and poking around so proceed with discretion.”
“Thank you, sir.” He did not betray himself, but digging up weapons grade bio-matter did not seem like a good idea and the more he thought about it the worse the idea became. He did not know what horrors lay buried beneath 10 metres of concrete and was not all that keen to find out.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="810D5C09-B57A-4BF5-936B-396EB4925C84">
            <Title>Elvis of the Travelator</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="C57DF245-7D6D-4C3A-8069-C882B644BA95">
            <Title>Dark Forces</Title>
            <Text>
Dark Forces
75 mins after bombing
The Commissioner cut Shakti off and immediately made another call. “He fucked it up even more than we could have hoped. He took one look, called it suicide and then went back to bed. There was no evidence collected. We can’t be accused of a cover-up if there is nothing to cover.”
“Good, Mr. Commissioner,” said the CEO. “The blank finished the job, but lost a Gyges disc and then disappeared. He is probably dead, but he must be located. My agents are surgical instruments and they work alone. We don’t have the investigative capacity to find him” The CEO lied. He already had an agent on the case.
 
“Consider it done.”
“Discreetly”
“Always.” Assured the Top Cop of the PTD.
He thought finding this dead blank should not be too difficult, it wasn’t the first time one had fallen off the grid. Finding a live blank was usually the difficult bit. They were invariably terminally ill and could drop dead at any second, but this made them very useful for assassinations. Even to company specialists, murder was a particularly thorny affair. Killing was no problem, of course. It is easy to pop someone off, but getting away with it was the hard part, especially when the killing was a part of a series directed at a specific political or, more usually, commercial end. How did you sever obvious links between the victims? How do you keep yourself safely out of the glare of an investigation? Termocorp enlisted the terminally ill with the promise of ongoing financial assistance for their families. All they had to do was kill and then die. If they were caught, they were expected to suicide or remain silent until death; about half an hour of interrogation would usually do it. Only one blank had ever been caught alive. Luckily, it was a woman who had children to protect. A man would have cracked, but she never uttered a sound, foolishly believing Termocorp would actually take care of her family.
Once in a while, when things went pear shaped, the Commissioner would be called upon to iron out some problems. Completely untrustworthy, he was a model public servant. Piloting safe through the pitfalls and moral ambiguities of a career in law enforcement required sheer genius and possession of highly malleable ethics. Throughout his career he had carefully and sometimes intuitively, selected the correct asses to lick and right dicks to suck. Naturally, the owners of the correct asses and the right dicks were, without exception, corrupt to the core. Once they had their hooks in, they were dug in deep, never to be removed. The Commissioner owed more than a few favours. 
He called upon his most competent and discreet Investigator, Detective Superintendent Hayley Lanning to head the search for the missing blank, Emil Kalheri. Lanning, a Doctor of Laws, possessed a brilliant analytical mind and unsurpassed knowledge of matters legal, easily matching any attorney. Her capacity to cut through the irrelevant and identify salient issues was matchless among her untalented peers, but her most peculiar attribute was her integrity – she had some, in fact she was the most prominent example of an exception proving the rule. She was unlike any other public servant in all of the Dominion in that she had the innate ability to combine honesty and tact, skills previously unknown in the public sector. She had her fair share of enemies within (and without) the ranks of the Police, but even her most ardent critics could not help but acknowledge her superior mind and envy her integrity, all longing to know what a clear conscience felt like.
“You want me to find a missing person?” quizzed an unbelieving senior detective. “This sounds more like a job for Shakti.”
“Definitely not Shakti.” The Commissioner instantaneously shot back, faintly smiling. “I need your special skills. It is a very sensitive matter and you are the best I have.”
“No need to piss in my pocket, sir. I am always happy to help.” This is just insulting. She didn’t add. Her internal bullshit-meter was madly flapping red flags and she needed to know what the hell was going on. “Is there anymore background on this gentleman?”
“I’ll transfer the file to you, but there isn’t much. He’s disappeared and I need him located.”
“Why is a level one labourer attracting this much attention? Why is he that important? He’s a nothing, in fact less than a nothing, but I can’t draw up a strategy without all the background.” She saw his smile had suddenly been replaced with a blank look and she decided she should push him no further. “You know, if you fail to plan you plan to fail.”
“Clichés from you? I never thought you would be so commonplace.” The Commissioner laughed, trying to deflect her question. It might have been better to give this one to Shakti, he silently considered, at least he doesn’t ask questions. “I know this is a little below your dignity, but you can keep things under wraps and that is what is needed here.”
“I’ll be on my own then?”
“Unfortunately, yes. You will have the resources of this office, but that is all.” He said austerely.
“I will have to dump a few other cases on my team, but I will get on it straight away. The Nightclub Vampire case will have to be put on hold. I am the only one who has a handle on that one.” 
“You’ll be working out of this building. I’ll arrange an office for you. That will be all, Superintendent.”
Fuck you, Commissioner.

* * *
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="3FBE0889-0957-4743-951B-9788037739B0">
            <Title>Vegas Show</Title>
            <Synopsis>Set in a bar in "The Quarter" where an Elvis Show (called a Vegas Show) was in full swing.  The establishment has nothing but Elvis clones as (male and female) workers - bartenders, waiters/waitresses.  You find out that you must be really (really) polite to an Elvis otherwise they could become violent.  Cracker and Sarah have a great night on the piss.  Is important as it is the introduction to the Elvi.</Synopsis>
            <Text>Vegas Show
(3.5 hours after bombing)
</Text>
            <Notes>(Elvine) First appears with: Sarah &amp; Cracker</Notes>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="4387B5D7-000A-40A9-B9E7-DC5A651394B1">
            <Title>Sarah &amp; Cracker get medical attention</Title>
            <Text>The cops didn’t take Sarah and Cracker to a hospital. They took them to one each.
They chauffeured Sarah to a palace in the sky where she would have access to the best care, including positive affirmation of her Airborne birthright. Cracker they rolled out of the cruiser onto a crumbling tenement clinic rooftop and left him there. Alexander agreed it would have to do.

The flowers, fruit baskets, muffin hampers, lingerie, and menageries of plush teddy bears, bunny rabbits, unicorns and vibrators in Sarah’s medical suite were refreshed daily. All of the cards were signed identically:

Best wishes and get well soon
I’m glad to have saved your life
—Alexander Shakti

Who was this loon? Sarah wondered. No time at all elapsed before she identified the celebrity host of Random Camera. In a fit of raging disgust, she put the day’s supply of vibrators among the muffins. The teddy bears and bunny rabbits quaked with fright. She cursed Alexander’s name. There was only one man who rescued her from the bombing: Mr Fairy Boxers.

Sarah required none of the advanced medicines and procedures available to her. Astonishingly, she recovered from her injuries within two days. Rather than investigate her remarkable abilities, doctors, nurses, bathroom orderlies and professorial boffins took them as further evidence of the genetic superiority of the Airborne, and were heard wondering aloud if eugenics hadn’t been retired rather too early.
Cracker regained consciousness, significantly ignored and untreated, in a sweaty bed in a maze of bacterially alarming smudges. The colours of everything Cracker saw were too organically decaying for description; the greys were too green and yellow, the greens and yellows were too brown, and the browns were too much like the vomit he coughed up when he registered how much his skull hurt. The nurses gave him dusty pills the diameter of party pies and told him to rest.

Suffering too much from pollen, Sarah checked herself out early. She felt fully recovered anyway, and was anxious to thank Cracker.
When she climbed the fire-escape stairs and entered the ward, she almost gagged with the stench. However, she was determined to press on.
“Can I help you?” the orderly at the ward desk asked.
“I doubt it.” Sarah strode past with her authoritative gait, between the rows of paint-chipped frames aching at holding saggy mattresses complaining of saggy patients. She almost passed Cracker without recognition.
“Sarah!” he called out.
She gasped when he held her gaze with his unblinking right eye. His blackened left eye was bruised shut, and seeped a dilute blood-ooze that trickled over the lumps, cuts, scratches, burns and bruises that contrived to construct something not too dissimilar to a face.
“Oh, my tax accountants! What are they doing to you?” She fled to his side and grasped his hand.
“Ow!”
She released his hand. “Are they even treating you?”
“The doctors diagnosed it as acute sunburn.”
“What? But … the cuts and bruises … you eyebrows and most of your hair is singed off.”
“Shaving accident.”
“This is appalling!” she sputtered. “I’ve never seen such … squalor …” Only a quick hover-copt jump from where I live, she thought, but could not bring herself to admit it.
“You’ve never seen Cloudborne health care before then. All they prescribe is sticking plaster and suppositories.” Cracker was about to remark that he had a better medical kit at home, but thought wiser of making such facts known in public spaces.
Sarah made her spine straight with decision making. “You’re coming with me,” she said. “He’s coming with me,” she announced to the ward.
“You can do better, dearie,” said a withered nurse stumbling by on a cane.
“Thank you, that would be most kind,” Cracker said to Sarah.
“I need a wheelchair,” Sarah demanded.
The withered nurse cackled.

Sarah returned a half-hour later, having purloined (temporarily) a wheelchair from an Airborne hospital. Cracker constructed himself into it with admirable resolve and tenacity. Sarah wheeled him to the stairwell, where they met two orderlies she paid to carry Cracker in wheelchair to the ground floor. From there, she pushed and shoved across unreliable ground to the nearest hover-pad stalk, where she and Cracker made their escape.
They made most of the journey to Cracker’s apartment discreetly by Cloudborne groundcab, which served to enhance Sarah’s experience and understanding of the deteriorating Cloudborne lifestyle. Everything she saw was worn-down, broken, taped, jury-rigged, bent to shape, chipped, corroded, smeared, or tied up with string. “These are are a few of my ghastliest things,” Sarah sang under her breath. The seats of the groundcab were torn and exploded springs. The doors to the back seat were clearly from another model of vehicle entirely, and didn’t shut properly. Gaps between the doors and wheel arches allowed Sarah to see through to the street. The steering wheel was almost completely missing. The driver steered by grasping the stubs of the steering wheel spokes. She braked by stomping on the floor like she was exterminating cockroaches. She accelerated by rocking back and forward in her seat, as if that would quicken the tired vehicle’s pace.
When they reached Cracker’s front door, he presented his armpit, but his door stayed resolutely shut.
“It didn’t even read me,” he complained.
“You might have to stand up,” Sarah said. She helped him to his feet and supported him while he lifted his arm again. The door clicked open, and Cracker slumped into the wheelchair.
“Not exactly disability-friendly,” Cracker said solemnly.
“No,” Sarah agreed.
Sarah was dismayed by the size and condition of Cracker’s living space, but did her best to hide it. Cracker wheeled himself to his room, bundled onto the bed, and passed out.

Over the next few days, Sarah devoted her spare time and spare thoughts spaces to Cracker. She risked the trip to his apartment twice a day, so she could feed, clothe, and bathe him (boxer shorts resolutely stayed on), and tend to his wounds with Airborne bandages, creams, unguents, and toenail clippers. She filled his freezer with microwave meals. She bought him new socks, cologne and vodka he could actually drink. She subscribed him to the best on-demand Xtreme Fighting Porn, and he marvelled at the tag-team doggy hammer throws. Although scars remained encamped on his face, hands and chest, his health and demeanour improved. 
When Sarah felt his wounds were settled enough, she thought it would improve his appearance if she could exfoliate all the flaking dead skin away. One night she sat him on bath’s edge, naked but for his boxer shorts, and produced a purple-black cloth which she used to buff his head.
“Ow!” Cracker complained.
“Men are such babies,” Sarah scolded. “Look, it’s working.”
Cracker checked out his noggin in the mirror, and was pleased with the result. He rubbed his hand over his scalp and enjoyed the smoother feel where Sarah had worked. He submitted to her resumption. “What is that cloth anyway?”
“Giraffe tongue.”
Cracker pushed her away. “What the AF? Aren’t they extinct?”
“Well they are now,” Sarah said as she rubbed behind his ears.
“Great. You’re a giraffe exterminator. Is there anything you Airborne won’t kill?”
“Well, I didn’t kill it. My grandmother left me this when I was a child. It has great sentimental value.”
“Really?”
“Yeah. She used to polish her bowling balls with it.”
When Sarah finished, Cracker proudly modelled for her. She applauded. He felt reinvigorated.
“Hey, I’m gonna take a shower. What’s say we go out for a couple of drinks? I’m dying to splash on some of that new vodka you got me.”
Sarah looked at him askance. “Are you asking me on a date?”
Cracker blushed and shrugged. “Nah, just some karaoke, you know …”
“At an ELVIS bar?”
“Of course. What other kind is there?”
Sarah beamed as fully as if he asked her on a weekend riverboat cruise.
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="9E7A4961-68C3-4334-B9F0-AAAB6B70C85C">
            <Title>Arrest</Title>
            <Synopsis>Sets up the CEO sending Shakti to arrest Cracker.  Shakti meets Sarah for the first time and recognises her as his "dream woman".  Cracker is arrest, even though Hayley knows there was insufficient info in the brief to identify anyone.  She tags along and offers to help Shakti.
</Synopsis>
            <Text>I think you got me
Arrest
2 to 3 weeks after Bombing
Shakti found himself standing tall before the man, wondering what he had done this time. He had received genuine congratulations for his thorough investigation into Director…… murder, so it couldn’t be that could it? All the information pointed to a random murder-suicide, but Shakti was convinced there was a conspiracy at play. I’ve looked under every crook and nanny, haven’t I? No arrests so far, but I’ll get there.
“I have it on good authority, Lieutenant, that you faked the recording on the Hammersmith case! You are in big trouble Shakti.” The Commissioner was yelling and screaming, but his heart wasn’t in it. He knew there was not much he could do to Shakti without upsetting his mother and no-one wanted to make her unhappy. Her tendrils reached everywhere.
“I don’t know what you mean.” Wiley old colleagues had once told him never admit anything. The cops age old adage - They can charge me with breathing and I’ll plead not guilty, was repeating itself in his mind and he would hold out no matter how long it took.
“Look Shakti, I know you did it and you know you did it. I don’t care about some L.2 you loaded-up. I want you to do me a favour.”
“Okay. I will do it.” Shakti capitulated. He had held out for all of three seconds.
“I want you to be the lead officer in the arrest of the Nightclub Vampire. We have only just identified him and you have to act fast or he might get away. The files are on this stick. Get an arrest team together and move!”
“Yes, sir.” Shakti thought he was going to be punished but this arrest would really give him the credibility he desired. This serial killer had been operating with impunity for years and his apprehension had become even more urgent in recent times when a couple of L.5. girls became victims. 
“Do not fuck this up.” He said those precise words so often, the Commissioner reflected, it could be his epitaph. He was perfectly aware that Shakti would make a meal of this arrest, but it didn’t matter. He had already offered a bounty on the head of the suspect, a promotion for the man or woman who disposes of him. The Nightclub Vampire would be dead before morning.
Returning to his office, Shakti was shocked to see the name of the one suspect when he opened the file. Anthony Hardy, Cleaner L.2, his only friend in the world, was a serial killer. He was hurt profoundly and deeply, but only allowed a few tears to escape before taking some solace in the knowledge that having no friends meant there was no one left to betray him. It appears, he thought, that the old cliché is true, friends do always shit on you. 
“No one can hurt you now.” He steeled himself for the task ahead, arresting and charging his ex-best friend with a capital offence. He studied the contents of the file as best he could, but found it far too complicated and decided to trust the Commissioner on this one. The evidence, he thought, had to be solid, didn’t it?
Shakti and the arrest team approached the entrance of the teetering, rusted iron and concrete structure, forensic teams and tactical support in the rear waiting for the all clear. There was a member of the public, a man dressed in a black suit, in the foetal position on the concrete just adjacent to the entrance, crying like a baby. After some considerable time one of the arrest team approached him and gave him a good kick and told him to fuck off. Agent Jones scuttled off, sobbing and howling. At the moment he was to give the team the go order, Shakti saw two women emerge from the entrance. He recognised one as the much lauded Detective Supt Hayley Lanning, but at a distance he was unable to recognise the blonde. He sent in the team as soon as the ladies were clear of the access point. He waved them over as his men stormed the building.
Detective Lanning stomped over to Shakti leaving Sarah trailing in her wake, “What is going Lieutenant? What is the meaning of this?”
“The Commissioner selected me to lead the team to arrest the Nightclub Vampire.” 
“That’s my brief! Who is your suspect?” Hayley could not believe the Commissioner would hand such a sensitive matter over to this fool. “Answer me!”
Sarah had caught up and Shakti finally got a good look at her. “Dream woman!” Shakti blurted out and stumbled back several steps. His stomach was churning and his body shaking noticeably as his system flooded with adrenaline, reacting to the deep shock of literally meeting the woman of his dreams. All sorts of liquids wanted to squirt out the nearest orifice as he tried desperately not to piss, shit or vomit.
“Dream woman? Is that a nick-name for the perp?”
“No, sorry. His name is Anthony Hardy, Cleaner, L.2.”
“You are kidding. We were just speaking to him about another Commissioner’s special investigation. He seems harmless. I trust your evidence is solid.”
“The Commissioner said it was.”
“You had better hope he is right.” She knew the brief back to front. Forensics and physical evidence were almost non-existent. She wondered what breakthroughs had been made in her absence and lamented being dragged away by the Commissioner to find a missing labourer. 
“What’s all the fuss, Detective?” Sarah caught some of the conversation and urgently wanted to fill in the blanks. 
“Lieutenant Shakti, this is a Sarah Jacobson, Ph.D. L.5. Sarah this is Lieutenant Shakti, L.5.” Hayley decided introductions were the first order of business, deliberately highlighting Shakti’s lack of tertiary qualifications. It was rumoured that he didn’t even get through high school, but all those records were unavailable to the public thanks to intervention from his mother.
“Lieutenant, pleased to meet you. I have heard so much about you.” Anyone within hearing immediately began to giggling. He wasn’t famous for his professionalism.
“Please, call me Alex. No rank.” He hoped she was not in the public service. His mother would not like that.
“I’m a privateer on a long term contract.” Sarah was also keen to distance herself from the public service.
“Alex here is in the process of arresting the Nightclub Vampire, Anthony Hardy. On Commissioner’s instructions no less.” Hayley thought to bring Sarah up to speed.
“What? He’s harm…., he seems harmless. Theft I would believe, but multiple murders?” Sarah was exasperated. What had Cracker been up to in his spare time? As she let her thoughts settle, a loud “All Clear, suspect in custody” crackled over the air. The support crews grabbed the equipment and headed inside and up the stairs. 
“Do you mind if I tag along, Alex?” Hayley was going in no matter what, but thought she should try diplomacy as a first port of call. “Sarah you go back to the office and have this disk checked out. Try our tech-teams first, but I think this thing will be military hardware and way out of their league. Perhaps you can wrangle an expert from your contacts in the cyber-security universe. It seems to be right up your alley, actually.”
“Can you put it in an evidence bag, Lieutenant?” Sarah was not keen for her skin to touch another one of these wretched discs.
“Yes, yes. Anything.” He grabbed an evidence bag from the forensics truck and returned smiling wide, mission accomplished. “Well I will be off then. Nice to meet you Lieutenant.”</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="67C67EE5-9C28-4D76-80F9-EC5CE5FBB9A7">
            <Title>Random Camera</Title>
            <Synopsis>An Episode of Random Camera.  

This needs to be removed.
I had a quick look and I can't see how this assists in telling the story and I don't think it will effect too much.</Synopsis>
            <Text>
Random Camera
9 months BTD (48 hrs after bombing)
Shakti was sucking in big breaths, his nerves were always shot just before going to air and on this occasion he knew the show could really go off the rails. The sponsors would be none too pleased if ratings fell again. He had been in the edit-suite with one of his minions, tidying up the profile on one of the human offerings for the evening show. There was not much evidence on this guy, he might have said he bombed the nightclub, but it could have been something else, the microphone did not quite pick it up. The magic of the post-production, however, allowed Shakti to clear up the ambiguity by dubbing in some really damning evidence. 
Shakti was not terribly happy with embellishing the evidence to ensure a conviction, but he was under real pressure lately to get the viewers back above 2 million. Below that mark the revenues were still reasonable, but once you crossed the 2 million barrier, the sponsors throw money at you. Shakti found himself in a compromising position for the second time in as many days. Cracker had really helped him out at the public hygiene unit, but he was concerned about when and in what form he would have to pay back the favour. He could handle forking out some cash, in fact, that was the preferred option, but from the sound of it, Cracker will require something entirely different and no doubt, unethical. 
“Good evening my friends. Welcome to a very special and solemn episode of Random Camera. We were all shocked to our core by the cowardly bombing of the Stick-it-Inn and we remain frustrated by the lack of action by the authorities. I, myself, made the only arrest on the night of the bombing, but he died before interrogation.” He paused, waiting for the canned applause to fire up the studio audience. “Tonight, right here on Random Camera, we will be bringing the first live offender to justice.” He raised his arms high above his head and was met with a wall of excited acclamation. “He a devious, level one scoundrel and a tradesman. Ladies and Gentlemen of the Management Class, please revile John Hammersmith.”
A poor emaciated looking sod was dragged out by two giant, well lubricated body builder types, who with contrived pomp and ceremony dumped the poor bastard on the studio floor, at the feet of the Shakti. 
“You have the wrong man. I haven’t done anything.” His pathetic petition only caused the crowd to laugh loudly, a few booing the doomed prisoner. 
Shakti smugly grinned directly into the camera, “That’s what they all say.” The crowd laughed even louder. “We shall see. Roll the file.”
The giant screen behind Shakti burst into life. The surveillance footage was grainy, deliberately so, to give Mr Hammersmith an air of suspicion. It followed him on his daily journey, digging trenches and gluing pipes together, laughing mindlessly with his dim-witted colleagues, each grab ending with a close-up of John’s evil face. At bars and in homes everywhere, level 4’s and 5’s were laughing and giggling with delight.
“Awe, yuck! Did you see that? He touched the dirt with his hands! Disgusting.” An audience member was heard to say as she elbowed her neighbour.
“He looks so guilty.” She replied.
The 15 minute hatchet-job reached its height in the final grab when, out of the blue, John Hammersmith was heard to say in a clear loud voice, “I bombed the Stick It Inn. I had to avenge my wife.” The crowd went wild, screaming and booing. 
“Guilty!” Was called out from the back of the crowd.
“He’s guilty. Death to the terrorist! Death, death.” The crowd followed and continued to chant, “Death, death, death…..”
The “Quiet Please” sign above the studio floor illuminated and the crowd settled quickly to a rumble of murmurs and disquiet. 
“What have you to say now, John Hammersmith?” Shakti read the autocue word for word.
“I don’t have a wife, I have never been married.” He pleaded. “I was in hospital on the night of the explosion.”
“He doesn’t have a wife and he was sick. Ohhh.” The auto-cue instructed him to deliver with a tone of sarcasm and he did a half-decent job. “Well John, thank you for your input, but it’s time for the verdict!” 
The studio audience, now more accurately described as a mob, anxious to deal out some justice, raised their hands, waiting for the signal to bash to guilty button. Their faces taking on manic, wide eyed, Nuremburg-esque masks.
“We all know how to vote by now, but for those who may be tuning in for the first time, here are the rules. Each home viewer has one vote and can cast it on the link, by communicator or, for those with holoscreens, grab the red ball on the top right and follow the prompts. The lucky members of the audience have 5,000 votes each. Please vote now. We will accept votes for the next five minutes.”
Votes poured in from every part of the Dominion, with zero votes for not guilty. No-one cared that he had an alibi or no wife to avenge, he was guilty of being working class. He touched the unclean earth with his bare hands. He was sickening example of what was wrong with the world and had to be put down. The victims of the night club bombing would have their justice.
“The votes are in my friends.” Shakti announced and the crowd became hushed with anticipation. “We have received 2.17 million votes. I think there might be some people out there voting more than once, but that’s okay. Vote early, vote often we say.” The crowd laughed heartily. They loved autocue jokes. “2.17 million votes for guilty, zero not guilty.” The autocue told him to be silent and there was silence for the longest time. “The people have spoken, Mr Hammersmith. You have been found guilty.”
The crowd went nuts. Jumping up and down in their little places.
“Mr Hammersmith and will now be sentenced. We only have two sentences available on this show, Death or Pardon. So what will it be people? Death or Pardon? We will be taking votes for the next five minutes” Shakti stared down the camera, sparkling a fake on-camera grin as the shot faded and advertisements took his place.
“Thanks for those important messages from our sponsors.” Shakti parroted on his return to the screen. “Remember for all your family planning needs, contact Pattinson’s Mobile Abortions. They come to you and take your worries away. Just call 1800-2229-4663. That’s 1800 BABY-GONE.” 
The crowd clapped and screamed loudly, the studio taking on a carnival atmosphere. “Wow! Is this a great crowd or what?” They yelled and clapped louder. “Let’s see what punishment has been meted out tonight. There are no votes for a pardon so Mr Hammersmith you have been sentenced to death. Now the only matter to decide is the manner of your execution. Bring out the wheel of death.” 
“Wheel of Death, Wheel of Death” The audience was chanting in full voice.
A large Chocolate Wheel was brought out by a pretty young girl in a cliched, but elaborate head-dress and swimsuit. “Now as you can see there are 10 possible methods of execution. Let’s see what they are tonight. Number 1, Boiling in oil. Number 2, Trebuchet Slap. Number 3, Clubbed to death by Dwarves, a crowd favourite. Number 4, Shark Tank. Number 5, One Thousand cuts. Number 6, Acid Injection to the Brain. Number 7, Snake Pit. Number 8, Cesspool Drowning. Number 9, Packet of Peanuts. Ooh, allergic are we? Number 10, Helium Chamber. What do you think of those options?”
“Oh, someone help me! I haven’t done a thing.” He was shaking uncontrollable and spit-froth had appeared at the corners of his mouth.
“Well, C’mon. Spin the Wheel!”
“Spin the wheel, spin the wheel, spin the wheel.” They were now stomping there feet in a slow cadence. 
The shivering man approached the wheel, but could not raise his hands. Two of the oiled body-builders shuffled him in position, placed his hand on the wheel and brought it down, spinning the wheel to a blur.
“Number 2. Trebuchet Slap!” Shakti raised his hands to the crowd and they responded in wild exaltation. 
The huge curtains at the rear of the set opened to reveal a tall and sturdy wood framed siege engine, replete with hulking iron brackets, giant round head nails, ropes and pulleys. The A-frame structure sat atop a set of solid wooden wheels. A long thick beam, about 6 metres long, was held at the apex of the frame at a pivot point, about two thirds along its length. Swinging from the short end of the beam was a large counter weight and a lengthy sling and pouch hung low from the far end. The pleading and screaming commenced as the condemned man was dragged over and strapped into the payload pouch. The Giant counter-weight was winched into place and the ratchet held it firmly. A drum roll built up, drowning out the wretched pleas. The audience became silent, eyes wide, clenched fists and clammy hands were wiped across thighs, adrenalin surged raising heart rates, escalating blood pressure. Short sharp breaths were snatched from the electric air and hair at the back of the all necks stood erect. 
As the drum roll reached fever pitch the swimsuit model released the ratchet and the giant weight fell towards to floor causing the far end of the beam to pivot up quickly, the condemned screaming man was dragged up by the sling and pouch and slapped into the floor with a sickening thud. He did not die, but moaned loudly until some playful music began and 6 dwarves armed with baseball bats marched onto the stage.
“Finishing him off!” The crowd yelled in synchronicity.
The dwarves descended upon the moaning bag of shattered bones and laid into him with rage and ferocity. The audience was now pointing and laughing hysterically. After a good clubbing he was declared dead and the crowd leapt to their feet clapping. Shakti stood centre stage and soaked up the adulation as slow motion replays of the execution were replayed over and over again on giant screens behind him.
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="6820E8BF-083E-4EB9-8D11-410FE7DCB156">
            <Title>Eloise </Title>
            <Synopsis>Sarah decides to place herself up for random allocation.  She is allocated Alexander Shakti.
Eloise goes to meet Sarah and thinks there is something wrong as Sarah is way too competent and beautiful to be placed on random allocation.
Sarah decides to tell her she is pregnant and expects to be rejected, but Eloise understands and is happy that she won't have any grandchildren from her genetic-car-crash of a son.  Eloise tells her to go consummate the relationships Shakti will think he is the father.

</Synopsis>
            <Text>
Eloise
3 to 4 weeks after bombing
Sarah was home alone when the list of new allocations were released. She did not want any company for this, the lowest point of her life. In her estimation, throwing her hat in for random allocation had diminished her in every way. Consoling herself she was making the best of a bad of an impossible situation did not help. Here goes. Fingers crossed.
She waved her hand and watched as the wall screen flicked and flitted, before the image became crisp. Allocations for level 4 had not concluded and as the names, all unknown to her, flashed on the screen she felt overwhelmed by panic - all of these people are going to marry someone they do not know, all for the good of the economy. Oh dear, so am I. She hoped that her allocation partner, whoever he may be, was going through the same hell. He was not. The randomness and integrity of this allocation had been completely corrupted by a vicious algorithm, smeared with the fingerprints of a well paid computer hacker. 
Then her name appeared on the screen along side of the name of her betrothed, Alexander Shakti L.5. She was motionless for a very long time and then laughed, then cried and laughed again. She would not have imagined her situation could get any worse. It now infinitely worse. 
Her holo-phone rang. She did not answer it then or for the rest of the day. Messages piled up in her in-box. When darkness arrived there was a knock at the front door. A knock? She thought, how did anyone get close to my front door without being trounced by security? Why wouldn’t they use the doorbell? With curiosity taking control, she finally uncoiled from the foetal position and hobbled on numb legs to the door. The one-way view panel showed a slender looking woman, in her late 50’s, her thin lips, painted red, pursed closely together. She was sensibly dressed in a subtle yellow dress suit with matching shoes and handbag. 
As Sarah opened the door, the woman stepped back, allowing a large man standing at the side of the doorway, to push his way in. Sarah was knocked backwards. Still upright she stood her ground.
“Don’t try it, little lady.” The man warned.
“That’s odd. I was about to issue you with the same warning, large oaf.” Sarah shot back, staring him down. He laughed and moved towards her. She was too quick and was twisting his arm behind his back in a flash. “Don’t fight it.” The giant man’s ego refused to obey and he tried to throw himself backwards to break free. Sarah raised his arm and loudly snapped his humerus under the weight of his own body. He screamed and screamed as Sarah threw him to the door. All the while, the lady calmly sat on the lounge, straightening out the hem of her skirt.
“Now, let us talk about you.” Sarah stood squarely in front of the odd lady.
“Well, aren’t you just a firebrand.” The woman smiled smugly. “I am Eloise Shakti, your future mother-in-contract.”
“Was the gorilla necessary?” Sarah was unimpressed with the mother-in-contract so far.
“Forgive me for Benjamin, my body guard. He sometimes gets enthusiastic.” Eloise smiled then her face turned to ash. “Sarah Jacobson, Ph.D. Level 5, Cyber Security Manager, currently under contract with the Police - you poor thing. You earn more than my boy, but unlike him, you have real career prospects.”
“Yes, I can’t wait to get back to profiteering.” She figured she had better sound sufficiently capitalist for her highly regarded mum-to-be.
“Indeed. Alexander said you were a lovely creature, but that does not even begin to describe you, does it? I did not expect you would be so stunning. And you can handle yourself, too, besting Benjamin so easily. So my question is, with all your obvious physical attributes, education and intellectual firepower, what are you doing putting yourself up for random allocation?” It would take a very decent argument to satisfy Eloise that there was nothing suspicious going on here.
“I am not sure I know what you mean. I am 27 and want some certainty in my life.” Sarah hoped that would work, but Eloise tittered quietly, showing her perfect teeth, for just a few seconds before she cut it short and allowed her calm dignity to return. 
“You will have to do better than that, my dear. I am not a person to meddle with.” The name of Eloise knew she was the subject of fear and disdain. It pleased her greatly. 
Sarah brain activity trebled. Neurones fired up as she searched the darkest reaches of her mind to come up with a plausible story. She knew that mother Shakti was here to vet her, but after many fictitious scenarios formed in her head, she concluded that she did not care if she was rejected by the Shakti family. In fact, she would be very pleased if they decided to call it off. “I am pregnant.”
“That makes perfect sense dear. Thank you for the truth. It is a rare commodity”
“I expect you will be calling off the allocation.” Sarah was not sad, even if this was the first time she had ever been rejected.
“Not on your life, my dear. I have been waiting years to pass him off to a wife or a husband, I honestly did not care which. He’s your problem now, my girl. I just wanted an answer to the question of why someone like you would accept someone like my son. I have my answer. Thank you, dear.”
“Should I tell him about the pregnancy?”
“Only to tell him that he is the father. You will have to consummate the relationship quickly for him to think it is his. I will accept the child as my grandchild, happy in the knowledge that whatever caused the genetic car-crash that is my Alexander, it will not happen to this child.” She stood and walked to the door. “Thank you my dear. I don’t think there is any reason to see you before the ceremony, do you?”
“Unless you and your family want to get to know me.” Sarah thought they might.
“See? No reason at all. See you at the ceremony.” And she was gone.
Sarah checked her in-box and deleted all the mail titled “SHAKTI? WTF?” She noted a number of messages from Eloise Shakti’s son and decided to take her advice. She must consummate the relationship quickly. It seemed very contrived and nothing like what she thought her first time would be like. I did not think I would be giving up my virginity to such an idiot, but all girls give it up for idiots don’t they? When she had imagined having sex for the first time, it would be with a man tall, strong, handsome, funny, intelligent, gentle and kind. Shakti ticked none of the boxes, but she nevertheless contacted him and invited herself to his apartment. 
She returned from his apartment later that evening feeling dirty, even though she had not actually engaged in sex with Shaki. She had managed to convinced convinced him that he had penetrated her, but in reality she had finished him off with a grandiose wank while riding reverse cowgirl. She also managed, she hoped, not to pass on the virus she carried. The last thing the world needed to see was a Super-Shakti. Perfection in idiocy. 
Her first shower took two hours, but she was still unclean and opted for a sauna and then took some steam before having one more shower. As the water trickled down the drain, she was reminded of the shower in the public facility. She enjoyed herself that night, even if the facilities had been disgusting. It seemed an age ago, so much water having passed under the bridge, but it had been less than two months.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="B9E1D0E3-BE47-487F-B5D1-194E34605B55">
