        <Document ID="E12AE1FA-470C-4C94-9A8F-D1BAA2BE8567">
            <Title>Shakti's Chance to shine</Title>
            <Text>Shakti’s Chance to Shine
Mid Morning Day After Bombing - Year zero
The CEO immediately called the Commissioner. “Commissioner, I think we have another investigation for Shakti to head up. It is right up his alley.”
“Okay, I’ll bite. What have you got for him?” replied a bemused Commissioner. 
“The Director of the Pre-emptive Reciprocity and Proactive Euthanasia has been murdered.”
“And you want Shakti to head the inquiry? With the greatest of respect sir. Are you fucking crazy? I am pretty sure we proved he can’t handle homicide, just yesterday.”
“Mr Commissioner, Lieutenant Shakti has the precise skill set I require for this investigation.”
“No skills whatsoever.”
“Indeed.”
“You want another debacle?”
“Now you are getting it. Can you arrange that for me?” The CEO asked.
“Consider it done.” The Commissioner was now greatly amused. Whatever the CEO was up to it was probably better that he did not know. </Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="95D0F33C-D9D0-4733-8719-34A203985961">
            <Title>Agent Jones</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="B408448D-C127-4E5B-9758-9BAC36063F97">
            <Title>Coughing Physiology</Title>
            <Text>

Cough is comprised of three phases (inspiratory, compressive and expiratory) and serves as a vital defensive mechanism for lung health. It prevents pulmonary aspiration, promotes ciliary activity and clears airway debris. The importance of an intact cough mechanism is reflected in the occurrence of pulmonary problems when cough is inefficient. Cough efficiency is dependent on physical/mechanical aspects (respiratory muscles, mucus, airway calibre and larynx) and integrity of the neurophysiological pathway of cough. The understanding of the latter has progressed significantly (albeit mostly in animals) with the discovery of vanniloid receptors (and subtypes) and, more recently, by the characterisation of distinct cough receptors. However, the relative contributions of previously described airway afferents/receptors to cough are still disputed. Plasticity of the peripheral and central afferent pathways in cough has recently been shown to be important in pathological states associated with increased cough. To date, little is known of the developmental aspects of cough.

</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="3B8AB0B5-3A90-402F-A3F2-2A040182BFFD">
            <Title>Ajent questions Dick and Rick</Title>
            <Text>Agent Jones traced the ambulance, and fumed with brick-walled impatience when he discovered the orderlies gave fake names: Dick van Dyke and Rick von Reich. Why would they bother? There is nothing beyond the reach of PAPER. Agent Jones turned up their true identities in seconds.
That’s when Agent Jones slipped into an anger that moved, not by storms clouds or jagged waves, but by the deep, inky power of the ocean. He quietly drove his fingernails into his thighs, and swore he wouldn’t swear. He made his breath even.
Dick and Rick were PAPER agents. Paranoia boiled Agent Jones’ thoughts. The company was running a side-operation on him.
Or, maybe, not. Agent Jones checked the agents’ contract records and remuneration history: they were part-timers. The meat wagon was a side-gig for them.
This made Agent Jones still angrier.

Guns are a first resort. Agent Jones corralled Rick and Dick at muzzle-point and took them to a stony, truncated-pyramid of a building known only as Truth. Inside, a receptionist allocated Agent Jones the required suite, where Truth clinicians dressed Dick and Rick in experience sheaths—tight,  translucent head-to-toe suits. Rick and Dick panicked (as they always do) and pulled at the thin, yet durable, film covering their faces, until clinicians restrained them on scratchy, plasti-carbon-fibre chairs Agent Jones had demanded for the occasion. Normally, they strapped Truth candidates into medical couches designed for extended use, but Agent Jones preferred these, his favorite interrogation chairs. Blood and tissue easily hosed off, facilitating a speedy turnover of informants during busy times. Agent Jones missed the old times of belting the crap out of a target, hoping confession would come none too soon. Sometimes, Agent Jones would continue anyway. For special occasions, he still carried a chef’s roll, filled with gleaming stainless steel knives, saws, drills, and other devices with cutting wheels and pointy bits.
Agent Jones stood behind the clinician sitting at the console, while a pair of others connected wires to the experience sheaths. The console clinician started the baseline diagnostics, and watched a pair of flat screens while Rick and Dick giggled and ouched as the sheaths delivered ever more fine-tuned stimuli. Eventually, Rick and Dick started moaning. Their sheaths stretched and accommodated their tumescence.
The console clinician flicked a switch. “They’re ready for experience,” she told Agent Jones.
“Cut off the pinkies.”
“What?” The clinician clamped her dropping jaw shut and gave Agent Jones large, defiant eyes.
“Cut off the pinkies.”
“But they’re compliant,” the clinician objected.
Agent Jones glared.
The clinician swallowed. “Cutting off subjects’ fourth fingers,” she confirmed.
Rick and Dick howled in gasping terror.
“Please!” Dick said. “We said we’d cooperate. Just tell us what you want us to say.”
“Tell me about Emil Kalheri,” Agent Jones said.
“Who?”
“Cut off the thumbs.”
“Special Agent,” the clinician began.
“Cut off the thumbs.”
The clinician shrank in her seat. “Cutting off subjects’ thumbs.” 
Dick and Rick keened and bucked against their restraints. Rick’s chair tipped over. Clinicians went to right it; Agent Jones waved them away. He looked upon the informants with disgust. They were already beyond useful. “Cut the stimuli.”
Relieved, the clinician complied. Rick and Dick whimpered in the afterglow of their pain.
“I ask about your delivery to Sunny Contemplations Funeral Home.”
“We make many—”
“Sure,” Dick interrupted Rick. “Whatever you want.”
“This suit.” Agent Jones pulled Emil’s suit coat from a bag and held it up like washing on a line.
“Yes! Yes!” Rick said, with a tone of hope he might escape this after all. “I remember the suit.”
“The guy was just lying there, dead in the street,” Dick added.
Agent Jones contemplated, which always hard work. “You stole from the body.”
“No! We’d never rob the dead.”
“Burn the cocks,” Agent Jones instructed the clinician.
The clinician recoiled, her hands jerked away from the console.
“No! Please! We’re telling the truth!”
“But I think I know who could have,” Rick offered. “That’s good, right? No need for roast cock-a-doodle?” He giggled like a maniac.
“Go on,” Agent Jones said, with some disappointment.
“There was this guy, you know? A cleaner.”
A cleaner? Agent Jones ground his teeth and exhaled. There was a cleaner at Travis Teh’s office, and a cleaner at the scene of Emil Kalheri’s death?
“We didn’t see him at first,” Dick rambled. “He was hiding behind the cleaning cart, kneeling over the body.”
“Now I think about it, he was probably rummaging through the dead man’s clothes.”
“You apprehended this cleaner?”
“Ah, no,” Rick said, trembling. “We just told him to tax off. That’s it.”
The console clinician took to her controls. “Burning subjects’ penises,” she confirmed, disgusted at the incompetence of these inadequate Company agents.
During the extended seconds of agony screams, violent thrashing, and the fouling of sheaths, Agent Jones watched, as if from the comfort of a private box at the opera. “Cut the stimuli,” he said, once boredom loomed.
It took Rick and Dick some time to regain their breath and regulate their sobbing.
“Describe the cleaner,” Agent Jones said.
“Eurasian male, early thirties, 1.8 tall, dark hair. Dressed in a pair of busted-arse overalls.”
“Describe the overalls.”
“Just normal overalls, dirty white, stuff in pockets, logo over the breast pocket.”
“Describe the logo.”
“I can’t remember. Jack’s cleaning? Maybe.”
Agent Jones charged and threw over the second chair. “Let me assist your memory.” He took a large needle as thick as a pencil from his pocket, and inserted it into the webbing of Dick’s fingers. Dick bucked, but Agent Jones held his hand firm while he twiddled the needle among sinews and blood vessels. 
Dick screamed with primeval pain and anger. 
“Do you remember now?”
Dick jerked and convulsed.
“How about now, tax-face? Huh? Put my contract on the line will you?” He stood and kicked Dick in the head. He turned on Rick.
“Please,” Rick cried. “Let me think! Please, just let me think! Jack … Jack’s Crack … Jack’s Cracker Cleaning? No, No. Cracker Jack Cleaning. That’s it: Cracker Jack Cleaning.”</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="D1EC0438-8DC9-4FB2-80D3-33FD32940B57">
            <Title>Suicide Homicide</Title>
            <Synopsis>Shakti is dreaming about his "dream woman" when he is contacted by the Police Commissioner - The dream woman is Sarah whom he has never met.  There is a description of a pristine beach - I like the first paragraph.  Also there is insight into his relationship with his mother Eloise.  A bit more background on Shakti.

Shakti is given the job of investigating the attack upon the water infrastructure but doesn't know what to do and calls in his friend Cracker. Other cops will not assist Shakti and he has to rely on Cracker.  There is some more background - this could be dumped, but I do like the backstory stuff.
</Synopsis>
            <Text>Suicide Homicide
2 hours after bombing? Place in a big crush ?
Shakti was deep in slumber. Visions of his blonde dream-woman filled his head, not with erotic montages or flashes of flesh, but unfamiliar feelings of warmth, safety and belonging. They were strolling aimlessly, it seemed, along a broad uncontaminated beach, holding hands. He had never seen anything quite as splendid and even his unconscious mind found difficulty in comprehending the beauty of its own creation. The white sand was uncomfortably hot and so dry, it squeaked under the pressure of each step, the tiny polished crystals clinging steadfastly to their feet, seeking moisture and a free ride to the cool, clean ocean. There were children splashing about in the shallows and floating around on strange blow-up devices, all laughing and playing in the bright sun with no U.V. suits in sight. The sky was an unblemished deep azure and the sun, a brilliant bright orb raining down a deluge of light and warmth. Shakti felt himself crying. No person alive had seen a blue sky or felt sunlight directly on their skin - the layer bludgeoned the life out of all but the most persistent photons and if you lived above the layer, the Swiss-cheese ozone hardly filtered out anything. Shakti looked at their intertwined hands, drew deep a lung-full of sweet sea air and looked in her face, but dream woman was gone. 
“Mother, what are you doing here?” He cried incredulously, desperately trying to disengage his hand from hers, but could not as he was now handcuffed to her. 
“Come with me, now!” Said Eloise, dragging him away from the water. “You’re going to college to become an accountant. Let’s go.” In the presence of his mother, Shakti was now a small boy and she was far too strong for him to repel. He could only repeatedly whimper, “No, mother, no” as she dragged him away from the beauty and light. He was saved when the Vid-phone burst into life. 
“Leave me alone, mother.” Shakti said aloud.
“It’s not your mother, moron,” screamed the Commissioner. “What are you doing there?”
“I had to get some sleep before tonight’s show, Sir.” He was now slightly more conscious.
“Did I not send you to investigate a murder?”
“Mr. Lee? That was suicide. He shot himself and left a dreadful mess. It’s all cleaned up now.”
“Only you could manage to conduct a 20 minute homicide inquiry.”
“Thank you, sir.”
“That was not a compliment, you imbecile. Who processed the crime-scene? Where are the samples, holographs, ballistics? Where?” The commissioner’s face had gone from red to blue and was now purple. Steam was shooting out both ears of the livid boss as he sucked in air as loudly and frequently as a charging bull. “All you had to do was tick the boxes.”
“No samples were taken, sir.”
“You are a fucking idiot.”
“So I have been told.” 
“Don’t be smart with me, Lieutenant. This is a stuff-up of monumental proportions. If you were anyone else, your head would roll. You can’t cut corners because you want to look good on TV. You wanted homicide experience and I gave it to you. You didn’t even have to solve it, shit, there are a hundred others out there just like it, all unsolved.”
“I’m sorry sir, it looked like a suicide.” 
“We will just have to let this slide and hope that there are no questions.”
“Thank you, sir.”
“You won’t be thanking me after this, Alex. There has been a terrorist bombing in Erskintown, followed by an attack on the water infrastructure. No one knows how, but the terrorists let off the bomb and then managed to redirect the water away from the fire.”
“A state security investigation, thank you.” Shakti was smiling and could see the award ceremony already.
“No. You are on clean-up duty. Go to Hardy Tower 97, and find out what went on down there. The computer system seems to be up to specs now, but there is a huge hole in data storage. For fuck’s sake – get those engineers to pull the finger out and get the water flowing in the right direction. Half the city is flooded and level fives are drinking recyc.”
“Level fives drinking recyc? This is serious.” He said grimly and then leapt into life and soon was in his designer finery and out the door. How am I going to do this? Fuck, fuck, fuck!!
He knew that he was going to need help, but could not for ask help from his police colleagues because there was no way they would give it to him. Competent investigators, the ones who had actually bothered to learn the craft, were sick to death of propping up idiots who could not lead an investigation to save their lives, but could blunder and fib their way through a job interview. A disturbing majority seemed entirely disinterested in work, but were eternally obsessed with jagging that next promotion. They were ceaselessly busy, working on their next application for advancement and wholly unavailable for getting their hands dirty with something as trivial as work – there were just so many promotions to go for. Confidence in themselves was only matched by their inability to do the job, but ridiculous egos had to be indulged and unlikely ambitions realised. It was not unknown for applicants to use a bit of poetic licence in describing the feats and achievements of their paltry careers, but these perjurers relied on a strategy of feeding selection panels with bald faced lies, peppered with bullshit and smothered in a rich, creamy layer of management-speak. Once they struck upon the right formula, they could cynically tweak the bullshit/jargon ratio and any job was reasonably within their grasp. The top brass would never allow the system to be brought into question, after all, that’s how they got their jobs.
To his peers, Shakti was no better than those self-aggrandizing professional job applicants. He had the effrontery to jump from Constable to Lieutenant, a rank that previously did not exist, on the flimsy premise that he his was level 5 and it was unfitting for social inferiors to command him. No-one was really sure where he fitted into the rank structure and all were mightily pissed off that a rank would be created just for him. 
His link-show, Random Camera, was considered a joke and a complete embarrassment to real cops, but it was popular with elite management level citizens and their pudgy, pallid families. The judiciary squawked themselves horse in opposition of a two tiered justice system, but when the revenue began to flow, corporate masters pushed judges concerns aside. Managers loved watching the dirty, the uneducated, the lazy and the poor get what was coming to them. Some genuinely wondered why they just couldn’t kill them all off. For reasons only known to the wealthy, they really hated the poor and Shakti had, accidentally, tapped into this sentiment. This was the true reason for his miraculous jump in the ranks and if the entire department thought it was just another example of insufferable cronyism, all the better.
Shakti only knew one person he could turn to for help. Although they had never, could never, socialise in any meaningful way, Shakti thought of Cracker as a friend. He had never let him down and hardly stolen anything from him which were two things he could not say about any of his colleagues. He had automatically assumed the cops did not steal, but was shocked to learn very early in his career that they knocked-off anything not nailed down. He had expected the odd bad-egg would skim a little money off crooks, but he had not expected that they would so readily steal from each other. Before he contacted Cracker he thought he had better arrange some transport and get some idea of what had been dumped in his lap.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="5BC904AB-3C23-42E9-A7BB-98B71A26F262">
            <Title>Elvis</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="F86CD1B2-6188-4B9B-8891-C0036019F181">
            <Title>Majority Report</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="375BDC9F-80C5-43D1-A0E1-218A600AC671">
            <Title>Cracker meets Emil</Title>
            <Text>
Cracker bunted his cart out the service doors on the lower ground level of the Tower of Sarah, morosely and fatalistically whistling a tune that, in countless other universes, would be known as a song called Puppet on a String. In a spooky coincidence that hinted at the true nature of the multiverse, Cracker sang a line, You can do most anything with me.
Given enough free time, he may well have channelled the whole song, but his embittered thoughts surrendered to cursing the Airborne whose engineered lack of care created the oppressively constant cloud over his head, which separated, both physically and symbolically, the privileged from the under-privileged, the entitled from the mis-titled.
One day, he mused, there’ll be a revolution. An army of champion troubadours—or was it matadors or pompadours?—will rise up and … what? Cracker could never decide, as the voltage of his dim imagination fizzled out.
Cracker grunted as he pushed the skidding cart up the ramp slippery with a film of condensed atmospheric chemicals. Later, he would have to clean the cart wheels and axels from the sticky residue that gummed up everything below the Layer. The weather had been aggressive all week; his van would need its third wash to slow down it worsening corrosion.
At ground level, he looked up, as if he possessed cloud-piercing vision, as if he knew which windows on which floor were Sarah’s office, as if she was at those windows, basking in a halo of warm, glorious sunlight, looking beneficently down upon him. About halfway to where he parked his van, at the last point before the Tower of Sarah would be obscured by stunted, Cloudborne buildings, Cracker turned and looked forlornly for the last time in her direction.
He didn’t see the body on the pitted cement. His cart jammed into the poor sod, lurched out of his hands, and threatened to topple. Cracker snatched at the cart, and it fell the other way. He overbalanced and toppled onto the body. Embarrassed, he looked furtively about. He needn’t have bothered; passers-by, with noses up and eyes straight, strode elliptical paths around the scene of death and clumsiness, and dispersed into the sabulous morning haze. 
While no one would credit him with intelligence, Cracker possessed a singular, acute street-urchin cunning. Stitching together the weave of simple observations, he would always spot the game, whereas the more educated and intellectually-blessed would have loftier things on their presumptive minds, and remain oblivious to things bleedingly obvious to Cracker.
For instance, this man wore an expensive, new off-black suit (Cracker would never know the colour was midnight blue, being unfamiliar with the colour of a crisp night sky above the Layer), yet the man’s hands were calloused, his fingers oddly bent, his nails filthy. Figuring there could be out-of-place valuables on the dead man’s person, and with the brazen face of a man living a largely invisible life, Cracker chiselled through the man’s pockets, shoes, and underwear.
“Hey, you!” A voice of petty authority came from behind Cracker’s cart.
Cracker rose and patted down his overalls. Looming down on him were a Coroner’s meat-wagon driver and his body-snatching off-sider. 
“I was just about to call you guys,” Cracker said with a sycophantic tone. “I think he’s been dead a while. He’s cold.”
“And how is it you know he’s cold?” the body-snatcher asked.
“I, ah … I’m sorry I didn’t see him! I … I ran over him, with my cart.”
“Oh, you have a heat-sensing cart now, do yer?”
“No, I fell over—I fell on him. I’m sorry.”
“Yeah, yeah,” the Coroner guys said. “Tax off, pal.”
Cracker taxed off, pushing his cart awkwardly with one hand while holding his overalls pocket tight, lest his loot spill out. He made for the safety of his van, illegally parked a few blocks more in a dead-end alley.
Once behind the wheel, Cracker dug into his pocket and fished out the meagre spoils: among them was what looked like a coin-sized chad of thick, grainy, carbon-coloured leather. In Cracker’s fingers, the disc was stiffly flexible, and sprang slovenly back into shape when he stopped bending it. For a moment, he thought he saw it flash pink. He waited to catch it again, but it stayed inert. He looked out the windshield, up towards the sky. The Layer was always playing tricks with the light.
He tossed the trinket on the passenger-side floor and coaxed the van’s motor into life.</Text>
            <Notes>Operational support guys, the underlings of the intelligence community, were always working on covert ops and difficult to locate. 

midnight blue suit</Notes>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="F8468CAE-06A6-499E-8ED9-6E37E82F77C3">
            <Title> A Good Time to Patch up</Title>
            <Synopsis>DNA examination of the sample (yes the morgue is well resourced) shows that the child is a clone of a person named Richard Dawkins.
She also notes added DNA sequences (from the disc)
They conclude the Sages are behind it.
Dr Logan explains there are black market cloning devices.</Synopsis>
            <Text>A Good Time to Patch Up
8 to 10 days after bombing
</Text>
