        <Document ID="EC45206A-D435-4EF4-8551-708CD4117CEB">
            <Title>Cloning the Arts</Title>
            <Synopsis>Background on the criminal geneticist, Linus Grawling (name should be changed) who hated modern music and went off gathering DNA samples from all of the old time singers.  He stumbled across an old time singer only named Elvis whose DNA is perfect for replicating.  You later find out he had to leave the country because of all the shocking cloning experiments he had undertaken.  </Synopsis>
            <Text>
Cloning the Arts
3 hours after Bombing &amp; Flashback to 20 or so years BTD
After many centuries harvesting music from a mere 8 notes, musicians finally and simply ran out of ideas. Everything musical had been thought of before. Either that or musical talent fell away as the centuries passed. Contemporary “music”, for want of a better term, was just awful. Talking in the stead of singing, rhythm but no melody or was it melody with no rhythm – either way, it was unrecognisable as music. It was better described as angry, rarely rhyming poetry set to shitty tunes ripped off some old TV show. Real music fans did not like it. They did not like it at all. 
The infamous geneticist and music devotee, Dr. Linus Grawling, despised the criminal wailings of allegedly talented musicians and decided action, radical action, must be taken. After years of questionable and mostly criminal research and after decades of scouring the globe, Grawling gathered together the DNA of all the iconic musicians, alive or dead, that he could reasonably lay his hands on. He raided mausoleums, stole from crypts, robbed graves and mantelpieces for ash-filled urns. He then began cloning and cloning and cloning. After the obligatory several hundred unspeakable failures and numerous run-ins with the law, he found one genetic code that would readily lend itself to petri-dish replication. He has stumbled across the DNA during his stint at the University of Memphis, while on the run from the PTD. It had belonged to a famous local singer of antiquity, a relic all the way from the mid-20th century, but records relating to this particular singer were not intact and it was, therefore, impossible to know exactly who he was other that than his first name, Elvis. 
The cloned Elvises, or Elvi as they were collectively known, were a huge hit. With just a little training they could sing and dance like no one else and if you kept them on a restrictive diet, they didn’t even get fat. Grawling had unearthed a rich vein of talented genes that could not be suppressed, however, the ease at which one could clone an Elvis was only matched by the difficulties met in controlling the powerful hard-wired urges every Elvis held within. They were all genius musicians and singers, but could not hold back their unyoked enthusiasm for popular memes, bizarre fashions and guns. For reasons Grawling could never uncover, they were especially susceptible to spiritual manipulation which he worked hard, over many years, to remove from the genome, but with limited success.
To fulfill their potential as entertainers the Elvi had to be quarantined from the influence of spirituality and religion, but fortuitously, in the Primary Trade Dominion shielding the Elvi from religious icons and fallacious spiritual memes wasn’t not too difficult. Religion having been squeezed out of the market decades prior. Even so, Elvi ownership came with a responsibility to ensure your Elvis or Elvises remained untouched and ignorant all things godly or spiritual, unless you wanted to void the manufacturer’s warranty.
These guys were meant to entertain and entertain they did. Every shitty bar, discotheque and nightclub had at least one Elvis, although most had many more and some had nothing but Elvis employees. They all had an overwhelming urge to show off wild karate moves while wearing bejeweled suits and flared trousers. They were consummate performers and would happily sing for supper and dance for dessert. </Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="B3F49F6F-B4BC-4A67-B7A5-D7264C8D0141">
            <Title>Vegas Show-1</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="0BFCA8EB-CA47-47B4-A447-909705EA6E55">
            <Title>Random Camera</Title>
            <Text>
Random Camera
9 months BTD (48 hrs after bombing)
Shakti was sucking in big breaths, his nerves were always shot just before going to air and on this occasion he knew the show could really go off the rails. The sponsors would be none too pleased if ratings fell again. He had been in the edit-suite with one of his minions, tidying up the profile on one of the human offerings for the evening show. There was not much evidence on this guy, he might have said he bombed the nightclub, but it could have been something else, the microphone did not quite pick it up. The magic of the post-production, however, allowed Shakti to clear up the ambiguity by dubbing in some really damning evidence. 
Shakti was not terribly happy with embellishing the evidence to ensure a conviction, but he was under real pressure lately to get the viewers back above 2 million. Below that mark the revenues were still reasonable, but once you crossed the 2 million barrier, the sponsors throw money at you. Shakti found himself in a compromising position for the second time in as many days. Cracker had really helped him out at the public hygiene unit, but he was concerned about when and in what form he would have to pay back the favour. He could handle forking out some cash, in fact, that was the preferred option, but from the sound of it, Cracker will require something entirely different and no doubt, unethical. 
“Good evening my friends. Welcome to a very special and solemn episode of Random Camera. We were all shocked to our core by the cowardly bombing of the Stick-it-Inn and we remain frustrated by the lack of action by the authorities. I, myself, made the only arrest on the night of the bombing, but he died before interrogation.” He paused, waiting for the canned applause to fire up the studio audience. “Tonight, right here on Random Camera, we will be bringing the first live offender to justice.” He raised his arms high above his head and was met with a wall of excited acclamation. “He a devious, level one scoundrel. A thief, a philanderer and a tradesman. Ladies and Gentlemen of the Management Class, please revile John Hammersmith.”
A poor emaciated looking sod was dragged out by two giant, well lubricated body builder types, who with contrived pomp and ceremony dumped the poor bastard on the studio floor, at the feet of the Shakti. 
“You have the wrong man. I haven’t done anything.” His pathetic petition only caused the crown to laugh loudly, a few booing the doomed prisoner. 
Shakti smugly grinned directly into the camera, “That’s what they all say.” The crowd laughed even louder. “We shall see. Roll the file.”
The giant screen behind Shakti burst into life. The surveillance footage was grainy, deliberately so, to give Mr Hammersmith an air of suspicion. It followed him on his daily journey, digging trenches and gluing pipes together, laughing mindlessly with his dim-witted colleagues, each grab ending with a close-up of John’s evil face. At bars and in homes everywhere, level 4’s and 5’s were laughing and gagging.
“Awe, yuck! Did you see that? He touched the dirt with his hands! Disgusting.” An audience member was heard to say as she elbowed her neighbour.
“He looks so guilty.” She replied.
The 5 minute hatchet-job reached its height in the final grab when, out of the blue, John Hammersmith was heard to say in a clear loud voice, “I bombed the Stick It Inn. I had to avenge my wife.” The crowd went wild, screaming and booing. 
“Guilty!” Was called out from the back of the crowd.
“He’s guilty. Death to the terrorist! Death, death.” The crowd followed and continued to chant, “Death, death, death…..”
The “Quiet Please” sign above the studio floor illuminated and the crowd settled quickly to a rumble of murmurs and disquiet. 
“What have you to say now, John Hammersmith?” Shakti read the autocue word for word.
“I don’t have a wife, I have never been married.” He pleaded. “I was in hospital on the night of the explosion.”
“He doesn’t have a wife and he was sick. Ohhh.” The auto-cue instructed him to deliver with a tone of sarcasm and he did a half-decent job. “Well John, thank you for your input, but it’s time for the verdict!” 
The studio audience, now more accurately described as a mob anxious to deal out some justice, raised their hands in wait for the signal to bash the guilty button. Their faces taking on manic, wide eyed, Nuremburg-esque masks.
“We all know how to vote by now, but for those who may be tuning in for the first time, here are the rules. Each home viewer has one vote and can cast it on the link, by communicator or, for those with holoscreens, grab the red ball on the top right and follow the prompts. The lucky members of the audience have 50,000 votes each. Please vote now. We will accept votes for the next five minutes.”
Votes poured in from every part of the Dominion, with zero votes for not guilty. No-one cared that he had an alibi or no wife to avenge, he was guilty of being working class. He touched the unclean earth with his bare hands. He was sickening example of what was wrong with the world and he had to be put down. The victims of the night club bombing might get justice, but the mob would have blood.
“The votes are in my friends.” Shakti announced and the crowd became hushed with anticipation. “We have received 2.17 million votes. I think there might be some people out there voting more than once, but that’s okay. Vote early, vote often we say.” The crowd laughed heartily. They loved autocue jokes. “2.17 million votes for guilty, zero not guilty.” The autocue told him to be silent and there was silence for the longest time. “The people have spoken, Mr Hammersmith. You have been found guilty.”
The crowd went nuts. Jumping up and down in their little places.
“Mr Hammersmith and will now be sentenced. We only have two sentences available on this show, Death or Pardon. So what will it be people? Death or Pardon? We will be taking votes for the next five minutes” Shakti stared down the camera, sparkling a fake on-camera grin as the shot faded and advertisements took his place.
“Thanks for those important messages from our sponsors.” Shakti parroted on his return to the screen. “Remember for all your family planning needs, contact Pattinson’s Mobile Abortions. They come to you and take your worries away. Just call 1800-2229-4663. That’s 1800 BABY-GONE.” 
The crowd clapped and screamed loudly and had now taken on a carnival atmosphere. “Wow! Is this a great crowd or what?” They yelled and clapped louder. “Let’s see what punishment has been meted out tonight. There are no votes for a pardon so Mr Hammersmith you have been sentenced to death. Now the only matter to decide is the manner of your execution. Bring out the wheel of death.” 
“Wheel of Death, Wheel of Death” The audience was chanting in full voice.
A large Chocolate Wheel was brought out by a pretty young girl in a cliched, but elaborate head-dress and swimsuit. “Now as you can see there are 10 possible methods of execution. Let’s see what they are tonight. Number 1, Boiling in oil. Number 2, Trebuchet Slap. Number 3, Clubbed to death by Dwarves. Number 4, Shark Tank. Number 5, One Thousand cuts. Number 6, Acid Injection to the Brain. Number 7, Snake Pit. Number 8, Cesspool Drowning. Number 9, Packet of Peanuts. Ooh, allergic are we? Number 10, Helium Chamber. What do you think of those options?”
“Oh, someone help me! I haven’t done a thing.” He was shaking uncontrollable and spit-froth had appeared at the corners of his mouth.
“Well, C’mon. Spin the Wheel!”
“Spin the wheel, spin the wheel, spin the wheel.” They were now stomping there feet in a slow cadence. 
The shivering man approached the wheel, but could not raise his hands. Two of the oiled body-builders shuffled him in position, placed his hand on the wheel and brought it down, spinning the wheel to a blur.
“Number 2. Trebuchet Slap!” Shakti raised his hands to the crowd and they responded in wild exaltation. 
The huge curtains at the rear of the set opened to reveal a tall and sturdy wood framed siege engine, replete with hulking iron brackets, giant round head nails, ropes and pulleys. The A-frame structure sat atop a set of solid wooden wheels. A long thick beam, about 6 metres long, was held at the apex of the frame at a pivot point, about two thirds along its length. Swinging from the short end of the beam was a large counter weight and a lengthy sling and pouch hung low from the far end. 
The pleading and screaming commenced as the condemned man was dragged over and strapped into the payload pouch. The Giant counter-weight was winched into place and the ratchet held it firmly. A drum roll built up drowned out the wretched pleas. The audience became silent, eyes wide, clenched fists and clammy hands were wiped across thighs, adrenalin surged raising heart rates, escalating blood pressure. Short sharp breaths were snatched from the electric air and hair at the back of the all necks stood erect. 
As the drum roll reached fever pitch the swimsuit model released the ratchet and the giant weight fell towards to floor causing the far end of the beam to pivot up quickly, the Condemned man was dragged up by the sling and pouch and slapped into the floor with a sickening thud. He did not die, but moaned loudly until some playful music began and 6 dwarves armed with baseball bats marched onto the stage.
“Finishing him off!” The crowd yelled in synchronicity.
The dwarves descended upon the moaning bag of shattered bones and laid into him with rage and ferocity. The audience was now pointing and laughing hysterically. After a good clubbing he was declared dead and the crowd leapt to their feet clapping. Shakti stood centre stage and soaked up the adulation as slow motion replays of the execution were replayed over and over again on giant screens behind him. 

</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="A5616EDC-A3F4-40B9-9989-42AC89DE4786">
            <Title>An afternoon at Cracker's</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="1E766E3E-928A-4D7B-869E-7E7E8D173316">
            <Title>neutrinos</Title>
            <Text>
Neutrinos! 
 

Neutrinos are everywhere. They permeate the very space all around us. They can be found throughout our galaxy, in our sun and every second tens of thousands of neutrinos are passing through your body. But there is no need to become alarmed for these tiny particles barely interact with anything. In fact, they can even pass through the entire Earth without being affected.
Neutrinos are fundamental particles that were first formed in the first second of the early universe, before even atoms could form. They are also continually being produced in the nuclear reactions of stars, like our sun, and nuclear reactions here on earth. Much is still unknown about these particle, they have an undetermined mass and travel at near the speed of light.
#
There are three types of neutrinos: electron neutrino, muon neutrino, and tau neutrino. According to the standard model ( see figure 1) there exist 12 fundamental particles. Each "flavor" of neutrino has a corresponding charged particle from which it gets its name. The Standard Model consists of three generations and each generation has two quarks a neutrino and a charged particle.The particles in the standard model are separated into two types: quarks and leptons. The quarks interact via the strong nuclear force while the leptons interact via the electromagnetic or the weak nuclear force. Neutrinos are nearly massless and have no electric charge. Therefore, unlike the other particles, they only interact via the weak nuclear force. Neutrino actually means "little neutral one." Since the weak nuclear force only acts at shot ranges, neutrinos can pass through massive objects without interacting with them. 
References:
Casper,Dave. What’s a Neutrino?. 
 
History
Because of the neutrinos' elusive behavior, their existence was not even known until 1959 even though they had been predicted back in 1931. Wofgang Pauli first predicted the neutrino in order to account for the apparent loss of energy and momentum that he observed when studying radioactive beta decays(see Figure 2).
#
Fig. 2 The neutron Decay.(Neutrinos)
He predicted that the energy was being carried off by some unknown particle. Then in 1959, Clyde Cowan and Fred Reines finally found a particle that fit the description of the proposed neutrino by studying the particles created by a nuclear power plant. By doing this they actually discovered the electron neutrino. The next big discovery was that of the muon neutron found by Leon Lederman, Mel Schwartz, and Jack Steinberger, scientists at CERN. They did this by firing a GeV proton beam through a target thus producing pions, muons, and muon neutrinos. 
The first experiment to attempt to detect electron neutrinos from the sun was conducted by a detector in the bottom of the Homestake mine in South Dakoda in 1968. However they detected only neutrinos about twice a week. It was predicted however that the detector should find about one of the 1016 solar neutrinos a day. This unexplainable lack of solar neutrinos detected became known as the Solar Neutrino Problem. It is thought that the neutrinos actually oscillate between the different "flavors" after being emitted from the sun as electron-neutrinos. Therefore they were not detecting all of the neutrinos because some had changed into muon and tau neutrinos. 
The existence of the third flavor of neutrino, the tau neutrino, was first inferred in 1978 with the discovery of the Tau particle at SLAC, the Stanford Linear Accelerator Center. They realized that the Tau particle was just a heavier version of the electron and muon and therefore should have a corresponding neutrino as well. The tau neutrino evaded detection though for many years. Firstly the Tau particle only lasts for about 300 fs, making them difficult to track and therefore making it difficult to track their corresponding neutrino. Secondly, tau neutrinos are incredibly rare. However, in 2000 the scientists at CERN on the DONUT detector were finally able observe a tau neutrino.
 
References:
Neutrinos. 
Verkindt,Didier. History of the Neutrino.
 
Where Neutrinos Come From and Why They Are So Cool 
A star implodes in a cataclysmic bust of energy, a scientist measures the small energy discrepancies of radioactive decay, the sun constantly baths us with warmth and showers us with particles, all around us the universe is saturated with the remnants of the Big Bang, and in all of these things there is one thing in common: neutrinos. Although they are hard to detect, these little particles can tell us about everything from the birth of the universe to the nuclear reactions that power our cities. 
Supernova
On February 23 1987 detectors deep underground that where designed to detect proton decay suddenly detected a huge number of neutrinos (8 in 5 seconds). Scientist where perplexed by this influx in neutrinos at first until on February 24 a grad student named Ian Shelton announced his observation of a supernova in the Large Magellanic Cloud. This was a core collapse supernova. When the core of a massive star collapses, it crushes the protons and electrons together and neutrinos form.
p + e → n + ν. 
The neutrinos pass straight through the collapsing star before the explosion takes place. This is why the neutrinos where detected before the supernova was visibly observed. When the neutrinos leave, they also take energy away from the star and the star continues to collapse and rebounds out in an explosion that can outshine the brightness of the entire galaxy. Neutrinos are very important to the study of supernovas because they provide an early warning signal and allow scientists to be looking in the right direction before the supernova even takes place.
The Sun
Neutrinos are also created in the nuclear reactions that power the core of stars like our sun. Neutrinos are formed in the proton- proton chain. 
p + p → deuteron + positron + neutrino, 
where the deuteron is the nucleus of deuterium. In the sun, 4 hydrogens are being fused into Helium by means of the proton-proton chain. Neutrinos are important because they allow scientists to peek into the interior of the sun and learn about the processes there. All other information about the sun is from electromagnetic radiation that has to pass through the many layers of the sun interacting and changing along the way before traveling through space to us. This whole process can take up 105 to 106 years. However, the neutrinos pass cleanly through the sun in a few seconds without interacting and take a mere 8 minutes to travel from the core where they are created to us. 
The Big Bang
The greatest source of neutrinos happened some 15 billion years ago. The neutrino was first created 10-4 seconds after the big bang. Then at only 1 second after the big bang the universe became transparent to the neutrino allowing them to travel freely through space. At this time the universe had a temperature of about 3*1010. Since the time of the big bang the universe expanded and cooled and continues to expand to this day. There are about 330 million of these neutrinos per m3; however, these neutrinos have very low energy. They form a cosmic background radiation that is only 2.73 degrees Kelvin today. By studying these neutrinos scientists are able to learn about the universe when it was forming.
References:
Chaisson, McMillan. Astronomy Today. Upper Saddle River, New Jersey: Pearson Education, Inc., 2005.
 
The Future of Neutrino Research
Scientists are constantly coming up with new and ingenious ways to study neutrinos from space. Neutrino telescopes like Super-Kamiokande in Japan use huge vats of water to detect neutrinos (see Figure 3).
#
Fig. 3 The Super-Kamiokande neutrino telescope (Casper,Dave)
The inside of the tank is lined with 11,146 photo-multiplier tubes that detect Cherenkov light. Cherenkov light is emitted by the neutrinos as they pass through the water at near the speed of light.Therefore the detector detects the effect of the neutrinos interacting with the water and not the neutrinos themselves. Telescopes like the Super-Kamiokande are deep underground in order to avoid detecting other particles from cosmic rays. The Homestake telescope uses Chlorine 37 coming from Argon and the GALLEX telescope uses Germanium 71 coming from Gallium as the medium to detect neutrinos instead of water. 
Currently, scientists are building a better neutrino telescope by using the clear polar ice as a medium by which to detect the neutrinos. IceCube is a one-cubic-kilometer new neutrino telescope being built in the South Pole (see Figure 4). It will be an array of 80 strings, each string having 60 optical moduals that are desigened to detect the cherenkov light from emitted from muons, which are a byproduct of the neutrinos interacting with the ice. Scientists are building this new telescope under the South Pole because it allows them to make it incredibly large, to have a very stable place for the detectors, to keep a stable temperature, and to built it deep enough to avoid interference from cosmic rays. 
#
Fig. 4 The IceCube neutrino telescope (IceCube) 
The arctic ice also makes a good medium because it is pure, transparent, and free from radioactivity. Scientists hope that by building IceCube they will be able to learn more about far away neutrino sources like gamma ray bursts, supernova, black holes and maybe even dark matter.
References:
Casper,Dave. What’s a Neutrino?.
Welcome to IceCube.
 
How The IceCube Telescope Works
Cherenkov Light 
Return to Top
Kirsten Larson's REU Home Page </Text>
