        <Document ID="C9C35755-FC58-47F7-93F3-1EA6C7A1C500">
            <Title>Slightly Red Wedding</Title>
            <Synopsis>Sarah is having bad dreams.  It is the child inside her sending messages - only emotions and images.  She reports this to Kathy during apregnacy checkup.The child  is telling her something bad is going to happen.
Wedding Ceremony
Shakti does something stupid and accidently stabs Sarah in the throat after cutting the cake.
The pattern noticed by Sarah in photographs shown to her are important.  They later find the pattern is a bunch of messages from before the big bang.
Hayley is suspicious of Haloren Smyth - he smiled when he saw Sarah's blood.
Cracker accidentally finds the doorway to Jamison Hardy's control centre it works for him, but not Kathy when he gets her to try.  Works on DNA.
There is a conversation between Sarah and Kathy at a later stage and they work out the pattern is a "Beta Mode identifier pattern" - it is supposed to be some pattern from around the time of the big bang, but I can't find the reference anymore.)  
As soon as Sarah   sees the pattern she knows it is the same pattern she has been dreaming and the aniety immediately stops (The unborn Richard is happy she is on the right track)
</Synopsis>
            <Text>Slightly Red Wedding
3 to 4 weeks after bombing
Sarah had been enduring nightmares every night for weeks. The imagery was not grotesque or even of itself frightening, but the underlying mood of the dream was a combination of angst, horror and impending doom. When she woke she was unable to nail down any specific narrative, recalling only one image each morning, burnt into her brain. A strange pattern that was oddly familiar, but she was unable to place I, but it stuck-fast. Being possessed of a sturdy mind, she had never experienced fear on such scale and was ignorant of the overwhelming power of the sub-conscious and its ability to identify, precisely, what will frighten the shit out of you. Outwardly, it did not take long for her to return to her natural serene state of being, but as the days turned into weeks and her unconscious mind refused mercy, her resolve began to erode incrementally. She had not bothered to mention the nocturnal torture to Dr Logan on her weekly visits, dismissing it as inconsequential, but would do so at the next appointment later that day. 
“Hey Sarah, or should I say Blessed Mother?” Dr Logan, the Holy Aunt, gushed as Sarah motioned her into her palatial apartment. “Pregnancy really suits, you are radiant!”
“You look fantastic, too.” Sarah noted Dr Logan was positively glowing these days and could quite rightly be regarded as beautiful. “What is your secret?” She hoped it wasn’t the same as her own, but it was.
 “Good clean living. Ha, Ha.” She giggled again and then looked around, “I never cease to be amazed by your apartment. This place is spectacular. How many bedrooms?”
“Four, plus a games room I could make into a bedroom if the need ever arose.” Sarah was house-proud, but did not feel like doing the tour. There were bigger issues to tackle.
“Where do you want to do this, Kathy?”
“Same as last week. Massage table in in the lounge area. I love looking out that window. I want to speak to you about some data I have been receiving from the abdominal patch.” She broke out a small electronic device that projected a tiny holo-screen. “For the past several weeks I have noted that while you are sleeping, usually between the hours of 2.00am and 5.00am, you and the baby’s heart rating rises rapidly, and remains your blood pressure becomes dangerously high.” She helped Sarah on to the table and gently laid her flat and prodded the little pot-belly Sarah had begun growing. 
“Dangerously high? I am just having a few nightmares.”
“Every night for the past several weeks?” Kathy countered.
“Yes, it is really quite annoying.” Sarah placed her hand on her tiny round abdomen, still barely showing. “Just thinking about it bothers me.” She confessed, her pulse now increasing dramatically and sweat appearing at her brow.
“Yes, I can see that. Baby Richard, you don’t mind if I give him a name, do you? He’s under stress right now. You must reduce your level of anxiety.”
“Are you kidding? How can I be calm when I have been allocated to Alexander Shakti?”
“Good point. I would have thrown a rope over a beam, quick smart. I can’t imagine your anguish, but Richard’s health is paramount. Let’s take a look at him then.” She passed her hand over the med-device and a three dimensional depiction of the child appeared in the air above the imaging emitter and floated in the space before them. She spun the image, sliced it through and examined each bodily system and organ in much detail. “So far, I cannot see any abnormalities with him. Internal organs and brain structures are completely normal, maybe forming a little quicker than average, but within acceptable norms. Heart strong, nocturnal tachycardia notwithstanding.”
“Thank you. Your visits really put my mind at rest.” She did not say that her relaxed state only lasts until Dr Logan leaves. 
“So when is the allocation ceremony?”
“Tomorrow evening. I haven’t even bothered sending out invitations, I am far too embarrassed.” Sarah’s mood darkened when she put her mind to the looming allocation. “It is a terrible thing to say, but I am so glad all of my family are dead. I could not face them. You can come along and bring Cracker and Hayley if you want.” Tears began welling up in her eyes.
“You will need someone in your corner and I would be honoured to attend. Cracker is level two so he can’t go, besides, he can’t show his face in public at the moment, you know, being dead and everything. I think I can drag Hayley along, though.” Dead or alive, Cracker would not be among the guests. There was no way he could bring himself to watch Sarah’s allocation to someone else, let alone to the biggest idiot in the entire Dominion. “Where and when?”
“St Branson’s of the Immaculate Assets, 5.00pm.” The melancholy tone was unmistakable. “It’s in Bloomberg Square,you know, opposite the statue of Zuckerberg the Redeemer.”
“Have you got a dress?” Every girl wanted a nice dress for their allocation day. 
“Second hand! I have the distinct honour of wearing the very same dress Eloise wore at her wedding. She thinks it is some sort of heirloom.” Yet another humiliation. 
- - -

“Dearly bejewelled, we are gathered here today to witness the contract exchange of these counter-parties in the matter of holy arbitrage and allocation of Sarah Jacobsen and Alexander Shakti. In praise of the economy we celebrate this allocation of Alexander and Sarah. In the name of the market, the investment and the bottom line.” Spake the Business Theology Manager.
“Money be with you.” All in the temple, eyes glued to the order of ceremony, replied in union.
“Almighty cash, hear our plea for Sarah and Alexander who have come here today to be united in the sacrament of allocation. As their bank accounts join and their assets become one, make them financially stable to increase their faith in each other and the economy.”
“May your taxes be small and your profits large.” All parroted from the cheat sheet.
“I now read from the book of Gates, 3:23. An it came to pass that St Murdoch, was in repose in his modest mansion when three tax collectors visited. They said unto him ‘Pay thy fair share of taxation for your accountants are unable to hide your assets. Render unto the exchequer.’ St Murdoch replied, ‘I will not, for I am wealthy and can dick you around in court for as long as I like. Furthermore, I shall litigate and ye shall render unto me.’ So sayeth the word and the word is money.” The Theology Manager closed the Holy Business Text and kissed it lightly. “If anyone here today holds any option over either counter-party, let them settle now or forever hold their peace.
“May your portfolio be fat and your assets hidden.” Refrained the gathering.
“Sarah and Alexander, you have been chosen so that you may seal and harden your resolve for profit. Your union will be strengthened to keep a mutual and lasting faith in each other to carry out the duties of allocation. In the presence of this congregation, I ask you to state you intentions. Sarah and Alexander were you brought here by the devine randomness of allocation?”
“Yes.” They both said. 
“Will you accept your children as gifts from the market and bring them up according to the tenets of business management theory?”
“Yes.” Said a lone Shakti. 
“Sarah?”
“Yes, sorry.”
“Alexander, please say after me. I, Alexander Caesar Shakti, take you Sarah Elaine Jacobson to be my lawfully contracted allocated female partner. I promise to create wealth enough to increase our social standing, for richer or filthy richer, as long as we both shall hedge.”
“I, Alexander Caesar Shakti, take you Sarah Elaine Jacobson to be my lawfully contracted allocated female partner. I promise to create wealth enough to increase our social standing, for richer or filthy richer, as long as we both shall hedge.”
“Now you, Sarah. Please say after me. I, Sarah Elaine Jacobson, take you Alexander Caesar Shakti to be my allocated male partner. I promise to create wealth enough to increase our social standing, for richer or filthy richer as long as we both shall hedge.”
 “I, Sarah Elaine Jacobson, take you Alexander Caesar Shakti to be my lawfully contracted allocated male partner. I promise to create wealth enough to increase our social standing for richer or filthy richer, as long as we both shall hedge.”
“You have declared your consent in this place, before your shareholders and debtors, to be male and female allocated partners. May the market in its infinite wisdom strengthen your consent and fill you with profitability. What the market has brought together, let no-one separate.”
“May the market bless this union.” Echoed around the temple.
“Now the exchange of cheques. Let these cheques be a symbol of the deep and contractual nature of this union.”
“Take this cheque as a symbol of my investment in this contract.” Alexander was out of the starting blocks immediately.
After a pregnant pause, Sarah handed a small slip of poly-paper to Alexander, “Take this cheque as a symbol of my investment in this contract.”
 “Let the cheques be cashed!” The Theology Manager shouted with delight as Sarah and Alexander carefully folded the cheques and placed them in gold wallets, “I now pronounce you male and female allocated partners. You may kiss your partner.”
When Shakti lifted the black veil to reveal her perfect face, all present held their breaths in collective awe. She had succumbed to pressure and let the beautician have her way and it had been a spectacular success. Shakti began to shake, but manged to lean in and peck her lips and the small group clapped enthusiastically.
The reception was held in the sumptuous Warren Buffett Buffet on the top floor of Hardy Tower One, in the most opulent and expensive restaurant in the world, Gravity Well. The food was average and portions tiny, but very pretty, plonked at the of enormous white plates. The ambiance under the gigantic glass dome was simply unbelievable. As the strange coloured light danced about, Sarah’s melancholy stared chanced upon a waiter standing in a darkened corner. He was wearing the worst example of a male wig and was scratching at it madly. Why can’t men age with dignity? Then again, I can’t talk, my face is covered in paint. He rotated the head piece almost back into place and then took a bee-line straight towards her. As he approached, his gait and stride were reminiscent of someone she knew. Cracker!
“Would the blushing female allocated partner like a beverage?” Cracker asked with as much dignity as the wig allowed.
“I am so glad to see you. How did you…..?” Sarah beamed light.
“I know people in low places. Don’t you know who is playing tonight? The High Elvis himself. He’ll be incognito, though. He’ll be in disguised as Elvis.” They both laughed, but cut it short when she felt the disapproving eye of her mother-in-law. 
“Better than your disguise.” For the sake of appearances, she bit down on a chuckle. “What if Alexander recognises you?”
“He won’t.” Cracker knew Shakti would be completely distracted by Sarah.
“I hope you haven’t got any gags lined up. Exploding cake, perhaps?”
“There is no need. I just have to stand back and watch Shakti fuck up. He’s bound to do something stupid.”
“I hope not. I get the impression his mother is not the forgiving type.”
“Here’s something that’s weird. Check out the man sitting two down from dear old mother Shakti. Recognise him?” Cracker was cryptic, but clearly amused.
“Yes, that’s your friend Halo, isn’t it?
“The very same. According to the seating chart he is Haloren Smythe, Director, L.6.”
“L.6? What is he doing here?”
“I don’t think that is the right question. He is not out of place here. Why was he hanging out with me and my dubious friends?”
“I don’t think we will find out tonight.”
“The High Elvis isn’t here just to play a few songs. He wants to congratulate you in behalf of the Elvi and get a couple of photos. It’s good P.R.” Cracker whispered. “Champagne? Of course, madam.” He scuttled away and disappeared into the kitchen. 
“It was good to see you smile, Sarah.” Shakti said, leaning into her right ear.
“Oh, you frightened me! Goodness.” Sarah laid her hands protectively upon her stomach and tried another smile.
“That’s better. I have noticed you don’t look happy. Sarah, I am perfectly aware of what I am and truly honoured that you would let me breath the same air as you, let alone agree to allocation. You must know how beautiful you are and getting stuck with me must be greatly disappointing.” His eyes fell to the floor and he fought off tears.
“Alex, we have exchanged cheques.” She touched his face gently and decided to fake it for the rest of the evening. “I haven’t been feeling well lately, a little nauseous that’s all.” 
“Nauseous? Are you okay?”
 “I am fine.” She didn’t want to have that conversation yet.
“Are you feeling well enough to cut the cake?”
“We should have speeches first, but if you want to cut the cake, we shall cut the cake, husband.” The word nearly choked her, but she saw it made him immensely happy. She thought to kiss him, but was too repulsed by the prospect.
“I have made sure the knife is sharp and it will be slightly heated so it will slice through the cake smooth as silk and there will be hardly any crumbs.” Shakti smiled. He had really put some thought into the cake cutting ceremony and was proud of himself. No-one else would think of that!
The small crowd gathered around a round table located in the centre of the restaurant, directly under the glass dome as pink and green auroras performed aerial gymnastics above. The pure white, three tiered cake took honoured position in the centre of the table and stood before Sarah and Shakti awaiting symbolic penetration. They both held the knife and without word or ceremony, plunged it into the heart of the extravagant gateau. As they received polite claps and a few hoorah’s and Sarah dutifully handed Alexander the first piece of cake. He bowed in deference and raised the cake to his mouth with his right hand, the same hand that held the knife. He stabbed himself in the eye and screamed, his arms flailed about and as Sarah stepped in to assist him, the knife poked shallowly into her throat, just sufficiently to severe her carotid artery. The crowd reeled back, screaming and yelling, but Cracker was by Sarah’s side in a second, applying direct pressure, but she quickly faded into unconsciousness.
“Will someone stop him please? He’s going to stab someone else” Cracker yelled out, but know one seemed to listen until he saw his colleagues, Detective Chief Superintendent Hayley Lanning and Dr Kathy Logan step up. 
“He barely scratched the cornea. He’ll be fine.” Kathy said, peeling off her patch. “Let me see, sir.”
“It’s me.” Cracker said,
“I know.” She said gravely, “That is the worst disguise ever. It looks like the artery has been severed. I will have to do a little surgery right here.”
“Madam. We have a small first aid room. The light is better there.” The maitre d’ suggested over her shoulder. He was running a restaurant and vascular surgery does not make for good ambiance. 
Sarah was frantically carried through the foyer and into a small, but most certainly well lit room with an examination table, a first aid kit and a small sink. “The severed ends of the artery have might have retracted. I’ll have to find them and and pull them back together. She flushed the wound clear and placed her fingers inside to dig about but miraculously the artery had not retracted, but was in perfect position to apply the glue. 
“Cracker hand me the suture kit.” Kathy asked quietly as she continue to apply pressure to the wound. She gradually reduced the pressure, waiting for it to gush, but it did not. She removed her fingers completely and it did not bleed at all. The artery had re-attached and the sliced tissue melded back into one when she pressed the skin tightly together. “I don’t think I will need to glue this.”
The repaired artery was now allowing oxygenated blood to the brain and within a couple of minutes Sarah had regained consciousness.
“I told you he would do something stupid.” Cracker laughed at the blood splattered bride, helping her to her feet. “You heal fast as well, huh? That disc must have had some magic shit in it.”
“Yes, it must have. Lucky for us both, now.” She vacantly replied and she stared at herself in the mirror. Her fingers gently caressing the bright red cut in the left side of her neck. “I am glad we have a tradition of black wedding gowns, my goodness can you imagine a white dress? Look, I will be fine. Tell them to mop the floor for the allocation waltz and I will wash up and be out in five minutes.”
The door of the small room burst open and a petite dignified woman gracefully stepped inside. Eloise had seen the amount of blood that spilled out and calculated Sarah would have insufficient blood left to sustain life. She wanted to confirm the death so she could get on with seizing her assets. Sarah had a nice little portfolio and it would fatten up Alex’s nest egg considerably. 
 “Where is my dear daughter?” Mother Shakti said, but was immediately stunned by the unlikely state of Sarah. She was breathing and standing upright. “My dear, I thought you to be dead.”
“No such luck, Eloise.” She smiled. “Are we ready to return to the reception?”
“Most of the guests have gone home.” Eloise did not invite anyone too important and would not suffer too much embarrassment following the wedding cake fiasco. At least she wasn’t going to spend the night protecting her idiot son from a manslaughter charge. Stabbing a bride to death at a wedding will alway raise a few questions, no matter what your social status.
“No speeches then? Good.” Sarah was glad the night was moving along fast. “How is Alexander?”
“He sliced the corner of his eye and scratched the cornea. There was not much blood. Unfortunately for us all, he is going to survive.” Eloise smiled and looked to laugh, but her grin faded.
“That’s my husband your are talking about.” Sarah laughed and walked out the door. Eloise grunted and followed.
It was during the allocation dance, when they were up close that Sarah thought to tell him the happy news. “Alexander, I am pregnant.”
“That is wonderful news, wonderful. I am so happy.” Said Long-John Shakti, looking ridiculous in an eye patch.
“I told you I haven’t been feeling well, right? My doctor said, just to be on the safe side, we shouldn’t have sex until the child is born.”
“What?”
“Doctor’s orders, husband.” She choked on the lie and from saying that husband word again.
“Anything for you, my wife.” Alex whispered in her ear. She began to cough loudly and had to separate from him. 
“Let’s sit down.” Wife? Tax me fully!
“I’ve got to got and tell mother the good news”
“What news?” 
“You’re pregnant. Have you forgotten already?” Shakti skipped off to his mother who feigned delight as they hugged. She looked over her son’s shoulder and winked at Sarah. She did not wink back. She was not cool with lying and she had set herself up for a lifelong deception.
“I didn’t think you would go through with it. Shakti, what a joke.” The Johnny was angry and drunk.
“Oh, Johnny, just give me a hug and be happy for me. Someone has to be.” They hugged uncomfortably, like two strangers. “I heard you blew up the lab.” She hope to lighten the mood.
“Yes. So what? Why do you want to embarrass me? Do you want to see the holographs so you can get a visual of my humiliation?” He lumbered out his hand device and was quickly scrolling through holographs of the lab. 
“Wait! Go back.” Sarah was breathing became instantly laboured and her heart was in her mouth. “Yes. That one.” She looked at the interior of the damaged lab. Through the water damage and the soot she could clearly see a distinct pattern. The manner in which the blue electric arcs had struck the walls was undeniably the same pattern as she had been dreaming of for weeks. “Send me a copy, now.”
“Okay.” His swiped his had across the device and the image was sent.
“Don’t get too comfortable, pal.” The waiter said to Johnny from behind.
“How dare you, you low-life scum! You, you… level one waiter!” As he yelled Cracker raised his wig and took off a fake nose. “I thought you were dead. I should have known it was too good to be true.”
“Alive and kicking.” Cracker smiled returning the wig to its original silly position.
“Damn it, this night just keeps getting worse.” The Johnny stomped off to the bar, but was refused service by a low-life level one bartender with great hearing.
“The High Elvis needs to speak to you after this set.” Cracker whispered to Sarah.
“Okay, do you know where Kathy is? I need to speak with her.”
“I think she is on the floor over behind the fountain. She’ll be back with us soon.”
Sarah could not wait and dashed over to the fountain to find Kathy waking from a knock-out hit of THC-acetate. “Kathy? Kathy?” She looked at Sarah but did not respond. “Dr Logan to reception. Dr Logan please come to reception. Hello?”
“Huh? Wow that was intense! What’s up?” Dr Logan rose slowly to her feet, the image of the lab presented before her. “A bit of a mess.”
“Its the inside of the lab Johnny blew up. See the pattern of damage?”
“Seems random to me.”
“It is exactly the same pattern as the one I have been dreaming about. I am certain of it.” She ran an index finger over the screen, slowly and carefully tracing the burn marks on the wall and floor, then pressed an icon at the bottom of the screen, causing the image to drop away, leaving only the trace marks. “I probably won’t be available for a couple of days, so could you do some research on this pattern. I need to know what it means.”
“Sure thing Mrs Shakti.” Kathy thought to try Sarah’s new title on for size.
“Oh, that hurts. I think I prefer Holy Mother.” She quietly joked as she hugged Kathy tight. “I’ve got to go and speak with the High Elvis, now.” 
* * * 
“Cracker? Can we speak?” Hayley Lanning sidled up from the rear.
“I am a waiter.” Cracker said looking in the other direction.
“I don’t think anyone is falling for it. Do you know who that man is? The one sitting at the bar by himself.”
“Haloren Smythe, Company Director, L.6.” Cracker replied.
“Level 6. You don’t see many of those around. Getting to level 6 takes serious money or serious corruption, usually a combination of both.” Hayley had never been impressed by any level 6 snob and just knew this guy was cut from the same cloth.
“I’ve met him heaps of times, in fact he was a part of my group of friends, but thinking back I can’t remember who introduced him to the group. He just seemed to appear a few years ago and attached himself to us. We all used to meet for drinks regularly, but it was pretty obvious he wasn’t that interested in speaking with us. His only concern was picking up and getting out as quickly as possible.” Crackers recalled with a curious frown across his brow.
“A level 6 Company Director with a alter-ego as a Level 2 pick up artist. Very unusual indeed. I noticed him when Sarah was bleeding all over the floor. He was staring at the blood and smiling. I swear he was about to laugh.” Hayley shook her head in disgust. “And he has been staring at you, I think with surprise, but it could be something more sinister.” Hayley’s internal alarm was ringing loudly in her head, red flags flapping madly.
“That doesn’t surprise me, he is very intense and has a dark sense of humour, I mean pitch black. I lost count of the number of times he said ‘All level one’s must die.’ He’s one extremely cold guy and takes himself very, very seriously.” Cracker liked him, but always knew not to cross him. “Sarah told me he has retinal enhancements with some sort of vid-screen grafted onto his corneas. According to her that was the secret of his success with women.” 
“How are you feeling about all this?” She saw how he looked at Sarah and it was obvious, to any casual observer, that his was infatuated. Hayley did not like aspirational lows and he was aiming far too high.
“Not happy at all, in fact I have been fighting back tears all night. I could fall into a depression or act out with anger, but what would that achieve? I am certain Sarah would find that unacceptable. I have to face reality, I am Level 2 and she is level 5.” 
“It is good you understand the limitations of your low status.” She was deliberately cruel. Weddings always made Hayley depressed and angry, something of which she was completely unaware. Level 2’s must know their place. The Great Teacher indeed!
“Yeah, thanks for that. I really appreciate your support.” Cracker felt the burn and decided on an exit strategy. “It looks like the High Elvis is finishing up his last set. I will have to fetch Sarah.” Hayley’s broadside had hit him as hard, as she had intended, but this only served to increase his resolve. One day he hoped to live in dignity, something that people like Hayley would always strive to deny him. 
* * *
It took Cracker no time to locate Sarah and the High Elvis chatting intently backstage in the Green Room and he left them to it. Pleased he had finally managed to get the High Elvis and The Holy Mother together, he slithered back into the banquet hall and kept close to the walls. Trying to stay out of Haloren’s line of sight, as he made good his escape, he vacantly slid behind a mammoth set of drapes, obscuring a polished stone wall. As he sidled closer a small section began to shimmer, then coalesce into a glistening metal door. It opened automatically and Cracker poked his head in, assuming it was a back door to the kitchen or a maybe a fire-exit, but seeing neither stairs or knife wielding chefs, he walked in. He heard the door close behind him and turned to see that, from this side, the door was transparent. Touching the door with his hand caused the door to automatically open and when he backed away, it closed again. Cool, I’m not locked in.
He spun around and found himself in a darkened room, the only light filtering in through the door and another similar door 10 metres to his left. In the dimness he stumbled across what he thought to be a console, but it had buttons and no back-light. He took a punt and gingerly pressed a button or three until the room illuminated to reveal a huge control room, appointed with old computers, monitors and other machines that he could not identify. Too scared to touch anything else, he returned to the reception and promptly located Kathy stumbling about on the dance floor.
“Hey Kathy, walk behind those drapes over there.” Cracker pointed.
“I am not so intoxicated as to fall for that one, sir.” Kathy wagged her index finger and shook her head.
“I am not kidding. Just walk behind those drapes. It’s worth it.”
“Okay.” A bemused Dr Logan walked over to the drapes and looked behind them. Nothing but a straight line of uninspiring stone wall. She began to walk back, but Cracker shook his head and against her better judgment she disappeared behind the curtains and re-appeared at the other end. “Very funny. What’s the joke?” 
“I don’t understand.” He took her by the arm and as they entered the space behind the curtains, the door began to appear before them. “You see that, don’t you?”
“Yes, but that didn’t happen for me.” She was now enthralled and entered without hesitation. “What’s in here?” 
“I don’t know. I think it may be an old office or control room. Look at all the old computers, they must be really old. It doesn’t look like anything has been used in years.”
“Decades I would say, judging by the dust.” She slid her fingers along several horizontal surfaces and considered the dust like a mother-in-law judging her son’s new wife.

* * *
 Sarah dropped in to see Kathy at the morgue as soon as she had returned from her honeymoon. Honeymoon was probably overstating the significance of her break from work. She was pleased that Shakti had not once tried anything with her the entire time, having taken her doctor’s advice to heart. 
“Holy Mother, I am happy to see you!” Kathy gushed and gave her a long and uncomfortable long hug. 
“I am getting used to Mrs Shakti, but bless you.” Sarah replied. “What of the damage pattern?” As soon as she turned her mind to the pattern, it popped into her mind in perfect clarity. She pressed down on the distress the image caused and held her stomach gently.
“Okay, I have researched that pattern as much as I can. I found many possible matches. Come in, and have a look. For example, this Jackson Pollack painting, when viewed with infra-red light, there is an 90% match in this area here. This massive constellation in the Hancock galaxy, Rinehardt’s Caboose, is an 80% match. There is one ancient civilisation from Equatorial Britain that used this icon as a symbol of knowledge and destruction. It is about an 85% match, but if you alter the perspective and look at it from an angle of 23 degrees, the match becomes 96%.”
“That one sounds promising. Why 23 degrees?”
“I don’t know exactly. Maybe the tilt in the earth’s axis? I tried it from every different angle, 23 degrees was the closest match.”
“Any others?”
“Quite a few. This one is a 98 % match without changing perspectives. It is called a Beta-mode identifier pattern. It is the pattern left by the gravity waves released at the moment of the big bang. Gravity waves were emitted even before light existed, so cool! It is like looking back in time, almost back to the beginning of time itself.” 
At that moment Sarah felt the accumulation of anxiety and fear that had recently piled up, wash away in a cleansing shower for her soul. The sick feeling in her stomach ceased immediately and she felt it replaced by a warm contented glow. “That’s it. Stop now. I am certain.”
“Why would you be freaking out about a beta-mode identifier pattern? It’s pretty obscure.” 
“I don’t know, but there is an obvious link. The damage pattern was caused by a Gyges Disc and it is believed they are powered by gravity waves. Can you send Johnny all of your data? He might find it interesting.”</Text>
