        <Document ID="19A4CB01-88B5-4394-B4D5-CF8E9E7D1315">
            <Title>Sages of the Travelator</Title>
            <Synopsis>The first part is to set up the meeting with Sarah's friend John Stoat. Known to Sarah as Johnny.  (I decided on Stoat as a surname as it is in the weasel family).  Cracker later names him "The Johnny" .  The Johnny is deliberately rude to a female Elvis and gets a blender smashed over his head for his troubles.  They are in a bit of trouble with the management, but Cracker has followed them and saves them (by promising to pay 3 times the bill).  None of this is of much consequence, the only thing that really matters is by the end of the evening, Sarah is carrying the clone of "Richard Dawkins."

The travelator itself is not important.  I just figured it was an easy way for the three sages to hide in plain sight.  
The sucking up to the "travelatorisi" probably could be wound back a bit.
The "Praise to the Dawkins" stuff  turned out to be a wank.  I was using it as a piss-take of Islamic extremists and the way they praise Allah whilst planning murder.
Anyway "Praise to the Dawkins" is also an acknowledgement that I have used some of Richard Dawkins' wording and concepts.
A great deal of the conversation is peculiar and probably should be changed/edited quite a bit.  
The whole chapter is to give an idea of the beliefs of Richard Dawkins and how they were co-opted into a religious framework by people who dislike religion.
I do have comments on male/female relations - "She and the rest of female kind.....does not bother them" &amp; "It's not sexist if a woman says it."
If you are prepared to accept the manner in which Sarah becomes pregnant, then we probably won't have to change too much.
The term "One-after-the-one" refers to great profit to come and is later used in an Elvis song.  We probably should reduce the amount of times the term is used.
</Synopsis>
            <Text> 
 
 Sages of the Travelator
Day After Bombing - Afternoon to evening - Year Zero
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="FC80FAC4-B97E-4922-99BF-E35476F07BF5">
            <Title>Damien gets a Promotion</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="B9641479-830C-40C9-A4A7-8D1A7FC0F440">
            <Title>Viral Lockdown</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="62A13018-A249-4968-AF4B-8DB3E634B367">
            <Title>Jamison Hardy</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="4D6AA558-8266-48A1-87B8-D6DA23A84830">
            <Title>Women's Business</Title>
            <Synopsis>This is to discuss the ethical issues for Sarah (in her particular position) in relation to abortion and the possibility that she should put herself for allocation.
Termination or allocation?  Sarah thinks they weigh the same.</Synopsis>
            <Text>Woman’s Business
2 to 3 weeks
“What happened with the Commissioner?” Sarah decided to start off feigning interest in Hayley’s meeting with her boss.
“Detective Chief Superintendent,” she announced proudly, letting out an adolescent giggle. “I am in charge of all criminal investigations across the entire dominion.”
“Did what did he say?”
“What could he say? The contract on Cracker was open for anyone. He could do nothing but what he promised or lose the support of the rank and file.” Hayley knew the Commissioner would be gunning for her now. “I will have to fly under the radar for a while, though.”
“Hayley I really need some female advice.” 
“You mean real advice?” Hayley chuckled.
“They don’t have a clue about important issues.” She looked at the tribe inside the private box. “You know about the clone, right? But you know it was deposited inside me without consent.”
“You were raped? Get rid of it.” Hayley shot back. “I will happily charge anyone involved.”
“It is not quite that easy. You see all the Elvi here? They believe I am carrying their Saviour. You may have heard about him, the Elvi call him the one-after-the-one.”
“I heard something along those lines, but didn’t give it much credence. I mean, it seems more than a little absurd. I have seen the posters everywhere, ‘The one-after-the-one is coming’, but I thought it was all some sort of advertising campaign. So why can’t you abort?” 
“The Elvi are all adolescents and they will go berserk if I terminate. There’s 100,000 of them. Can you imagine the carnage? I will most certainly be hunted down and killed, along with Cracker.”
“Okay then, don’t terminate.” 
“There is another problem. Do you remember what Dr Logan told us at the morgue?”
“Yes, but I didn’t take it all in, I was still in a bit of shock. With you shooting Cracker and then his remarkable recovery.”
“Don’t ask how, but there is synthetic DNA attached to the child’s DNA and I don’t know what he will turn out like. He could be hideous.” Sarah scrunched her face up and looked away.
“I see your dilemma.” Hayley said calmly. Holy tax fraud! What a mess!
“What will happen if I have him?”
“Are you sure it’s a him?”
“Yes, the clone will be the same sex as the original.” 
“Then I would not worry about the child being hideous. All men are.” Hayley quipped and Sarah laughed.
“So true, so true.” It was the first time Sarah had laughed since Kathy had told her the news. 
“When they’re not trying to fuck you or kill you, men are really funny.” Hayley was momentarily deadpan before both busted out giggling. “Men do create problems, but in this situation they, or at least one of them, may be the solution. You must realise the difficulties of raising a child as a single mother. Apart from the obvious problem of doing all the work yourself, you will find discrimination from your boss and disdain from your colleagues, male and female alike. No-one in authority will ever say out loud that you are not wanted, but you will see evidence of it piling up everyday. The treatment of management-level single mothers is unconscionable - upper management does not like square pegs. All their talk about diversity is just that, talk.” Hayley had seen it over and over again. 
“Treating mothers like that is disgraceful and just plain moronic.” Over many years, Sarah just like Hayley, had seen the terrible abuse single mothers received at the hands of a patriarchal zeitgeist.
“Don’t think it is an operation of puritanical morality. It is nothing of the sort. It is a feeding frenzy of the strong destroying the weak, utterly. Like chickens pecking to death the weakest of their number, you will be identified as vulnerable and your workmates will be compelled to humiliate and punish. Senior executives want, no, they demand compliance. It is contemptible but allocation will fix all of this. Just make sure you specify level 5 and above. You don’t want to allocate beneath your station.” Hayley pointed to Cracker who doing a maniacal-macarana with Kathy.
“Allocation? It’s not something that I had ever contemplated. I have always regarded marriage-at-random a sign of desperation.”
“Yes.”
“Termination or allocation. I think they weigh about the same. I still have some thinking to do, Hayley.”
‘Not too long I hope. You are not showing at the moment, so you might be able to pass the child off as his, although you would have to get someone pretty stupid though.”
“A stupid level 5? Not likely.”</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="4A2639EF-7903-4BA0-ADE3-7F2B21A58788">
            <Title>Panacea</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="B9C87C37-C2BE-4D00-84A1-89DBDE3ADB7C">
            <Title>Eloise Shakti</Title>
            <Text>All business like social status and money are all that counts.  She is the owner of most of the arms manufacturing in the PTD and is ultra rich and consequently ultra powerful.  Other ultra rich are frightened of her, including the CEO of the PTD.

She owned three houses and two apartments by the time she was 20 years old. By age twenty-two, she was running her own Accommodation Directorate and the fact that she managed to land a level 5 husband, whilst extremely rare, was no accident. With pure force of will she took on larger and by reputation, more ferocious competitors and crushed them beneath her stylish heels. When her business was publicly listed, there were protestations from the Stock Exchange directly to the Ministry of Moral Compliance. Although there was no legal impediment, it seemed that a Level 2 CEO could not list a company on the Stock Exchange, they simply would not have it. Before any public trading on her stock commenced, however, she was made an offer she could not refuse, the sale of the company at an inflated price and allocation with an upwardly mobile level 4. She knew that with her talent she and her new husband would be at level 5 in double quick time and their children would want for nothing, breaking into the senior executive ranks – level 7 and above.</Text>
            <Notes>First appears with: Sarah &amp; Alexander</Notes>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="302C8F4E-11B3-4F67-8DD6-4FC1D75CBAF9">
            <Title>Viral</Title>
            <Synopsis>Short Introduction to Jameson Hardy - 20 years before the Dawkins
His viral research labs are trying to create a virus that attacks specific races, groups and individuals.
They accidently create a good virus.</Synopsis>
            <Text>
Viral
20 years Before the Dawkins

It was only 3.30am when the alarm broke into song, but Jameson Hardy was already awake and had commenced his morning rituals. Sycophants and sundry minions had been scurrying about for some time now, preparing breakfast, clothing options and a bathing facilities for wealthiest man in all of Ulyssia. Finding his way to the wet-room, he sprightly stepped into the glass booth, not unlike an old shower recess, where a halo of bright radiant colour appeared from above and slowly lowered down over his head, down his body to the floor before it popped out of existence. 
“Zero pathogens detected, General Health at 90% optimum. Have a good day Mr Hardy.” The computer voice of the scanner, an exact replication of his own mother’s verbal patterns, soothed to the country’s foremost citizen and left him swimming in happy childhood memories.
 His enormous claw-foot bath was already drawn and gleaming in the centre of the bathroom, steam rising enticingly from the clear, still water. Standing silently, he entered slowly, testing the temperature with toes, then calves, thighs and after some considerable time his backside. After a few minutes of complete silence, the only he would enjoy for the entire day, he hit the remote, bringing to life a wall of beeping technology and comms-devices. The colossal array of holographs, vid screens and computer monitors linked him with every market, finance institution, insurance house and brokerage concern on the planet. Jamison had his fingers in as many pies and if there was a school-kid in the Hindu-Kush selling his pulao at lunch time, Jamison wanted in on the action.
His first order of business was wading through the list of vid-mails that inevitably lay in wait. Vid-mail took much longer to view and action than text as most messages were peppered with irrelevancy and unnecessary emotion. Although it was more time consuming, Jameson found that he was better able to judge character; not in spite of the irrelevant bullshit, but because of it. Unconscious affectations of voice and motion revealed more about the speaker to Jameson than the contents of the message itself. Scanning the vast list of his in box, his attention was immediately drawn to the 10 messages from Professor Hermann, the head lab-coat from his Bio-Weapons Division. The professor never made contact unless it was something of interest, unlike most department heads who only ever called to bleat about funding.
The first message was short and cryptic. “Eureka” the face on the main screen had said. He played the next message – “Eureka” the professor said once more. All messages were the same – “Eureka.” 
“Okay, I’m intrigued.” Jameson said, jumping out of his bath and heading for his office, grabbing only a robe for the sake of appearances and leaving behind retinue of butlers and sycophants holding a variety of garments in the air. He could have quite easily have remained in the bath and made the call, but vid-calling from the bath was just plain impolite and he was nothing if not polite. Smile to their faces and stab them in the back.
The professor was still in his lab when Jamison called at 5.00am. “What’s with the messages, Professor?” 
“I think you should come and see me here. I have had some remarkable results. Remarkable.” He was beaming with delight.
“I don’t have the time. Just give me a report.”
“No, not over interlink lines. I need to see you now.”
“This phone is hardwired and encrypted. It cannot be hacked. I simply cannot drop everything for you Professor Hermann. If you can’t give me a report, stop wasting my time.” Jameson said, not entirely amused.
“I am not trying to upset you Mr. Chairman, but this will change human existence. We have manufactured the virus.” 
 
“A Professor of Virology has found a virus, well done. Why don’t you contact the boys in Oncology and see if they have found any cancer lately? You’ll be able to exchange war stories.”
“No. Not a virus, the virus.” The professor said proudly, assuming the tycoon would connect the dots.
“The virus, huh? If you don’t start making sense soon, I am going to pull your funding.” Jameson knew this would get him talking.
“It targets and kills all known human pathogens. The TPlusVirus is an actual panacea. ”
“Shit, do you know what this means?” Jamison didn’t really know, but he did not think it would be good for business.
“A world without disease,” his faced beaming with pride.

</Text>
            <Notes>First acts in “Viral”</Notes>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="A60197CE-26C3-4B46-B88B-221A3EE8449A">
            <Title>CSI by remote control.</Title>
            <Synopsis>Shakti again seeks assistance from Cracker and uses an ear piece and a body worn camera so Cracker can tell him what to do.  (Cracker is just reading off policy and procedures and telling Shakti what to do.)

At the end of the chapter Shakti decides to request assistance from Hayley Lanning for the interviews - I did not take this any further.  The investigation is later complated, but there is no narrative about interviews.  The last paragraph probably creates more problems than it solves.  Could easily be removed.</Synopsis>
            <Text>CSI by Remote Control
Day after Bombing (Afternoon) - Year Zero
Alexander Shakti was very surprised when the Commissioner handed him such a sensitive matter to investigate. The Commissioner had told him it was a simple murder-suicide, but he was not going to fall for that one again. He was still stinging from the Lee debacle and this time he was not going to make any mistakes. No slip-shod investigation for him. Every piece of information available would be gathered, logged, categorized, examined and re-examined. He would drown himself in a tsunami of data. He would check all the boxes and most of all he would cheat. Random Camera had some of the best surveillance equipment going around and Shakti had unrestricted access to the entire inventory.
“Alex, the ear-piece is undetectable and quite comfortable. The microphone and camera are in these glasses and are already on. You do not have to adjust or fiddle with anything, okay.” Cracker instructed as he handed the spectacles to him. “I can hear and see everything you do. I will tell you what to say. Simple.”
“Thank you, Cracker. I am so very grateful.” Shakti had hundreds of workers at his disposal to help him navigate the crime scene, nevertheless he called on the L.2. cleaner, the only person he could trust.
Shakti arrived at Pre-emptive Reciprocity and was hurried to the director’s office. It had been cordoned off by the first responders and they had already called in the cavalry. There were investigators and machines of every kind taking samples, holo-grammetry, robotic vacuum cleaners scurrying about and little flying drones taking measurements and air samples. The bodies were still in-situ with the former Director slumped across his desk with a scorched entry wound in his forehead. Janine Wildsmith lay crumpled on her side with a gaping hole in her temple, skin still smoking from the photon blast. The gun, tightly grippped in her dead hand, clear testimony to her culpability. The forensic team were poking and prodding them with complete disregard to their dignity. With confidence and purpose Shakti marched to the bodies, making observations and taking notes, every once in while he changed his expression as if he had made some remarkable discovery.
“I want the entire building locked down. No-one in or out and I want the names of every one who had access to this floor. Get all of their comms-records and computer activity for the last six months.” Shakti announced to the room, a few heads turned but turned back in disinterest. “Who is the senior officer here?”
“I am the new director. Damien Hardman, MBA. L.5.” Damien was pleased to see that a senior investigator had finally arrived. He was not used to having to play second string to cops and from what he had seen so far they were altogether a lacklustre group with questionable intelligence. Shakti shot out his clammy right hand and shook Damien’s hand limply.
“Lieutenant Alexander Shakti, L.5. Pleased to meet you.” The new Director’s smile vanished upon hearing who had been sent to wash his dirty laundry. 
“Lieutenant Shakti.” Damien did not know what else to say. Shakti’s was a legendary fuck-up and wondered how he was going to mess this one up. “I was one of the first to arrive and in my opinion the secretary shot the boss and then shot herself. We would like this matter to be handled sensitively. Obviously we can’t keep a lid on something like this, but we can control how the information is released.” 
“I won’t be jumping to any conclusions on this one. I will treat it as if there are others involved. What can you tell me about the shooter?” 
“I knew her well, she was a good woman. I had been speaking to here only minutes before and she was excited about her son’s up-coming birthday. I cannot understand what happened.”
“Okay, I will arrange for you to give a holo-statement later, but in the mean-time could you arrange for the records I requested to be collected?”
“Yes, of course. Agent Jones?” He called out and Agent Jones was immediately by his side. “This is important. Have this building locked down and get the names of all the people who have access to this floor. I need all the communications and computer logs for everyone on the list for the last six months.” 
“Yes, sir.” Agent Jones ran his boney fingers over his bumpy head, through his this black locks and turned for the door. “I think we already locked it down.”
“One more thing, Agent Jones.”
“Yes, Director?”
“Two cappuccinos. You want a coffee, Lieutenant? Yes? Two cappuccinos.” Damien quietly smiled. “That’s how you deal with minions. Let them think they are doing something important and then bring them back to earth with the mundane.” 
“Yes, fuck them. A good Policy.” Shakti repeated what Cracker told him, not believing he had just released the “F” word for the first time in his life.
“Exactly, fuck them.” Damien laughed and was amazed that, despite the rumour-mill, Shakti seemed almost competent and even likeable. 
“I don’t want to get in the way of your operations, but I will have to interview every person on this floor and anyone else who was here today. I would like some office space for a few weeks. Do you think you can swing it for me, Director Hardman?”
“I will get Agent Jones onto it as soon as he appears with our coffee and please, call me Damien.”
Shakti spent the next several hours inspecting raw data and the haul of samples taken from the Director’s office. He projected an air of confidence, asking salient questions of the forensic team and even throwing a few jokes in for good measure. Team members could not help but notice the dramatic change in their idiot boss. He seemed to know what he was doing. When the list of witnesses and suspects were transmitted to him, he quickly set to the task of prioritizing interviews and allocating resources skillfully and judiciously.
Shakti knew that he could not interview for shit and decided to look for help. He had found it difficult enough to interview a witness to a traffic accident and he knew that he was no match for the army of agents, all trained in the art of deception. Although every request for assistance was met with an unambiguous, “No,” he had heard of one Detective that was, reputedly, honest and quite the best interviewer in the service of the Dominion. She was also said to be a formidable character with impeccable ethics who could spot deception in an instant. Hayley Lanning was smart enough to take these agents on. 
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="8FB39485-3D22-4498-AEB5-135D908F374C">
            <Title>Leiutenant Shakti</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="F7BCF4F0-A148-45ED-81C7-9A4A588635D6">
            <Title>Elvi Rising</Title>
            <Synopsis>Relying on Elvis' susceptibility to religion/ideology this chapter is used to expose the Elvi to The Dawkins, Evolution and Science and reason.
They start writing songs about these subjects and they are all big hits.
The Elvi become enriched and begin to realise the power of the collective.
They form into a self-governed group.
They know their "laws" have no force of law but the entire Elvi begin to act as "moral agents".
I haven't really touched upon what the reaction the PTD would initially have.  I have settled upon the fact that the Elvi are making money and the PTD is all for that.  (They don't act until it is clear the Elvi are a social/political movement promoting HOPE!!
The CEO finally decides to wipe the Elvi off the face of the earth.
I mention a world elvis convention in the PTD, but I think that is pretty lame.  I think we can just assume the Elvis' are only in the PTD.
The conversation at the meeting of all of the directors and the CEO can be and should be lengthened.  I ended it because I could not think of any other things these guys might say at a meeting.  I hope you have better ideas.
There is a bit about Tasmanian Scotch (yes Scotch).  Probably should be deleted but I like the idea of Tassy pinching the name Scotch.
To kill the Elvi they need Jameson Hardy's recipe for a virus that targets a specific group.  The CEO plans to develop a virus that will only kill the Elvi.</Synopsis>
            <Text>Elvi Rising.
3 weeks after bombing
Cracker was in misery. Since the news of her pregnancy, Sarah had dropped off the radar, keeping her head down, responding to no correspondence. He knew she did not want his ident on her comms records, but had not seen nor heard from Sarah and had lost track of how long it had been. Just over a week maybe? A week, without seeing her? Intolerable. He had more than once demanded the non-existent god to end his pain. He had completed a full 6 hour session staring at the wall, the very wall that she had also gazed upon, wallowing in self-pity, and thought to take his sorry carcass on a pub crawl. He never thought that drinking alone was a good idea and had no reason to think that this escapade would end well, but not even he could have guessed what would be the consequences of this little night on the turps.
“Good afternoon sir, would you like a seat at the bar or a table?”
“Thanks Elvine, I would love a seat at the bar.” Cracker beamed saccharine.
“Elvina.”
“My apologies Elvina.” Cracker smiled again, hoping this would be the last time for the day.
“You are forgiven, darling. Now just you follow me.” Elvina exposed all her perfect white teeth in a disingenuous smile that screamed ‘Give me a big tip.’
He sat on a stool at the bar and gazed upon the seriously impaired occupants of the other stools, but none looked up from their drinks. A couple of Elvi had just finished a set and were hanging around the bar scamming free drinks when they zeroed in on Cracker. He had hardly gulped down his fifth drink when they sat either side of him.
“Great set fellas.” He had not seen the set, but Cracker knew that was the only way to start a conversation with musicians who have just walked off stage.
“Thank you, thank you very much.” Elvis 1 said.
“You look like you have a lot on your mind. Do you want a couple of Elvi to listen? We are very good listeners.” Elvis 2 said.
“Sit down fellas and I’ll buy you both a drink.” Cracker didn’t mind being taken for a bit of a ride if he could unload some misery. “Just don’t write a song about it.”
“We are always looking for new inspiration.” Elvis 2 said looking intensely at his brand new shoes. “Sometimes songs come real easy, other times the screen remains blank.” He had desperately been trying to construct viable lyrics for a song about his new shoes. They were the most comfortable he had ever owned, the supple suede a tasteful dark blue, but he was at his wit’s end. “Take for example these shoes. I love them, they are so comfortable and supple. Just look at that blue suede. I had them specially imported in from Tel Aviv, but I can’t seem to write a song.”
“Jew made shoes?” Cracker slurred back at him.
“No, I don’t think so.” Elvis 2 looked at his clone and almost imperceptibly rolled his eyes, just a little and just enough for only his buddy to notice.
“Do you know about the Dawkins?” Cracker giggled, the alcohol warming his body and dulling his head. “Let me tell you about the Dawkins. You’ll love this story.” Cracker spoke and as he became more intoxicated, he became increasingly loose with his tongue and told them everything of how much he loved Sarah, the bombing, the disc, the strange men on the travelator and their love of evolution, science and reason. He told them of The Dawkins and of the expected birth of The Dawkins 2.0, the one-after-the-one. When he had unloaded fully, he was spent and said, “Praise be to the Dawkins.”
“Tell me more about this evolution, man. It sounds great.” Elvis 1 said. 
“Yeah. Evolution sounds like the real thing.” Elvis 2 enthusiastically agreed. He had no choice but to agree with his doppelganger. Elvi not only looked the same, they thought the same. Exactly the same. Generally speaking, if you can convince one Elvis of something, you can convince them all. “I must know more of this Dawkins guy. He must have had it all together.”
“Wait a minute, I’ll get the others. They will want to hear about this.” Elvis 1 proposed.
He shortly returned with 4 more and by the end of the evening, Cracker had an audience of 20 Elvi transfixed by the evolution thing this Dawkins fellow had invented. Every Elvis that had worked in entertainment and hospitality, found it eminently plausible that their clients were related to monkeys. For the first time in his life Cracker was looked upon with admiration and it made him feel very uneasy. They had broken out brain-dump caps and were downloading everything they could on evolution, science and reason. The Chronicles of Dawkins etched into the brains of each Elvis before left he bar to go home. 
“Please tell us of the blessed mother.” Elvis 18 asked.
“Virgin mother, actually.” Cracker was truthful, but deliberately mischievous. It seemed he still had something of a sense of humour, even when he was feeling so retched. “Praise be to the Dawkins.”
“Praise be to the Dawkins!” A couple uttered, a little bit unsure.
Some with notepads out were already writing songs in praise of the great natural force of evolution, Charles Darwin, The Dawkins and the one to come. 
“I have written a song. Let us sing the praises of the great one and the one-after-the-one.” An Elvis proudly announced.
“Dawkins be praised” The Elvi fell into line pretty quickly.
An irate bar owner with a company of black clad security Elvi, waded through the crowd and stood before Cracker. “What are you doing? You can’t teach these guys anything spiritual or philosophical. It voids my warranty! Get the fuck out and never come back.” At that the security Elvi picked up Cracker clean off his stool, carried him high above the crowd and out the door where they slammed him head first into the black stinking cement. The line of punters waiting to get inside, pointed and laughed heartily at Cracker, crumpled face first in the shit with his legs flapping about in the air. The laughs turned silent as faces, painted pale in shock and fright, as they saw Cracker stand with the right side of neck bulging, white bones splitting the skin and his head lolling to the left. He straightened his head as best he could, ran a four-finger comb through his black hair and walked off with as much dignity as a shit-faced man with a busted neck could muster. 
He woke feeling no hangover and his neck felt great when he moved his painless head in any and all directions. I could have sworn I was really hurt last night. Then the memories of his chat with the Elvi popped to life in his head. At the time Cracker thought it pretty funny, but the dim morning light brought feelings of shame and guilt. “Oh dear.” Cracker rolled his eyes. “What have I done?” 
The meme spread though out the Elvi and after only a few days polished, full orchestral renditions of new masterpieces were rolled out and became massive hits on the club scene and were soon being heard on audio platforms all over the world. No individual Elvis became rich, they had to share the royalties among their number, but collectively they became a powerful player in entertainment. Managers and agents were soon swimming in money as a brand new industry arose. The first song to hit the big time was a beautiful ballad of sincere love for Darwin, science and evolution. There was rarely a dry eye in the house when played live.
EVOLVE ME TENDER
Evolve me tender, 
Evolve me true. 
Evolve me at your will.
Turn on a few dormant genes
And I’ll grow some gills.
Evolve me slender, 
Evolve me dear, 
My genome with no flaws.
I don’t need no dorsal fin,
Or no eagle claws.
Evolve me tender, 
Evolve me strong, 
Two legs a good start. 
A voice to always sing your song,
And a four chambered heart.
Evolve me tender, 
Evolve me true, 
The Dawkins dream fulfilled,
For my Darwin I love you.
And I always will.
The next song raised awareness of environmental concerns, a topic not spoken of in a number of generations.
Burning Coal
Earth Almighty, 
I feel the temperature rising.
Higher, Higher.
Someone is burning some coal.
Burn, Burn, Burn
We gotta stop using this fu-el.
My brain is blaming, 
I don’t know who’s got to go.
The miners are all liars, 
It ain’t no signal fire.
You choke my morning sky, 
With burning coal.
A-Choo, Choo, Choo.
I feel my bronchial tubes closing,
Help me, I’m choking.
I must get an oxygen mask.
Cough, cough, cough
We are beginning to fall.
Choking on smoke.
I don’t feel fine, not at all.
 Pollies all are liars,
They say there’s clean coal fire.
You block my morning light.
With burning coal.
It’s coming closer,
The end is nearer than you think.
Won’t you help?
I feel we’re slipping away.
It’s hard to breath
And my chest is a-heaving.
Please have mercy, 
It’s burning a hole in my day.
‘Cause science ain’t no liar, 
Unlike power suppliers, 
Who darken morning sky,
With burning coal.
With burning coal.
Ah, ah, burning coal.
Don’t want a chunk, a chunk of burning coal.
A chunk, a chunk of burning coal.
A chunk, a chunk of burning coal.
A chunk, a chunk of burning coal.
The Elvi were artists and scientists reborn, eagerly brain-dumping to the limit, gobbling down science texts and jamming their brains with libraries of information, expanding their appreciation of the elegant complexity and unlikelihood of existence. A nation of evolutionary abominations fearlessly jumping to the defence of Darwin. Their souls, filled with the joy of simply being alive, crooned the praises of reason to anyone who would listen, their songs as uplifting as they were solemn. Sales continued to shoot upward, to heights previously unknown. Elvi concerts always sold out, the message of The Dawkins and the imminent arrival of his replacement, the one-after-the-one, receiving wild applause and unquestioned acceptance. The ovation would always be accompanied by the crowd screaming in maniacal unison, “Dawks, Dawks, Dawks.” 
The popularity of the Elvi drew no official attention from the Directorate of Moral Observance for a very long time. Conformity to the laws of business and adherence to the social hierarchy, ensured the distribution of wealth always flowed upward and the shit always flowed down. Respect was only permitted to look up and was always to be returned with scorn. 
At first glance, the Elvi served this purpose very well. Considered as only one entity and unable to claim individual royalties or accumulate sufficient wealth to wield any individual power, they were ripe for exploitation. Deprivation of economic advancement was usually sufficient to crush any fanciful dreams of social climbing and even if there were one hundred thousand of them jitter-bugging around the Dominion, no Elvis would wield power.
Fans of the Elvi, the Dawks, saw themselves as a benign, loosely affiliated group of wildly enthusiastic music fans that, for the most part, did not really care about social change, the environment or some dude that might get born in the future. About the only seditious act they indulged in was pasting “The one-after-the-one is coming” posters on any and every vertical surface that would readily accept glue. A few appeared on walls here and there, but before long, the posters became ubiquitous. The Pale was transformed into a thicket of papier-mâché monoliths, gathered together in praise of The Dawkins and silently awaiting the one-after-the-one. Where most saw a collection of useless, drug fucked stoners, the Moral Compliance Directorate saw the Dawks representing doctrinal divergence of the worst kind, an organised threat to the natural order. 
The Elvi managed to fly under the radar of Moral Compliance until they spoke of the “One after the One”, the great leader to come, as the deliverer of hope. Moral Compliance cared not about silly songs righteously regaling the merits of saving trees and whales. There were fuck-all trees and no whales left to save anyway. They could sing all they liked, it would amount to nothing. Prophesy was not something the company gave much credence, but the Elvi were preaching social change, equality and freedom. Giving hope to working class scum was something that would not be tolerated, the lows would not be permitted to taste of such rich fruit. The minority of the opulent must be protected from the majority. Radical and decisive action was needed to ensure they dined on ashes forever.
 
The CEO had not anticipated how dangerously contagious was the meme of hope, now threatening to spread throughout the dominion. There were, suddenly, so many Dawks that they had not the resources or the time to arrest and process what appeared to be about half the population. The infection was not just limited to the lows, management-class music platforms and vid-shows were replete with praise for the latest Elvis masterpiece, which were played ad infinitum. Much to his chagrin, the CEO had found himself, on more than one occasion, humming an Elvis classic. 
A meeting with the CEO was hastily arranged when the analysis from Moral Observance hit the in-boxes of all the Department Directors. The Elvi and their army of Dawks were challenging the power of the state and had to be stopped, but there was just so many of them. Damien saw the enormity of the task ahead, getting rid of one hundred thousand much loved entertainers, was not be something he would want appearing on his resume and hoped his department didn’t get the gig. The logistics of killing off all of the Dawks, far more numerous than the Elvi, did not bear thinking about.
“Concentration Camps.” The Director of Corrections immediately placed on the table. He never missed an opportunity to increase his budget. 
“We can’t imprison them all, we don’t have the resources. Unless your department can pay for all of it.” Damien shot him down.
The Director of Prisons coughed and blurted out, “Well, we would have to discuss the fiscal arrangements.” He could feel his face going red.
 “Killing them all is not an option either.” He looked straight at the No 1 screw, knowing he had snuffed out his little bit for more power. “It’s a logistical nightmare and we still would not get them all. The cost would be astronomical.” Damien knew his opposition to wholesale slaughter would be interpreted as moral weakness and had couched his argument in terms of economic burden. Fiscal responsibility was the only safe ground upon which to take a contrary position to prevailing wisdom. 
“We can round them all up at the World Elvis Convention, it’s being held here this year.” The Director of Intelligence liked killing people, so popping off a bunch of clones would not raise any moral quandary. 
“There would be an outcry here and worldwide. The Elvi around the world number in their millions and they take care of their own.” The Commissioner of Police did not want to see any social disquiet or an invasion of foreign Elvi for that matter. He wanted the chaos at the usual level of insanity. 
“There is a huge industry based around these clones. Many L.5’s have invested heavily in Elvis stocks and they would be severely affected.” The Director of Economic Advancement cared little for the Elvi themselves, but he had the duty to ensure the money flowed, particularly when he was one of the many who had padded their portfolios with Elvis. 
The CEO was deep in thought. How do I eliminate all of the Elvi here and at the same time ensuring Foreign Elvi would be too scared to avenge their brothers? 
“What is your plan, sir?” The bravest person in the room, the newly promoted Damien, was the first the actually ask what the CEO wanted. He hoped his question sounded as neutral as he had intended, but the CEO was notorious for exploding at the slightest breach in etiquette and Damien hunkered down for a good tongue lashing.
“Everyone out!” The CEO yelled. The Department Directors all stood and gently slid their chairs under the giant marble table. Each walked calmly toward the boardroom door, increasing their pace as the exit beckoned, breaking into a trot for the last two strides. Damien wanted to join the rats scurrying off the ship, but knew he had to stay and face the captain.
“Thank fuck those idiots have gone. Do you want a drink?” the CEO was all smiles. “You were the only one who would dare speak his mind. They just want to protect their own little fiefdoms. I despise their craven self-interest. All of them are too busy preserving their positions to actually do their jobs. How does Scotch sound?”
“It’s only midday, sir. So yes, Scotch for me.” Damien really had no clue what to expect from this guy, but he hoped a decent glass of scotch would allow him to regain some balance.
The CEO made the order and an assistant appeared out of nowhere with two fat, chunky glasses of scotch, planting them silently on the table and in an instant, was gone. 
“Tasmanian Scotch, the best there is.” The CEO raised his glass and clashed it into Damien’s, still untouched on the table. “Now, what we speak of now will can never speak of again.”
“Yes, sir. Total quarantine of information.” Damien sipped the whiskey and was over whelmed by the flavours exploding in his mouth. “Tasmanian certainly is the best, but how is it called Scotch?”
“I own the naming rights.” He thought that was all he needed to say to establish who possessed real power in this meeting and he did not want to bore Damien with the convoluted and literally murderous negotiations with the Scots. “You are to form a team to dig down to Jamison Hardy’s abandoned Bio-Weapons research facility. If you don’t already know, the facility was involved in re-sequencing viral DNA to specifically attack certain genomes. Ultimately, it was hoped that the virus would be developed to the point that it would attack a single individual, leaving no identifiable cause of death. The perfect tool for assassination, a virus that could infect an entire civilisation and all but one survives.”
“I see sir, that is ingenious. The Elvi have exactly the same DNA, so if you can infect one you can infect them all. One infected Elvis at the Elvis Convention and they will be wiped out world-wide.” Damien was sickened by the appalling scale of the genocide, but truly admired the audacity of the plan. “I’m in. How can I help?” 
“I will need samples of as many pathogens as you can recover from each of the seven decommissioned labs. I will give you access to all the old archives, but they have not been formatted for brain-dump, so you will have to read it all. Most of it is science beyond the comprehension of you, or I for that matter. I will assign you an interpreter, an eminent Bio-engineer. He will point you in the right direction, but your first problem will be digging down to the laboratories. If you drill it out by hand you will need an army of agents and to maintain bio-security you cannot use explosives. A plasma cutter or thermal lances might suffice, but you will have to be very careful where you slice. Whatever plan you adopt, it will have to be done safely, quietly and quickly.”
“Works of that magnitude could not help but draw attention. It is a tall order.” 
“The other Director’s must be kept in the dark, if they get a sniff this, they will most certainly try steal the bio-agents themselves. There is a gold mine of research waiting to be dug up and they will want a piece of the action. Safely, quietly and quickly. We have to recover the pathogens, identify a suitable candidate and then re-sequence the genes to attack the Elvi genome. All this, before the World Elvi Convention in December, just eight months away.”
“My operatives will have their contracts terminated at the conclusion of works. I will choose those who will not be missed. With your permission, there is one guy I really want to get on the team, Agent Jones. He really needs his contract terminated. Get this, he has officially changed his first name to Agent.” Damien was nodding his head, “True story.” 
“I find that really quite peculiar, that and the fact that you found it necessary to bring it to my attention.” The CEO really hated it when underlings became relaxed and familiar enough to regale him with anecdotes from their pointless lives. “You can use and discard anyone you deem fit, just as long as they can keep a secret until the work is done.” The CEO was grim. “The Elvi must to be wiped off the face of the earth.”
* * *
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="3A593019-D0DB-4F34-AD65-2CBE42E57080">
            <Title>Anthony "Cracker" Hardy</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="31398765-24BF-4861-ABC3-4787B76669BB">
            <Title>Anti-Virus Virus</Title>
            <Text>
 Anti-virus Virus
2 months after bombing
It took quite some time to clean up the scientists and give them their first decent meal in several decades, but after only five or so hours they were gathered together, before the High Elvis and his Elvisian Cabinet. Holy Aunt Hayley and Holy Aunt Kathy were seated among the cabinet members, with the Blessed Mother and the Great Teacher either side of the High Elvis. 
“What do you have to say for yourself, Mr Treasurer?” The High Elvis raised his eyebrows and stared across the cabinet table at his old friend. Shaking his head he knew nothing would save the Treasurer, the report from the Blessed Mother and the Great Teacher was unequivocal.
“I thought we would have more time. The funds were locked in term deposits and if we withdrew early we would have lost hundreds of thousands.” The Treasurer pleaded, but he knew his fate was sealed.
“I care not for your fiscal responsibility! Your greed has probably cost us everything. What good is money when we are all dead? The time you wasted could have been the time we needed to make the mission a success. Furthermore, your stupidity placed the The Great Teacher, The Blessed Mother and her child in mortal danger. The one-after-the-one could have died!” 
“I am so sorry, High Elvis.”
“Silence! Your decisions have consequences. You are fired! Now leave the cabinet room.” He stood and quickly headed to the Cabinet room exit, no-one there could even stomach looking to ensure he had left, instead relying on the sound of the door closing behind him.
“Professor Hermann, from what I understand this T-virus operates inside the body in a positive manner? It makes you live a long and healthy life?” The High Elvis did not believe such a thing could exist. Every science book he had brain-dumped told him otherwise.
“The T-virus does much more than that. If you want to know if there is any downside, I haven’t found one. I am 62 years old, Mr Elvis, but I am reliably informed I only look 30 and whether or not that is true, I can honestly tell you I feel 20.”
“What about these other viruses you have designed?
“I only managed to get two others out of the lab. One acts like a disinfectant. It can be delivered in aerosol or liquid form and it survives and multiplies on surfaces, actively hunting down human pathogens in the open environment. It is non-toxic to plants and animals and leaves a pleasant lemony fresh scent. The only limit I placed upon this organism is that it cannot survive in temperatures over 35 degrees centigrade, it cannot survive inside a human host.”
“Self cleaning houses?”
“Self cleaning environments.” The Professor knew he was on a winner with that one. A couple of applications per year and an entire house, factory or suburb would be free of human pathogens.
“The Blessed Mother mentioned another virus.”
“Yes. I have an anti-virus that inoculates against the T-virus. One shot of this and you are guaranteed a normal life of soul sapping misery.” The Professor happily announced.
“Is Agent Jones still alive? I understand he was not protected by the T-virus.” The High Elvis asked of the Minister for Health.
“Yes. I think he was only suffering from the toxins in the sewage and will be fine. He tells us they will be kidnapping 6 Elvises for testing, very soon.” Keeping updated on the progress of Agent Jones was a priority for The Minister for Health. He was not going to drop the ball like the treasurer. 
“If we stop it they will only try again. They must have their tests and they must think they have their killer bio-agent.” Holy Aunt Hayley interjected.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="7D287FF4-175F-4F65-A4C2-AFADEDFC9AEA">
            <Title>Shakti does something right</Title>
            <Synopsis>He and Sarah are picked up in a hover copter by Shakti.  One of the police escorts jumps out of the copter and shoots a few people - it is not racist as he only shot white people and he has to kill them to keep up his shot to kill ratio.  They are taken back to the public bathing facility - the scene of the crime.  I don't mind this part, but if "Hot time in the ol' town tonight" is significantly altered, then this will have to be altered proportionally.

</Synopsis>
            <Text>Shakti does something right

Nothing scared Cracker more than being squashed or smothered and he was not about to get caught up in a crush now. He was desperately looking around trying to find an escape route, his mind spinning trying to grasp at any idea, but he had nothing. He considered the possibility of using his Vid-phone, but whom would he call and how would they get here? Sarah did not look like she was coming up with anything either. In an automatic response to terrorist events all bars, hotels, clubs and public buildings had now locked their doors and unless you were directly outside your residence, you were locked out. If you were locked inside a pub, you could do nothing but watch bedlam erupt, sipping a cold one. 
Cracker’s Vid-phone began vibrating in his pocket, but his arms were now wedged too tightly against his body to grab it quickly. He wriggled his pelvis and wedged his hip into Sarah’s side. “Grab my phone, will you?”
“A girl would be pretty stupid falling for that one.” She said, sliding her hand in his pocket nevertheless. “Why don’t you have an implant?” 
“Too much of a pain in….I was going to say backside, but it really is a pain in the head. I just don’t fancy a device being implanted in my brain. Besides, I don’t want my head cracked open every time a new model hits the market.” 
“That is an exceedingly unenlightened view. Okay I think I have it.” She removed her hand and the vid device from his pocket and opened it, holding it in front of his face.” 
“Shakti here. Over.”
“You don’t have to say “over”. It’s a fucking phone.” Cracker said, immediately exasperated by his dopey confederate.
“Okay. I forgot. Where are you? I need you for a big clean up.”
“I’m stuck in a crush on the Grand Parade, outside Hardy Tower 87. You’ll have to get someone else.”
“No. I will come and get you. It’s on my way. Expect me in five minutes.”
“Make sure you bring 2 sets of protective gear, I’ve got my assistant with me.”
“An assistant, eh? Very fancy. Over and out.”
The transmission terminated abruptly and Sarah put the phone back in his pocket, hand lingering longer than was strictly necessary, but withdrawn abruptly and well before any inference could be drawn.
 
“I don’t know how he is going to get through this crowd, but I don’t think we can get out any other way.”
“An assistant cleaner? My mother would be proud.” 
“That’s all I could think of. At least I remembered the suits.” 
“What do you want with suits?”
“It will stop our clothes contaminating the crime scene. They’ve got machines that can ID gnat shit a thousand metres away and we would leave residue all over the place. It wouldn’t take long to track its source”
“Yes, I should have known that.” She said, acknowledging his keen judgment and scolding herself for this intellectual slip. She did not entertain the idea of any man being her intellectual equal and while he had not issued her a serious challenge, she had to admit he did have moments of clarity. It was surprising to meet a low that was bright and even more surprising to find one she liked. From her tower above, they had all seemed dirty uneducated brutes. Nothing to concern herself with and certainly not mingle with, but here she was sidling with thousands of prime examples of reeking lows. Her initial disgust was replaced by a deep sense of sympathy and guilt. How many of these people have to live in squalor in order for me to live in luxury. I stand on many shoulders. Just as these thoughts began to settle in there was a loud commotion above. Shakti had commandeered a Hover-Copter and was descending fast. Incredibly, the crowd managed to part sufficiently for the small craft to hover a metre or so above the ground and allow a storm trooper to jump out, particle rifle at the ready. All but two of the crowd reeled and cowered in fear as the policeman approached, “Cleaner and assistant?” They nodded. “Put these on and get in.”
His rifle remained horizontal, pointing and shooting haphazardly at the crowd, only killing two or three to keep the rest of the horde in check.
The protective suits were no more than white poly-paper overalls with silly hoods and booties but would conceal signs of the explosion. Face masks covered any facial injuries, of which Sarah now had none and provided welcome relief from the acrid stench. 
“Did you see me pop those citizens, sir? Smote them good.” A grinning young constable said as he high-fived the pilot. “Woo, hell yeah!”
“Yes, nice shooting constable, but did you have to kill them?” asked a rather perplexed Shakti.
“No, but there is no point just wounding them, it wrecks your shot to kill ratio. My stats are down by 5 per cent this month.” Shakti remained a little grave and was not overly convinced of the need to summarily execute random citizens. “Don’t worry sir. I shot white people, so it wasn’t racist or anything.” 
“In that case, well done and keep up the good work.” Said an unconvinced Shakti.
“Thank you sir. Don’t forget to buckle up – safety first, remember?” Smiled the young constable as he high-fived with pilot one more time. “I love this shit!”
As the small craft ascended, Shakti quietly filled Cracker and Sarah in, “There’s been a coordinated terrorist attack. They let off an explosive device in a crowded club and managed to divert the water away from the fire. The city engineers are stumped and I’ve got to sort out the mess. We’re on our way to a public hygiene facility, it’s just up ahead.” 
Cracker and Sarah glared at each other with alarm. “What do you want us for?” stammered Cracker. Returning to the scene of the crime was not what Cracker had in mind. Sarah suddenly was not terribly concerned for the fate of the crowd below, but dreadfully afraid for her own. Adrenaline flooded their systems, hearts bouncing about madly. 
“C’mon, Cracker, you know me.” Shakti said leaning in to whisper. “I don’t have a clue how to clear this up. You’re a cleaner. Show me how to clean.” 
“Wait a minute.” Cracker said then turned to Sarah and she nodded in silent agreement. “I think we might be able to help, but it will cost you. You will owe me big for this one my friend.”
“Oh, anything. Just get met through this.” He truly didn’t have a clue.
“I am fucking serious. I will collect on this debt. In the future I will ask for something and you will deliver, no questions asked.” Cracker sounded like he knew what he was saying, but in reality he was shitting himself and blurting out anything that came to mind. 
“Okay. I agree. Anything to get me through this. I am already in enough trouble with the Commissioner about the Lee death. He seemed to think it was murder, but it was obviously suicide, wasn’t it?”
“Shit Alex, I got there after the body-snatchers. Let’s concentrate on the task at hand, huh?” Lee was murdered?
“Alright. Just fix this for me.” Shakti was now pleading, tears welling in his dull, unremarkable eyes.
“I’ll try. Just keep out our way and keep our names out of this.” He felt odd ordering the senior cop around, but he knew him too well. If you put him in the corner and asked him not to move, things went a hell of a lot smoother. When they arrived they strode straight to the control room and were met by a swarm of engineers and computer geeks all claiming they could do nothing to stop the flow of water.
“What has occurred here is impossible” said the head geek. “Impossible.”
“Don’t worry. I will have this set straight shortly. My assistants have their instructions and my full confidence. Just do what they say.” Shakti assertively announced and wandered off as if to survey the scene. His career was in the hands of a cleaner and some random chick, but it was better than it being in his own. 
“If you gentlemen could give me a bit of room I will see what I can do.” Sarah requested, but the frazzled men stood firm and mute. “Please leave.” 
“It will do you no good.” One said as he tramped out the door. 
“We’ll have to use explosives.” Another added before disappearing from view. 
Why were men such bloody whiners? They did naught but whinge, bitch and complain. Now alone, Sarah immediately went to work, but merely reversing her previous key strokes had no effect, after a bit of trial and error she finally managed direct the water back into the recyc system. She received no congratulations from the engineers though. 
“She was just lucky” they all decided and promptly left nursing battered egos. “This is a disgrace, Lieutenant, who are these people?”
“Don’t worry, I won’t mention them in my report. I will give you all the credit. Okay?”
“Yes. That seems acceptable,” said a senior engineer. Fixing this will look good on my resume.
The choked drain had caused a cascade of brown water spill out the entrance and flood the basement, all three levels and then out into the street. Blue prints of the building proved entirely inaccurate, causing the city workers to wade around hopelessly in a fruitless search for the blockage. Cracker, hooked on a life line and waded in, heading straight for the logjam. The water was chest deep and he had to duck-dive to unblock it. He dragged and jerked at the main chunks of flotsam and finally heaved up the bloated corpse of a communist lard-arse. His reign of socialist terror lasting long enough for him to slip and wedge his enormous behind in the drain.
“Here’s your terrorist, Shakti.” Cracker proudly announced as he floated the body to the stair. After a few more diving missions, the drain was unclogged and sucking water down greedily. “You’re a hero now, mate. The rest is just a simple mop and squeegee job, other contractors can take care of it.”
“I’ll have one of my minions take you both home. Neither of you were here. Understand?” Now that the crisis was over, he did not want these two hanging around to take the limelight. He had a boss to impress.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="8089DD95-9E4E-4828-B858-A44FE87CE672">
            <Title>The Blank</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="AC80BE2D-A6AE-4FC8-9E92-60573A7AAECC">
