            <Title>Research on Tardegrades</Title>
            <Text>Tardigrades are small (&lt;1 mm) aquatic ecdysozoans that occupy a distinct phylum, Tardigrada. This controversial phylogenetic positioning provides important insights into the evolution of arthropods and nematodes1,2. Limno-terrestrial tardigrades can withstand almost complete desiccation through a mechanism called anhydrobiosis3,4, and tardigrades can survive extreme environments, including extreme temperature5, pressure6,7, radiation8,​9,​10, and even exposure to the vacuum of space11. Although it is a weak anhydrobiote12, Hypsibius dujardini is a model tardigrade due to its ease of culturing13, transparent body color that is suited for developmental biology studies, and its feasibility for RNA interference-based genetic studies14.
Recently, Boothby et al. reported the sequencing of H. dujardini and demonstrated that as many as 17.5% of the genes of this organism may have been acquired through horizontal gene transfer (HGT) and that these foreign origin genes may be the key to their extremotolerance15. However, the anhydrobiotic capabilities of H. dujardini are limited compared with those of other terrestrial tardigrades12, and the non-axenic nature of tardigrade culture is prone to contamination, especially in light of the unusually large size of assembly (212.3 Mb) compared with previous estimates13. Hence, a calculation of the HGT rate requires comprehensive experimental evidence and careful data screening. Three research groups have provided evidence demonstrating that the HGT candidates presented by Boothby et al. were, in fact, mostly contaminant artifacts. Delmont and Eren employed metagenomics approaches and analyzed multiple DNA and RNA libraries, and Bemm et al. filtered Moleculo reads by k-mer frequencies, to identify contamination (including near-complete bacterial genomes) and effectively removed it from the assembly16,17. Koutsovoulos et al. presented a thorough comparison using an independently sequenced assembly of the same strain of H. dujardini and found no evidence of extensive HGT in this species18.
We have also been studying the same strain, but with an ultra-low input sequencing approach. Tardigrades are small animals, mostly in the range of several hundred micrometers in length and comprising approximately one thousand cells, with only several hundred picograms of DNA per individual. Therefore, naive approaches require thousands of animals to extract sufficient DNA or RNA for the construction of libraries for high-throughput sequencing; the collection of that many animals from a non-axenic culture is inherently prone to contamination. Such contamination can be screened after assembly using bioinformatics and metagenomic approaches but would ideally be minimized in the library preparation steps. To this end, we employed an ultra-low input methodology to sequence a single individual of H. dujardini to minimize contamination and provided supporting evidence that there is no extensive HGT19. Here, we describe the details of our sequencing data and the ultra-low input library preparation methodologies. Although we also describe a series of single individual mRNA-Seq data in this work, due to the low reproducibility in some of the samples, we provide these data sets as supporting materials to possibly identify tardigrade-specific genes. Therefore, the rest of this descriptor is predominantly focused on the genome sequencing, reproducibility of which was confirmed by replications.
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="249A41E5-E859-44F0-A64F-F29FE03DD57D">
            <Title>Editing</Title>
            <Text>Woman's Business
2 to 3 weeks
"What happened with the Commissioner?" Sarah decided to start off feigning interest in Hayley's meeting with her boss.
"Detective Chief Superintendent,” she announced proudly, letting out an adolescent giggle. "I am in charge of all criminal investigations across the entire dominion."
"Did what did he say?"
"What could he say? The contract on Cracker was open for anyone (to close). He could do nothing but what he promised or lose the support of the rank and file." Hayley knew the Commissioner would be gunning for her now. "I will have to fly under the radar for a while, though."
"Hayley, I really need some female advice." (Sarah had had her fill of the masculine variety.)
"You mean real advice?" Hayley chuckled.
"They don't have a clue about important issues." She looked at the tribe inside the private box. "You know about the clone, right? But you don't know it was deposited in me without consent."
"You were raped? Get rid of it.(“ Hayley, like every other female cop, was no-nonsense when it came to rape. ") I will happily charge anyone involved."
"It is not quite that easy. You see all the Elvi here? They believe (think that) I am carrying their Saviour. You may have heard about him, the Elvi call him the one-after-the-one."
"I (had) heard something along those lines, but didn't give it much credence. I mean, it seems more than a little absurd. I have seen the posters everywhere, 'The one-after-the-one is coming', but I thought it was all some sort of advertising campaign. So why can't you abort?" (Hayley had been through the mill a couple of times and it was no big deal for her.) 
"The Elvi are all adolescents. They will go berserk if I terminate. There's 100,000 of them. Can you imagine the carnage? I will most certainly be hunted down and killed, along with Cracker."
"Okay then, don't terminate." 
"There is another problem. Do you remember what Dr Logan told us at the morgue?"
"Yes, but I didn't take it all in, I was still in a bit of shock. With you shooting Cracker and then his remarkable recovery."
"Don't ask how, but there is synthetic DNA attached to the child’s. I don't know what he will turn out like. He could be hideous." Sarah scrunched her face up and looked away.
"I see your dilemma." Hayley said calmly. (“From one perspective you must have the baby and from another perspective you must not.”) Holy tax fraud! What a mess!
"What will happen if I have him?"
"Are you sure it's a him?"
"Yes, the clone will be the same sex as the original." 
"Then I would not worry about the child being hideous. All men are." Hayley quipped and Sarah laughed.
"So true, so true." It was the first time Sarah had laughed since Kathy had told her the news. 
"When they're not trying to fuck you or kill you, men are really funny." Hayley was momentarily deadpan before (they) both busted out giggling. "Men do create problems, but in this situation they, or at least one of them, may be the solution. You must realise the difficulties of raising a child as a single mother. Apart from the obvious problem of doing all the work yourself, you will (be) find discrimination from your boss and disdain from your colleagues, male and female alike. No-one in authority will ever say out loud that you are not wanted, but you will see evidence of it piling up everyday. The treatment of management-level single mothers is unconscionable—upper management does not like square pegs. All their talk about diversity is just that, talk." Hayley had seen it over and over again. 
"Treating mothers like that is disgraceful and just plain moronic." Over many years, Sarah, (just) like Hayley, had seen the terrible abuse single mothers received at the hands of a patriarchal zeitgeist.
"Don't think it is an operation of puritanical morality. It is nothing of the sort. It is a feeding frenzy of the strong destroying the weak, utterly. Like chickens pecking to death the weakest of their number, you will be identified as vulnerable and your workmates will be compelled to humiliate and punish. Senior executives want—no—they demand compliance. It is contemptible but allocation will fix all of this. Just make sure you specify level 5 and above. You don't want to allocate beneath your station." Hayley pointed to Cracker who doing a maniacal-macarana with Kathy.
"Allocation? It's not something that I had ever contemplated. I have always regarded marriage-at-random a sign of desperation."
"Yes."
"Termination or allocation. I think they weigh about the same. I still have some thinking to do, Hayley."
'Not too long I hope. You are not showing at the moment, so you might be able to pass the child off as his, although you would have to get someone pretty stupid though."
"A stupid level 5? Not likely."
#
Eloise
3 to 4 weeks after bombing
Sarah was home alone when the list of new allocations were released. She did not want any company for this, the lowest point of her life. In her estimation, throwing her hat in for random allocation had diminished her completely, her self-esteem evaporated. (This was not a difficulty she had ever contemplated encountering, but) She was trying to make best of this impossible situation. Here goes. Fingers crossed.
She waved her hand over the lounge chair console and watched as the wall screen flicked and flitted, before the image became crisp. Allocations for level 4 had not concluded and as the names, all unknown to her, flashed on the screen she felt overwhelmed by panic - all of these people are going to marry someone they do not know. Oh dear, so am I. She hoped that her allocation partner, whoever he may be, was going through the same hell. He was not. The randomness and integrity of this allocation had been completely corrupted by a vicious algorithm, smeared with the fingerprints of a well paid computer hacker.
Then her name appeared on the screen along side of the name of her betrothed, Alexander Shakti L.5. She was motionless for a very long time and then laughed, then cried and laughed again. (Five minutes prior) She would not have imagined her situation could get any worse. It now infinitely was. 
Her holo-phone rang. She did not answer it then or for the rest of the day. Messages piled up in her in-box(, but she successfully ignored them). When darkness arrived there was a knock at the front door. A knock? She thought, how did anyone get close to my front door without being trounced by security? Why wouldn't they (just) use the doorbell? With curiosity taking control, she (finally) uncoiled from the foetal position, hobbled on numb legs to the door. The one-way view panel showed a slender looking woman, in her late 50's, her thin lips, painted red, pursed closely together. She was sensibly dressed in a subtle yellow dress suit with matching shoes and handbag. 
As Sarah opened the door, the woman stepped back, allowing a large man, standing to the side of the doorway, to push his way in. Sarah was knocked backwards. Still upright, she stood her ground.
"Don't try it, little lady." The man warned.
"That's odd. I was about to issue you with the same warning, large oaf." Sarah shot back, staring him down. He laughed and moved towards her. She was too quick and was twisting his arm behind his back in a flash. "Don't fight it." The giant man's ego refused to obey and he tried to throw himself backwards (in an effort) to break free. (but) Sarah raised his arm and loudly snapped his humerus under the weight of his own body. He screamed and screamed as Sarah threw him to the door. All the while, the lady calmly sat on the lounge, straightening (out) the hem of her skirt.
"Now, let us talk about you." Sarah (returned to the lounge area and) stood squarely in front of the odd lady.
"Well, aren't you just a firebrand?” The woman smiled smugly. "I am Eloise Shakti, your future mother-in-law."
"Was the gorilla necessary?" Sarah was unimpressed with the mother-in-law so far.
"Forgive me for Benjamin. My body guard. He sometimes gets enthusiastic." Eloise smiled (for another second or two) and then her face turned to ash. "Sarah Jacobson, Ph.D. Level 5, Cyber Security Manager, currently under contract with the Police, you poor thing. You earn more than my boy, but unlike him, you have real career prospects."
"Yes, I can't wait to get back to profiteering." She figured she had better sound sufficiently capitalist for her highly regarded mum-to-be.
"Indeed. Alexander said you were a lovely creature, but that does not even begin to describe you, does it? I did not expect you would be so stunning. And you can handle yourself, too, besting Benjamin so easily. So my question is: with all your obvious physical attributes, education and intellectual firepower, what are you doing putting yourself up for random allocation?" It would take a very decent argument to satisfy Eloise that there was nothing suspicious going on here.
"I am not sure I know what you mean. I am 27 and want some certainty in my life." Sarah hoped that would work, but Eloise tittered quietly, showing her perfect teeth, for just a few seconds before she cut it short and allowed her calm dignity to return. 
"You will have to do better than that, my dear. I am not a person to meddle with." (The name of Eloise Shakti was known throughout the dominion as a power broker of the highest order.) Eloise knew she was the subject of fear and disdain. It pleased her greatly. 
Sarah's mind was (now) spinning at triple the normal rate. Neurones fired up as she searched the darkest reaches of her mind to come up with a plausible story. She knew that mother Shakti was here to vet her, but after many fictitious scenarios formed in her head, she concluded that she did not care if she was rejected by the Shakti family. In fact, she would be very pleased if they decided to call it off. "I am pregnant."
"That makes perfect sense dear. Thank you for the truth. It is a rare commodity.”
"I expect you will be calling off the allocation." Sarah was not sad, even if this was the first time she had ever been rejected (by anyone for any reason).
"Not on your life, my dear. I have been waiting years to pass him off to a wife or a husband. I honestly did not care which. He's your problem now, my girl. I just wanted an answer to the question of why someone like you would accept (allocation with) someone like my son. I have my answer. Thank you, dear."
"Should I tell him about the pregnancy?"
"Only to tell him that he is the father. You will have to consummate the relationship quickly for him to think it is his. I will accept the child as my grandchild, happy in the knowledge that whatever caused the genetic car-crash that is my Alexander, it will not happen to this child." She stood and walked to the door. "Thank you my dear. I don't think there is any reason to see you before the ceremony, do you?"
"Unless you and your family want to get to know me." Sarah (actually) thought they might.
"See? No reason at all. I will see you at the ceremony." And she was gone.
Sarah checked her in-box and deleted all the mail titled "SHAKTI? WTF?" She noted a number of messages from Eloise Shakti’s grandson and decided to take her advice. She must consummate the relationship quickly. It seemed very contrived and nothing like what she thought her first time would be like. I did not think I would be giving up my virginity to such an idiot, but all girls give it up for idiots don't they? Whenever she imagined having sex for the first time, it would be with a man tall, strong, handsome, funny, intelligent, gentle and kind. Shakti ticked none of the boxes, but she nevertheless contacted him and invited herself to his apartment. 
She returned from his apartment later that evening, feeling dirty. She had not actually engaged in sex with Shakti, but convinced him he had penetrated her. In reality she held his penis in her hand and finished him off with a grandiose wank while riding reverse cowgirl. She (had) also managed, she hoped, not to pass on the virus she carried. The last thing the world needed to see was a Super-Shakti. Perfection in idiocy. 
Her first shower took (a mere) two hours, but she was still unclean and opted for a sauna and then took some steam before having one more shower. As the water trickled down the drain, she was reminded of the shower in the public facility. She (had) enjoyed herself that night, even if the facilities had been (plain) disgusting. It seemed an age ago, so much water having passed under the bridge, but it had been less than two months.
&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="547176D0-2399-4613-8659-7018E2D90F81">
            <Title>Alibi Interview</Title>
            <Synopsis>All to set up the interview where Shakti is Crackers alibi. This farce is supposed to be amusing, but needs some humouring up.   They get Sarah to enter the interview room (with a disc in hand) and the surveillance feed to the Commissioner goes to white noise.  Hayley demands the truth from Cracker and Sarah - they tell her everything.</Synopsis>
            <Text>Alibi Interview
2 to 3 weeks after Bombing
“Alex, we have to speak. This brief is very skinny. It relies entirely on eyewitness identification in only one murder to link him with the rest. The forensic evidence, identical stab wounds patterns, is pretty flimsy and the cause of death for Mr Marrin is listed as both trampling and blood loss.” There wasn’t too much more evidence in the file that when she had left the brief with the Commissioner, only a few extra pages of intell and one statement.
“So?”
“Novus actus intervenens?”
“Yes, of course.” Shakti pretended to know.
“You have no idea what I am talking about, do you Alex?”
“Not one bit.”
“Don’t worry about it, that defence probably won’t fly, but if he has a decent alibi for the Marrin murder, everything falls to pieces. There is only suspicion attached to the disappearance of Andrea Novetine, but no evidence. His credit card records show he was at the very same places the victims were last seen, but that does not mean murder.” 
“It’s easier on T.V.” Shakti wished he had just put in a couple of cameras in Cracker’s house and then dubbed in a confession.
“Well this is an actual criminal justice matter and to me, evidence counts. If you want to get someone to beat a confession out of him, now is the time to find them.” Hayley waited, but Shakti remained silent. “I suggest we start with the most recent murder, James Marrin and the possible connection with the disappearance of Ms Novetine. When we have questioned him for a few hours he will make a mistake. Once that happens he should crack and we can zero in on the other murders. We might be lucky enough to get confessions.”
“No beatings at all?” 
“Not my style, Lieutenant.” Like all of her colleagues, she had, on occasion roughed up a few crooks during an arrest, but when it came to interviewing she was completely by the book. “Even if it does fast track confessions, resorting to violence is the hallmark of a poorly trained and poorly prepared investigator.”
They entered the interview room and saw Cracker, black hood moving back and forth as he sucked in air, seated and handcuffed to a non-descript desk in the centre of the brightly lit room. Shakti ripped off the hood.
“In the name of The Dawkins, what is this all about Alex?” As per procedure, Cracker had not been told why he had been arrested and was feeling very uneasy. 
“You are the Nightclub Vampire. We have the evidence. It’s all here. Admit it and we will go easy on you. Admit it.” Shakti tried to be professional.
“Lieutenant? Perhaps I may speak to Mr Hardy.” Hayley had to concentrate hard not to roll her eyes, shake her head or punch him in the face. This is amateur hour.
“Okay.”
“Mr Hardy. As you are aware, my name is Detective Superintendent Lanning and this is Lieutenant Shakti. We are investigating the death of James Marrin and the disappearance of Andrea Novetine. Shortly I propose to interview you. My questions and any answers you give to those questions will be recorded on both audio and holograph as the interview takes place. Do you understand that?”
“Okay. Which interview technique are we using today? Cognitive, Reid or P.E.A.C.E?” Cracker desperately hoped that this might distract her from finishing off her little pre-interview spiel, during which time he hoped he could talk his way out of this. I hope it is Reid, that is the worst method by far.
“What do you know of interview techniques, L.2?” She knew he was trying to distract her, but sometimes, most times, when you let them yap, they trip themselves up. Suspects are convinced they are smarter than any dumb-ass cop and try to establish dominance by ceasing control of the interview. To accomplish this they have to talk and getting a suspect to talk was the battle half won. He could have the illusion of dominance, but she would have control.
“I have been required, recently, to brain-dump all of the police regulations and procedures. I have been helping a friend out.” Cracker stared directly at Shakti and shook his head. “Look, I don’t even know James Minion. When am I supposed to have killed him?”
“James Marrin. Between 10.00pm on 21 February and 12.30am on 22 March this year.” Hayley had every detail of this brief memorised and categorized. 
“That does not ring a bell.”
“Let me refresh your memory. It was the night of the Stick it Inn bombing. He was found stabbed and trampled to death in the street after the crowds had been cleared.”
“I have two people who can account for my whereabouts on the night. I assisted a woman by the name of Sarah Jacobson out of the bombed nightclub and then later we both went to help Lieutenant Shakti clean up the public hygiene facility. Isn’t that right Shakti?” The Lieutenant was looking for a place to hide, but no hole appeared in the floor to mercifully swallow him up.
“Oh, Come on! You know him, Shakti? You knew he was with you when Marrin was killed? You cannot be fucking serious.” Detective Lanning was beside herself.
“I didn’t look at the dates.”
“I am exasperated beyond belief, Alex. Did you even read the file?”
“I tried, it was too long and complicated. All those statements and reports looked pretty impressive and the Commissioner told me to arrest him.”
“I am very glad none of this has been recorded. An alibi witness interviewing the suspect! Our illustrious co-workers would have pinned it up on the link for all to see. It’s going to be embarrassing enough to explain, but that will be your problem, Alex. You will have to drag this eyewitness in and check out his story. If Mr Hardy was with you and Sarah, then the witness must be lying.” 
Hayley stood up and opened the door and poked her head out into the main office and said to Sarah, “How did you go with the disc?”
“No-one here knows what it is, but I know someone who might be able to help us. I want official authority to brief him.” Sarah did not want to be accused of leaking information.
“That should not be a problem, but I need you in here for a minute, Sarah. Make sure you bring the disc with you.” It may come in handy.
As Sarah entered the room, the surveillance directly linked to the Commissioner’s Office was interrupted. He jumped up and bashed the emitter, but there was nothing by white noise. He had heard more than enough. Picking Shakti for this job was a major mistake, but he reflected, picking him for any job was an act of sheer stupidity. He knew Shakti would fuck up the interview, but it did not matter, once Hardy was placed into processing his fate was certain.
“Shakti, go and chase up that eyewitness and record Anthony Hardy as a suspect in custody, but not charged. Okay?” Hayley was happy for any excuse to get rid of him.
“Yes.” Shakti hoped he would remember.
“Now, you two are going to tell me everything.” Detective Lanning sat down and commenced the interrogation. </Text>
        </Document>
