        </Document>
        <Document ID="ECD14FEB-F44C-44C3-A552-F693FC5C2CEA">
            <Title>The Big Sleep</Title>
            <Synopsis>The story here stretches credulity a bit.  At the end I want the virus locked down in the vault, but Sarah (who is only young girl at the time) also has to get the virus.  The lab gets locked down - so how does she get the virus?
I chose a pretty banal storyline whereby the Professor is the partner of Sarah's mother.  Knowing that they will be locked down, he sends a sample to Sarah's mother so she could inoculate herself and Sarah (and I think I might have a sister somewhere too, but I may have taken her out of the book)  I think this bit needs a re-think/edit.
Further to this, (in another chapter) the lab complex is dug up 20 years later and I have the occupants inside the lab alive because they have the virus and they had planned for such an eventuality. They weren't even affected by mental illness because of the virus.</Synopsis>
            <Text>
			The Big Sleep

The unmistakable sound of the outer security doors of the complex closing and sound of the interlock rang out as the small team of micro-biologists and genetic engineers gathered in the meeting room.
“What did he say?” Dr. Janine Anstey asked. She was the real genius in this gathering of the scientific elite. A virologist in her mid-fifties, but she looked thirty, tops. Not one silver hair atop her shapely skull and her eyes sparkled brightly a youthfully iridescent pale blue. She had little need of make-up, the faint acne scarring of her youth completely faded, giving way to silk smooth skin.
“We are to remain in lock-down until further notice. No messages in or out. Chairman Hardy says there is a credible terrorist threat and he doesn’t want the virus getting out.” Professor Herrman tried out the official lie. “We would be a high value target.” He added to see if he could whip up a little anti-terrorist hysteria.
“He doesn’t want us getting out.” Interjected Howard Millicent, the youthful post doctoral research assistant. “Look at me! I am 29 and I look like I just finished high school! Someone was bound to notice and Mr Hardy does not want this secret to get out. We are now a problem, a problem to be solved. I shudder at what Mr. Hardy has in store for us.”
“I concur. They don’t want to lock terrorists out, they want to lock us in. What do you have to say Professor Herrman?” Dr. Anstey politely inquired.
“We all agreed that this was a possibility. I must admit I had hoped he had embraced it for the earth shattering discovery that it is, but that appears to have been wishful thinking. What of the packages, Sun Ye? Were they delivered?”
“I only got yours out, professor. The rest of us thought we would have more time.” She took a short breath. “I may never see my family again.” She too was blooming from the effects of the virus. Her fine Asian features had always been pleasing to the eye, but her two front teeth, possessed of a bitter hatred, had grown far apart, each twisting to face away in disgust. The other teeth, cramped for room, had poked out at peculiar angles making her most able to eat an apple through a tennis-racquet. Of late however, her teeth had reconciled and become good friends, cuddling up in neat rows of gleaming white. “You are not married, are you professor? Who did you send it to?”
“Someone very reliable.” He hoped that was true. He had only known her for a short time, but was certain she was the one for him. Her delightful daughters were as intelligent and stunning as her mother and brought much longed for joy to his soulless life of science.
“Did you tell Mr Hardy how it effects evolution?” Dr Anstey was stern, but just bursting out of that blouse.
“I didn’t have time.” Professor Herman was unconvincing. He thought to tell his boss but then got caught up in preparing the innoculant and then the leading man of Ulyssia was gone. “Where was I supposed to begin? Mr Hardy, sir, in the name of pure research……”
“Pure research? In the name of your vainglorious curiosity more like it!” Anstey was having none of it. “What on earth made you splice tardigrade DNA into the virus? Such a cliche.”
“Because they are nearly impossible to kill. They survived 5 extinction events over millions of years, almost unchanged. It seems to me that they are either a perfect organisms or so immediately adaptable to vastly different conditions, evolution can do no more. They are so tough that they can live in boiling water, in ice, on the highest mountain and depths of the sea. They can survive with no water or food, they can survive in open space. Why wouldn’t I want to make my designs just as durable?” It seemed to be a no-brainer to the Professor. 
“Don’t try to teach me how to suck eggs. I know how tough a tardigrade is.” Dr Anstey did not like to be lectured on the incredibly obvious. Everyone knew about tardigrades, didn’t they? 
 “I didn’t just use the tardegrade, I spliced in a number of other gene-profiles including three types of extremo-phile organisms from deep sea volcanic vents and from anaerobic lakes filled with sulpher and cyanide. I have spliced together the toughest hybrid DNA known. This is tardegrade DNA on steroids.”
She was, however, most interested to know how the gene transfer was achieved without contamination. “What was your methodology? How did you keep the sample clear of impurities?”
“I did not want to transfer any foreign genes from unwanted bacteria so the selected individual was placed in a sterile agar dish, prepared with with 1% penicillin streptomycin. I left it there for 48 hours without food to clear the gut content and remove surface microorganisms. After 48 hours, it was repeatedly washed on a sterile nylon mesh with 30 μm pores and observed under the microscope at 1000 times magnification to ensure there were no remaining surface bacteria. Then I just followed normal protocols. You know the drill.”
“How many freeze-thaw cycles?”
“Two. The cleaned animal was placed in a low-binding PCR tube and a genomic lysis buffer with 0.5% beta-mercaptoenthanol. It was then subjected to the two freeze-thaw cycles and the genomic DNA was extracted and sheared to target fragments. It was a mere matter of collecting, purifying and quantifying for sequencing. I was prepared for a long trial and error period of splicing and re-splicing, but the very first attempt produced our Tardigrade-Hybrid Virus. It is every bit as tough as the Tardigrade itself.” He could not believe his luck.
“Your ego has condemned us to a slow death, no doubt even slower with the un-killable virus prolonging our agony.” Dr Anstey walked towards the door, as if to make a grand exit, but stopped and turned to looked at her colleagues. “They are going to lock us in here forever, you know that don’t you?”
“We have made certain plans.” Professor Hermann sounded confident, but his mind was filled with doubt. “I think we can hold out for as long as it takes.”
“I hope those plans involve strippers and hookers. I am presently suffering with the hormone profile on a 17 year old boy and I don’t think I can hold out for even 10 more minutes, let alone 10 years. My balls are about to explode.” Howard cupped his crotch gently. His post graduate work could wait, there was other business, most urgent.
“Sorry, no hookers.”
“Man, this is going to a be a drag.”
“I wouldn’t be too certain.” Dr Anstey eyes were lit up with mischief. “I presently have the hormone profile of a 30 year old woman and we are going to have a bit of time on our hands. Besides, you look so cute as a 17 year old.”
“Thank you, thank you. If you don’t mind Dr Anstey. My need appears to be a little urgent.” He said.
“Of course. My office?” She suggested, but he was already there. “This conversation is not over Dr Hermman, just postponed” She said as she scurried out. 

</Text>
        </Document>
